    skos:definition "Update Network Premise based on aligned overrides"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.1.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perform overrides (with rationale) in Network Premise"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-1-4 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3-1 ;
    skos:definition "Review out of cycle constraints and update premise as needed"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.1.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Identify changes needed out of cycle for network premise"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-2-1 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3-2 ;
    skos:definition "Run DPO model input spreadsheet and generate DPO base plan"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.2.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Generate DPO Base Plan"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-2-2 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3-2 ;
    skos:definition "Review DPO Base Plan output in XPIMS <>DPO Reconciliation Meeting and reconcile model constraints between XPIMS and DPO"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.2.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Share DPO Base Plan in XPIMS DPO Reconciliation"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-3-1 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3-3 ;
    skos:definition "Review distribution network constraints and evaluate impacts to distribution plan (e.g., network maintenance activitiy)"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.3.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Receive updated constraints from Schedulers for impacts to Distribution Plan"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-3-2 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3-3 ;
    skos:definition "Make updates to Distribution Plan in DPO"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.3.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perform updates to Distribution Plan"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-4 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "Check and Update (as needed) Demand, Pricing, Crude, Premise, and Inventory Levels for Local Level for month M"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Handover Final Distribution Plan"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-5-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16501" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16501"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Rolling Network Plan"@en,
        "Short-Term Supply Plan"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3-5 ;
    skos:definition "Produce the rolling days-to-weeks network supply plan that bridges the approved regional plan and daily execution — projecting stock, demand, production, and movements by location and product, and setting the movement quantities and timings the schedule will execute."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.5.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Develop Short-Term Network Plan"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers building and refreshing the short-term network plan: netting projected supply, demand, and stock by location and product; identifying surpluses, shortfalls, and timing constraints; setting planned movements and their windows; reconciling to the regional plan and flagging material deviations back to planning. Excludes plan-period optimization, owned by Regional Optimization (CM-1-1-2); excludes issuing nominations and movement administration (CM-1-1-3-6); consumes replenishment requirements from Manage Inventory Replenishment (CM-1-1-3-7-9) rather than re-deciding them, per the batch 04 decision-right rule.
In scope: Project supply, demand, and stock by location and product | net surpluses and shortfalls and set planned movements | reconcile the short-term plan to the regional plan | flag material deviations to planning
Out of scope: Plan-period optimization (Regional Optimization, CM-1-1-2) | nomination and movement administration (CM-1-1-3-6) | stock-point replenishment decisions (Manage Inventory Replenishment, CM-1-1-3-7-9), whose requirements this plan consumes"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "approved regional plan | replenishment requirements | stock projections | production and receipt forecasts"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "rolling short-term network plan with planned movements"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Set the executable days-to-weeks network movement plan within the approved regional plan."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "weekly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-ORP-DPSC-001 | SRC-LNL-T2S-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Regional Optimization | uses-input: Manage Inventory Replenishment | precedes: Plan & Execute Nominations"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 05 (2026-09-19). Bridges the monthly regional plan and daily movement administration; the replenishment decision right stays with CM-1-1-3-7-9 per batch 04 — this plan consumes those requirements. APQC 16501 is an unrelated channel-improvement candidate; comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-5-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16820" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16820"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Schedule-Balancing Trade Coordination"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3-5 ;
    skos:definition "Identify schedule imbalances that cannot be resolved with the company's own supply — surpluses to place or shortfalls to cover — and coordinate with Supply And Trading to request, evaluate, and incorporate the purchases, sales, or exchanges that balance the short-term schedule."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.5.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Coordinate Product Trading"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the scheduling-side interface to trading: quantifying the imbalance and its product, quantity, timing, location, and quality requirements; requesting trading action; evaluating proposed trades for schedulability; and incorporating agreed volumes into the schedule. Excludes trade negotiation and execution, owned by Supply And Trading; excludes plan-period supply sourcing, owned by Regional Optimization (CM-1-1-2); excludes exchange agreement management, owned by Supply And Trading. Trading decides the deal; scheduling decides the schedule.
In scope: Quantify schedule surpluses and shortfalls needing trade action | request and specify trading requirements | evaluate proposed trades for schedulability | incorporate agreed trade volumes into the schedule
Out of scope: Trade negotiation and execution (Supply And Trading) | plan-period supply sourcing (Regional Optimization, CM-1-1-2) | exchange agreement management (Supply And Trading)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "short-term network plan imbalances | product quality and location requirements | trading availability and proposals"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "trade requests and schedulable trade volumes incorporated into the schedule"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Get schedule imbalances covered through trading without owning the trade."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-ORP-DPSC-001 | SRC-EIA-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Develop Short-Term Network Plan"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 05 (2026-09-19). Read as the scheduling-side interface to trading, mirroring Coordinate Feedstock Trading (CM-1-2-5-2-1) on the feedstock side and the established decide-versus-advise pattern: this node specifies what the schedule needs and consumes the result; Supply And Trading owns the trade. Whether this coordination is consistently owned by Commercial & Marketing across operating regions is a follow-up noted in the batch 05 PR. No APQC candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-5-3 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3-5 ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.5.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Process Business Renewal Request"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-6-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20851" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20851"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Nominations Management"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3-6 ;
    skos:definition "Prepare, submit, and manage volume nominations to pipelines, vessels, rail, and terminal operators — matching the short-term schedule to each carrier's cycles, batch specifications, and deadlines, and managing confirmations, revisions, and allocations through the cycle."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.6.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Plan & Execute Nominations"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the shipper-side nomination process: translating planned movements into nominations per carrier tariff and cycle rules, including segregated versus fungible batch specifications and cycle deadlines; submitting, confirming, and revising nominations; managing carrier allocation and prorationing effects; and keeping the schedule aligned with confirmed nominations. Excludes the carrier's own pipeline scheduling and batching operation; excludes movement quantity decisions, owned by Product Scheduling (CM-1-1-3-5); excludes physical transport, owned by Midstream and carriers.
In scope: Prepare nominations per carrier cycles and specifications | submit, confirm, and revise nominations | manage carrier allocations and prorationing effects | align the schedule with confirmed nominations
Out of scope: Carrier pipeline scheduling and batching operation (carriers, Midstream) | movement quantity decisions (CM-1-1-3-5) | physical transport (Midstream, carriers)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "planned movements | carrier tariffs, cycles, and deadlines | batch specifications | confirmed capacity"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "confirmed nominations aligned to the schedule"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Secure and maintain the carrier capacity the schedule needs, cycle by cycle."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "monthly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-DOE-RPPL-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Develop Short-Term Network Plan | precedes: Loading Shipment"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 05 (2026-09-19). Nomination practice per the DOE backgrounder: shippers nominate to common carriers roughly monthly, movements schedule in multi-day cycles, batches are segregated or fungible per tariff specification; revision and allocation management is intra-cycle. Evidence is contextual — nomination frequency, tariffs, and cycle structures vary by carrier and are not asserted as this organization's model. 'Execute' is read as executing the nomination process, not the physical movement. APQC 20851 is an unrelated IT candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-6-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10226" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10226"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Load Administration"@en,
        "Shipment Loading Administration"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3-6 ;
    skos:definition "Administer the loading of scheduled shipments at refineries and terminals — authorizing the lifting, confirming loaded quantities and quality documentation, and capturing the load into movement and inventory records — so each shipment leaves accounted for and per schedule."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.6.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Loading Shipment"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers shipper-side load administration: releasing and authorizing scheduled loads, confirming loaded volumes and documentation (measurement tickets, bills of lading, quality certificates) per despatch practice (EI HM 32), recording the load against movement and stock records, and flagging load exceptions. The physical loading operation is owned by the terminal operator (Midstream or third party) per the locked Midstream rule. Excludes nomination management (CM-1-1-3-6-1) and in-transit tracking (CM-1-1-3-6-3).
In scope: Authorize and release scheduled loads | confirm loaded quantities and quality documentation | record loads into movement and inventory records | flag load exceptions
Out of scope: Physical loading operation (terminal operator: Midstream or third party) | nomination management (CM-1-1-3-6-1) | in-transit tracking (CM-1-1-3-6-3)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "confirmed schedule and nominations | measurement tickets and quality certificates | terminal load confirmations"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "documented, recorded loads"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Get each scheduled load authorized, documented, and recorded."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EI-HM32-001 | SRC-DOE-RPPL-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Plan & Execute Nominations | precedes: Track Movements and Inventory Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 05 (2026-09-19). Owner-versus-executor per the locked Midstream rule, following the batch 04 Measure Inventory precedent; despatch measurement and documentation practice per EI HM 32. Preferred normalized label 'Administer Shipment Loading' queued for the naming pass (Hamid, batch 05) — the current label reads as an activity in progress rather than a stable process name. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-6-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10353" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10353"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "In-Transit Inventory Tracking"@en,
        "Movement Tracking"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3-6 ;
    skos:definition "Track scheduled movements from load to discharge — monitoring progress against the schedule, maintaining in-transit inventory visibility by movement, and updating scheduling and inventory records as movements progress — so the network always knows what is en route, where, and when it will arrive."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.6.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Track Movements and Inventory Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers in-transit tracking and its inventory effects: movement status against schedule, arrival estimates and delays, in-transit stock by product and movement, and feeding position updates to inventory and scheduling processes. The label's 'Inventory Management' is bounded to movement-linked in-transit inventory only; stock-point position stewardship is owned by Manage Inventory (CM-1-1-3-7-12) per batch 04. Excludes carrier transport operation, owned by Midstream and carriers.
In scope: Monitor movement status and arrival estimates against schedule | maintain in-transit inventory visibility by movement | update scheduling and inventory records as movements progress | flag movement delays and exceptions
Out of scope: Stock-point position stewardship (Manage Inventory, CM-1-1-3-7-12) | carrier transport operation (Midstream, carriers) | discharge administration (CM-1-1-3-6-4)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "load records | carrier status and arrival estimates | movement schedule"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "current in-transit positions and movement status"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep en-route product visible and the schedule honest about arrivals."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-LNL-T2S-001 | SRC-EI-HM32-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Loading Shipment | enables: Manage Inventory | precedes: Discharge & Reconcile Load"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 05 (2026-09-19). The label's 'Inventory Management' is bounded to in-transit, movement-linked inventory; stock-point positions belong to CM-1-1-3-7-12 per batch 04. APQC 10353 'Track inventory deployment' is contextual evidence only (review-link)."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-6-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12940" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12940"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Discharge Administration"@en,
        "Outturn Reconciliation"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3-6 ;
    skos:definition "Administer the discharge of arriving shipments — confirming received quantities and quality documentation, reconciling discharged against loaded volumes to surface in-transit gains and losses per movement, and closing the movement in scheduling and inventory records."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.6.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Discharge & Reconcile Load"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers receipt-side load administration per EI HM 32 receipt practice: confirming outturn quantities and documentation, comparing loaded against discharged volumes per movement, resolving or escalating discrepancies, and closing the movement. The physical discharge operation is owned by the terminal operator (Midstream or third party). Per-movement outturn reconciliation feeds, but does not replace, periodic book-to-physical reconciliation, owned by Perform Inventory Reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5); claims against carriers follow the relevant commercial agreements.
In scope: Confirm outturn quantities and documentation | reconcile discharged against loaded volumes per movement | resolve or escalate per-load discrepancies | close movements in scheduling and inventory records
Out of scope: Physical discharge operation (terminal operator: Midstream or third party) | periodic book-to-physical reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5) | carrier claims settlement (commercial agreements)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "outturn measurement tickets | load records | movement documentation"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "closed, reconciled movements with per-load gain and loss"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Close each movement with received quantities confirmed and losses surfaced."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EI-HM32-001 | SRC-API-MPMS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Track Movements and Inventory Management | enables: Perform Inventory Reconciliation"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 05 (2026-09-19). Boundary to CM-1-1-3-7-5: per-movement outturn reconciliation here; periodic book-to-physical reconciliation there. Receipt measurement and loss-surfacing practice per EI HM 32. APQC 12940 'Reconcile inventories' is a WEAK review-link."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-7-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10346" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10346"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Crude & Feedstock Inventory Policy"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3-7 ;
    skos:definition "Establish and maintain the policy governing crude and feedstock inventories — target and minimum operating levels, safety and strategic stock requirements, tankage and segregation rules, and review triggers — so feedstock supply to refineries is protected at an efficient working-capital cost."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.7.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Develop Crude/Feedstock Inventory Policy"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the recurring development, approval, and review of crude and feedstock inventory policy across refinery and terminal storage, including minimum operating and safety stock levels, quality-segregation requirements by grade, and the conditions that trigger policy review. Excludes day-to-day management of feedstock positions against the policy, which sits with Supply And Trading (Manage Feedstock Inventory, CM-1-2-5-4-2) per the batch 04 decision; excludes plan-period inventory choices, owned by Regional Optimization (CM-1-1-2); excludes physical tank operation, owned by Midstream and Refining.
In scope: Set target, minimum operating, and safety stock levels for crude and feedstock | define segregation and tankage policy by grade | define policy review triggers and cadence | approve policy exceptions
Out of scope: Day-to-day feedstock position management (Manage Feedstock Inventory, CM-1-2-5-4-2) | plan-period inventory positioning (Regional Optimization, CM-1-1-2) | crude purchasing (Supply And Trading) | physical storage operation (Midstream, Refining)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "refinery feedstock requirements | supply lead times and variability | tankage and segregation constraints | working-capital guidance"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "approved crude and feedstock inventory policy"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Set the binding policy for how much crude and feedstock is held, where, and under what rules."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EI-HM31-001 | SRC-ASCM-CSCP-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Refinery Planning | enables: Manage Inventory"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 04 (2026-09-19). One of three sibling policy nodes split by material class; the split follows the physical flow (feedstock → intermediates → finished product), and each policy binds the processes managing that class. APQC 10346 is a WEAK delivery-service-policy candidate; comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-7-10 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12942" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12942"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Batch-Tracked Inventory Management"@en,
        "Lot Traceability Management"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3-7 ;
    skos:definition "Manage inventory that must be tracked by lot or batch identity — such as specialty products, additives, and quality-certified stock — preserving batch identity, certificates, and traceability from receipt through storage to issue."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.7.10" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Lot/Batch Inventory"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers lot- and batch-level stock control where identity must survive storage and movement: assigning and preserving batch identity in stock records, holding certificates of analysis and quality status against each lot, applying lot-selection rules at issue, and supporting recall and claims traceability. Distinct from fungible bulk stock managed as commingled entitlements (CM-1-1-3-7-7), where batch identity is deliberately not preserved. Excludes laboratory testing and certificate generation, owned by the operating asset's quality function; excludes batch production and blending execution, owned by Refining.
In scope: Assign and preserve lot/batch identity in stock records | hold quality certificates and status by lot | apply lot-selection rules at issue | maintain lot traceability for recall and claims
Out of scope: Laboratory testing and certification (operating asset quality function) | batch production and blending execution (Refining) | fungible bulk entitlement tracking (CM-1-1-3-7-7)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "batch and lot identifiers from production and receipt | certificates of analysis | lot-selection rules"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "traceable lot/batch stock records with quality status"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Preserve batch identity, quality status, and traceability for stock that must not be treated as fungible."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-ASCM-CSCP-001 | SRC-EI-HM32-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Perform Inventory Reconciliation | informed-by: Develop Finished Product Inventory Policy"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 04 (2026-09-19). The lot/batch-versus-fungible split is the organizing boundary with CM-1-1-3-7-7: identity-preserved stock here, entitlement-tracked fungible stock there — modeled as arrangements or control requirements, not permanent asset classifications. Relevant to specialty and additive lines where certificates and recall traceability are required."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-7-11 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12944" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12944"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Leased Storage Management"@en,
        "Third-Party Terminal Storage Management"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3-7 ;
    skos:definition "Manage company inventory held in third-party storage facilities — leased tankage, throughput agreements, and commercial storage — administering capacity entitlements, monitoring stock positions and operator statements, and managing performance against the storage agreements."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.7.11" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Third Party Storage"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the inventory-side administration of third-party storage: tracking company stock by facility and agreement, reconciling operator stock statements, monitoring utilization of leased capacity and throughput commitments, and flagging agreement performance issues. Excludes negotiating storage and terminalling agreements, owned by the commercial owner (Supply And Trading); excludes the operator's physical facility operation; excludes commingled entitlement mechanics, owned by Manage Commingled Inventory (CM-1-1-3-7-7) where stock in a third-party facility is fungible and shared.
In scope: Track company stock by third-party facility and agreement | reconcile operator stock statements | monitor leased capacity and throughput utilization | flag storage-agreement performance issues
Out of scope: Storage agreement negotiation (Supply And Trading) | physical facility operation (third-party operator) | commingled entitlement tracking (CM-1-1-3-7-7)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "storage and throughput agreements | operator stock statements | company movement records"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "reconciled third-party storage positions and utilization status"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep company stock in third-party facilities visible, reconciled, and within agreement entitlements."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EI-HM32-001 | SRC-ASCM-CSCP-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Perform Inventory Reconciliation | uses-input: Measure Inventory"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 04 (2026-09-19). APQC 12944 'Manage third party bulk storage' scored 0.889 STRONG with no validator source; used as supporting comparative evidence, with the local definition bounded to inventory administration rather than facility operation. Governing distinction is the facility/custody boundary: company-owned stock in someone else's facility. Orthogonal to consignment (ownership boundary, CM-1-1-3-7-6) — both can apply simultaneously — and to commingled (entitlement in shared fungible stock, CM-1-1-3-7-7)."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-7-12 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12942" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12942"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Inventory Position Management"@en,
        "Monitor and Control Inventory Positions"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3-7 ;
    skos:definition "Monitor and control actual and projected inventory positions for finished products and intermediates: identify operating-limit, availability, ownership, quality, location, and balance exceptions, coordinate timely response, and provide reliable current-position information to planning and operational stakeholders."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.7.12" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Inventory"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the recurring review of physical and book inventory positions, movements, and commitments; minimum, maximum, safety-stock, and operating-limit exceptions; short-term availability and imbalance risks; escalation and coordination of corrective actions; and maintenance of the operational inventory-position view that planning and execution consume — a recurring operational control rhythm that complements planning cycles. Excludes inventory-policy design (CM-1-1-3-7-1/-2/-3), formal measurement methodology (CM-1-1-3-7-4), reconciliation and adjustment governance (CM-1-1-3-7-5), and replenishment planning (CM-1-1-3-7-9); excludes the specialized consignment, commingled, re-brand, lot/batch, and third-party-storage arrangements (CM-1-1-3-7-6/-7/-8/-10/-11), except where those arrangements create an operational position exception requiring response; excludes day-to-day crude and feedstock position management, owned by Manage Feedstock Inventory (CM-1-2-5-4-2).
In scope: Review physical and book positions, movements, and commitments by location | detect operating-limit, availability, and balance exceptions | escalate and coordinate corrective actions | maintain the operational position view for planning and execution
Out of scope: Inventory policy design (CM-1-1-3-7-1/-2/-3) | measurement methodology (CM-1-1-3-7-4) | reconciliation governance (CM-1-1-3-7-5) | replenishment planning and decisions (CM-1-1-3-7-9) | specialized arrangement controls (CM-1-1-3-7-6/-7/-8/-10/-11) | feedstock position management (CM-1-2-5-4-2)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "official measured positions | movement schedules | policy operating limits | demand offtake"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "current position picture and excursion responses"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep day-to-day finished-product and intermediates positions inside operating limits and make the current position visible."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EI-HM32-001 | SRC-LNL-T2S-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Measure Inventory | triggers: Manage Inventory Replenishment | governed-by: Develop Finished Product Inventory Policy"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 04 (2026-09-19). Q2 decision (Hamid, Option A, tightened): approved in place as a distinct operational process — position stewardship and exception response, which no sibling owns — with material scope limited to finished products and intermediates so it cannot silently reclaim feedstock (Q3). Preferred normalized label 'Monitor and Control Inventory Positions' recorded for the consolidated naming pass; current label retained for traceability, and the collision with the L4 label is queued with it. Decision right: this process identifies and escalates replenishment needs; Manage Inventory Replenishment (CM-1-1-3-7-9) owns the replenishment decision. Daily stock control as distinct from periodic reconciliation follows EI HM 32 practice. APQC 12942 'Manage bulk inventory' STRONG with no validator; comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-7-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10346" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10346"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Intermediates & Blendstock Inventory Policy"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3-7 ;
    skos:definition "Establish and maintain the policy governing intermediate and blendstock inventories held between process units and blending — target working levels, segregation and quality-protection rules, and review triggers — so conversion and blending operations are decoupled without trapping excess working capital."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.7.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Develop Intermediates Inventory Policy"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers policy for unfinished streams held between refinery processing stages and blending, including working-level ranges that buffer unit outages and campaign changes, segregation rules that protect intermediate quality, and policy review cadence tied to unit campaigns and turnarounds. Excludes intermediate tank and transfer operation, owned by Refining; excludes the production decisions that generate intermediates, owned by Refinery Planning (CM-1-1-4) and Refining; excludes finished-product policy, owned by Develop Finished Product Inventory Policy (CM-1-1-3-7-3).
In scope: Set working-level ranges for intermediates and blendstocks | define segregation and quality-protection rules | define review triggers tied to campaigns and turnarounds | approve policy exceptions
Out of scope: Intermediate tank and transfer operation (Refining) | production planning of intermediates (Refinery Planning, CM-1-1-4) | finished-product policy (CM-1-1-3-7-3) | crude and feedstock policy (CM-1-1-3-7-1)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "unit campaign and turnaround plans | blending requirements | intermediate tankage constraints | quality-segregation requirements"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "approved intermediates inventory policy"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Set the binding policy for intermediate and blendstock holdings between processing and blending."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EI-HM31-001 | SRC-ASCM-CSCP-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Refinery Planning | enables: Manage Inventory"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 04 (2026-09-19). 'Intermediates' read as unfinished refinery streams and blend components held between processing and blending. Middle node of the three material-class policy siblings. Operational accountability for intermediates (Refining versus planning domains) is a follow-up noted in the batch 04 PR; it does not affect this node's placement or definition."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-7-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16801" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16801"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Finished Goods Inventory Policy"@en,
        "Product Inventory Policy"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3-7 ;
    skos:definition "Establish and maintain the policy governing finished-product inventories across refineries and the distribution network — target and minimum operating levels, safety stock by product and location, brand and quality segregation rules, and review triggers — so customer supply reliability is protected at an efficient working-capital cost."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.7.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Develop Finished Product Inventory Policy"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers policy for saleable products at refinery rack, terminal, and depot level, including service-level-driven safety stocks, seasonal and regulatory grade-transition stock rules, and segregation of branded versus unbranded stock. Sets the policy that Plan Finished Goods Inventory (CM-1-1-2-13) plans within — the plan-period target decision itself is owned there per its approved boundary. Excludes replenishment management, owned by Manage Inventory Replenishment (CM-1-1-3-7-9); excludes physical terminal operation, owned by Midstream.
In scope: Set target, minimum operating, and safety stock levels by product and location | define brand and grade segregation policy | set seasonal and grade-transition stock rules | define review triggers and cadence
Out of scope: Plan-period inventory targets (Plan Finished Goods Inventory, CM-1-1-2-13) | replenishment management (CM-1-1-3-7-9) | physical storage operation (Midstream) | sales and offer decisions (Marketing)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "demand forecast and service-level requirements | seasonal and regulatory grade calendars | terminal tankage constraints | working-capital guidance"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "approved finished-product inventory policy"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Set the binding policy for finished-product holdings across the network."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EI-HM32-001 | SRC-ASCM-CSCP-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Plan Finished Goods Inventory | enables: Manage Inventory Replenishment | informed-by: Demand Forecasting"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 04 (2026-09-19). Boundary to CM-1-1-2-13 was fixed in batch 01 (Hamid, 2026-09-18): policy here, plan-period target decision there. 'Finished product' preferred in text; the batch-01 sibling retains 'finished goods' in its label, with both carried as alt labels there."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-7-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16436" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16436"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Physical Inventory Measurement"@en,
        "Stock Measurement"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3-7 ;
    skos:definition "Establish the measured physical quantity of crude, intermediate, and product inventory across the network — from tank gauging, metering, and vessel and pipeline measurements converted to standard conditions — so book stock, reconciliation, and planning rest on accurate physical positions."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.7.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Measure Inventory"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the recurring determination of physical inventory quantities: setting the measurement basis and standards applied (per API MPMS practice), collecting gauge, meter, temperature, and density data, converting observed to standard volumes, and establishing the official measured position by tank, terminal, and location. Asset operators — Midstream and Refining — operate the tanks and instruments and take the physical readings; this process owns the standards applied and the official measured position, consistent with the owner-versus-executor pattern in the locked rules. Excludes reconciling measured against book stock, owned by Perform Inventory Reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5); excludes custody-transfer measurement disputes, which follow the relevant commercial agreements.
In scope: Define the measurement basis and applicable standards | collect gauging, metering, temperature, and density data | convert observed to standard volumes | establish the official measured position by location
Out of scope: Book-versus-physical reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5) | operating tanks, meters, and instruments (Midstream, Refining) | inventory valuation (Finance)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "tank gauge and meter readings | temperature and density observations | vessel and pipeline measurement tickets | measurement standards and procedures"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "official measured inventory position by tank, terminal, and location"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Produce the accurate, standards-based physical inventory position the network runs on."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-API-MPMS-001 | SRC-EI-HM32-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "precedes: Perform Inventory Reconciliation | enables: Manage Inventory"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 04 (2026-09-19). Measurement practice follows API MPMS (Chapter 3 tank gauging and related chapters) as the reference standard set. Ownership-versus-execution split follows the locked EHS pattern: asset owners execute readings on assets they operate; this process owns the measurement basis and the official measured position. APQC 16436 'Measure and benchmark' is a WEAK organizational-performance candidate; comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-7-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10507" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10507"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Book-to-Physical Reconciliation"@en,
        "Stock Reconciliation"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3-7 ;
    skos:definition "Reconcile book inventory against measured physical inventory by location and material, quantify and classify gains and losses, investigate variances beyond tolerance, and post agreed corrections so stock records remain accurate and losses are made visible."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.7.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perform Inventory Reconciliation"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the recurring book-to-physical comparison across tanks, terminals, and in-transit stock: computing over/short by material and location, separating apparent loss (measurement and accounting effects) from physical loss, investigating out-of-tolerance variances, and agreeing corrections to book stock, following hydrocarbon-management good practice (EI HM 32). Excludes taking the physical measurements, owned by Measure Inventory (CM-1-1-3-7-4); excludes financial write-off approval and valuation entries, owned by Finance under the locked Finance rule. A standing loss-control capability that would own systemic loss-reduction programs has no home in the current tree — an open cross-model question carried in the playbook (step 7), deliberately not resolved here.
In scope: Compare book with measured stock by material and location | quantify and classify gains and losses (apparent versus physical) | investigate out-of-tolerance variances | agree and post book corrections
Out of scope: Physical measurement (CM-1-1-3-7-4) | financial write-offs and valuation entries (Finance) | systemic loss-control programs (no established owner; playbook step 7 open question) | custody-transfer dispute settlement (commercial agreements)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "official measured positions | book stock and movement transactions | measurement tolerances | prior variance history"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "reconciled stock records with classified, investigated gain/loss"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep book stock true to physical stock and make gains and losses visible and explained."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "monthly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EI-HM32-001 | SRC-API-MPMS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Measure Inventory | enables: Distribution Backcasting | enables: Manage Inventory"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 04 (2026-09-19). APQC 10507 'Perform benefit reconciliation' was REJECTED at the Step 3b human gate as a false lexical match (HR benefits); authored independently, honoring that rejection. Apparent-versus-physical loss classification follows EI HM 32. Loss-control ownership beyond reconciliation reporting is deliberately left open per playbook step 7 — naming an owner here would silently resolve a cross-model question."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-7-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12942" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12942"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Consignment Stock Management"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3-7 ;
    skos:definition "Manage product held under consignment arrangements — stock positioned at a customer's or partner's site, or held for a third party at company sites, that remains the consignor's property until drawn — tracking positions, draws, and settlement triggers so ownership and stock records stay correct on both sides."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.7.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Consignment Inventory"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers administering consignment stock over its lifecycle: recording consignment positions by agreement, tracking draws and returns, triggering settlement per the agreement, and keeping consignment stock distinct from owned stock in positions and reconciliation. Excludes negotiating the consignment agreement, owned by the commercial owner of the customer or supplier relationship; excludes invoicing and settlement postings, owned by Finance; excludes physical storage operation, owned by the site operator (Midstream or third party).
In scope: Record and track consignment positions by agreement | track draws, returns, and settlement triggers | keep consignment stock segregated from owned stock in records | support consignment reconciliation
Out of scope: Consignment agreement negotiation (commercial relationship owner) | billing and settlement postings (Finance) | physical site storage operation (site operator)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "consignment agreements | draw and return transactions | site stock reports"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "accurate consignment stock positions and settlement triggers"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep consignment stock, its ownership, and its settlement triggers correctly tracked."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-ASCM-CSCP-001 | SRC-EI-HM32-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Perform Inventory Reconciliation | informed-by: Develop Finished Product Inventory Policy"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 04 (2026-09-19). Consignment is defined by ownership: stock remains the consignor's property until a defined drawdown, sale, or title-transfer event — the ownership boundary, not the storage location, distinguishes this node from Manage Third Party Storage (CM-1-1-3-7-11), which covers company-owned stock in third-party facilities. The two are orthogonal arrangements that can apply simultaneously (consignment stock at a third-party terminal); they remain peer processes because the control activities differ, and a future data model should not force them into exclusive subclasses."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-7-7 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12942" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12942"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Commingled Stock Management"@en,
        "Fungible Inventory Management"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3-7 ;
    skos:definition "Manage company stock held commingled with other owners' fungible product in shared tanks, pipelines, and terminals — tracking each party's ownership share, quality basis, and allocated gains and losses under the operative agreements — so the company's entitlement in shared stock is always known and defensible."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.7.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Commingled Inventory"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers stewardship of ownership entitlements in fungible, jointly stored product: recording ownership shares by facility and product, applying the allocation rules of terminalling, exchange, and pipeline agreements to receipts, deliveries, and losses, and reconciling entitlement statements from facility operators. Excludes operating the shared facility, owned by Midstream or the third-party operator; excludes negotiating exchange and terminalling agreements, owned by Supply And Trading; excludes the physical measurement itself, owned by Measure Inventory (CM-1-1-3-7-4).
In scope: Track ownership shares in commingled stock by facility | apply agreement allocation rules to receipts, deliveries, and losses | reconcile operator entitlement statements | keep entitlement records audit-ready
Out of scope: Shared facility operation (Midstream or third-party operator) | exchange and terminalling agreement negotiation (Supply And Trading) | physical measurement (CM-1-1-3-7-4)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "terminalling, exchange, and pipeline agreements | operator entitlement statements | receipt and delivery transactions"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "verified ownership entitlements in commingled stock"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep the company's ownership entitlement in shared, fungible stock known and defensible."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EI-HM32-001 | SRC-EIA-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Perform Inventory Reconciliation | uses-input: Measure Inventory"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 04 (2026-09-19). 'Commingled' read in the downstream fungible-product sense: multiple owners' product physically mixed in shared tankage or pipeline systems, with each party's position represented as a measured or contractual entitlement rather than physically segregated stock. Commingled and lot/batch are arrangements, not permanent asset classifications — one facility can support both for different tanks, products, or periods. APQC 12942 'Manage bulk inventory' STRONG with no validator; comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-7-8 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12410" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12410"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Inventory Reclassification"@en,
        "Product Re-designation"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3-7 ;
    skos:definition "Manage the re-designation of existing inventory between brand, grade, or specification categories — such as converting unbranded stock to branded through additive treatment, or regrading product that qualifies against a different specification — so stock records, quality status, and documentation reflect what the product now is."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.7.8" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Inventory Re-brands"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the decision and record-keeping process of re-designating stock: verifying the product qualifies for the target designation, authorizing the re-brand, updating inventory and quality records, and tracking re-brand volumes and reasons, subject to specification, contractual, tax, and inventory-accounting rules. The physical work — additive injection, blending, laboratory testing — is executed by the operating asset (Midstream terminals or Refining). Excludes brand strategy and consumer brand management, owned by Marketing; excludes off-spec and contaminated-product incident handling, which is an event-driven quality matter, not routine re-branding.
In scope: Verify stock qualifies for the target designation | authorize and record re-brands | update inventory, quality, and documentation records | track re-brand volumes and reasons
Out of scope: Physical additive injection, blending, and testing (Midstream, Refining) | brand strategy and brand management (Marketing) | off-spec and contaminated-product incident handling (event-driven quality process, not routine re-branding)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "stock quality certificates and test results | brand and grade specifications | re-brand authorizations"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "authorized, recorded inventory re-designations"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Ensure re-designated stock is qualified, authorized, and correctly reflected in records."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EI-HM32-001 | SRC-EIA-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Manage Inventory | uses-input: Measure Inventory"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 04 (2026-09-19). 'Re-brand' read in the fuels-inventory sense — controlled re-designation of existing stock between branded/unbranded, grade, commercial identity, or market designation categories — not consumer brand management. The node governs the redesignation decision and records; approved physical blending or additive work is execution by the operating asset. APQC 12410 'Develop and manage brands' scored STRONG lexically but is marketing brand management; treated as a false match, comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-7-9 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12942" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12942"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Replenishment Management"@en,
        "Stock Replenishment Planning"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3-7 ;
    skos:definition "Plan and trigger the replenishment of finished-product stock points across the distribution network — determining when and how much to resupply each terminal and depot within inventory policy and the approved plan — so locations stay between minimum and maximum operating levels without emergency movements."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.7.9" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Inventory Replenishment"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the recurring replenishment decision for network stock points: monitoring projected positions against policy levels, determining replenishment quantities and timing, issuing requirements to the scheduling processes, and managing replenishment exceptions and shortfall risk. Works within the approved regional and distribution plans and the policies set by the policy siblings. Excludes plan-period inventory positioning, owned by Plan Finished Goods Inventory (CM-1-1-2-13); excludes scheduling the physical movements, owned by Physical Distribution Scheduling (CM-1-1-3-6) and Secondary Distribution Management (CM-1-1-3-8); excludes transport and terminal execution, owned by Midstream; excludes feedstock resupply, which sits with Supply And Trading per the batch 04 boundary.
In scope: Monitor projected stock against policy levels by location | determine replenishment quantities and timing | issue replenishment requirements to scheduling | manage replenishment exceptions and shortfall risk
Out of scope: Plan-period inventory targets (CM-1-1-2-13) | movement scheduling (CM-1-1-3-6, CM-1-1-3-8) | transport and terminal execution (Midstream) | feedstock replenishment (Supply And Trading, per the batch 04 boundary)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "projected stock positions | inventory policy levels | approved distribution plan | demand offtake at stock points"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "replenishment requirements by location and timing"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Decide when and how much each network stock point is resupplied within policy and plan."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-LNL-T2S-001 | SRC-ASCM-CSCP-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Develop Finished Product Inventory Policy | precedes: Physical Distribution Scheduling | informed-by: Plan Finished Goods Inventory"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 04 (2026-09-19). Batch 01 already carved 'replenishment execution' out of Plan Finished Goods Inventory to this node; authored to receive that boundary. Scope is finished-product network replenishment; crude and feedstock resupply sits with Supply And Trading per the Q3 decision. Decision right confirmed in batch 04: this node owns the replenishment decision (when and how much); Manage Inventory (CM-1-1-3-7-12) detects and escalates exceptions but does not own the decision; movement scheduling and physical execution remain excluded."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-8-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10136" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10136"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Automatic Order Generation"@en,
        "Managed Replenishment Orders"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3-8 ;
    skos:definition "Create the delivery orders that feed secondary distribution scheduling — deriving replenishment needs for managed retail sites and customer tanks from stock readings, consumption forecasts, and agreed service levels, and converting them into deliverable orders with quantities and windows."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.8.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Generate Orders"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers forecast-driven order generation for managed sites: monitoring site tank levels and consumption, computing run-out risk, and generating and sizing orders within site and vehicle constraints. Excludes customer-initiated orders, owned by Order Management (CM-1-3-7); excludes network terminal replenishment, owned by Manage Inventory Replenishment (CM-1-1-3-7-9); excludes scheduling the deliveries, owned by Schedule Secondary Distribution (CM-1-1-3-8-2).
In scope: Monitor site tank levels and consumption | compute run-out risk and replenishment need | generate and size delivery orders within constraints | set required delivery windows
Out of scope: Customer-initiated order capture (Order Management, CM-1-3-7) | terminal and depot replenishment (CM-1-1-3-7-9) | delivery scheduling (CM-1-1-3-8-2)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "site tank levels and consumption forecasts | service-level agreements | vehicle and site constraints"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "delivery orders with quantities and windows"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Turn site stock and consumption signals into deliverable orders before run-outs."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-LNL-T2S-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "precedes: Schedule Secondary Distribution | informed-by: Demand Forecasting"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 05 (2026-09-19). Three order/replenishment boundaries hold together: customer-initiated orders (CM-1-3-7), network stock-point replenishment (CM-1-1-3-7-9, batch 04), and managed-site delivery orders (here). APQC 10136 'Generate sales forecast' is an unrelated candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-8-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10303" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10303"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Route and Load Scheduling"@en,
        "Secondary Delivery Scheduling"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3-8 ;
    skos:definition "Build and maintain the daily secondary delivery schedule — assigning orders to vehicles, compartments, routes, and time windows within fleet, driver, site-access, and product-segregation constraints — to meet required delivery windows at efficient fleet utilization and cost."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.8.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Schedule Secondary Distribution"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the daily routing and loading schedule: assigning orders to vehicles and compartments, sequencing routes, respecting driver, access, and product-compatibility constraints, and rescheduling on disruption. Excludes creating the orders, owned by Generate Orders (CM-1-1-3-8-1); excludes physical haulage execution, performed by the designated distribution operator, carrier, or logistics provider — outside the scope of this scheduling process; excludes primary movements (CM-1-1-3-6).
In scope: Assign orders to vehicles and compartments | sequence routes and delivery windows | respect fleet, driver, access, and segregation constraints | reschedule on disruption
Out of scope: Order generation (CM-1-1-3-8-1) | physical haulage execution (designated distribution operator, carrier, or logistics provider) | primary distribution scheduling (CM-1-1-3-6)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "delivery orders | fleet, driver, and compartment constraints | site access windows"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "daily delivery schedule with vehicle and route assignments"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Get every order onto the right vehicle, route, and window at least cost."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-LNL-T2S-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Generate Orders | precedes: Monitor Secondary Distribution Performance"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 05 (2026-09-19). Mota et al. 2017 documents exactly this process at Galp (tank-to-station scheduling under normal and disrupted operations). Executor wording per Hamid's batch 05 edit: operating-model neutral. APQC 10303 'Schedule production' is a lexical near-match to a different domain; comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-8-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10198" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10198"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Maintain Secondary Distribution Scheduling Basis"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3-8 ;
    skos:definition "Maintain the reference data, constraints, and operating parameters used for secondary-distribution scheduling — including sites, tanks, vehicles, compartments, drivers, routes, access windows, product-compatibility rules, and scheduling parameters — so generated orders and delivery schedules reflect the current operating network."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.8.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Setup Scheduling System"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers stewardship and controlled maintenance of the secondary-distribution scheduling basis: onboarding, changing, validating, and retiring sites, tanks, vehicles, compartments, drivers, routes, constraints, and scheduling parameters; validating the impact of material changes; and confirming that generated schedules remain operationally feasible. Excludes selection, implementation, configuration, enhancement, integration, technical support, infrastructure operation, and cybersecurity of the scheduling application or platform, which belong to Process Excellence & IT. Excludes enterprise master-data policy, canonical-data-model design, and platform integration architecture, governed through the applicable enterprise data-governance and Process Excellence & IT capabilities. Excludes commercial customer and site onboarding, and the scheduling decisions made in Schedule Secondary Distribution (CM-1-1-3-8-2).
In scope: Onboard, change, validate, and retire sites, tanks, vehicles, compartments, and drivers in the scheduling basis | maintain constraints, compatibility rules, and scheduling parameters | validate the impact of material changes | confirm schedules remain operationally feasible after changes
Out of scope: Scheduling application implementation, support, and platform operation (Process Excellence & IT) | enterprise master-data policy and canonical-data-model design (enterprise data governance) | delivery scheduling decisions (CM-1-1-3-8-2) | commercial customer and site onboarding (Marketing)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "site, tank, and vehicle onboarding and change requests | constraint and parameter definitions | schedule feedback after changes"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "validated scheduling basis and parameters"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep the scheduling basis true to the real fleet, sites, tanks, and constraints."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-ASCM-CSCP-001 | SRC-LNL-T2S-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Schedule Secondary Distribution | enables: Generate Orders"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 05 (2026-09-19). Q2 decision (Hamid, Option A): a recurring process — maintenance of the secondary-distribution scheduling basis — not system implementation, consistent with the master-data precedent (CM-1-1-2-12, CM-1-1-1-1-1, CM-1-1-7-2-1). 'Tanks' added at Hamid's direction as essential to managed-site ordering and run-out prevention; definition opens with 'Maintain' to avoid the one-time-implementation reading. Preferred normalized label 'Maintain Secondary Distribution Scheduling Basis' queued for the naming pass; current label retained for traceability. The parked master-data consolidation question (CM-1-1-2-12, 2026-09-18) applies to this node as well. APQC 10198 is an unrelated sales-order candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-8-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16574" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16574"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Delivery Performance Monitoring"@en,
        "Secondary Distribution Performance Monitoring"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3-8 ;
    skos:definition "Measure and report secondary distribution execution against the issued delivery schedule — delivery reliability, fleet utilization, and cost efficiency at agreed levels — investigating recurring shortfalls and feeding improvements back into order generation, scheduling, and the scheduling basis."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.8.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Monitor Secondary Distribution Performance"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers operational execution monitoring: schedule adherence and delivery completion, run-out and retained-delivery incidents, utilization and cost signals, recurring-issue analysis, and improvement recommendations. Actuals are compared with the schedule as issued, keeping the contemporaneous-baseline discipline. Excludes plan-versus-actual distribution variance analysis, owned by Distribution Backcasting (CM-1-1-6) under its locked rule; excludes defining enterprise KPIs, which belongs to the KPI workstream; excludes haulier contract management.
In scope: Track schedule adherence and delivery completion | monitor run-out and retained-delivery incidents | analyze utilization and delivery cost signals | recommend improvements to orders, scheduling, and the basis
Out of scope: Plan-versus-actual backcasting (Distribution Backcasting, CM-1-1-6) | enterprise KPI definition (KPI workstream) | haulier contract management (commercial owner)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "issued delivery schedules | delivery completion records | fleet utilization and cost data"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "delivery performance report with improvement recommendations"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Establish whether secondary delivery execution is reliable and efficient, and why not when it is not."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-LNL-T2S-001 | SRC-ASCM-CSCP-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Schedule Secondary Distribution | enables: Distribution Backcasting"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 05 (2026-09-19). Bounded against the locked Distribution Backcasting rule: CM-1-1-6 explains variance against the approved distribution plan; this node monitors execution against the issued delivery schedule. Horizon set to 'continuous' per Hamid's batch 05 edit — continuous operational monitoring with periodic reporting and review; the controlled vocabulary takes a single value, so the reporting cadence is recorded here. Specific metrics deliberately not named (KPI workstream). APQC 16574 is a marketing-channel candidate; comparative evidence only."@en .

