proc:CM-1-1-7-1-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20954" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20954"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Issue Production-Schedule Instructions"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-7-1 ;
    skos:definition "Maintain and issue the approved daily production-schedule instructions — dated campaign commitments, production and movement requirements, product priorities, planning assumptions, and approved schedule changes — so the refinery's operating teams receive a current, traceable scheduling basis."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.7.1.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Update Refinery Execution Instructions"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers consolidation, versioning, communication, and controlled revision of the approved scheduling instruction set. It may communicate schedule-level requirements and constraints to refinery operations, but does not prescribe unit feed rates, operating modes, operating procedures, tank or transfer line-ups, blend execution, process-control settings, or other detailed execution methods — those remain within Refining. Excludes changing the monthly plan, owned by Refinery Planning (CM-1-1-4); material schedule deviations flow through Manage Production Schedule Exceptions (CM-1-1-7-2-4).
In scope: Consolidate approved schedule decisions into the instruction set | version and communicate instruction updates | control revisions on approved schedule changes | confirm instructions reflect the current approved schedule
Out of scope: Unit feed rates, operating modes, and process-control settings (Refining) | operating procedures, line-ups, and blend execution (Refining) | monthly plan changes (CM-1-1-4) | schedule exception decisions (CM-1-1-7-2-4)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "current schedule and approved changes | schedule exception outcomes"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "issued, versioned schedule-level instruction set"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep the refinery's schedule-level instruction set current, traceable, and aligned with the approved schedule."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-BJCE-REFPS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Daily Execution Planning | informed-by: Manage Production Schedule Exceptions"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 06 (2026-09-19). Rewritten at Hamid's direction before approval: the original draft named feed rates, operating modes, unit targets, and blend targets, which collide with the locked Refining boundary — instructions here are schedule-level commitments and requirements, never unit-level operating parameters. 'Execution instructions' in the label is read as the scheduling instruction set that provides the approved basis for refinery execution. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-7-1-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20882" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20882"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Schedule-Level Resource Assignment"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-7-1 ;
    skos:definition "Assign and confirm the schedule-level shared resources each day's plan depends on — tankage assumptions, movement and rack windows, berth and loading slots, and other capacity the schedule reserves — so the daily scheduling basis is physically feasible before it is issued."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.7.1.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Schedule Resources"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers schedule-level resource assignment: checking availability, reserving and assigning capacity against the day's plan, resolving conflicts among competing schedule needs, and confirming feasibility. Resource assignment represents capacity reservations and schedule assumptions; it does not direct tank line-ups, valve states, transfers, or other operating configurations. Excludes physical line-ups, transfers, and operation of the resources, owned by Refining and Midstream per the locked rules; excludes maintenance resource and crew planning, owned by Refining's engineering and maintenance functions — outage windows enter the schedule through Manage The Interface to Planning, Operations, Engineering & Maintenance (CM-1-1-7-2-6).
In scope: Check and reserve schedule-level capacity | assign resources to the day's plan | resolve competing schedule resource conflicts | confirm physical feasibility of the daily basis
Out of scope: Physical line-ups, valve states, and resource operation (Refining, Midstream) | maintenance and crew planning (Refining) | outage window intake (CM-1-1-7-2-6)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "daily plan | resource availability and outage windows | competing schedule demands"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "confirmed resource assignments for the daily basis"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Make sure the day's plan has the capacity it assumes."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-SNF-REFPS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Daily Execution Planning | informed-by: Manage The Interface to Planning, Operations, Engineering & Maintenance"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 06 (2026-09-19). 'Resources' read as schedule-level shared capacity — tankage, windows, slots — not personnel rostering or maintenance crews; the capacity-reservation sentence was added at Hamid's direction to protect the boundary where capacity allocation could be confused with refinery operations. APQC 20882 is an IT-services candidate; false lexical match, comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-7-1-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10774" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10774"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Production and Yield Accounting"@en,
        "Refinery Material Balance"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-7-1 ;
    skos:definition "Close the refinery's daily production balance — reconciling measured feed receipts, unit charges and yields, blending, shipments, fuel and loss, and inventory changes into an accounted material balance — and actualize production against the schedule so planning, scheduling, and backcasting run on verified actuals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.7.1.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perform Production Accounting"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers daily production and yield accounting per refinery hydrocarbon-management practice (EI HM 31): assembling measured quantities, balancing the site by unit and material, attributing imbalance and apparent loss for investigation, and publishing the day's actuals. Excludes owning the physical measurement basis, which sits with Measure Inventory (CM-1-1-3-7-4) with readings taken by asset operators; excludes periodic network book-to-physical stock reconciliation, owned by Perform Inventory Reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5), which this balance feeds; excludes financial and cost accounting, owned by Finance under the locked rule.
In scope: Assemble measured receipts, charges, yields, shipments, and inventory changes | balance the site by unit and material daily | attribute imbalance and apparent loss for investigation | publish actualized production against schedule
Out of scope: Measurement basis and standards (CM-1-1-3-7-4) | network book-to-physical reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5) | financial and cost accounting (Finance)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "measured quantities and movement tickets | unit charge and yield data | shipment and inventory records"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "closed daily production balance with actualized yields"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Deliver a verified daily account of what the refinery actually received, made, shipped, and lost."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EI-HM31-001 | SRC-API-MPMS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Measure Inventory | enables: Perform Inventory Reconciliation | enables: Regional Backcasting"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 06 (2026-09-19). Production accounting is the refinery's daily material balance and yield actualization — distinct from network stock reconciliation (batch 04) and from financial inventory accounting. APQC 10774 'Perform inventory accounting' scored STRONG but is financial asset accounting; treated as a false match, comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-7-2-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10252" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10252"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Scheduling Master Data Maintenance"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-7-2 ;
    skos:definition "Create, maintain, and retire the reference and parameter data the production scheduling model depends on — unit capacities and constraints, product and recipe definitions, transition and changeover rules, tankage parameters, and scheduling calendars — so scheduling results remain accurate and reproducible."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.7.2.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Maintain Production Scheduling Master Data"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers stewardship of production-scheduling-specific reference and parameter data over its lifecycle: requesting and validating changes, applying effective dates, and confirming scheduling results remain consistent after a change. Excludes supply-planning master data, owned by Maintain Supply Planning Master Data (CM-1-1-2-12); excludes demand-forecast data preparation (CM-1-1-1-1-1); excludes customer, price, product, tax, and location master data (Manage Master Data, CM-1-3-7-1); excludes enterprise master-data policy and governance, which sit with the Data Governance workstream.
In scope: Maintain unit capacity, constraint, and recipe data | maintain transition and changeover rules | apply and validate effective-dated changes | confirm scheduling consistency after changes
Out of scope: Supply-planning master data (CM-1-1-2-12) | demand-forecast data preparation (CM-1-1-1-1-1) | commercial master data (CM-1-3-7-1) | enterprise master-data policy (Data Governance workstream)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "change requests from scheduling and operations | unit test-run and constraint updates | effective-dating rules"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "validated scheduling reference and parameter data"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep the scheduling model's reference data true to the real refinery."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-ASCM-CSCP-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Create Production Scheduling and Sequencing Plan | enables: Review Production Wheel and Run Length"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 06 (2026-09-19). One of the four parallel master-data nodes named in CM-1-1-2-12's approved note (2026-09-18); the parked consolidation question applies here as well and is deliberately not resolved. Whether refinery scheduling runs on a dedicated scheduling system whose master-data ownership needs linking to a later system-integration workstream is a follow-up noted in the batch 06 PR. APQC 10252 scored STRONG but is distribution master data; comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-7-2-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12916" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12916"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Campaign Sequence and Run-Length Review"@en,
        "Product Wheel Review"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-7-2 ;
    skos:definition "Review and maintain the production wheel — the recurring sequence of product campaigns — and each campaign's run length, balancing transition losses and changeover costs against inventory, demand coverage, and unit constraints, so the schedule is built on an economically sound campaign structure."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.7.2.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Review Production Wheel and Run Length"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the periodic review of campaign sequence and duration: the transition structure (which product can follow which at what cost), wheel cycle time, run lengths per product against demand and storage, and wheel exceptions for demand shifts and outages. Excludes building the dated schedule itself, owned by Create Production Scheduling and Sequencing Plan (CM-1-1-7-2-3); excludes plan-period product mix decisions, owned by Refinery Planning (CM-1-1-4); excludes executing transitions, owned by Refining.
In scope: Maintain the campaign transition structure and costs | set wheel cycle time and run lengths | review the wheel against demand, inventory, and constraints | approve wheel exceptions
Out of scope: Dated schedule construction (CM-1-1-7-2-3) | plan-period product mix (CM-1-1-4) | transition execution (Refining)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "demand and inventory coverage by product | transition cost and constraint data | unit campaign performance"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "maintained production wheel with approved run lengths"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep the campaign structure the schedule runs on economically sound."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-ASCM-CSCP-001 | SRC-BJCE-REFPS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Create Production Scheduling and Sequencing Plan | informed-by: Demand Forecasting"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 06 (2026-09-19). 'Production wheel' is the standard campaign-scheduling structure where sequence-dependent changeovers matter — directly relevant to specialty and blended product lines. Whether the wheel structure applies across all units or only campaign-oriented lines is a follow-up noted in the batch 06 PR. APQC 12916 is a WEAK review-link; comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-7-2-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10223" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10223"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Build Production Schedule"@en,
        "Sequencing Plan Development"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-7-2 ;
    skos:definition "Build the dated production schedule and sequencing plan for the scheduling horizon — assigning campaigns, schedule-level unit commitments, blends, and movements to dates and sequences within the approved plan, the production wheel, and current constraints — producing the schedule that provides the approved basis for refinery execution."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.7.2.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Create Production Scheduling and Sequencing Plan"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers constructing and refreshing the dated schedule: sequencing within wheel and transition rules, scheduling blends and movements at schedule level, validating feasibility against constraints and outage windows, and publishing the schedule. Excludes unit-operation scheduling and tank and transfer line-ups, owned by Refining per the locked rule; excludes daily instruction updates (CM-1-1-7-1-1); excludes the plan targets themselves (CM-1-1-4).
In scope: Sequence campaigns, schedule-level unit commitments, blends, and movements to dates | validate feasibility against constraints and outage windows | publish and refresh the dated schedule | reconcile the schedule to the approved plan
Out of scope: Unit-operation scheduling and line-ups (Refining) | daily instruction issuance (CM-1-1-7-1-1) | monthly plan targets (CM-1-1-4)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "approved monthly plan | production wheel and run lengths | scheduling master data | outage windows"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "published dated production schedule and sequencing plan"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Produce the dated, feasible schedule that implements the plan."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "weekly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-BJCE-REFPS-001 | SRC-SNF-REFPS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Maintain Production Scheduling Master Data | follows: Review Production Wheel and Run Length | precedes: Daily Execution Planning"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 06 (2026-09-19). 'Schedule-level unit commitments' used at Hamid's direction in place of 'unit operations' to preserve the boundary: the schedule commits what a unit should produce and when, never how it is operated. The scheduling-layer output per SNF/BJCE practice; the operations layer is Refining's per the locked rule. APQC 10223 'Create materials plan' is a WEAK generic candidate; comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-7-2-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10224" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10224"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Schedule Exception Management"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-7-2 ;
    skos:definition "Detect, assess, and resolve deviations from the production schedule — unit upsets, feed or quality surprises, outage changes, and demand shifts — deciding schedule responses, triggering rescheduling, and escalating deviations that break the approved plan to planning."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.7.2.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Production Schedule Exceptions"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the schedule exception rhythm: monitoring execution feedback against the schedule, assessing deviation impact, deciding and coordinating the schedule response, triggering rescheduling through Create Production Scheduling and Sequencing Plan (CM-1-1-7-2-3), escalating plan-level breaks to Refinery Planning (CM-1-1-4), and recording exception causes for performance measurement. The operational decision to depart from the schedule for safety, reliability, or immediate process conditions belongs to Refining under the locked rule; this process manages the schedule consequence of such departures.
In scope: Monitor execution against schedule and detect deviations | assess deviation impact | decide and coordinate schedule responses | escalate plan-breaking deviations to planning | record exception causes
Out of scope: Operational departures for safety and reliability (Refining) | rescheduling construction (CM-1-1-7-2-3) | plan revision (CM-1-1-4)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "execution feedback and deviation reports | schedule and plan constraints | impact assessments"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "decided schedule responses and escalations with recorded causes"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep the schedule valid when reality diverges from it."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-BJCE-REFPS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "triggers: Create Production Scheduling and Sequencing Plan | informed-by: Daily Execution Planning | enables: Measure Production Scheduling Performance"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 06 (2026-09-19). Refining decides safe operational departures (locked); this node owns the schedule response, not the operational decision. APQC 10224 STRONG with no validator; comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-7-2-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10314" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10314"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Schedule Adherence Measurement"@en,
        "Scheduling Performance Measurement"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-7-2 ;
    skos:definition "Measure and report how well execution followed the issued production schedule and how stable the schedule itself was — adherence, rescheduling frequency, and exception causes at agreed levels — feeding improvements back into scheduling practice and master data."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.7.2.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Measure Production Scheduling Performance"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers schedule-performance measurement against the schedule as issued, keeping the contemporaneous-baseline discipline: adherence and stability analysis, exception-cause patterns, and improvement recommendations to scheduling practice, the wheel, and master data. Excludes plan-versus-actual variance explanation, owned by Regional Backcasting (CM-1-1-5) under its locked rule; excludes asset and yield performance ownership, which belongs to Refining with actuals supplied by production accounting; excludes enterprise KPI definition (KPI workstream).
In scope: Measure adherence to the issued schedule | measure schedule stability and rescheduling frequency | analyze exception-cause patterns | recommend improvements to practice, wheel, and master data
Out of scope: Plan-versus-actual backcasting (Regional Backcasting, CM-1-1-5) | asset and yield performance ownership (Refining) | enterprise KPI definition (KPI workstream)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "issued schedules and revisions | actualized production from production accounting | recorded exception causes"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "scheduling performance report with improvement recommendations"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Establish whether the schedule is being followed and whether it is worth following."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-BJCE-REFPS-001 | SRC-ASCM-CSCP-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Perform Production Accounting | follows: Manage Production Schedule Exceptions | enables: Production Scheduling"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 06 (2026-09-19). Horizon set to 'continuous' per the batch 05 precedent (Hamid's -8-4 edit) — continuous operational monitoring with periodic reporting and review; the controlled vocabulary takes a single value, so the cadence is recorded here. Specific metrics deliberately not named (KPI workstream). APQC 10314 is a WEAK generic candidate; comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-7-2-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10734" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10734"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Scheduling Interface Management"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-7-2 ;
    skos:definition "Run the structured coordination between production scheduling and its counterparties — feasibility and deviation feedback to Refinery Planning, executability alignment with refinery operations, and outage, turnaround, and maintenance windows from engineering and maintenance — so the schedule and its neighbors stay mutually consistent."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.7.2.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage The Interface to Planning, Operations, Engineering & Maintenance"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers recurring interface management: scheduled coordination and handoffs, intake of maintenance and turnaround windows into the schedule, executability confirmation with operations, feasibility feedback to planning, and documentation of interface agreements. Excludes the counterparties' own work: plan targets (Refinery Planning, CM-1-1-4), maintenance planning and turnaround execution (Refining, per the locked rule), and operational execution (Refining). The concept is the recurring coordination process, not an organizational liaison role.
In scope: Run scheduled coordination and handoffs | intake outage, turnaround, and maintenance windows | confirm schedule executability with operations | feed feasibility and deviations back to planning
Out of scope: Plan targets (CM-1-1-4) | maintenance planning and turnaround execution (Refining) | operational execution (Refining)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "maintenance and turnaround plans | operations executability feedback | plan updates and constraints"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "consistent interface agreements and window intake into the schedule"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep the schedule and its neighboring functions mutually consistent."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "weekly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-SNF-REFPS-001 | SRC-ASCM-CSCP-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Create Production Scheduling and Sequencing Plan | informed-by: Refinery Planning"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 06 (2026-09-19). Modeled as the recurring coordination process per the process-concept rule, not a role or an organizational unit. APQC 10734 'Manage treasury operations' is an unrelated false match."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-7-3-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10113" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10113"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Energy Make-versus-Buy Optimization"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-7-3 ;
    skos:definition "Decide the economically optimal mix of self-generated and externally purchased energy — fuel gas, fuel oil, steam, and power, including cogeneration versus grid supply — for the planning period, within reliability, contractual, and emissions constraints."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.7.3.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Optimize Internal vs. External Energy Sources"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the recurring sourcing optimization: comparing marginal costs of internal generation against purchases, setting the sourcing plan by utility, respecting contract terms and reliability minimums, and reflecting emissions-allowance cost in the choice. Excludes negotiating energy supply contracts, owned by the commercial owner of energy supply; excludes operating generation and distribution assets, owned by Refining; excludes the sitewide balance targets, owned by Optimize Plant Energy Balance (CM-1-1-7-3-2).
In scope: Compare marginal costs of internal generation versus purchase | set the sourcing plan by utility and period | respect contractual and reliability constraints | reflect allowance cost in sourcing choices
Out of scope: Energy contract negotiation (commercial owner of energy supply) | generation and distribution operation (Refining) | sitewide balance targets (CM-1-1-7-3-2)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "internal generation costs | purchased energy prices and terms | reliability requirements | allowance prices"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "period energy sourcing plan"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Choose the cheapest reliable energy mix the refinery can run on."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "monthly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EI-HM31-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Optimize Plant Energy Balance | informed-by: Manage and Support Emission Trading"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 06 (2026-09-19). APQC 10113 is an unrelated strategy candidate; comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-7-3-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12979" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12979"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Energy Balance Target Setting"@en,
        "Site Energy Balance Planning"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-7-3 ;
    skos:definition "Establish and maintain planning and scheduling targets for the refinery's sitewide energy balance — such as expected steam, fuel-gas, power, and energy-recovery requirements — so production schedules reflect energy cost, availability, emissions exposure, and operating constraints."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.7.3.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Optimize Plant Energy Balance"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers integrating energy-balance assumptions and economic targets into the production schedule; assessing deviations that materially affect scheduled energy cost or emissions exposure; and recommending schedule-level adjustments. Refining owns real-time utility balancing, equipment dispatch, process control, flaring response, and execution of energy-balance moves. Excludes sourcing mix decisions, owned by Optimize Internal vs. External Energy Sources (CM-1-1-7-3-1); excludes energy-efficiency capital projects, owned by Refining and engineering.
In scope: Integrate energy-balance assumptions and economic targets into the schedule | assess deviations affecting scheduled energy cost or emissions exposure | recommend schedule-level adjustments | maintain energy-balance planning assumptions
Out of scope: Real-time utility balancing, dispatch, process control, and flaring response (Refining) | sourcing mix decisions (CM-1-1-7-3-1) | energy-efficiency capital projects (Refining, engineering)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "site energy balance data | sourcing plan | production schedule | operating constraints"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "energy-balance planning targets and schedule-level recommendations"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Give the schedule sound energy-balance assumptions and targets, and flag deviations that change its economics."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EI-HM31-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Optimize Internal vs. External Energy Sources | enables: Daily Execution Planning"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 06 (2026-09-19). Rewritten at Hamid's direction before approval: the original draft's 'set the targets the refinery runs to' and 'minimizing purchased energy, flaring, and losses' read as operational control — this node sets planning and scheduling targets and recommendations only; real-time balancing and dispatch are Refining's. APQC 12979 'Optimize plant units' is a WEAK review-link; comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-7-3-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19562" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19562"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Emissions Allowance Position Management"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-7-3 ;
    skos:definition "Manage the refinery's emissions-allowance and credit position from the planning side — tracking emissions against allowances by program and period, forecasting surplus or shortfall from the production and energy plans, and supporting allowance purchase, sale, and banking decisions — so compliance obligations are met at the best economic outcome."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.7.3.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage and Support Emission Trading"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers allowance position management per emissions-trading program mechanics: maintaining the position by program, forecasting surplus and shortfall from schedule and energy plans, preparing the economic case for trades and banking, incorporating allowance prices into scheduling and energy decisions, and supporting compliance true-up. Excludes regulatory emissions monitoring and reporting, owned by EHS & Government Reporting under its locked rule; excludes trade negotiation and execution, owned by the trading function (Supply And Trading where modeled); excludes the financial accounting treatment of allowances and credits, owned by Finance. 'Support' in the label reflects this advisory position: the node prepares and supports the decisions without executing trades or filing reports.
In scope: Maintain the allowance and credit position by program | forecast surplus and shortfall from plans | prepare the economic case for trades and banking | support compliance true-up and reflect allowance cost in decisions
Out of scope: Regulatory emissions monitoring and reporting (EHS & Government Reporting) | trade negotiation and execution (trading function) | financial accounting of allowances and credits (Finance) | emissions-reduction capital projects (Refining, engineering)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "emissions forecasts from production and energy plans | allowance holdings and prices | program rules and compliance deadlines"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "managed allowance position with trade and banking recommendations"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep the allowance position known, forecast, and economically managed without owning the trade or the report."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "monthly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EPA-EMTR-001 | SRC-EI-HM31-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Production Scheduling | enables: Optimize Internal vs. External Energy Sources"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 06 (2026-09-19). The decide-versus-advise pattern applies twice: EHS owns the regulator-facing report (locked rule); trading owns execution; Finance records the accounting treatment. 'Emissions allowances and credits' used throughout because programs use different, not always interchangeable, instruments. Preferred normalized label 'Manage and Support Emissions Trading' (plural) queued for the naming pass per Hamid. Program mechanics (cap, allowances, trading, banking, true-up) per EPA emissions-trading practice as reference evidence; applicability is jurisdiction- and program-specific. APQC 19562 'Manage support utilities' is a WEAK lexical match; comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-1-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12901" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12901"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Production Forecast Intake"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1-1 ;
    skos:definition "Receive and incorporate the refinery production plans and schedules into the trading forward view — validating completeness, translating volumes into tradable supply by product, location, and period, and refreshing the view as plans and schedules change."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.1.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Receive Production Forecast"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the intake side only: receiving the approved production plan and current schedule from Refinery Planning (CM-1-1-4) and Refinery Scheduling (CM-1-1-7), reconciling product, location, and period granularity to what the trading books use, and flagging material changes to traders. It consumes these plans and does not produce or alter them — their owners hold that right. Excludes the demand-side intake, owned by Receive Demand Forecast (CM-1-2-1-1-2).
In scope: Receive the approved plan and current schedule | validate completeness and version | translate volumes to tradable supply granularity | flag material changes to trading
Out of scope: Producing or altering the plan or schedule (CM-1-1-4, CM-1-1-7) | demand forecast intake (CM-1-2-1-1-2) | trading decisions on the supply (Trade Capture, strategy processes)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "approved production plan | current production schedule | product and location mappings"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "production supply view refreshed in the trading forward view"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep the trading view current with what the refinery is currently planned and scheduled to produce."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "monthly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EIA-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Refinery Planning | informed-by: Production Scheduling | enables: Perform Asset Optimization"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 07 (2026-09-19). A consume-not-produce node, per the precedent batch 02 recorded on its sibling CM-1-2-1-1-2. APQC 12901 'Forecast equity crude production' is a WEAK review-link; comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-1-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20634" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20634"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Demand Forecast Intake"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1-1 ;
    skos:definition "Receive the published demand forecast and incorporate it into the trading forward view — validating the version, translating product, market, and period detail into the granularity trading uses, and refreshing trading assumptions when a new version publishes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.1.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Receive Demand Forecast"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the intake of the approved commercial planning forecast, which is owned by Demand Forecasting (CM-1-1-1-1) and released by Publish Forecast and Generate Component Level Forecast Plan (CM-1-1-1-1-4) per the batch 03 decision — this node consumes the published version and must not claim its production. Excludes Supply And Trading's own operational refined-product demand forecasting (Forecast Refined Product Demand, CM-1-2-6-2-3), which serves the supply-chain-management context under the batch 03 constraint. Excludes adjusting or overriding the forecast — trading views that differ from the published forecast are trading positions, not forecast revisions.
In scope: Receive and validate the published forecast version | translate to trading granularity | refresh trading assumptions on new versions | flag material forecast changes to trading
Out of scope: Forecast production and ownership (CM-1-1-1-1) | operational refined-product demand forecasting (CM-1-2-6-2-3) | forecast adjustment or override (trading positions are not forecast revisions)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "published versioned demand forecast | trading granularity mappings"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "demand assumptions refreshed in the trading forward view"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep trading assumptions aligned to the current published demand forecast."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "monthly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EIA-STEO-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Demand Forecasting | uses-input: Publish Forecast and Generate Component Level Forecast Plan | enables: Perform Asset Optimization"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 07 (2026-09-19). Batch 02 already cited this node as 'the existing precedent for a consume-not-produce node'; authored to honor that reading and the batch 03 Q2 decision (Demand Forecasting owns the approved forecast). APQC 20634 scored STRONG but is IT-service demand; false lexical match, comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-1-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10129" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10129"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Renewables Position Forecasting"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1-1 ;
    skos:definition "Develop and maintain a forward view of renewable-fuel volumes, renewable-energy output or procurement, compliance obligations, and associated program-specific environmental attributes or credits — such as Renewable Identification Numbers where applicable — so commercial, trading, and compliance stakeholders can anticipate expected shortfalls, surpluses, and deadlines."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.1.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Forecast Renewables"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers forecasting renewable-fuel production, blending, acquisition, and obligation exposure; expected generation, acquisition, retirement, or need for program-specific environmental attributes and credits; and the resulting forward long and short positions by program and compliance period. Excludes regulatory monitoring, filing, attestation, and submission, owned by EHS & Government Reporting under its locked rule; excludes execution of credit purchases, sales, transfers, or retirements, owned by the trading processes; excludes accounting actualization of issued, acquired, sold, retired, or expired instruments, owned by Perform RINs/REC Actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-7). Program applicability is jurisdiction-specific.
In scope: Forecast renewable-fuel volumes and obligation exposure | forecast generation, acquisition, retirement, and need for program-specific attributes and credits | maintain forward long and short positions by program and compliance period | flag expected shortfalls, surpluses, and deadlines
Out of scope: Regulatory monitoring, filing, attestation, and submission (EHS & Government Reporting) | credit purchase, sale, transfer, and retirement execution (trading processes) | credit actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-7)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "production and blending plans | program obligations and rules | instrument holdings and prices"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "forward renewables position by program and compliance period"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Know the renewables obligation and instrument position before the deadline does."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "monthly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EPA-RFS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Receive Production Forecast | enables: Perform Asset Optimization | precedes: Perform RINs/REC Actualization"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 07 (2026-09-19). Reworded at Hamid's direction to treat environmental instruments as program-specific: RINs (RFS-type fuel programs) and RECs (renewable power programs) arise under different program architectures with different eligibility, lifecycle, compliance, and market rules and are not interchangeable. Which instruments are genuinely in scope (RINs, RECs, low-carbon fuel credits, allowances, guarantees of origin) is a follow-up noted in the batch 07 PR. EPA RFS practice is US-specific reference evidence for RVO and RIN mechanics only. APQC 10129 is an unrelated sales-forecast candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-1-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19253" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19253"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Asset-Backed Trading Optimization"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1-1 ;
    skos:definition "Assess and optimize the commercial use of approved asset flexibility — including storage, transportation and terminal capacity, supply commitments, exchanges, and generation availability — against market conditions, identifying and valuing opportunities and providing recommendations to approved trading strategies within established plans and constraints."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.1.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perform Asset Optimization"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers evaluating storage economics and time spreads; capacity utilization, release, and throughput opportunities; exchange and location-arbitrage opportunities; and the commercial value of generation availability. It identifies and quantifies trading opportunities within approved regional, refinery, and scheduling plans. Excludes changing physical plans, owned under the locked Regional Optimization and Refinery Planning rules; excludes operating or dispatching assets, owned by Refining, Midstream, and the relevant system operators; excludes defining or approving trading strategies, owned by Trading Strategy Management (CM-1-2-1-4); excludes negotiating, executing, capturing, or settling trades, owned by Trade Capture (CM-1-2-1-3) and the Settlements cluster; excludes risk-limit setting and risk measurement, owned by Market Risk Management (CM-1-2-2-3).
In scope: Evaluate storage, capacity, exchange, and generation-availability economics against the market | identify and quantify opportunities within approved plans | value opportunities and their sensitivities | provide recommendations to approved trading strategies
Out of scope: Physical plan changes (Regional Optimization, Refinery Planning — locked) | asset operation and dispatch (Refining, Midstream, system operators) | trading strategy definition and approval (CM-1-2-1-4) | trade negotiation, execution, capture, and settlement (CM-1-2-1-3, Settlements) | risk-limit setting and risk measurement (CM-1-2-2-3)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "forward view of supply, demand, and generation | market prices and forward curves | asset capacities and constraints | approved plans"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "valued asset-optimization opportunities and recommendations to approved strategies"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Identify and value the market opportunities the asset portfolio's approved flexibility offers."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001 | SRC-EIA-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Receive Production Forecast | uses-input: Receive Demand Forecast | enables: Trading Strategy Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 07 (2026-09-19). Q2 decision (Hamid, Option A, narrowed): approved plans define the base; this process identifies, values, and recommends commercial use of the flexibility those plans leave open — it does not initiate strategies (Trading Strategy Management owns the strategy lifecycle) and does not own dispatch ('generation availability', not dispatch, at Hamid's direction). Whether the desk may submit pre-authorized strategy-linked trade orders, rather than only recommend, is a follow-up to be modeled through the future Trade Capture relationship, noted in the batch 07 PR. APQC 19253 'Perform asset maintenance' scored STRONG but is maintenance; false lexical match, comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-1-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10129" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10129"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Forward Trading Economics Outlook"@en,
        "Trading Revenue Projection"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1-1 ;
    skos:definition "Project the trading organization's expected economics — projected trading revenue, gross margin, and economic exposure from current positions, planned flows, the forward view, and market prices — so management and strategy decisions rest on a current forward-looking outlook."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.1.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Forecast Revenue"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the front-office economic projection: projected revenue, gross margin, and exposure by book, strategy, and period, refreshed on market and position changes, with sensitivity to price scenarios. Excludes financial forecasting, budgeting, and reporting ownership, which sit with Finance under the locked rule; excludes official, controlled position and realized/unrealized P&L reporting, owned by Perform Position & PNL Analysis (CM-1-2-2-3-9) and the Accounting cluster (CM-1-2-4-2) — this is a forward-looking planning projection, not the governed result.
In scope: Project revenue, gross margin, and exposure by book, strategy, and period | refresh projections on market and position changes | run price-scenario sensitivities | supply the economic outlook to management and strategy
Out of scope: Financial forecasting and budgeting (Finance) | official, controlled position and realized/unrealized P&L reporting (CM-1-2-2-3-9, CM-1-2-4-2) | trading decisions themselves (strategy and capture processes)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "current positions and planned flows | forward view and market prices | price scenarios"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "projected trading revenue, gross margin, and exposure outlook"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Provide management and strategy owners with a current forward-looking view of projected trading revenue, gross margin, and economic exposure."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "monthly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Perform Asset Optimization | enables: Trading Strategy Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 07 (2026-09-19). Reframed at Hamid's direction as revenue, gross margin, and economic exposure — in a trading environment expected value, exposure, and scenario sensitivity matter alongside accounting-defined revenue; Finance's ownership of official financial forecasting and governed P&L is unchanged. Whether optionality valuation and mark-to-market scenario impact are in scope, or only expected physical-trading margin, is a follow-up noted in the batch 07 PR. APQC 10129 is an unrelated sales-forecast candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-1-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10129" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10129"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Generation Forecast Intake"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1-1 ;
    skos:definition "Receive and incorporate power generation forecasts — from company cogeneration and any contracted or merchant generation — into the trading forward view, validating them and translating expected generation into tradable positions by period."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.1.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Receive Power Generation Forecast"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the intake side only: generation forecasts are produced by the asset operators (Refining for cogeneration) or external suppliers; this node consumes them into the forward view, reconciles period granularity, and flags material changes. Excludes producing generation forecasts (asset operators and suppliers); excludes physical generation dispatch and grid/system operations, owned by the asset operators and the relevant system authorities; excludes power trade execution and settlement, owned by Trade Capture (CM-1-2-1-3) and the Settlements cluster.
In scope: Receive and validate generation forecasts | translate expected generation to tradable positions by period | refresh the forward view on forecast changes | flag material generation changes to trading
Out of scope: Generation forecast production (asset operators, suppliers) | physical generation dispatch and grid/system operations (asset operators, system authorities) | power trade execution and settlement (CM-1-2-1-3, Settlements)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "cogeneration and contracted generation forecasts | period granularity mappings"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "generation positions refreshed in the trading forward view"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep the trading view current with expected power generation."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EIA-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Energy & Utility Management | enables: Perform Asset Optimization"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 07 (2026-09-19). Third of the three consume-not-produce intake siblings; dispatch and grid-operations exclusion worded per Hamid's batch 07 edit for precision. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-2-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10147" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10147"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Counterparty Onboarding"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Coordinate onboarding of a prospective trading counterparty by collecting required legal-entity, tax, settlement, and commercial documentation; initiating and recording required compliance, sanctions/KYC, legal, and credit clearances through their accountable owners; and creating the authorized counterparty and settlement records once required approvals are complete."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.2.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Create New Counterparty"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers counterparty onboarding coordination: documentation collection and completeness validation, initiation and tracking of required clearances through their owning processes, setup of the counterparty record with settlement instructions and hierarchy links, and activation for trading once approvals are complete. Credit reviews and limits are owned by Perform Counterparty Credit Reviews (CM-1-2-2-1-1) and Manage Credit Limits (CM-1-2-2-1-2); compliance policy by Regulatory & Compliance (CM-1-2-4-3); legal review by Legal & Corporate Communications. Excludes independent KYC/sanctions determination, beneficial-owner due diligence, and regulatory-risk acceptance, which sit with the appropriate Regulatory & Compliance or Legal process when its detailed taxonomy is authored. Excludes commercial customer master data, owned by Manage Master Data under Order Management (CM-1-3-7-1) — trading counterparty records live here.
In scope: Collect and validate completeness of counterparty documentation | initiate and record required clearances through their accountable owners | create the counterparty record and settlement details once approvals complete | activate the counterparty for trading
Out of scope: Independent KYC/sanctions determination, beneficial-owner due diligence, and regulatory-risk acceptance (Regulatory & Compliance / Legal) | credit reviews and limit setting (CM-1-2-2-1-1, CM-1-2-2-1-2) | legal review (Legal & Corporate Communications) | commercial customer master data (CM-1-3-7-1)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "counterparty applications and legal documentation | clearance outcomes from accountable owners | settlement instructions"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "activated counterparty records with recorded clearances"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "No trading with an unverified or incompletely cleared counterparty."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "precedes: Create New Contract | requires: Perform Counterparty Credit Reviews"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 09 (2026-09-19). Reworded at Hamid's direction to distinguish process coordination from independent verification: this process validates completeness and reconciles data; Compliance, Legal, and Credit retain independent decision rights. Which function operationally performs onboarding (Trading, middle office, Legal Operations, Compliance, Shared Services, or an MDM team) is a follow-up noted in the batch 09 PR. Trading counterparty records here versus commercial customer master data at CM-1-3-7-1 is the record boundary. APQC 10147 is an unrelated budget candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-2-10 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12944" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12944"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Storage Contract Administration"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Administer storage and throughput contracts through their life — recorded capacity, injection and withdrawal rights, fee structures, amendments, and renewals — so storage deals, inventory administration, and settlement operate against current, correct contract terms."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.2.10" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Storage Contract"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the contract-administration position in the storage split: negotiation is owned by Negotiate Storage / Throughput Agreements (CM-1-2-7-1-3); where storage capacity is commercially traded, allocated, acquired, released, or represented as a distinct transaction it is captured by Capture Storage Deals (CM-1-2-1-3-5), and otherwise the contract record maintained here remains the governed source for contractual storage rights and charges; company stock in the facilities is administered by Manage Third Party Storage (CM-1-1-3-7-11); storage charges settle at Settle Terminal/Storage (CM-1-2-4-1-14). This node maintains the contract record itself — terms, rights, fees, versions, renewals — and keeps the others supplied with current terms.
In scope: Maintain storage and throughput contract records, rights, and fees | administer amendments and renewals | keep terms synchronized with deals, inventory administration, and settlement | track expiries and renewal windows
Out of scope: Negotiation (CM-1-2-7-1-3) | capacity deal capture where a distinct transaction exists (CM-1-2-1-3-5) | third-party storage inventory administration (CM-1-1-3-7-11) | storage settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-14)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "negotiated storage terms | amendment requests | deal and settlement feedback"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "current storage contract records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Storage rights and fees in the systems match the contract in force."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001 | SRC-EI-HM32-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Negotiate Storage / Throughput Agreements | enables: Capture Storage Deals | enables: Manage Third Party Storage"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 09 (2026-09-19). The storage split (negotiate, administer, capture, use, settle) is stated on all participating nodes; the conditional-capture wording was added at Hamid's direction — not every storage contract produces a distinct captured deal, and where none exists the contract record here is the governed source of rights and charges. APQC 12944 is the third-party-bulk-storage candidate; comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-2-11 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10291" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10291"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Contract Obligation Monitoring"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Monitor active agreements against their terms — tracking obligations, commitments, expiries, renewal windows, and utilization against contracted volumes and service levels — surfacing issues to the responsible owners before they become breaches or lapses."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.2.11" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Monitor Contracts"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers contract-obligation monitoring across the portfolio: obligation and commitment tracking, expiry and renewal-window alerting, utilization against contracted volumes, and escalation of at-risk items to the contract, commercial, or legal owner. Excludes trading compliance monitoring, owned by Regulatory & Compliance (Monitor External Trading Compliance, CM-1-2-4-3-2; Monitor Internal Trading Compliance, CM-1-2-4-3-3); excludes dispute handling (CM-1-2-2-2-5, CM-1-2-4-1-17); excludes acting on the findings — amendments and renewals run through Manage Existing Contract (CM-1-2-1-2-6).
In scope: Track obligations, commitments, and service levels against terms | alert on expiries and renewal windows | monitor utilization against contracted volumes | escalate at-risk items to responsible owners
Out of scope: Trading compliance monitoring (CM-1-2-4-3-2, CM-1-2-4-3-3) | dispute handling (CM-1-2-2-2-5, CM-1-2-4-1-17) | amendments and renewals (CM-1-2-1-2-6)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "contract terms and calendars | utilization and delivery data | commitment schedules"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "monitored obligations with escalated at-risk items"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "No agreement lapses, breaches, or under-uses unnoticed."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Manage Existing Contract | triggers: Manage Existing Contract | enables: Trading Strategy Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 09 (2026-09-19). Monitoring surfaces; the lifecycle siblings act — the same detect-versus-decide split established in batch 04. APQC 10291 comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-2-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11743" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11743"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Counterparty Record Maintenance"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Maintain active counterparty records through their life — processing changes to legal identity, ownership, settlement details, and documentation, keeping records synchronized with credit and compliance status, and periodically reconfirming record accuracy."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.2.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Existing Counterparty"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers ongoing counterparty record maintenance: change processing, periodic reconfirmation, documentation refresh, and synchronization with credit and compliance outcomes owned by their processes. Excludes the credit and compliance judgments themselves (CM-1-2-2-1, CM-1-2-4-3); excludes termination and novation, owned by Terminate/Novate Counterparty (CM-1-2-1-2-3); excludes M&A-driven changes, owned by Manage M&A Activity (CM-1-2-1-2-4).
In scope: Process counterparty record changes | refresh documentation periodically | synchronize records with credit and compliance status | reconfirm record accuracy on a defined cadence
Out of scope: Credit and compliance judgments (CM-1-2-2-1, CM-1-2-4-3) | termination and novation (CM-1-2-1-2-3) | M&A-driven changes (CM-1-2-1-2-4)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "change notices and documentation | credit and compliance status updates"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "current, reconfirmed counterparty records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Counterparty records stay true to the counterparty."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Create New Counterparty | enables: Trade Capture"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 09 (2026-09-19). APQC 11743 is an unrelated routing candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-2-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10208" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10208"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Counterparty Offboarding & Novation Administration"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Coordinate the administrative counterparty offboarding or transfer — confirming that affected positions, agreements, collateral, payment instructions, and reporting responsibilities have been closed, settled, transferred, or queued for legally effective novation — and deactivate or transition counterparty records with preserved history."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.2.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Terminate/Novate Counterparty"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers controlled exit and transfer coordination: verifying no unresolved positions, obligations, disputes, or collateral remain or are queued for legally effective novation; coordinating consents, effective dates, and counterparty communication; and executing the administrative record transfer or deactivation with audit history. The legally effective novation is the legal event, executed through documents owned by Legal & Corporate Communications; this process performs the administrative transfer of records once that event is effective. Collateral release is owned by Manage Collateral (CM-1-2-2-1-3); settlement of remaining obligations by the Settlements cluster (CM-1-2-4-1).
In scope: Verify positions, agreements, collateral, and reporting responsibilities are closed, settled, transferred, or queued for legally effective novation | coordinate consents, effective dates, and communication | execute the administrative transfer or deactivation | preserve record history
Out of scope: Legal execution of novation and termination documents (Legal & Corporate Communications) | collateral release (CM-1-2-2-1-3) | settlement of remaining obligations (CM-1-2-4-1)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "open position and obligation reports | novation consents and effective dates | master agreement terms"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "cleanly transferred or deactivated counterparty records with preserved history"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Counterparty exits leave nothing open and nothing lost."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001 | SRC-ISDA-MA-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Manage Existing Counterparty | requires: Manage Collateral"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 09 (2026-09-19). Reworded at Hamid's direction: 'legally effective novation' names the legal event (Legal owns its documents); 'administrative transfer' names the operational record change this process owns — the two are never conflated. Novation terminology per the CFTC glossary; master-agreement close-out mechanics per ISDA documentation as reference evidence for applicable OTC frameworks. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-2-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20729" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20729"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "M&A Counterparty & Contract Transition"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Administer the counterparty, agreement, trade-record, and obligation consequences of merger, acquisition, divestiture, restructuring, or legal-entity change events affecting either a trading counterparty or the company's own trading entities — coordinating, recording, and operationalizing the resulting novations, assignments, renamings, and record transfers once required legal documentation and approvals are complete."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.2.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage M&A Activity"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers event-driven M&A consequence administration in both directions: identifying affected counterparties, agreements, and open positions when a corporate event occurs; planning and coordinating the resulting legal-entity renames and identifier changes, counterparty-master updates, assignments, consent requests, novations, trade and portfolio transfers, and changes to payment, settlement, collateral, tax, and reporting entities; and verifying continuity of obligations throughout. Excludes corporate-development strategy, target identification, due diligence, valuation, transaction negotiation, financing, regulatory merger approval, and legal execution of transaction or novation documents; credit reassessment of surviving entities is owned by Credit Risk Management (CM-1-2-2-1); updates to delegated authority follow Market Risk Management (CM-1-2-2-3-2).
In scope: Identify counterparties, agreements, and positions affected by a corporate event | plan and coordinate renames, assignments, novations, and transfers | operationalize record changes once legal documentation and approvals are complete | verify continuity of obligations, collateral, and reporting
Out of scope: Corporate-development strategy, diligence, valuation, negotiation, financing, and regulatory merger approval (outside the taxonomy) | legal execution of transaction and novation documents (Legal & Corporate Communications) | credit reassessment (CM-1-2-2-1) | delegated-authority updates (CM-1-2-2-3-2)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "corporate event notices | affected counterparty and agreement inventories | novation and assignment consents | completed legal documentation"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "completed administrative transitions with valid records and continuous obligations"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Corporate events never leave a trade, agreement, or obligation pointing at the wrong entity."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001 | SRC-ISDA-MA-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "triggers: Terminate/Novate Counterparty | triggers: Terminate/Novate Contract"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 09 (2026-09-19). Q2 decision (Hamid, Option A): both directions — counterparty-side and company-side corporate events — because both land as the same administrative novation, assignment, and transfer work; restructuring and legal-entity changes included at Hamid's direction. The process coordinates, records, and operationalizes; it never executes legal documents. The Step 3b human gate already confirmed the mergers-and-acquisitions reading (APQC 20729 rejected as a false token match); authored consistent with that rejection."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-2-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10147" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10147"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Contract Establishment"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Establish new trading agreements in the contract record — capturing negotiated terms, verifying required approvals and legal review are complete, linking the agreement to its counterparty and settlement basis, and activating it — so transactions governed by the agreement can be captured, confirmed, actualized, and settled against controlled terms."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.2.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Create New Contract"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers agreement establishment: recording master agreements (such as ISDA-type frameworks), general terms and conditions, and standing commercial contracts; verifying legal review and required approvals occurred with their owners; linking agreements to counterparty records; and activating them for use. Negotiation and legal drafting are owned by the commercial negotiating owner and Legal & Corporate Communications; deal-level transactions under an agreement are captured by Trade Capture (CM-1-2-1-3) and confirmed by Confirmations Management (CM-1-2-2-2); pre-trade authorization of individual transactions is owned by Obtain Trade Approval (CM-1-2-1-3-2), not by contract activation.
In scope: Record negotiated agreement and master agreement terms | verify legal review and approvals are complete | link agreements to counterparties and settlement basis | activate agreements for use
Out of scope: Commercial negotiation (negotiating owner) | legal drafting and review (Legal & Corporate Communications) | deal capture under the agreement (CM-1-2-1-3) | pre-trade authorization of transactions (CM-1-2-1-3-2) | deal confirmation (CM-1-2-2-2)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "negotiated terms | legal review outcomes | counterparty records"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "activated, governed agreement records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Ensure agreements required for trading are recorded, approved, current, and linked to the appropriate counterparty before dependent transactions are captured or processed."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-ISDA-MA-001 | SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Create New Counterparty | enables: Trade Capture"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 09 (2026-09-19). Purpose reworded at Hamid's direction to avoid an absolute gate: agreements required under enterprise policy must be recorded and approved before dependent transactions are processed, but spot-market, exchange-traded, and standing-terms transactions may be governed by standard terms or market rules rather than a bespoke contract record. Master-agreement structure (agreement, schedule, confirmations, credit support) per ISDA documentation as reference evidence for applicable OTC frameworks. APQC 10147 is an unrelated budget candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-2-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11743" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11743"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Contract Administration"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Maintain active agreements through their life — processing approved amendments, extensions, and renewals, keeping recorded terms synchronized with what the parties have agreed, and managing agreement documentation and versions — so the contract record always reflects the operative agreement."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.2.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Existing Contract"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers ongoing agreement administration: amendment and renewal processing with required approvals, version and document control, term synchronization with trading and settlement systems, and administration of agreement-linked references. This process administers approved commercial and legal changes; it does not negotiate terms, draft legal amendments, or determine legal enforceability — the record is updated after commercial and legal agreement and approval, never independently. Excludes renegotiation of commercial terms (negotiating owner, with Legal & Corporate Communications for legal changes); excludes termination and novation (CM-1-2-1-2-7); excludes obligation monitoring (Monitor Contracts, CM-1-2-1-2-11).
In scope: Process approved amendments, extensions, and renewals | control agreement versions and documents | synchronize recorded terms with trading and settlement systems | administer agreement-linked references
Out of scope: Commercial renegotiation (negotiating owner) | legal drafting and enforceability determination (Legal & Corporate Communications) | termination and novation (CM-1-2-1-2-7) | obligation monitoring (CM-1-2-1-2-11)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "approved amendment and renewal requests | approval outcomes | agreed term changes"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "current, version-controlled agreement records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The contract record always reflects the operative agreement."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-ISDA-MA-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Create New Contract | enables: Monitor Contracts"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 09 (2026-09-19). The administers-approved-changes sentence added at Hamid's direction: record updates follow agreement and approval; this process never independently changes terms. APQC 11743 is an unrelated routing candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-2-7 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10208" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10208"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Contract Termination & Novation Administration"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "End or transfer an agreement in a controlled way — confirming obligations under it are fulfilled, settled, or queued for legally effective novation, executing the administrative termination or transfer steps, and closing the agreement record with its history preserved."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.2.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Terminate/Novate Contract"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers controlled agreement exit: verifying open deals, deliveries, and payments under the agreement are complete or queued for legally effective novation, coordinating effective dates and consents, applying master-agreement close-out mechanics where relevant, and archiving the record. Legal execution of termination and novation documents is owned by Legal & Corporate Communications; close-out settlement amounts by the Settlements cluster (CM-1-2-4-1); disputes follow their dispute processes (CM-1-2-2-2-5, CM-1-2-4-1-17).
In scope: Verify obligations are fulfilled, settled, or queued for legally effective novation | coordinate consents and effective dates | execute administrative termination or transfer | archive the agreement record with history
Out of scope: Legal document execution (Legal & Corporate Communications) | close-out settlement (CM-1-2-4-1) | dispute resolution (CM-1-2-2-2-5, CM-1-2-4-1-17)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "open obligation reports | consents and effective dates | master agreement terms"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "cleanly terminated or transferred agreement records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Agreements end clean or transfer whole."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-ISDA-MA-001 | SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Manage Existing Contract | enables: Settlements"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 09 (2026-09-19). Same administrative-versus-legally-effective distinction as CM-1-2-1-2-3. Close-out mechanics per ISDA documentation as reference evidence for applicable OTC frameworks; novation per the CFTC glossary. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-2-8 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11743" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11743"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Lease Purchase Contract Administration"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Administer crude and condensate lease purchase contracts — first-purchase arrangements at the lease or wellhead — through their life: recorded terms, price and deduction bases, division-of-interest references, amendments, and renewals, so lease purchases actualize and settle against current, correct contract terms."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.2.8" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Lease Contract"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the contract-administration side of lease purchasing: maintaining lease contract records with their pricing, gravity and quality adjustment, deduction, and interest-distribution references; processing amendments and renewals; and keeping terms synchronized with actualization and settlement. Included only where the downstream Supply & Trading organization purchases crude or condensate at the lease/wellhead or administers associated first-purchaser arrangements; exploration and production leasehold management, land administration, and upstream royalty accounting remain outside scope. Lease volume actualization is owned by Perform Lease Actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-6); first-purchaser settlement by Settle First Purchaser/Leases (CM-1-2-4-1-6); royalty and interest payment execution by Manage Marketing Interests & Royalty Payments (CM-1-2-4-2-3).
In scope: Maintain lease purchase contract records and pricing bases | administer amendments and renewals | keep division-of-interest and deduction references current | synchronize terms with actualization and settlement
Out of scope: Exploration and production leasehold management, land administration, and upstream royalty accounting (outside scope) | lease volume actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-6) | first-purchaser settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-6) | royalty and interest payments (CM-1-2-4-2-3) | negotiation of lease purchase terms (commercial owner)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "lease contract terms and pricing bases | division-of-interest data | amendment requests"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "current lease purchase contract records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Lease purchases settle against the terms actually in force."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EIA-GLOSS-001 | SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Perform Lease Actualization | enables: Settle First Purchaser/Leases"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 09 (2026-09-19). 'Lease' read in the crude-marketing sense — first-purchase contracts at the lease or wellhead — corroborated by the Perform Lease Actualization and Settle First Purchaser/Leases siblings; not real-estate or equipment leasing. Scope caveat added at Hamid's direction; whether lease-purchase activity is genuinely in scope for the downstream organization or inherited from an integrated enterprise taxonomy is a follow-up noted in the batch 09 PR. APQC 11743 is an unrelated routing candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-2-9 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10291" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10291"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Carrier Contract Administration"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Administer contracts with pipeline, marine, rail, and truck carriers through their life — recorded rates, capacity commitments, service terms, amendments, and renewals — so transportation deals, nominations, and freight settlement operate against current, correct carrier terms."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.2.9" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Carrier Contracts"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the contract-administration side of carrier relationships across modes: maintaining carrier contract records, rate and accessorial references, and commitment terms; processing amendments and renewals; and keeping terms synchronized with capacity deals and settlement. Boundary rule with the products branch: carrier capacity, tariff, freight-rate, charter, pipeline, railcar, trucking, and network-access agreements that support supply and trading movements are administered here; customer-facing and commercial product-delivery agreements, including delivery terms linked to product sale and marketing arrangements, belong to Manage Product Transport Agreements (CM-1-2-7-1-2). Transportation capacity deals are captured by Capture Transportation Deals (CM-1-2-1-3-9); nominations by Plan & Execute Nominations (CM-1-1-3-6-1); mode-specific settlement by the Settlements cluster; demurrage by Demurrage Management (CM-1-2-4-4).
In scope: Maintain carrier contract records, rates, and commitments by mode | administer amendments and renewals | keep terms synchronized with deals and settlement | track carrier contract expiries and renewal windows
Out of scope: Customer-facing product-delivery agreements (CM-1-2-7-1-2) | transportation deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3-9) | nominations and capacity use (CM-1-1-3-6-1) | freight settlement and demurrage (CM-1-2-4-1, CM-1-2-4-4) | physical movement execution (Midstream, distribution operator, carrier)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "carrier contract terms and rates | amendment requests | deal and settlement feedback"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "current carrier contract records by mode"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Freight moves and settles against the carrier terms actually in force."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001 | SRC-DOE-RPPL-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Capture Transportation Deals | enables: Plan & Execute Nominations"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 09 (2026-09-19). The carrier-versus-product-transport rule was sharpened at Hamid's direction: supply/trading-movement carrier agreements here; customer-facing delivery agreements at CM-1-2-7-1-2 — a decision-log-worthy rule that prevents duplication by transport mode alone. Confirming the intended boundary with the products branch is a follow-up noted in the batch 09 PR. APQC 10291 'Manage contracts' scored 0.8 STRONG with no validator; supporting comparative evidence."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-3-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20102" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20102"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Pre-Deal Analysis"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "Evaluate a prospective trade before execution — its economics, strategy fit, credit and limit headroom, operational feasibility where applicable, and regulatory treatment — so the trader and approver see the deal's value and consequences before committing the company."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.3.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Pre-Trade Analysis"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers per-deal analysis ahead of execution: deal economics and sensitivities, fit with the approved strategy, pre-checks against credit exposure and market-risk limit headroom, operational feasibility for physical, capacity, transportation, storage, and structured trades where applicable, and identification of regulatory treatment including reportability. Pre-trade analysis may evaluate available credit exposure and risk-limit headroom, but it does not set limits or independently authorize an override, exception, or breach — those sit with Credit Risk Management (CM-1-2-2-1) and Market Risk Management (CM-1-2-2-3). Distinct from Perform Asset Optimization (CM-1-2-1-1-4), which identifies and values portfolio opportunities — this node analyzes the specific prospective transaction, including ones arising from those recommendations. Excludes the approval decision itself (CM-1-2-1-3-2).
In scope: Analyze deal economics and sensitivities | check strategy fit | pre-check credit exposure and limit headroom | assess operational feasibility where applicable | identify regulatory treatment and reportability
Out of scope: Portfolio opportunity identification (CM-1-2-1-1-4) | credit and risk limit setting, overrides, and exceptions (CM-1-2-2-1, CM-1-2-2-3) | the approval decision (CM-1-2-1-3-2)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "prospective deal terms | asset-optimization recommendations | credit and limit headroom | market prices"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "pre-trade analysis supporting the approval decision"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Ensure the economics, authority, credit exposure, risk-limit headroom, operational feasibility, and reporting implications of a specific prospective trade are understood before execution."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Perform Asset Optimization | precedes: Obtain Trade Approval"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 08 (2026-09-19). Pre-check versus formal clearance distinction added at Hamid's direction: analysis informs, it never clears an exception. Operational feasibility applies to physical, capacity, transportation, storage, and structured trades — not asserted for every financial derivative. Closes the batch 07 follow-up loop: asset-optimization recommendations enter execution through this analysis and the approval sibling. APQC 20102 is an unrelated claims candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-3-10 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11175" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11175"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Swap Data Reporting (Creation Data)"@en,
        "Trade Regulatory Reporting"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "Determine whether an executed swap or other transaction is reportable under an applicable trade-reporting regime and, where the organization is the reporting counterparty or has delegated reporting responsibility, submit required creation data to the designated registered swap data repository within applicable deadlines; correct errors and retain required reporting evidence."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.3.10" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Report Trades to SDR"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers reportability determination, creation-data submission, corrections, resubmissions, acknowledgements, and evidence retention under applicable regimes. Under US CFTC Part 45, this includes creation-data reporting as defined by the applicable rule. Confirmation-event and continuation reporting may be handled by Report Confirmation To SDR (CM-1-2-2-2-4) where the reporting model and regulation require it. Excludes regulatory-policy ownership and monitoring, which belong to Regulatory & Compliance (CM-1-2-4-3); excludes the legal determination of reporting responsibility where Legal/Compliance retains that authority; excludes regimes, jurisdictions, instruments, and reporting events not applicable to the organization.
In scope: Determine reportability under applicable regimes | submit required creation data within deadlines where the organization holds reporting responsibility | correct, resubmit, and track acknowledgements | retain required reporting evidence
Out of scope: Regulatory-policy ownership and compliance monitoring (CM-1-2-4-3) | legal determination of reporting responsibility (Legal/Compliance) | confirmation-event and continuation reporting (CM-1-2-2-2-4) | non-applicable regimes, instruments, and events"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "captured deals with reporting attributes | applicable reporting rules and deadlines | repository specifications"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "submitted, evidenced creation-data reports under applicable regimes"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every reportable trade the organization must report reaches the repository on time, once, correctly."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-P45-001 | SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Capture Financial (OTC) Trades | precedes: Report Confirmation To SDR | governed-by: Regulatory & Compliance"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 08 (2026-09-19). Scoped at Hamid's direction to applicable regimes and assigned reporting responsibility — CFTC Part 45 is US-specific evidence for the creation-data versus continuation-data distinction (17 CFR 45.3 versus 45.4), which is the boundary with CM-1-2-2-2-4, recorded so the Confirmations batch inherits it; it does not make every trade, entity, or jurisdiction reportable. Which entity is the reporting counterparty or delegated reporter per regime is a follow-up noted in the batch 08 PR. APQC 11175 is an unrelated profitability candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-3-11 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11522" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11522"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Power Transmission Deal Capture"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "Record executed power transmission deals — transmission service, capacity, and congestion-related rights, with path, quantity, period, and charges — so transmission positions and obligations are represented from execution onward."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.3.11" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Capture Transmission Deals"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers deal entry for power transmission transactions supporting the power side of the portfolio, consistent with the power nodes elsewhere in the branch (Receive Power Generation Forecast, CM-1-2-1-1-6; Settle Power, CM-1-2-4-1-15; Settle ISO/Power Pools, CM-1-2-4-1-7). Excludes physical transmission scheduling and grid/system operations, owned by the relevant system authorities; excludes power and ISO settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-15, CM-1-2-4-1-7).
In scope: Capture transmission deal terms with path, period, and charges | represent transmission rights and obligations | link deals to books and strategies | correct capture errors
Out of scope: Transmission scheduling and grid/system operations (system authorities) | power and ISO settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-15, CM-1-2-4-1-7)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "executed transmission deal terms | path and period references | charge structures"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "captured transmission deal records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Transmission rights are on the book with their path and period intact."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Obtain Trade Approval | informed-by: Receive Power Generation Forecast | precedes: Settle Power"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 08 (2026-09-19). The dispatch/grid-operations exclusion follows the batch 07 wording on CM-1-2-1-1-6. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-3-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20074" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20074"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Trade Authorization"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "Secure the approvals a trade requires before execution — verifying the trader's authority under the delegation of authority, escalating deals beyond that authority, and documenting each approval — so every executed trade is traceably authorized."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.3.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Obtain Trade Approval"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the pre-execution authorization step: checking the deal against the trader's delegated authority, routing above-authority and non-standard deals (including structured deals) for escalated approval, and recording who approved what. Includes escalation and documented approval, rejection, or conditional approval of trades that exceed delegated authority, approach or breach a limit, are non-standard, or require a documented risk or compliance exception; it does not itself set, waive, or permanently amend risk limits, credit limits, or delegation-of-authority policy. The delegation of authority is set and maintained by Market Risk Management (Establish & Maintain Delegation Of Authority, CM-1-2-2-3-2); this process applies it. Excludes strategy approval, owned by Trading Strategy Management (CM-1-2-1-4); excludes post-trade compliance monitoring (Regulatory & Compliance, CM-1-2-4-3).
In scope: Verify trader authority against the delegation of authority | route above-authority, near-limit, and non-standard deals for escalated approval | approve, reject, or conditionally approve under the documented escalation procedure | document approvals traceably and block unapproved execution
Out of scope: Setting, waiving, or amending limits and delegation-of-authority policy (CM-1-2-2-3-2, CM-1-2-2-3-6) | strategy approval (CM-1-2-1-4) | compliance monitoring (CM-1-2-4-3)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "pre-trade analysis | delegation of authority | deal terms"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "documented trade approvals, rejections, and conditions"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "No trade executes without traceable authority."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Manage Pre-Trade Analysis | governed-by: Market Risk Management | precedes: Capture Physical Trades"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 08 (2026-09-19). Escalation wording added at Hamid's direction: exceptions may be approved under the formal escalation procedure, but this process never owns authority-policy design — applying versus setting the delegation of authority is the front-office/middle-office split. Structured deals route here for escalated approval per CM-1-2-1-3-8. APQC 20074 STRONG with no validator; generic approval candidate, comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-3-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20022" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20022"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Physical Deal Entry"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "Record executed physical purchases and sales of crude, feedstocks, and products completely and promptly — counterparty, product and quality, quantity, price and pricing basis, delivery terms, location, period, and title-transfer terms — linked to book and strategy, so the trade record is available to confirmation, risk, actualization, and settlement."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.3.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Capture Physical Trades"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers deal entry and validation for physical commodity transactions: capturing full commercial terms including delivery basis and title-transfer terms, validating against execution evidence, linking to book, strategy, and contract, and correcting capture errors. Excludes negotiating or deciding the trade (strategy processes and the authorized execution role); excludes confirmation with the counterparty (CM-1-2-2-2); excludes movement actualization (CM-1-2-3-1) and settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-1, CM-1-2-4-1-2); excludes product exchange agreements, administered under Manage Product Exchange Agreements (CM-1-2-7-1-1).
In scope: Capture full commercial terms of executed physical deals, including delivery and title-transfer terms | validate against execution evidence | link deals to book, strategy, and contract | correct capture errors
Out of scope: Trade negotiation and execution (strategy processes, authorized execution role) | confirmation (CM-1-2-2-2) | actualization and settlement (CM-1-2-3-1, CM-1-2-4-1) | product exchange agreement administration (CM-1-2-7-1-1)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "executed deal terms | execution evidence | book and strategy assignments"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "validated physical deal records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "One accurate record per physical deal, immediately after execution."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001 | SRC-EIA-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Obtain Trade Approval | precedes: Confirmations Management | enables: Actualizations"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 08 (2026-09-19). First of the eight deal-type capture variants; each shares the capture discipline and differs by instrument terms. Title-transfer, delivery-point, and quality terms retained per Hamid's review — they drive actualization, invoicing, tax, and settlement downstream. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-3-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19582" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19582"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Exchange-Traded Deal Capture"@en,
        "Futures Trade Capture"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "Record exchange-executed futures and listed-options trades with contract, delivery month, quantity, price, account, clearing-broker, and allocation details; validate captured execution data against available execution and broker messages; and resolve trade-capture exceptions so the trading record reflects the executed cleared position."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.3.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Capture Exchange Trades"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers capture of exchange-executed, cleared instruments: deal entry from execution records, allocation to books and strategies, validation against execution and broker messages, and resolution of capture exceptions. 'Exchange' here means executed on a futures exchange and cleared — not product exchange agreements, which are administered under Manage Product Exchange Agreements (CM-1-2-7-1-1) and Manage Refined Product Exchanges (CM-1-2-6-2-2). Excludes independent broker, clearing, and exchange reconciliation controls where those are performed by the middle office, risk, finance operations, or another designated control function; excludes margining and collateral, owned by Credit Risk Management (Perform Margining, CM-1-2-2-1-5); excludes broker settlement (Settle Broker, CM-1-2-4-1-16) and exchange-trade settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-3).
In scope: Capture exchange-executed trades with contract and clearing detail | allocate to books and strategies | validate captured data against execution and broker messages | resolve trade-capture exceptions
Out of scope: Independent broker, clearing, and exchange reconciliation controls (designated control function) | product exchange agreements (CM-1-2-7-1-1, CM-1-2-6-2-2) | margining and collateral (CM-1-2-2-1-5) | broker and exchange settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-16, CM-1-2-4-1-3)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "execution records and broker messages | account and book mappings"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "captured, exception-resolved exchange trade records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The trading record reflects the executed cleared position, exception by exception."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Obtain Trade Approval | enables: Perform Margining | precedes: Settle Exchange Trades"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 08 (2026-09-19). Reworded at Hamid's direction to separate capture validation from independent reconciliation controls: this process resolves its own execution and capture breaks; the independent broker/clearing reconciliation control is owned by a designated control function, whose identity is an open follow-up noted in the batch 08 PR. 'Exchange' disambiguated: exchange-executed cleared instruments, corroborated by the Settle Exchange Trades and Settle Broker siblings; barrel exchanges live in the products branches. APQC 19582 is foreign-exchange trading; false lexical match, comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-3-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11522" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11522"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Storage Capacity Deal Capture"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "Record executed storage and throughput deals — facility, capacity, injection and withdrawal rights, fees, and period — so capacity positions, obligations, and costs are represented in the trading system from execution onward."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.3.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Capture Storage Deals"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers deal entry for storage capacity transactions: leased tankage and cavern capacity, throughput commitments, and capacity resale, with their rights, fee structures, and periods. The negotiation of storage and throughput agreements is owned by Negotiate Storage / Throughput Agreements (CM-1-2-7-1-3); this node captures the executed deal into the trading record. Excludes storage inventory administration (Manage Third Party Storage, CM-1-1-3-7-11); excludes storage settlement (Settle Terminal/Storage, CM-1-2-4-1-14).
In scope: Capture storage and throughput deal terms | represent capacity rights, obligations, and fees | link deals to books and strategies | correct capture errors
Out of scope: Agreement negotiation (CM-1-2-7-1-3) | third-party storage inventory administration (CM-1-1-3-7-11) | storage settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-14)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "executed storage deal terms | facility and capacity references"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "captured storage deal records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Capacity deals exist in the book the moment they exist in the market."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Obtain Trade Approval | enables: Manage Third Party Storage | precedes: Settle Terminal/Storage"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 08 (2026-09-19). Negotiate-versus-capture split with CM-1-2-7-1-3 mirrors the established decide-versus-record pattern. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-3-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11522" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11522"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Environmental Instrument Deal Capture"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "Record executed purchases, sales, and transfers of program-specific environmental instruments — such as RINs, RECs, and emissions allowances — with program, vintage, quantity, price, and compliance-period detail, so instrument positions are accurate from execution onward."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.3.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Capture Environmental/Renewable Deals"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers deal entry for environmental and renewable instruments, treating each as program-specific per the batch 07 decision: capturing program, instrument type, vintage, and compliance-period attributes alongside commercial terms. Excludes forecasting the position (Forecast Renewables, CM-1-2-1-1-3); excludes instrument actualization (Perform RINs/REC Actualization, CM-1-2-3-1-7); excludes emissions settlement (Settle Emissions, CM-1-2-4-1-8); excludes regulatory filing (EHS & Government Reporting, locked rule) and the allowance-position economics (Manage and Support Emission Trading, CM-1-1-7-3-3). Program applicability is jurisdiction-specific.
In scope: Capture environmental and renewable deal terms with program, vintage, and period attributes | link deals to compliance positions, books, and strategies | validate against execution evidence | correct capture errors
Out of scope: Position forecasting (CM-1-2-1-1-3) | instrument actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-7) | settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-8) | regulatory filing (EHS & Government Reporting) | allowance-position economics (CM-1-1-7-3-3)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "executed instrument deal terms | program and vintage references | compliance-period calendars"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "captured environmental instrument deal records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every credit bought, sold, or transferred is on the book with its program identity intact."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EPA-RFS-001 | SRC-EPA-EMTR-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Obtain Trade Approval | informed-by: Forecast Renewables | precedes: Perform RINs/REC Actualization"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 08 (2026-09-19). Program-specific instrument treatment per the batch 07 Forecast Renewables decision; the in-scope instrument set remains the open follow-up recorded there. Jurisdiction-specific applicability per the EPA source limitations. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-3-7 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11522" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11522"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "OTC Derivatives Capture"@en,
        "Swap Trade Capture"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "Record executed over-the-counter derivative trades — swaps, options, and other bilateral instruments, with underlying index, fixed and floating terms, notional quantity, tenor, and counterparty — flagging reportable transactions, so financial positions and obligations are accurate from execution onward."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.3.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Capture Financial (OTC) Trades"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers deal entry for bilateral financial instruments: full instrument terms, index and pricing references, and the regulatory attributes that determine reportability under applicable trade-reporting regimes. Excludes trade reporting submission itself (Report Trades to SDR, CM-1-2-1-3-10); excludes confirmation (CM-1-2-2-2); excludes valuation and curves (Market Risk Management: Setup And Manage Curves, CM-1-2-2-3-3; Manage Commodity Valuations, CM-1-2-2-3-15); excludes OTC settlement (Settle Financial OTC Trades, CM-1-2-4-1-11).
In scope: Capture OTC instrument terms and references | flag reportable transactions and their attributes | link deals to books, strategies, and master agreements | correct capture errors
Out of scope: Regulatory reporting submission (CM-1-2-1-3-10) | confirmation (CM-1-2-2-2) | valuation and curve management (CM-1-2-2-3) | OTC settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-11)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "executed OTC terms | index and reference data | master agreement links"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "captured OTC deal records with reporting attributes"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Bilateral derivatives are on the book with the attributes regulation and risk need."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001 | SRC-CFTC-P45-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Obtain Trade Approval | precedes: Report Trades to SDR | precedes: Confirmations Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 08 (2026-09-19). Reportability flagging here, submission at CM-1-2-1-3-10 — under US CFTC rules the capture data drives creation-data reporting; other regimes apply their own rules. Swap terminology per the CFTC glossary. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-3-8 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11522" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11522"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Structured Transaction Capture"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "Record executed structured and non-standard transactions — multi-leg, embedded-optionality, tolling, prepay, and similar bespoke deals — decomposing them into capturable components so their positions, risks, and obligations are fully represented rather than approximated."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.3.8" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Capture Structured Deals"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the specialized capture of deals that do not fit standard templates: decomposing the transaction into legs and components the systems can represent, verifying the representation reproduces the deal's economics and risks, and coordinating with the designated valuation, risk, legal, accounting, and operations stakeholders as required to ensure the captured representation is complete, controllable, and suitable for downstream valuation, confirmation, accounting, and settlement. Structured deals route through escalated approval (Obtain Trade Approval, CM-1-2-1-3-2) before execution. Excludes structuring and negotiating the deal (strategy and origination); excludes independent valuation methodology, model validation, and valuation-control approval, owned by the designated valuation and control functions (Market Risk Management for valuation methodology, CM-1-2-2-3-15); excludes each component's downstream confirmation and settlement, which follow their instrument types (Confirmations Management, CM-1-2-2-2; Settlements, CM-1-2-4-1).
In scope: Decompose structured deals into capturable components | verify the representation reproduces deal economics and risk | coordinate with designated valuation, risk, legal, accounting, and operations stakeholders | correct representation errors
Out of scope: Deal structuring and negotiation (strategy, origination) | independent valuation methodology, model validation, and valuation-control approval (designated valuation and control functions; CM-1-2-2-3-15) | component confirmation and settlement per instrument type (Confirmations Management, CM-1-2-2-2; Settlements, CM-1-2-4-1)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "executed structured deal terms | component and leg templates | stakeholder representation requirements"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "decomposed, verified structured deal records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Bespoke deals are represented whole, not approximated."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Obtain Trade Approval | requires: Manage Commodity Valuations | precedes: Confirmations Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 08 (2026-09-19). Coordination widened at Hamid's direction to the designated valuation, risk, legal, accounting, and operations stakeholders — independent validation and accounting treatment are not assumed identical to Market Risk ownership; which reviewers are mandatory is a follow-up noted in the batch 08 PR. The representation-fidelity duty is the distinguishing purpose: a structured deal captured as the wrong legs is a silent risk error. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-3-9 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11522" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11522"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Transport Capacity Deal Capture"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "Record executed transportation capacity deals — pipeline capacity, vessel charters, railcar commitments, and trucking capacity, with route or path, capacity, period, and rates — so transport positions, obligations, and costs are represented from execution onward."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.3.9" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Capture Transportation Deals"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers deal entry for transportation capacity transactions across modes, including capacity acquired, released, or resold. The ongoing administration of carrier relationships and contracts is owned by Manage Carrier Contracts (CM-1-2-1-2-9) and Manage Product Transport Agreements (CM-1-2-7-1-2); nominations that use the captured capacity are owned by Plan & Execute Nominations (CM-1-1-3-6-1); transport settlement follows the mode-specific settlement nodes (CM-1-2-4-1-12/-13/-4/-5); physical transport operation is owned by Midstream and carriers.
In scope: Capture transportation capacity deal terms by mode | represent capacity rights, obligations, and rates | link deals to books and strategies | correct capture errors
Out of scope: Carrier contract administration (CM-1-2-1-2-9, CM-1-2-7-1-2) | nominations and capacity use (CM-1-1-3-6-1) | mode-specific settlement (CM-1-2-4-1) | physical transport operation (Midstream, carriers)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "executed transport deal terms | route, path, and mode references | rate structures"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "captured transportation deal records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Transport capacity is on the book the moment it is committed."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001 | SRC-DOE-RPPL-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Obtain Trade Approval | enables: Plan & Execute Nominations | precedes: Settle Pipelines"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 08 (2026-09-19). Deal capture here; contract lifecycle at Contract Management; usage at nominations; settlement at the mode-specific settlement nodes — a durable taxonomy rule per Hamid's review. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20674" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20674"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Trading Strategy Development & Stewardship"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1 ;
    skos:definition "Develop, approve, and steward the trading strategies the front office runs — defining each strategy's objective, markets, instruments, and horizon, aligning it with approved authorities and risk limits, reviewing performance, and adjusting or retiring strategies as conditions change."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Trading Strategy Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the strategy lifecycle: proposing and documenting strategies, securing approval within the delegation of authority, aligning with risk limits, monitoring strategy performance and adherence, and adjusting or retiring strategies. Excludes setting the delegation of authority and the risk limits themselves, owned by Market Risk Management (Establish & Maintain Delegation Of Authority, CM-1-2-2-3-2; Manage Risk Limits, CM-1-2-2-3-6); excludes portfolio-level risk appetite, limit-setting, independent valuation governance, and risk-control challenge, which belong to Market Risk Management (CM-1-2-2-3); excludes trade execution and capture, owned by Trade Capture (CM-1-2-1-3); excludes trading compliance monitoring, owned by Regulatory & Compliance (CM-1-2-4-3).
In scope: Propose and document trading strategies | secure approval within the delegation of authority | align strategies with risk limits | review strategy performance and adjust or retire
Out of scope: Delegation of authority and risk limit setting (CM-1-2-2-3-2, CM-1-2-2-3-6) | portfolio risk appetite, independent valuation governance, and risk-control challenge (CM-1-2-2-3) | trade execution and capture (CM-1-2-1-3) | compliance monitoring (CM-1-2-4-3)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "forward view and revenue projections | delegation of authority and risk limits | strategy performance results"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "approved, monitored trading strategies"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Ensure every strategy the front office runs is defined, authorized, within limits, and worth running."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Forecast Revenue | uses-input: Perform Asset Optimization | governed-by: Market Risk Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 07 (2026-09-19). A leaf L4 with no children in the map; the front-office/middle-office split is preserved — strategy stewardship here; authority, limits, risk appetite, independent valuation governance, and risk-control challenge at Market Risk Management (exclusion made explicit at Hamid's direction so strategy approval and risk-appetite approval cannot blur); compliance at Regulatory & Compliance. APQC 20674 is an IT-strategy candidate; false lexical match, comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-1-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19587" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19587"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Counterparty Credit Assessment"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2-1 ;
    skos:definition "Assess a counterparty's creditworthiness — at onboarding, on a periodic cycle, and on trigger events — analyzing financial statements, ratings, ownership and support structures, payment behavior, and qualitative factors, and assigning or updating the internal credit assessment that limits and monitoring run on."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.1.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perform Counterparty Credit Reviews"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the credit assessment process: initial reviews supporting onboarding (Create New Counterparty, CM-1-2-1-2-1, coordinates them), scheduled periodic reviews, and event-triggered reassessments on deterioration signals or corporate events. The output is the internal credit assessment consumed by Manage Credit Limits (CM-1-2-2-1-2). Excludes setting the limits themselves (CM-1-2-2-1-2); excludes continuous surveillance (Monitor & Analyze Credit, CM-1-2-2-1-7); excludes commercial customer credit assessment (CM-1-3-7-4).
In scope: Perform onboarding, periodic, and event-triggered credit reviews | analyze financials, ratings, ownership, and payment behavior | assign and update internal credit assessments | recommend limit and credit-support consequences
Out of scope: Limit setting (CM-1-2-2-1-2) | continuous surveillance (CM-1-2-2-1-7) | customer credit assessment (CM-1-3-7-4)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "counterparty financials and ratings | ownership and support structures | payment and dispute history"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "current internal credit assessments"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every counterparty's creditworthiness is currently and defensibly assessed."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CCRO-001 | SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Manage Credit Limits | enables: Create New Counterparty | informed-by: Monitor & Analyze Credit"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 11 (2026-09-19). Review versus surveillance split: this node reassesses on cycle and trigger; CM-1-2-2-1-7 watches continuously and triggers this node. APQC 19587 STRONG with no validator; supporting comparative evidence."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-1-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20929" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20929"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Credit Limit Administration"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2-1 ;
    skos:definition "Establish, maintain, publish, and adjust credit limits for trading counterparties and counterparty groups — based on approved credit assessments, risk appetite, exposure methodology, and credit-support arrangements — administer documented temporary-excess and exception decisions under delegated authority, and reduce, suspend, or withdraw limits when deterioration or policy triggers require it."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.1.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Credit Limits"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the limit lifecycle: establishing limits from assessments and appetite, structuring them by counterparty, group, tenor, and product where applicable, publishing them to the connected controls, administering and recording temporary-excess approvals made under the approved delegation of authority, and cutting limits on deterioration. Enforcement occurs through the connected controls — pre-trade checks (Manage Pre-Trade Analysis, CM-1-2-1-3-1), surveillance and escalation (CM-1-2-2-1-7), and applicable transaction controls — not through this process itself. Excludes the credit assessment (CM-1-2-2-1-1); excludes portfolio risk appetite, which is governance above this process; excludes customer credit limits in order-to-cash (CM-1-3-7-4).
In scope: Establish and structure limits from assessments and appetite | maintain and publish limits by counterparty and group | administer and record temporary-excess approvals under the delegation of authority | reduce, suspend, or withdraw limits on deterioration or policy triggers
Out of scope: Credit assessment (CM-1-2-2-1-1) | limit enforcement through connected controls (CM-1-2-1-3-1, CM-1-2-2-1-7) | portfolio risk appetite (risk governance) | customer credit limits (CM-1-3-7-4)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "internal credit assessments | risk appetite | exposure and utilization data"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "current, approved, and published credit limits and exception decisions"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Exposure capacity always matches assessed creditworthiness."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CCRO-001 | SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Perform Counterparty Credit Reviews | enables: Manage Pre-Trade Analysis | enables: Monitor & Analyze Credit"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 11 (2026-09-19). Reworded at Hamid's direction: this process establishes, publishes, and administers limits and delegated exception decisions; enforcement happens through connected pre-trade, surveillance, and transaction controls. Temporary excesses are administered and recorded under the approved delegation of authority — which function holds that exception authority is a follow-up noted in the batch 11 PR. APQC 20929 is a corporate-card candidate; false lexical match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-1-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19586" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19586"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Collateral Administration"@en,
        "Credit Support Administration"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2-1 ;
    skos:definition "Administer contractual credit support both held and posted — cash collateral, securities, letters of credit, guarantees, and other eligible security, with their haircuts, thresholds, independent amounts, and substitutions — calling, receiving, returning, valuing, and reconciling credit support under the applicable arrangements, so credit exposure is secured to the extent the agreements require."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.1.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Collateral"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers administration of bilateral and other contractually agreed credit-support arrangements across held and posted positions: issuing and answering calls per credit-support annexes and credit terms, valuing collateral with applicable haircuts, managing eligibility, thresholds, independent amounts, and substitutions, reconciling balances with counterparties, and coordinating release at offboarding (Terminate/Novate Counterparty, CM-1-2-1-2-3). Includes letters of credit, guarantees, and eligible security where in scope. Excludes CCP, exchange, and broker margin workflows, which belong to Perform Margining (CM-1-2-2-1-5) — bilateral margin required under a credit-support arrangement remains within this process unless the detailed operating model intentionally separates it. Credit-support documentation is established through Contract Management (CM-1-2-1-2) with Legal & Corporate Communications; the cash and securities movements themselves execute through the settlement and finance processes.
In scope: Issue and answer credit-support calls per agreements | value collateral with applicable haircuts | manage eligibility, thresholds, independent amounts, and substitutions | reconcile credit-support positions and coordinate release
Out of scope: CCP, exchange, and broker margin workflows (CM-1-2-2-1-5) | credit-support documentation (CM-1-2-1-2, Legal & Corporate Communications) | cash and securities movement execution (settlement and finance processes)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "credit-support terms | exposure calculations | collateral valuations | counterparty statements"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "reconciled, agreement-compliant credit-support positions"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Exposure is secured exactly as the agreements require, both ways."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-ISDA-MA-001 | SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001 | SRC-CCRO-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Calculate Credit Exposure | enables: Terminate/Novate Counterparty | informed-by: Contract Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 11 (2026-09-19). Broadened at Hamid's direction from 'bilateral versus cleared' to credit support versus CCP/exchange/broker margin: contractual credit support of all forms — cash, securities, letters of credit, guarantees — is administered here, and bilateral margin under a credit-support arrangement stays here unless the operating model separates it. Which collateral forms are genuinely in scope, and whether bilateral IM/VM workflows sit with Credit Risk, Treasury, or a specialized team, are follow-ups noted in the batch 11 PR. Credit-support structure per ISDA documentation for applicable OTC frameworks. APQC 19586 scored 0.8 STRONG with no validator; supporting comparative evidence."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-1-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12893" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12893"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Credit Exposure Measurement"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2-1 ;
    skos:definition "Calculate current and, where required by approved methodology, potential future credit exposure for each counterparty and group — aggregating trading positions, mark-to-market values, delivered-but-unpaid and other settlement-cycle exposures, and applicable netting, collateral, and credit-support effects — so limits, monitoring, and credit support run on a defensible exposure number."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.1.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Calculate Credit Exposure"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the exposure measurement process: assembling positions, mark-to-market, and settlement-cycle amounts by counterparty and group; applying enforceable netting and credit-support offsets per the governing agreements; computing potential future exposure where the approved methodology requires it; and publishing exposures to limits, monitoring, and credit support. Exposure measurement may incorporate wrong-way-risk adjustments, concentration considerations, and stressed assumptions where required by approved methodology; portfolio-level analysis remains in Perform Advanced Credit Risk Analytics (CM-1-2-2-1-6). Valuation inputs (curves, marks) are owned by Market Risk Management (CM-1-2-2-3-3, CM-1-2-2-3-15); netting enforceability is a legal determination (Legal & Corporate Communications).
In scope: Assemble positions and amounts by counterparty and group | apply enforceable netting and credit-support effects | compute current and methodology-required potential future exposure | publish exposures to limits, monitoring, and credit support
Out of scope: Valuation and curve ownership (CM-1-2-2-3) | netting enforceability determination (Legal & Corporate Communications) | portfolio-level analytics (CM-1-2-2-1-6) | limit decisions (CM-1-2-2-1-2)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "positions and mark-to-market | settlement-cycle amounts | netting and credit-support terms"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "published current and potential exposures by counterparty and group"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "One defensible exposure number per counterparty, every day."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001 | SRC-ISDA-MA-001 | SRC-CCRO-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Manage Commodity Valuations | enables: Manage Credit Limits | enables: Manage Collateral"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 11 (2026-09-19). Methodology-qualified at Hamid's direction: potential future exposure, wrong-way-risk adjustments, and stressed assumptions apply where the approved methodology requires them — not silently in every number; the exposure-versus-analytics split with CM-1-2-2-1-6 is preserved. Consumes marks and curves owned by Market Risk; owns the aggregation and offsets. APQC 12893 is a WEAK review-link."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-1-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10954" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10954"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Margin Management"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2-1 ;
    skos:definition "Administer margin obligations for cleared and other applicable margined positions — receiving, validating, forecasting, and issuing or responding to margin calls as applicable, reconciling margin balances against CCP, exchange, broker, and counterparty statements, and escalating failed or disputed calls — so margin obligations are met and margin held matches what positions require."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.1.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perform Margining"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the margining cycle: validating externally calculated margin requirements and calculating internal forecasts or independent checks where required, issuing or responding to calls within deadlines, reconciling margin balances against CCP, exchange, broker, and counterparty statements, escalating failures and disputes, and forecasting margin requirements for liquidity visibility. Cleared positions arrive from Capture Exchange Trades (CM-1-2-1-3-4); contractual credit support is administered at Manage Collateral (CM-1-2-2-1-3); the cash movements execute through settlement and finance processes (Settle Broker, CM-1-2-4-1-16).
In scope: Validate externally calculated margin requirements and run internal forecasts or independent checks where required | issue or respond to margin calls within deadlines | reconcile margin balances against CCP, exchange, broker, and counterparty statements | escalate failures and disputes and forecast requirements
Out of scope: Contractual credit-support administration (CM-1-2-2-1-3) | exchange trade capture (CM-1-2-1-3-4) | margin cash movement execution (CM-1-2-4-1-16, finance processes)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "cleared positions and prices | CCP, exchange, and broker margin statements | margin methodologies and deadlines"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "validated, reconciled, escalation-managed margin positions"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Margin obligations are always met, and margin held always matches the positions."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Capture Exchange Trades | uses-input: Calculate Credit Exposure | precedes: Settle Broker"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 11 (2026-09-19). Reworded at Hamid's direction: the CCP, clearing broker, or exchange typically computes formal margin requirements — this process validates, forecasts, and operates the calls rather than asserting in-house calculation authority for every cleared product. Margin versus credit support split per the -3 rule. APQC 10954 'Perform abandonment' is an unrelated false match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-1-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19568" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19568"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Portfolio Credit Analytics"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2-1 ;
    skos:definition "Analyze credit risk at portfolio level — concentrations, stress and scenario impacts, default-likelihood signals, and correlation effects such as wrong-way exposure — so appetite, limits, and mitigation decisions rest on more than single-counterparty views."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.1.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perform Advanced Credit Risk Analytics"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers portfolio credit analytics: concentration analysis by counterparty group, sector, geography, and product; credit stress testing and scenario impacts; default-likelihood and early-warning modeling; and wrong-way risk analysis where exposure and default likelihood move together. Findings feed limit structures (CM-1-2-2-1-2) and reporting (CM-1-2-2-1-8). Excludes decision-making on risk appetite and portfolio credit concentration limits where those are set by the designated risk-governance body — this node analyzes and recommends; excludes market risk scenarios, owned by Manage And Run Risk Scenarios (CM-1-2-2-3-11) and Conduct Advanced Risk Analytics (CM-1-2-2-3-12); excludes independent model validation (designated validation function).
In scope: Analyze portfolio concentrations | run credit stress tests and scenarios | model default likelihood and early-warning signals | analyze wrong-way risk and recommend mitigation
Out of scope: Risk-appetite and concentration-limit approval (designated risk-governance body) | market risk scenarios and analytics (CM-1-2-2-3-11, CM-1-2-2-3-12) | independent model validation (designated validation function) | limit decisions (CM-1-2-2-1-2)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "exposures and limits | counterparty groupings and sectors | stress scenarios | default and ratings data"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "portfolio credit analyses with mitigation recommendations"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The portfolio's credit weak points are found before a default finds them."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CCRO-001 | SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Calculate Credit Exposure | enables: Manage Credit Limits | enables: Manage Credit Risk Reporting"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 11 (2026-09-19). Analyze-and-recommend only: appetite and concentration-limit approval sit with the designated risk-governance body, per Hamid's edit; independent validation not assumed owned here, per the batch 08 precedent. APQC 19568 is a process-control candidate; false lexical match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-1-7 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11215" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11215"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Credit Surveillance"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2-1 ;
    skos:definition "Continuously watch counterparty credit — exposures against limits, breach detection and aging, and early-warning signals from ratings, news, payment behavior, and market indicators — escalating breaches and deterioration per policy and triggering reassessment where warranted."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.1.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Monitor & Analyze Credit"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers continuous credit surveillance: daily exposure-versus-limit monitoring, breach identification, escalation, and tracking to resolution, and early-warning monitoring of deterioration signals that trigger event-driven reviews (CM-1-2-2-1-1) or limit actions (CM-1-2-2-1-2). Excludes the periodic reassessment itself (CM-1-2-2-1-1); excludes limit decisions (CM-1-2-2-1-2); excludes customer credit monitoring in order-to-cash (Monitor Credit Against Limit, CM-1-3-7-4-3).
In scope: Monitor exposures against limits daily | detect, escalate, and track breaches | watch early-warning deterioration signals | trigger reviews and limit actions
Out of scope: Periodic credit reassessment (CM-1-2-2-1-1) | limit decisions (CM-1-2-2-1-2) | customer credit monitoring (CM-1-3-7-4-3)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "published exposures and limits | ratings, news, and payment signals | breach policy"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "escalated breaches and deterioration alerts with tracking"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "No breach and no deterioration signal goes unseen or unescalated."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CCRO-001 | SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Calculate Credit Exposure | triggers: Perform Counterparty Credit Reviews | triggers: Manage Credit Limits"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 11 (2026-09-19). The detect-versus-decide split: surveillance here, decisions at -1 and -2 — the batch 04 pattern applied to credit. APQC 11215 STRONG with no validator; supporting comparative evidence."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-1-8 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10522" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10522"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Credit Risk Reporting"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2-1 ;
    skos:definition "Produce governed internal credit-risk reports — exposures, limit utilization, breaches, credit-support and margin positions, and concentrations, at agreed cadences and levels for management and committees — and provide controlled inputs to applicable external or regulatory reporting through their accountable owners, so the credit risk picture is current, consistent, and decision-ready."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.1.8" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Credit Risk Reporting"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers credit-risk report production and distribution: standard management and committee reporting, breach and exception reporting, credit-support and concentration views, and controlled inputs to external and regulatory reporting owned by their accountable processes. Excludes financial reporting and disclosures, owned by Finance under its locked rule; excludes regulatory filing ownership (Regulatory & Compliance, CM-1-2-4-3); excludes defining enterprise KPIs (KPI workstream).
In scope: Produce management and committee credit-risk reporting | report breaches and exceptions | report credit-support, margin, and concentration positions | provide controlled inputs to external reporting through accountable owners
Out of scope: Financial reporting and disclosures (Finance) | regulatory filing ownership (CM-1-2-4-3) | enterprise KPI definition (KPI workstream)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "exposures, limits, and breaches | credit-support and margin positions | concentration analyses"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "distributed, governed credit-risk reports and controlled external-reporting inputs"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The credit risk picture reaches its decision-makers current, consistent, and on time."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CCRO-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Monitor & Analyze Credit | uses-input: Perform Advanced Credit Risk Analytics | enables: Trading Strategy Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 11 (2026-09-19). Internal report production here; external and statutory reporting is supported through, never owned instead of, its accountable owners — Hamid's edit prevents reading report production as regulator submission. APQC 10522 is an HR-reporting candidate; false lexical match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-2-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10136" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10136"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Confirmation Generation & Matching"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2-2 ;
    skos:definition "Produce and dispatch outgoing trade confirmations from captured deal records — using the templates and terms of the governing agreement — and match incoming counterparty confirmations or equivalent contractual acknowledgements against the captured deal record, using the applicable product, agreement, and confirmation standards, chasing unconfirmed trades within applicable timeliness requirements."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.2.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Generate Confirms"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers confirmation generation and matching: assembling confirmations from captured terms per the governing master agreement or standard terms, dispatching through the agreed channel (including electronic confirmation platforms where used), matching incoming confirmations or equivalent acknowledgements against the capture record per the applicable standards, and escalating aging unconfirmed trades. Confirmation methods vary by product and agreement — bilateral confirmations, platform matching, exchange rules, and contractual notices are all accommodated. Mismatches route to Manage Confirm Disputes (CM-1-2-2-2-5); capture errors surfaced by matching route back to Trade Capture (CM-1-2-1-3) for correction. Excludes confirmation template and documentation control, owned by Manage Confirmation Documentation (CM-1-2-2-2-2).
In scope: Assemble and dispatch confirmations from captured terms | match incoming confirmations or equivalent acknowledgements per applicable standards | chase and escalate unconfirmed trades within timeliness requirements | route mismatches and capture errors to their owners
Out of scope: Template and documentation control (CM-1-2-2-2-2) | dispute resolution (CM-1-2-2-2-5) | capture correction (CM-1-2-1-3)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "captured deal records | governing agreement templates | incoming counterparty confirmations and acknowledgements"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "dispatched and matched confirmations with chase status"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every trade is confirmed or visibly chased, never silently unconfirmed."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-ISDA-MA-001 | SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Trade Capture | precedes: Manage Confirm Disputes | uses-input: Manage Confirmation Documentation"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 10 (2026-09-19). Reworded at Hamid's direction: matching runs against 'counterparty confirmations or equivalent contractual acknowledgements' per applicable product, agreement, and confirmation standards — not asserted as universal literal field-by-field automated comparison; timeliness requirements vary by regime, instrument, and counterparty classification. APQC 10136 is an unrelated sales-forecast candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-2-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11743" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11743"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Confirmation Template & Records Control"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2-2 ;
    skos:definition "Maintain the confirmation documentation basis — approved templates and standard wording aligned to governing agreements, the archive of dispatched and matched confirmations, and the evidence trail that shows what was agreed and when — so confirmations are consistent, current, and provable."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.2.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Confirmation Documentation"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers documentation control for the confirmation process: template and standard-wording maintenance aligned to master agreements and standard terms, version control, the confirmation archive, and retention of matching and dispatch evidence. Legal approval of template wording is owned by Legal & Corporate Communications under its locked rule; the governing agreements themselves are administered by Contract Management (CM-1-2-1-2); regulatory record-retention policy is owned by Regulatory & Compliance (CM-1-2-4-3).
In scope: Maintain approved confirmation templates and standard wording | control template versions against governing agreements | archive dispatched and matched confirmations | retain dispatch and matching evidence
Out of scope: Legal approval of wording (Legal & Corporate Communications) | governing agreement administration (CM-1-2-1-2) | retention policy setting (CM-1-2-4-3)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "governing agreement terms | legal-approved wording | dispatched and matched confirmations"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "current templates and a complete confirmation archive"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Confirmations are consistent with the governing agreements and provable years later."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-ISDA-MA-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Generate Confirms | informed-by: Contract Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 10 (2026-09-19). Template-versus-agreement split: templates implement what the governing agreements say; changing the agreement is Contract Management's lifecycle, reflected here afterward. APQC 11743 is an unrelated routing candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-2-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11040" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11040"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Trade Amendment Processing"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2-2 ;
    skos:definition "Coordinate the confirmation-side processing of agreed post-execution changes to a trade — including amendments, allocations, partial terminations, and early terminations — by validating counterparty agreement, initiating or validating the required controlled trade-record update, re-confirming revised terms, and triggering applicable lifecycle reporting."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.2.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Trade Modifications"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the confirmation-side lifecycle of trade changes: intake and validation of modification requests, verification that both parties agree the change, initiation or validation of the required controlled trade-record update, re-confirmation of modified terms, and triggering of continuation reporting through Report Confirmation To SDR (CM-1-2-2-2-4) where required. The authorized trade-record owner performs the controlled capture update; this process verifies that the record and confirmation reflect the mutually agreed change — it does not independently decide commercial amendments, execute legal novations, or correct internal capture errors that do not alter mutually agreed terms. Excludes the commercial decision to modify (trading and strategy processes); excludes novations and assignments, owned by Contract Management (CM-1-2-1-2-3, CM-1-2-1-2-7); excludes unilateral capture corrections (CM-1-2-1-3).
In scope: Intake and validate agreed modification requests | verify counterparty agreement to the change | initiate or validate the controlled trade-record update | re-confirm revised terms and trigger applicable reporting
Out of scope: Commercial modification decisions (trading, strategy) | novations and assignments (CM-1-2-1-2) | unilateral capture corrections (CM-1-2-1-3) | performing the capture update itself (authorized trade-record owner, CM-1-2-1-3)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "agreed modification requests | capture records | counterparty consents"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "re-confirmed modified trades with triggered reporting"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Modified trades are as provably agreed as new ones."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-ISDA-MA-001 | SRC-CFTC-P45-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Generate Confirms | triggers: Report Confirmation To SDR | requires: Trade Capture"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 10 (2026-09-19). Reworded at Hamid's direction: this process coordinates and verifies; the authorized trade-record owner performs the controlled update. Modification versus novation: a modification changes agreed terms between the same parties and stays here; a change of party is a novation and belongs to Contract Management per batch 09. Aligned with the batch 08 capture-correction distinction. APQC 11040 is an unrelated trade-association candidate; false lexical match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-2-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11175" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11175"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Swap Data Reporting (Continuation Data)"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2-2 ;
    skos:definition "Report confirmation and lifecycle events for reportable transactions under an applicable trade-reporting regime — submitting required continuation data for amendments, terminations, and other post-execution events to the designated registered swap data repository within applicable deadlines, where the organization holds reporting responsibility — correcting errors and retaining reporting evidence."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.2.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Report Confirmation To SDR"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers continuation-side reporting under applicable regimes: determining which confirmation and lifecycle events require reporting, submitting continuation data within deadlines, corrections, resubmissions, acknowledgements, and evidence retention. A confirmation, modification, or lifecycle event triggers continuation-data reporting only where the applicable regime requires reporting, the event is reportable, and the organization has reporting responsibility or an assigned delegated-reporting obligation. Under US CFTC Part 45 this is continuation-data reporting (45.4); trade creation data is reported at capture by Report Trades to SDR (CM-1-2-1-3-10) — the boundary recorded in batch 08. Excludes regulatory-policy ownership and monitoring (Regulatory & Compliance, CM-1-2-4-3); excludes the legal determination of reporting responsibility (Legal/Compliance); excludes regimes, instruments, and events not applicable to the organization.
In scope: Determine reportable confirmation and lifecycle events under applicable regimes | submit required continuation data within deadlines where responsible | correct, resubmit, and track acknowledgements | retain reporting evidence
Out of scope: Creation-data reporting (CM-1-2-1-3-10) | regulatory-policy ownership and monitoring (CM-1-2-4-3) | legal determination of reporting responsibility (Legal/Compliance) | non-applicable regimes and events"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "confirmation and lifecycle events | applicable reporting rules and deadlines | repository specifications"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "submitted, evidenced continuation-data reports under applicable regimes"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every reportable lifecycle event the organization must report reaches the repository on time, once, correctly."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-P45-001 | SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Report Trades to SDR | informed-by: Manage Trade Modifications | governed-by: Regulatory & Compliance"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 10 (2026-09-19). Trigger made explicitly conditional at Hamid's direction: reporting occurs only where the regime requires it, the event is reportable, and the organization holds or is delegated reporting responsibility — not every confirmation event produces a reportable continuation event. Relations use 'informed-by' rather than 'follows' because the controlled relationship vocabulary has no conditional form; the conditionality is recorded here. CFTC Part 45 (45.3 versus 45.4) is US-specific evidence for the boundary with CM-1-2-1-3-10; it is not a universal model for physical confirmations, exchange-traded futures, or other jurisdictions. APQC 11175 is an unrelated profitability candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-2-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11743" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11743"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Confirmation Dispute Resolution"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2-2 ;
    skos:definition "Resolve confirmation mismatches and disputes — logging discrepancies between the capture record and the counterparty's confirmation, investigating against execution evidence, agreeing the correct terms with the counterparty, and driving the resolution into the records and any required re-reporting."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.2.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Confirm Disputes"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the confirmation dispute lifecycle: discrepancy logging and classification, investigation against capture and execution evidence, counterparty negotiation of the correct terms, escalation of aging or material disputes, and closure through corrected records, re-confirmation, and re-reporting where required. Excludes settlement disputes, owned by Manage Settlement Disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17); excludes actualization disputes (Manage Actualization Disputes, CM-1-2-3-1-8); excludes demurrage claims (CM-1-2-4-4); excludes legal proceedings, owned by Legal & Corporate Communications.
In scope: Log and classify confirmation discrepancies | investigate against capture and execution evidence | negotiate and agree correct terms with the counterparty | drive resolution into records, re-confirmation, and re-reporting
Out of scope: Settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17) | actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8) | demurrage claims (CM-1-2-4-4) | legal proceedings (Legal & Corporate Communications)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "confirmation mismatches | capture and execution evidence | counterparty correspondence"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "resolved disputes with corrected, re-confirmed records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "No confirmation mismatch survives unexamined or unresolved."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-ISDA-MA-001 | SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Generate Confirms | triggers: Manage Trade Modifications | enables: Settlements"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 10 (2026-09-19). The dispute partition rule (Hamid, batch 10): confirmation disputes answer 'what did the parties agree the terms were' (here); actualization disputes answer 'what was physically delivered, received, measured, or performed' (CM-1-2-3-1-8); settlement disputes answer 'what is owed, invoiced, received, paid, netted, or applied' (CM-1-2-4-1-17); demurrage claims answer 'was delay or capacity usage compensable' (CM-1-2-4-4); legal proceedings belong to Legal & Corporate Communications. The lifecycle-stage question is the placement test. APQC 11743 is an unrelated routing candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-3-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16385" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16385"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Risk Book Structure Governance"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2-3 ;
    skos:definition "Establish and maintain the governed trading-book and portfolio hierarchy used for risk aggregation, valuation, position control, and P&L reporting — defining risk-reporting book attributes, responsible owners, aggregation relationships, and effective-dated changes — and keep trading, risk, and reporting systems aligned to it."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.3.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Establish And Maintain Book Structure"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers governance of the risk-reporting and control structure: defining risk books with their attributes, owners, and aggregation relationships; controlling openings, closures, and moves with approvals and effective dates; and keeping capture, risk, and reporting systems aligned to one structure. Trading Strategy Management (CM-1-2-1-4) defines and stewards business strategies; Trade Capture (CM-1-2-1-3) assigns individual deals into the approved structure; this process governs the risk-reporting and control hierarchy that strategies and deals map into. Excludes legal-entity, ledger, cost-center, profit-center, and chart-of-accounts structures, owned by Accounting (CM-1-2-4-2-8).
In scope: Define risk books, attributes, owners, and aggregation relationships | control structure changes with approvals and effective dates | keep capture, risk, and reporting aligned to one structure | maintain structure history
Out of scope: Business strategy definition and stewardship (CM-1-2-1-4) | deal-to-book assignment at capture (CM-1-2-1-3) | legal-entity, ledger, and chart-of-accounts structures (CM-1-2-4-2-8)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "structure change requests | ownership and aggregation assignments"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "governed, versioned risk-book and portfolio hierarchy"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "One governed risk-reporting structure that every position and P&L number rolls up through."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CCRO-002A"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Trade Capture | enables: Perform Position & PNL Analysis"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 12 (2026-09-19). Narrowed at Hamid's direction: strategy is business intent (Trading Strategy Management); the book is a controlled risk and P&L aggregation construct governed here — the two never collide. APQC 16385 is a generic framework candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-3-10 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10935" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10935"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Risk Framework Compliance Monitoring"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2-3 ;
    skos:definition "Monitor adherence to the organization's own market-risk policies and control framework — mandate and delegation-of-authority adherence, control-cycle completion, exception-procedure use, and policy-required documentation — surfacing violations and control gaps to the accountable owners."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.3.10" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Monitor Risk Policy Compliance"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers internal risk-policy compliance monitoring: checking that trading activity stays within mandates and the delegation of authority, that control cycles complete with their designated approvals, that exceptions follow their documented procedures, and that policy-required records exist; escalating violations and recurring gaps. The boundary with regulatory compliance is internal-versus-external rules: adherence to the organization's own risk framework is monitored here; compliance with external law, regulation, and market rules is owned by Regulatory & Compliance (CM-1-2-4-3). Excludes policy authorship (risk governance) and disciplinary action (management, HR).
In scope: Monitor mandate and DOA adherence | verify control-cycle completion and designated approvals | check exception-procedure use and documentation | escalate violations and control gaps
Out of scope: Regulatory compliance monitoring (CM-1-2-4-3) | policy authorship (risk governance) | disciplinary action (management, HR)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "policies, mandates, and DOA | control-cycle records | exception logs"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "escalated policy violations and control-gap findings"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The risk framework is followed in fact, not just on paper."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CCRO-002A"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Establish & Maintain Delegation Of Authority (DOA) | enables: Manage Risk Reporting"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 12 (2026-09-19). Fully drafted and conditionally approved by Hamid; held pending on the recorded cross-batch dependency because an approved row may carry no material open question. Internal-versus-external rules is the boundary with CM-1-2-4-3. Batch 16 resolution (Hamid, 2026-09-20): the cross-batch boundary with Monitor Internal Trade Controls is resolved. Monitor Risk Policy Compliance monitors adherence to the internal market-risk framework, including risk appetite, approved limits, risk-book mandates, delegated trading authority, valuation and risk-control procedures, control-cycle evidence, and risk-exception approvals. Monitor Internal Trade Controls (CM-1-2-4-3-4) monitors the operation and evidence of internally implemented controls supporting external trading obligations. Neither process tests or owns the other's framework. Governance note (Hamid, batch 16): the market-risk framework is governed by the designated Risk Governance body, not Compliance — the former 'governed-by: Regulatory & Compliance' relation is removed; no 'Risk Governance' taxonomy node exists and 'related-to' is not a controlled relationship_type, so the non-hierarchical relationship to Regulatory & Compliance is carried here in prose per the conditional-relation precedent (batches 10/13/14). Row content otherwise unchanged from the batch 12 conditional approval."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-3-11 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11211" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11211"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Market Risk Scenario & Stress Testing"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2-3 ;
    skos:definition "Define, maintain, and run the market-risk scenario and stress set — historical and hypothetical market shocks, commodity and basis stresses, and event scenarios — quantifying their P&L and risk impacts so the organization knows what adverse markets would do to the book before they happen."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.3.11" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage And Run Risk Scenarios"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers trading-book scenario and stress analysis: maintaining the approved scenario library, running scenarios on the control calendar and on demand, quantifying P&L and measure impacts, and feeding results to limits, appetite discussions, and reporting. This is the trading and market-risk scenario home that batch 02's approved Perform Scenario Analysis (CM-1-1-4-7-8) explicitly points to — production-case scenario analysis stays there. Excludes credit stress testing (CM-1-2-2-1-6); excludes scenario methodology approval (risk governance).
In scope: Maintain the approved scenario and stress library | run scenarios on calendar and on demand | quantify P&L and measure impacts | feed results to limits, appetite, and reporting
Out of scope: Production-case scenario analysis (CM-1-1-4-7-8) | credit stress testing (CM-1-2-2-1-6) | scenario methodology approval (risk governance)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "positions and valuations | scenario library | market histories"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "quantified scenario and stress impacts"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Adverse markets are rehearsed before they are real."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CCRO-002A"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Measure Risk | enables: Manage Risk Limits | enables: Manage Risk Reporting"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 12 (2026-09-19). Closes the batch 02 pointer: trading and market-risk scenarios here; production scenarios at CM-1-1-4-7-8. APQC 11211 is a WEAK review-link."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-3-12 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20728" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20728"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Advanced Market Risk Analytics"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2-3 ;
    skos:definition "Analyze market risk beyond the standard measures — backtesting the measures against outcomes, analyzing model behavior and measure limitations, investigating risk concentrations and emerging exposures, and developing analytical improvements — recommending changes to methodologies, limits, and controls."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.3.12" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Conduct Advanced Risk Analytics"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers advanced market-risk analysis: backtesting and performance analysis of the risk measures, deep-dives into concentrations, correlations, and emerging exposure patterns, analysis of measure and model limitations, and recommendations to the governance and control owners. Analyze-and-recommend only: methodology approval and independent model validation sit with the designated governance and validation functions (model-risk practice per the CCRO Model Risk publication as reference evidence). Excludes credit analytics (CM-1-2-2-1-6); excludes running the standard measures (CM-1-2-2-3-7).
In scope: Backtest risk measures against outcomes | analyze concentrations, correlations, and emerging exposures | analyze measure and model limitations | recommend methodology, limit, and control changes
Out of scope: Methodology approval and independent model validation (designated functions) | credit analytics (CM-1-2-2-1-6) | standard measure production (CM-1-2-2-3-7)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "measure histories and outcomes | positions and market data | model documentation"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "analytical findings with methodology and control recommendations"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The risk framework's blind spots are found by analysis, not by losses."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CCRO-002B | SRC-CCRO-002A"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Measure Risk | enables: Manage Risk Limits | enables: Manage Risk Reporting"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 12 (2026-09-19). Analyze-and-recommend per the batch 11 rule; model-risk boundaries per CCRO Model Risk practice as evidence. APQC 20728 is an IT-threat candidate; false lexical match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-3-13 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10522" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10522"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Market Risk Reporting"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2-3 ;
    skos:definition "Produce governed internal market-risk reports — measures, limit utilization, breaches, scenario results, positions, and P&L, at agreed cadences for management and committees — and provide controlled inputs to applicable external or regulatory reporting through their accountable owners."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.3.13" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Risk Reporting"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers market-risk report production and distribution: daily and periodic management and committee reporting, breach and exception reporting, scenario and stress reporting, and controlled inputs to external reporting owned by its accountable processes. Excludes financial reporting and disclosures (Finance, locked rule); excludes regulatory filing ownership (CM-1-2-4-3); excludes credit-risk reporting (CM-1-2-2-1-8); excludes enterprise KPI definition (KPI workstream).
In scope: Produce management and committee risk reporting | report breaches, exceptions, and scenario results | distribute on the agreed calendar | provide controlled inputs to external reporting through accountable owners
Out of scope: Financial reporting and disclosures (Finance) | regulatory filing ownership (CM-1-2-4-3) | credit-risk reporting (CM-1-2-2-1-8) | enterprise KPI definition (KPI workstream)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "measures, limits, and breaches | scenario results | position and P&L results"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "distributed, governed market-risk reports and controlled external inputs"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The risk picture reaches its decision-makers current, consistent, and on time."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CCRO-002A"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Monitor Risk | uses-input: Perform Position & PNL Analysis | enables: Trading Strategy Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 12 (2026-09-19). Mirrors the batch 11 reporting rule: internal production here; external reporting supported through, never owned instead of, accountable owners. APQC 10522 is an HR-reporting false match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-3-14 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11743" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11743"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Volatility Surface & Correlation Management"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2-3 ;
    skos:definition "Source, construct, validate, and publish the volatility and correlation parameters that option valuation and risk measurement depend on — by commodity, location, and tenor per approved methodology — so valuations and measures rest on governed market parameters, not ad hoc inputs."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.3.14" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Volatilities And Correlations"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers volatility and correlation ownership: sourcing implied and historical inputs, constructing surfaces and matrices per approved methodology, validating and handling exceptions, and publishing the official parameter set to valuation (CM-1-2-2-3-15) and risk measurement (CM-1-2-2-3-7). Methodology approval and independent validation sit with the designated governance and validation functions; curves are the sibling at CM-1-2-2-3-3.
In scope: Source implied and historical volatility and correlation inputs | construct surfaces and matrices per approved methodology | validate parameters and handle exceptions | publish the official parameter set
Out of scope: Methodology approval and independent validation (designated functions) | curve construction (CM-1-2-2-3-3) | option trading decisions (front office)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "implied and historical market data | parameter methodologies"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "published official volatility and correlation set"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Governed parameters behind every option value and correlated measure."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CCRO-002A | SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Manage Commodity Valuations | enables: Measure Risk"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 12 (2026-09-19). The market-data sibling to curves; same ownership pattern. APQC 11743 is an unrelated routing candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-3-15 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12953" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12953"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Mark-to-Market Management"@en,
        "Trading Book Valuation"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2-3 ;
    skos:definition "Produce and control the official valuations of the trading book — marking positions to market or, where approved, to model per approved methodology from the official curves and parameters, applying approved valuation adjustments, investigating exceptions, and publishing the valuations that P&L, risk, exposure, and reconciliation run on."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.3.15" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Commodity Valuations"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers valuation ownership: running mark-to-market and approved mark-to-model valuations from the official curves (CM-1-2-2-3-3) and parameters (CM-1-2-2-3-14), applying approved valuation adjustments, independent price verification against external sources where required, exception investigation, and publication to position and P&L (CM-1-2-2-3-9), risk measurement (CM-1-2-2-3-7), and credit exposure (CM-1-2-2-1-4). Valuation adjustments may include approved liquidity, model, credit, funding, or other adjustments where applicable to the portfolio and approved methodology; this process executes the approved valuation-control procedures — it does not approve valuation policy, accounting classification, or model methodology, which sit with the designated governance, Accounting (Manage Accounting Treatments, CM-1-2-4-2-1), and validation functions. Structured-deal representation fidelity is the capture side's duty (CM-1-2-1-3-8) with valuation representation coordinated here.
In scope: Run mark-to-market and approved mark-to-model valuations | apply approved valuation adjustments per procedure | perform independent price verification where required | investigate exceptions and publish official valuations
Out of scope: Valuation policy, methodology approval, and independent model validation (designated governance and validation functions) | accounting classification and valuation treatments (CM-1-2-4-2-1) | structured-deal capture representation (CM-1-2-1-3-8)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "validated positions | official curves and parameters | valuation methodologies and approved adjustments"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "published official trading-book valuations"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "One official value per position, methodologically defensible, every day."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CCRO-002A | SRC-CCRO-002B | SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Setup And Manage Curves | uses-input: Manage Volatilities And Correlations | enables: Perform Position & PNL Analysis"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 12 (2026-09-19). Adjustment ownership clarified at Hamid's direction: this process executes approved valuation-control procedures — adjustment categories apply only where relevant to the portfolio and approved methodology, and policy, accounting classification, and model methodology are owned elsewhere; which adjustments are material for the portfolio is a follow-up noted in the batch 12 PR. The valuation node batches 08 and 11 point at. Model-risk boundaries per CCRO Model Risk practice as evidence. APQC 12953 STRONG with no validator; supporting comparative evidence."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-3-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16385" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16385"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Trading Delegation of Authority Management"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2-3 ;
    skos:definition "Establish and maintain the trading delegation of authority — who may trade what products, instruments, tenors, and sizes, and which approvals are required beyond each authority — keeping it current with roles, strategies, and governance decisions so every trade's authorization has a defined basis."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.3.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Establish & Maintain Delegation Of Authority (DOA)"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the authority framework: defining and documenting delegated trading authorities by role, product, instrument, tenor, and size; maintaining them as people, strategies, and appetite change; and publishing them to the processes that apply them. The DOA is applied — never set — by Obtain Trade Approval (CM-1-2-1-3-2) and aligned to by Trading Strategy Management (CM-1-2-1-4), per the batch 07-08 decisions; authority levels derive from governance decisions owned by the designated risk-governance body. Excludes HR role administration; excludes system access provisioning (Process Excellence & IT).
In scope: Define and document delegated authorities by role, product, instrument, tenor, and size | maintain authorities as roles, strategies, and appetite change | publish the DOA to the applying processes | keep authority history auditable
Out of scope: Applying the DOA to individual trades (CM-1-2-1-3-2) | risk-appetite decisions (designated risk-governance body) | HR role administration | system access provisioning (Process Excellence & IT)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "governance and appetite decisions | role and strategy changes"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "current, published, auditable delegation of authority"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every trade's authorization has a defined, current, auditable basis."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CCRO-002A"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Obtain Trade Approval | enables: Trading Strategy Management | governed-by: Regulatory & Compliance"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 12 (2026-09-19). Set here, applied at CM-1-2-1-3-2 — closing the set-versus-apply loop recorded in batches 07-09. Which body owns risk appetite and final limit-exception approval is a follow-up noted in the batch 12 PR. APQC 16385 is a generic framework candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-3-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11743" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11743"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Forward Curve Management"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2-3 ;
    skos:definition "Construct and maintain the forward price curves the organization values and measures risk with — sourcing market data, building and validating curves by commodity, location, and tenor per approved methodology, and publishing the official curve set on the control calendar."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.3.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Setup And Manage Curves"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers curve ownership: market-data sourcing and quality checks, curve construction per approved methodology including illiquid-tenor and basis treatment, validation and exception handling, and publication of the official daily curve set consumed by valuation, risk measurement, credit exposure (CM-1-2-2-1-4), and revenue projection (CM-1-2-1-1-5). Curve methodology approval and independent validation sit with the designated governance and validation functions — this process constructs and publishes, it does not approve its own methodology. Excludes market-data vendor selection and licensing, which sit with the applicable procurement and Process Excellence & IT processes per the operating model; excludes volatilities and correlations (CM-1-2-2-3-14).
In scope: Source and quality-check market data | construct curves per approved methodology | validate curves and handle exceptions | publish the official curve set on the control calendar
Out of scope: Curve methodology approval and independent validation (designated governance and validation functions) | market-data vendor selection and licensing (procurement, Process Excellence & IT) | volatilities and correlations (CM-1-2-2-3-14) | trading views on prices (front office)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "market prices and broker quotes | curve methodologies | control calendar"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "published official curve set"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "One official, defensible curve set that everything values against."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CCRO-002A | SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Manage Commodity Valuations | enables: Calculate Credit Exposure | enables: Measure Risk"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 12 (2026-09-19). The curves consumed by batches 07 and 11 are owned here; methodology-qualified per the batch 11 discipline. Market-data vendor and licensing ownership is a follow-up noted in the batch 12 PR. APQC 11743 is an unrelated routing candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-3-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19568" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19568"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Daily Risk Control Cycle"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2-3 ;
    skos:definition "Coordinate and evidence the daily trading-risk control cycle — performing or obtaining capture-completeness checks, freezing the official market-data set, running or obtaining valuation, position, P&L, and risk-measure outputs, investigating material exceptions, and obtaining the designated control approvals for the daily controlled result."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.3.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perform End-of-day (EOD) Process"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the end-of-day control cycle as coordinated orchestration: capture-completeness checks performed or obtained from their owners, the official market-data and curve freeze, valuation and risk runs, position and P&L production, material-exception investigation, and the designated control approvals — which may sit with different functions (front-office attestation of completeness, risk sign-off of control results, operations validation of settlement status) per the operating model; no single universal sign-off authority is asserted. Orchestrates outputs owned by the sibling processes (curves at CM-1-2-2-3-3, valuations at CM-1-2-2-3-15, measures at CM-1-2-2-3-7, position and P&L at CM-1-2-2-3-9) without owning their methodologies. Excludes the monthly cycle (CM-1-2-2-3-5); excludes accounting close (CM-1-2-4-2).
In scope: Perform or obtain capture-completeness checks | freeze the official market-data set | run or obtain valuation, position, P&L, and risk outputs | investigate material exceptions and obtain designated control approvals
Out of scope: Methodology and output ownership (sibling processes) | monthly cycle (CM-1-2-2-3-5) | accounting close (CM-1-2-4-2)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "captured trades | market data and curves | exception reports"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "evidenced daily controlled result with designated approvals"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every trading day closes once, evidenced, with its designated approvals obtained."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CCRO-002A"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Setup And Manage Curves | uses-input: Manage Commodity Valuations | precedes: Perform End-of-Month (EOM) Process"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 12 (2026-09-19). Reworded at Hamid's direction: the cycle is coordinated and evidenced with designated control approvals rather than a single universal sign-off — different functions may attest different elements per the operating model. An orchestration process over sibling-owned outputs. APQC 19568 is a process-control candidate; false lexical match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-3-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19568" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19568"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Monthly Risk Control Cycle"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2-3 ;
    skos:definition "Coordinate and evidence the month-end trading-risk control cycle — reconciling and reviewing the month's daily controlled results, producing month-end economic valuation, position, P&L, and risk outputs, resolving or formally carrying forward approved exceptions — and provide the controlled economic inputs required by Accounting's reconciliation and close processes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.3.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perform End-of-Month (EOM) Process"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the end-of-month control cycle: month-end valuation and risk runs, reconciliation and review of daily controlled results across the month, resolution or documented carry-forward of approved exceptions, provision of the controlled economic inputs to Reconcile Economic PNL To Accounting PNL (CM-1-2-4-2-6) and to reporting, and the designated month-end control approvals per the operating model. Excludes the accounting close and postings, owned by Accounting (CM-1-2-4-2); excludes reconciliation ownership itself — CM-1-2-4-2-6 owns the economic-to-accounting reconciliation and this process supplies the economic side, a boundary the Settlements & Accounting batch inherits.
In scope: Run month-end valuations and risk measures | reconcile and review the month's daily controlled results | resolve or formally carry forward approved exceptions | provide controlled economic inputs to Accounting's reconciliation and close
Out of scope: Accounting close and postings (CM-1-2-4-2) | economic-to-accounting reconciliation ownership (CM-1-2-4-2-6) | financial reporting (Finance)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "evidenced daily results | month-end market data | accounting calendar"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "evidenced month-end economic result set with controlled inputs to Accounting"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The month closes once, reconciled, with risk and accounting telling one story."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "monthly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CCRO-002A"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Perform End-of-day (EOD) Process | enables: Reconcile Economic PNL To Accounting PNL | enables: Manage Risk Reporting"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 12 (2026-09-19). Reworded at Hamid's direction: coordinated and evidenced with designated approvals, and framed as the economic-input provider to Accounting's reconciliation and close — supply, never ownership, of CM-1-2-4-2-6's economic side. APQC 19568 false lexical match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-3-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11211" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11211"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Market Risk Limit Administration"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2-3 ;
    skos:definition "Establish, maintain, publish, and adjust market-risk limits — by book, strategy, commodity, tenor, and measure — derived from approved risk appetite; administer documented temporary-excess and exception decisions under delegated authority; and tighten or suspend limits when conditions or policy triggers require it."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.3.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Risk Limits"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the market-risk limit lifecycle: establishing limits from appetite across the measures the approved framework uses (such as value-at-risk, position, stop-loss, and tenor limits), publishing them to the applying and monitoring processes, administering and recording temporary-excess approvals under the delegation of authority, and adjusting limits on regime change or deterioration. Enforcement occurs through connected controls — pre-trade checks, monitoring and escalation (CM-1-2-2-3-8), and policy-compliance monitoring (CM-1-2-2-3-10). Risk appetite itself is set by the designated risk-governance body. Excludes credit limits (CM-1-2-2-1-2).
In scope: Establish and structure market-risk limits from approved appetite | publish limits to applying and monitoring processes | administer and record temporary-excess decisions under delegated authority | tighten or suspend limits on triggers
Out of scope: Risk-appetite setting (designated risk-governance body) | limit enforcement through connected controls (CM-1-2-2-3-8, CM-1-2-2-3-10, pre-trade checks) | credit limits (CM-1-2-2-1-2)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "approved risk appetite | risk measures and utilization | governance decisions"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "current, approved, published market-risk limits and exception decisions"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Market exposure capacity always matches approved appetite."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CCRO-002A | SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Monitor Risk | enables: Trading Strategy Management | informed-by: Measure Risk"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 12 (2026-09-19). Mirrors the batch 11 credit-limits rule exactly: establish, publish, administer; enforcement through connected controls; appetite above. APQC 11211 STRONG with no validator; supporting comparative evidence."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-3-7 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16436" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16436"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Market Risk Measurement"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2-3 ;
    skos:definition "Compute the organization's market-risk measures — value-at-risk, sensitivities, position and tenor exposures, and the other measures the approved framework requires — from validated positions, curves, and volatilities, publishing them on the control calendar so limits, monitoring, and reporting run on one measured picture."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.3.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Measure Risk"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers risk measurement: running the approved measures per methodology against validated inputs, quality-checking and explaining day-on-day moves, and publishing results to monitoring (CM-1-2-2-3-8), limits (CM-1-2-2-3-6), and reporting (CM-1-2-2-3-13). Which measures apply is set by the approved framework; measure methodologies and their independent validation sit with the designated governance and validation functions. Excludes scenario and stress analysis (CM-1-2-2-3-11); excludes credit exposure measurement (CM-1-2-2-1-4).
In scope: Run approved risk measures per methodology | quality-check and explain measure movements | publish measures on the control calendar | maintain measurement input traceability
Out of scope: Measure methodology approval and independent validation (designated functions) | scenario and stress analysis (CM-1-2-2-3-11) | credit exposure (CM-1-2-2-1-4)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "validated positions | official curves and volatilities | approved measure methodologies"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "published market-risk measures"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "One measured, methodology-true risk picture per control cycle."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CCRO-002A | SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Setup And Manage Curves | uses-input: Manage Volatilities And Correlations | enables: Monitor Risk"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 12 (2026-09-19). Methodology-qualified per the batch 11 discipline: the measures the approved framework requires, never an asserted universal metric set. APQC 16436 is a generic benchmarking candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-3-8 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20729" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20729"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Market Risk Surveillance"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2-3 ;
    skos:definition "Continuously watch market risk against limits — detecting breaches and near-breaches, escalating them per policy, tracking them to resolution, and watching concentration and regime signals — so no excursion goes unseen or unescalated."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.3.8" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Monitor Risk"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers market-risk surveillance: measure-versus-limit monitoring, breach and near-breach detection, escalation and resolution tracking, and watching for concentrations and market-regime changes that warrant limit or appetite review. The detect-versus-decide split holds: this process surfaces; limit actions are taken at Manage Risk Limits (CM-1-2-2-3-6) and appetite questions go to the risk-governance body. Excludes credit surveillance (CM-1-2-2-1-7); excludes risk-policy compliance monitoring (CM-1-2-2-3-10).
In scope: Monitor measures against limits | detect and escalate breaches and near-breaches | track breaches to resolution | watch concentration and regime signals
Out of scope: Limit decisions (CM-1-2-2-3-6) | appetite decisions (risk-governance body) | credit surveillance (CM-1-2-2-1-7) | policy-compliance monitoring (CM-1-2-2-3-10)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "published risk measures | current limits | escalation policy"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "escalated, tracked limit excursions and regime alerts"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "No limit excursion goes unseen or unescalated."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CCRO-002A"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Measure Risk | triggers: Manage Risk Limits | enables: Manage Risk Reporting"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 12 (2026-09-19). The batch 04 detect-versus-decide pattern, as applied to credit in batch 11. APQC 20729 is an IT-risk candidate; false lexical match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-3-9 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11783" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11783"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "P&L Attribution"@en,
        "Position & P&L Control"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2-3 ;
    skos:definition "Produce and analyze the official, controlled position and realized and unrealized P&L picture — explaining daily P&L by its drivers (price, position, new deals, amendments, and other effects), investigating unexplained amounts, and delivering the validated P&L that management, risk, and the accounting reconciliation consume."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.3.9" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perform Position & PNL Analysis"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers official position and P&L production and attribution: daily P&L explanation by driver, unexplained-P&L investigation and thresholds, position validation against capture, and delivery of the controlled result to management, reporting, and Reconcile Economic PNL To Accounting PNL (CM-1-2-4-2-6). This is the official, controlled figure that batch 07's Forecast Revenue explicitly defers to — the forward-looking projection lives there (CM-1-2-1-1-5); the governed result lives here. Excludes accounting P&L and postings, owned by Accounting (CM-1-2-4-2); excludes valuation methodology (CM-1-2-2-3-15).
In scope: Produce the official controlled position and P&L | attribute P&L to drivers and investigate unexplained amounts | validate positions against capture | deliver controlled results to management, reporting, and reconciliation
Out of scope: Forward revenue projection (CM-1-2-1-1-5) | accounting P&L and postings (CM-1-2-4-2) | valuation methodology (CM-1-2-2-3-15)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "validated positions and valuations | prior-day results | deal activity"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "official, explained daily position and P&L"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "One official P&L per day, explained to its drivers."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CCRO-002A | SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Manage Commodity Valuations | enables: Reconcile Economic PNL To Accounting PNL | enables: Manage Risk Reporting"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 12 (2026-09-19). Closes the batch 07 deferral: projected economics at CM-1-2-1-1-5, official controlled results here, accounting results at CM-1-2-4-2, reconciled at CM-1-2-4-2-6. Business-facing label 'Perform Position & P&L Analysis' queued for the naming pass per Hamid — 'PNL' retained in the technical label, 'P&L' used in prose. APQC 11783 is the competitive-analysis false match batch 02 already rejected."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-3-1-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20042" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20042"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-3-1 ;
    skos:definition "Actualize pipeline movements of crude, feedstocks, and products — recording actual receipt and delivery volumes, qualities, and dates from pipeline meter tickets, carrier statements, and shipper reports, applying pipeline loss allowance, quality-bank, proration, or other carrier and tariff adjustments where applicable, and reconciling actuals against nominated and scheduled volumes — so pipeline transactions settle on verified actuals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.3.1.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perform Pipeline Actualization"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers matching pipeline carrier evidence to deals and scheduled movements, recording actual volumes on the tariff measurement basis, recording applicable carrier and tariff entitlement and quality-adjustment results, recording actual pipeline tariff and charge determinants and validating applicable transportation charges for settlement, and surfacing discrepancies between carrier-reported and scheduled volumes. Loss allowance, quality-bank, and proration mechanisms are tariff- and carrier-specific and apply only where the governing tariff provides them; a quality bank may adjust an entitlement or economic balance rather than a physical quality measurement. Excludes pipeline nomination and scheduling (the owning transportation and scheduling processes); pipeline settlement and invoicing (Settle Pipelines, CM-1-2-4-1-12); transportation deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3-9); actualization disputes (Manage Actualization Disputes, CM-1-2-3-1-8).
In scope: Match carrier tickets and statements to deals and movements | record actual volumes, qualities, and dates per tariff basis | record applicable carrier and tariff entitlement and quality-adjustment results where applicable | record actual pipeline tariff and charge determinants and validate applicable transportation charges for settlement
Out of scope: Nomination and scheduling (owning transportation processes) | pipeline settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-12) | transportation deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3-9) | actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Pipeline meter tickets and carrier statements | shipper reports | scheduled movements and nominations | tariff terms"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Actualized pipeline movement records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Make pipeline movement records reflect carrier-measured actuals under the governing tariff terms."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-API-MPMS-001 | SRC-DOE-RPPL-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "precedes: Settle Pipelines | follows: Capture Physical Trades"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 13 (2026-09-19). Pipeline loss allowance (PLA), quality banks, and proration are tariff mechanisms recorded at actualization only where the governing tariff provides them; the tariff itself is administered by the carrier under the applicable regulatory regime. Q2 charge-determinant rule applied: this process records determinants and validates charges; Settle Pipelines owns settlement. APQC 20042 'Monitor pipeline' is a WEAK operational-monitoring candidate, not this accounting activity."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-3-1-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10954" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10954"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-3-1 ;
    skos:definition "Actualize truck movements of crude, feedstocks, and products — recording actual loaded and delivered volumes, qualities, and dates from bills of lading, terminal lifting records, and truck tickets, recorded on the contractually applicable measurement and temperature/volume basis — so truck transactions settle on verified actuals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.3.1.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perform Truck Actualization"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers matching truck shipment evidence — rack lifting records, bills of lading, delivery tickets — to deals and scheduled movements, recording actual volumes and qualities on the contractually applicable measurement basis, recording verified freight-charge determinants and validating charge inputs for settlement, and surfacing load-versus-delivery discrepancies. Crude gathered from lease tanks by truck actualizes under Perform Lease Actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-6) with its run-ticket and contract-terms context. Excludes truck dispatch and scheduling (the owning logistics processes); truck settlement (Settle Trucks, CM-1-2-4-1-4); actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8).
In scope: Match bills of lading and lifting records to deals and movements | record actual volumes, qualities, and dates on the contractually applicable measurement basis | record verified freight-charge determinants and validate charge inputs for settlement | surface load-versus-delivery discrepancies
Out of scope: Lease-tank truck gathering (CM-1-2-3-1-6) | truck dispatch and scheduling (logistics processes) | truck settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-4) | actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Bills of lading and delivery tickets | terminal rack lifting records | scheduled movements | freight terms"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Actualized truck movement records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Make truck movement records reflect measured lifted and delivered actuals on the contractual basis."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-API-MPMS-001 | SRC-EI-HM32-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "precedes: Settle Trucks | follows: Capture Physical Trades"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 13 (2026-09-19). The governing unit-of-measure basis may be gross, net, observed, standard, mass, or another contractual basis; not every truck transaction involves a temperature correction. Boundary to CM-1-2-3-1-6: mode-based rows partition by evidence and contract context — lease run tickets and first-purchase terms belong to Lease Actualization even though a truck performs the movement. Q2 charge-determinant rule applied. APQC 10954 'Perform abandonment' is a false lexical match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-3-1-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10954" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10954"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-3-1 ;
    skos:definition "Actualize railcar movements of crude, feedstocks, and products — recording actual loaded and received quantities, weights, qualities, and dates from railcar shipping documents, scale weights, and gauging records on the contractually applicable basis — so rail transactions settle on verified actuals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.3.1.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perform Railcar Actualization"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers matching rail shipment evidence — bills of lading, railcar weight and volume determinations, inspection records — to deals and scheduled movements, recording actuals per railcar and shipment on the contractual basis, recording verified rail freight and accessorial charge determinants for settlement, and surfacing origin-versus-destination discrepancies. Quantities may be weight-, volume-, or mass-balance-based according to commodity, equipment, carrier practice, and contract. Excludes railcar fleet management and rail logistics scheduling (the owning logistics processes); rail settlement (Settle Rail, CM-1-2-4-1-13); actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8).
In scope: Match rail shipping documents to deals and movements | record actual quantities, weights, qualities, and dates per railcar on the contractual basis | record verified rail freight and accessorial charge determinants for settlement | surface origin-versus-destination discrepancies
Out of scope: Railcar fleet management and rail scheduling (logistics processes) | rail settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-13) | actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Railcar bills of lading and shipping documents | scale weights and gauging records | scheduled movements | freight terms"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Actualized rail movement records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Make rail movement records reflect measured shipped and received actuals on the contractual basis."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-API-MPMS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "precedes: Settle Rail | follows: Capture Physical Trades"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 13 (2026-09-19). Rail quantities may be determined by scale weight, gauging, or mass balance according to commodity, equipment, carrier practice, and contract; the contractual measurement basis governs. Q2 charge-determinant rule applied. APQC 10954 is a false lexical match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-3-1-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10954" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10954"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Marine Actualization"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-3-1 ;
    skos:definition "Actualize marine movements by vessel and barge — recording actual loaded and outturn quantities, qualities, and dates from bills of lading, independent inspection reports, and ship and shore figures per marine measurement practice, and identifying apparent voyage gains, losses, and quantity differences according to the contractual measurement basis — so marine transactions settle on verified actuals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.3.1.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perform Vessel & Barge Actualization"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers matching marine cargo evidence — bill of lading and outturn quantities, load- and discharge-port inspection reports, ship and shore measurements, timesheets and statements of fact for date actuals — to deals and scheduled voyages, following marine cargo measurement practice (API MPMS Ch. 17); recording actuals on the contractual bill-of-lading or outturn basis; identifying and recording apparent voyage gains, losses, and quantity differences according to the contractual measurement basis, agreed measurement hierarchy, and applicable claim process; and recording verified freight, laytime, and voyage-cost determinants for settlement. Relevant time, notice, and event facts may be made available to Demurrage Management (CM-1-2-4-4); that process determines potential demurrage under the applicable charter party, contract, or terminal terms. Excludes vessel chartering and voyage operations (the owning chartering and marine logistics processes); marine settlement (Settle Vessels & Barge, CM-1-2-4-1-5); demurrage claim identification and management (CM-1-2-4-4); actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8).
In scope: Match bills of lading, outturn, and inspection reports to deals and voyages | record actuals on the contractual bill-of-lading or outturn basis | identify and record apparent voyage gains, losses, and quantity differences per the agreed measurement hierarchy | record verified freight, laytime, and voyage-cost determinants for settlement
Out of scope: Chartering and voyage operations (marine logistics processes) | marine settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-5) | demurrage determination and claims (CM-1-2-4-4) | actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Bills of lading and outturn reports | independent inspection reports | statements of fact and timesheets | voyage and freight terms"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Actualized marine movement records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Make marine movement records reflect inspected load and outturn actuals and make apparent voyage differences visible."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-API-MPMS-001 | SRC-EI-HM32-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "precedes: Settle Vessels & Barge | follows: Capture Physical Trades | enables: Identify Potential Demurrage"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 13 (2026-09-19). Marine measurement per API MPMS Ch. 17; whether a cargo settles on bill-of-lading or outturn quantity is a contractual term captured with the deal — actualization applies the contractual basis, it does not choose it. Load/discharge differences are recorded as apparent gains or losses: they may reflect measurement, temperature, density, timing, sampling, or contractual-settlement effects rather than physical loss. Actualization supplies time, notice, and event facts but does not make the demurrage determination (CM-1-2-4-4). Receipt-side outturn confirmation for distribution movements is owned by Discharge & Reconcile Load (CM-1-1-3-6-4); this row owns the trading-record actualization. Q2 charge-determinant rule applied. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-3-1-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10954" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10954"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-3-1 ;
    skos:definition "Actualize terminal and storage transactions — recording transaction-level receipt, delivery, throughput, and contractually relevant storage-balance actuals for the associated deal or capacity arrangement from terminal operator statements, tank gauging records, and throughput reports, and recording verified throughput, storage, blending, and handling charge determinants and applicable contractual bases — so terminal and storage transactions settle on verified actuals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.3.1.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perform Terminal/Storage Actualization"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers matching terminal operator evidence — receipt and delivery tickets, tank gauging and inventory statements, throughput reports — to deals, scheduled movements, and capacity arrangements; recording transaction-level receipt, delivery, throughput, and contractually relevant storage-balance actuals per movement and period; recognizing terminal gains and losses per the agreement's loss-allowance terms; and recording verified throughput, storage, blending, and handling charge determinants and applicable contractual bases. Storage-deal terms come from capture (Capture Storage Deals, CM-1-2-1-3-5). Excludes terminal operations and physical measurement (terminal operator); terminal and storage settlement (Settle Terminal/Storage, CM-1-2-4-1-14); network book-to-physical stock reconciliation, owned by Perform Inventory Reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5); actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8).
In scope: Match terminal statements and tickets to deals and movements | record transaction-level receipt, delivery, throughput, and contractually relevant storage-balance actuals | recognize terminal gains and losses per agreement terms | record verified throughput, storage, blending, and handling charge determinants and applicable contractual bases
Out of scope: Terminal operations and physical measurement (terminal operator) | terminal and storage settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-14) | network stock reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5) | actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Terminal receipt and delivery tickets | tank gauging and inventory statements | throughput reports | terminal and storage agreement terms"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Actualized terminal and storage transaction records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Make terminal and storage transaction records reflect operator-measured actuals and contractual charge determinants."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-API-MPMS-001 | SRC-EI-HM32-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "precedes: Settle Terminal/Storage | follows: Capture Storage Deals"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 13 (2026-09-19). Trading-record versus stock-record boundary applied: terminal statements actualize transaction-level actuals and charge determinants for the associated deal or capacity arrangement here, while periodic book-to-physical stock reconciliation across the network remains with CM-1-1-3-7-5 (EI HM 32 practice). Q2 charge-determinant rule applied. APQC 10954 is a false lexical match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-3-1-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10954" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10954"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "First-Purchase Actualization"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-3-1 ;
    skos:definition "Actualize crude and condensate lease purchases — recording actual volumes, gravities, and qualities from run tickets and LACT meter records taken at lease-tank custody transfer, applying the contractual gravity, quality, and deduction adjustments and division-of-interest references — so first-purchase transactions settle on verified actuals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.3.1.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perform Lease Actualization"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers matching lease custody-transfer evidence — run tickets from manual gauging or LACT meter records — to lease purchase contracts; recording actual volumes and qualities per lease and run; applying gravity and quality adjustments, deductions, and division-of-interest references maintained under Manage Lease Contract (CM-1-2-1-2-8); and closing lease runs for settlement. Measurement procedures at the lease tank follow API MPMS Ch. 18 custody-transfer practice, performed by the gathering party. Included only where the downstream organization purchases at the lease/wellhead, per the batch 09 scope caveat. This process does not include upstream leasehold administration, land management, production allocation, royalty calculation policy, or exploration-and-production accounting. Excludes lease contract administration (CM-1-2-1-2-8); first-purchaser settlement (Settle First Purchaser/Leases, CM-1-2-4-1-6); royalty and interest payment execution (Manage Marketing Interests & Royalty Payments, CM-1-2-4-2-3); actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8).
In scope: Match run tickets and LACT records to lease contracts | record actual volumes, gravities, and qualities per lease and run | apply contractual adjustments, deductions, and division-of-interest references | close lease runs for settlement
Out of scope: Upstream leasehold administration, land management, production allocation, royalty calculation policy, and exploration-and-production accounting (outside scope) | lease contract administration (CM-1-2-1-2-8) | first-purchaser settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-6) | royalty and interest payments (CM-1-2-4-2-3) | physical gauging and metering (gathering party per API MPMS) | actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Run tickets and LACT meter records | lease contract terms and division-of-interest references (CM-1-2-1-2-8) | scheduled lease runs"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Actualized lease purchase records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Make lease purchase records reflect run-ticket and meter actuals under contractual terms."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-API-MPMS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Manage Lease Contract | precedes: Settle First Purchaser/Leases"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 13 (2026-09-19). 'Lease' in the crude-marketing first-purchase sense per batch 09; the batch 09 follow-up on whether lease-purchase activity is genuinely downstream-owned still stands and is carried in the batch 13 PR. Run-ticket measurement per API MPMS Ch. 18.1 (manual gauging, lease tanks to truck); LACT (lease automatic custody transfer) metering where installed. APQC 10954 is a false lexical match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-3-1-7 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10954" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10954"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Environmental Instrument Actualization"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-3-1 ;
    skos:definition "Update environmental-attribute and renewable-instrument trading records from authoritative registry lifecycle events — including issuance or generation, separation where applicable, transfer, acquisition, sale, retirement, and cancellation — and reconcile internal instrument positions to the applicable registry or regulatory system of record."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.3.1.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perform RINs/REC Actualization"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers transaction-level recording, controlled submission or instruction of registry events where authorized, receipt of registry confirmations, internal-to-registry position reconciliation, exception investigation, and evidence retention for RIN, REC, and other in-scope environmental instruments. For US RFS transactions, EMTS transaction reporting is subject to the applicable EPA rules and account-holder and delegation model (40 CFR 80.1452). For RECs, registry actions are subject to the applicable regional tracking system and program rules; program applicability is jurisdiction-specific. Excludes regulatory-policy interpretation, compliance-applicability and compliance-accountability decisions, periodic regulatory filing, attestation, audit response, renewable-volume-obligation demonstration, and broader EHS compliance reporting, owned by EHS & Government Reporting under its locked rule; position forecasting (Forecast Renewables, CM-1-2-1-1-3); deal capture (Capture Environmental/Renewable Deals, CM-1-2-1-3-6); environmental instrument settlement (Settle Emissions, CM-1-2-4-1-8); actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8).
In scope: Record registry lifecycle events against deals and positions | submit or instruct registry events where the organization has assigned registry-account authority | reconcile internal instrument positions to EMTS and registry records | investigate exceptions and retain registry evidence
Out of scope: Regulatory policy, compliance accountability, periodic filings, attestations, audit response, and RVO demonstrations (EHS & Government Reporting, locked rule) | position forecasting (CM-1-2-1-1-3) | deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3-6) | settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-8) | actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Captured environmental and renewable deals (CM-1-2-1-3-6) | EMTS transaction records | registry statements and confirmations | blending and production data for RIN separation events"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Actualized instrument positions reconciled to EMTS and registries"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep instrument positions verified against the applicable regulatory and registry systems of record."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EPA-RFS-001 | SRC-EPA-EMTS-001 | SRC-EPA-REC-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Capture Environmental/Renewable Deals | informed-by: Forecast Renewables | precedes: Settle Emissions"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 13 (2026-09-19). Q3 decision (Hamid): approve operational registry-event processing where the function is authorized — this process records, submits, receives, reconciles, and evidences transaction-level registry lifecycle events where the organization has assigned registry-account authority and the event is part of the transaction lifecycle; EHS & Government Reporting retains regulatory policy, compliance applicability, periodic compliance demonstrations, attestations, regulatory reporting, and final compliance accountability. Registry events are not necessarily submitted by this process — they may be initiated by a compliance team, producer, obligated party, registry administrator, or delegated service provider per the operating model (organizational neutrality, batch 10). EMTS is the RFS database of record for RIN transactions (40 CFR 80.1452); RECs are tracked in regional energy-attribute tracking systems whose specific operating rules are registry-specific — EPA material is context, not a universal registry-operating standard. Report Trades to SDR precedent (batch 08) applied. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-3-1-8 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11743" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11743"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-3-1 ;
    skos:definition "Manage disputes over what was physically delivered, received, measured, or performed — logging and classifying quantity, quality, and measurement discrepancies, investigating against custody-transfer evidence and independent inspection, coordinating investigation, evidence exchange, and agreement or adjudication of corrected actuals with the relevant external parties under the governing agreement and applicable measurement hierarchy, and closing disputes through corrected actualized records."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.3.1.8" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Actualization Disputes"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the actualization dispute lifecycle across all modes: discrepancy logging and classification; investigation against tickets, inspection reports, and statements; coordination of investigation, evidence exchange, and agreement or adjudication of corrected actuals with counterparties, carriers, inspectors, and terminal operators under the governing agreement and applicable measurement hierarchy — some disputes resolve through independent surveyor findings, contract-defined measurement hierarchies, or contractual claims handling; escalation of aging or material disputes; and closure through corrected actualized records. Under the batch 10 dispute partition, this process answers 'what was physically delivered, received, measured, or performed'. An actualization dispute may inform, coexist with, or give rise to a settlement dispute; it is not universally a predecessor of one. Excludes confirmation disputes over agreed terms (Manage Confirm Disputes, CM-1-2-2-2-5); settlement disputes over amounts owed and paid (Manage Settlement Disputes, CM-1-2-4-1-17); demurrage claims (CM-1-2-4-4); financial recovery of carrier claims, which follows the relevant commercial agreements through settlement; legal proceedings, owned by Legal & Corporate Communications.
In scope: Log and classify quantity, quality, and measurement discrepancies | investigate against custody-transfer and inspection evidence | coordinate evidence exchange and agreement or adjudication of corrected actuals under the governing agreement and measurement hierarchy | escalate and close disputes through corrected records
Out of scope: Confirmation disputes (CM-1-2-2-2-5) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17) | demurrage claims (CM-1-2-4-4) | carrier claim recovery (commercial agreements via settlement) | legal proceedings (Legal & Corporate Communications)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Actualization discrepancies from the mode processes | custody-transfer documents and inspection reports | counterparty, carrier, inspector, and operator communications"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Closed disputes with corrected actualized records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Resolve physical-delivery and measurement disagreements so the actualized record is correct and settlement proceeds on agreed actuals."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-API-MPMS-001 | SRC-EI-HM32-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Actualizations | informed-by: Manage Confirm Disputes"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 13 (2026-09-19). Dispute partition rule (Hamid, batch 10) applied verbatim: confirmation disputes answer 'what did the parties agree the terms were'; actualization disputes answer 'what was physically delivered, received, measured, or performed' (here); settlement disputes answer 'what is owed, invoiced, received, paid, netted, or applied'; demurrage claims answer 'was delay or capacity usage compensable'; legal proceedings belong to Legal & Corporate Communications. The requested 'may-inform: Manage Settlement Disputes' relation has no controlled relationship_type; per the batch 10 conditional-relation precedent the rule is recorded here verbatim — an actualization dispute may inform, coexist with, or generate a settlement dispute — and the reciprocal 'informed-by: Manage Actualization Disputes' is queued for CM-1-2-4-1-17 in the Settlements batch. This process coordinates evidence and controlled correction; it does not claim unilateral power to settle every dispute — some require independent surveyor findings, contractual claims handling, or legal escalation. APQC 11743 is an unrelated routing candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-1-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12901" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12901"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-1 ;
    skos:definition "Settle physical crude oil and condensate purchases and sales — applying contractual pricing, including provisional pricing, deferred pricing, or subsequent price determination where the contract provides, and contractually defined quality adjustments to actualized volumes, issuing and validating invoices, netting where agreements allow, and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions — so crude transactions complete financially on verified actuals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.1.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Settle Crude Oil"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers crude and condensate commodity-value settlement: assembling actualized volumes and qualities, applying contract price formulas, differentials, escalators, and contractually defined quality adjustments, managing provisional-to-final invoice cycles where the contract provides for provisional pricing, deferred pricing, or subsequent price determination, validating inbound invoices, netting, and preparing and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions through the designated finance processes. Lease/first-purchase crude settles under Settle First Purchaser/Leases (CM-1-2-4-1-6). Transportation charges for the movements settle under the mode processes (CM-1-2-4-1-4/-5/-12/-13); bundled delivered-price contracts follow the L4 allocation rule. Excludes actualization (CM-1-2-3-1); exchange-cleared transactions (CM-1-2-4-1-3); settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).
In scope: Apply contractual pricing and quality adjustments to actualized volumes | manage provisional and final invoice cycles where the contract provides | validate counterparty invoices and net where agreements provide | prepare and submit authorized payment or receipt instructions through the designated finance processes and track status
Out of scope: First-purchase/lease settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-6) | transportation-charge settlement (mode processes) | actualization (CM-1-2-3-1) | exchange-cleared settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-3) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Actualized crude movements (CM-1-2-3-1) | contract pricing terms | price indices and quotations | counterparty invoices"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Settled crude transactions with validated invoices and authorized payment instructions"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Complete crude purchases and sales financially on actualized volumes and contractual pricing."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001 | SRC-EIA-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Perform Pipeline Actualization | follows: Perform Vessel & Barge Actualization | precedes: Manage AR/AP & Actuals"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Provisional/final invoicing applies only where the contract provides for provisional pricing, deferred pricing, or subsequent price determination. Quality adjustments follow the contractually defined specifications (gravity, sulfur, water and sediment, contaminants, volume-temperature basis, and other contract terms) — the contract governs, not a fixed list. Q2 commodity-value reading applied. APQC 12901 is an unrelated forecasting candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-1-10 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19948" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19948"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-1 ;
    skos:definition "Settle natural gas purchases, sales, transportation, and storage — applying index and contract pricing to scheduled and flowing-gas quantities, validating pipeline and counterparty invoices per the applicable business-practice standards, settling imbalances and cash-outs per tariff and contract, and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions — so gas transactions complete financially on verified quantities."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.1.10" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Settle Natural Gas"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers natural gas settlement: applying first-of-month and daily index and contract pricing to confirmed scheduled and allocated flowing-gas quantities, validating transporter and counterparty invoices following the applicable wholesale gas business-practice standards (NAESB WGQ invoicing standards in US interstate practice), settling transportation, fuel, and imbalance charges and cash-outs per tariff and contract, netting, and preparing and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions through the designated finance processes. Pipeline allocation, flow, imbalance, and invoice statements provide the applicable quantity and charge basis under the relevant tariff, contract, and allocation methodology (no separate actualization sibling, per the batch 14 Q5 note); the specific pipeline's tariff and the bilateral contracts govern the transaction. Gas scheduling and nominations are owned by the gas scheduling processes. Excludes gas trading and capture (CM-1-2-1-3); settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).
In scope: Apply index and contract pricing to allocated gas quantities | validate transporter and counterparty invoices per applicable standards | settle imbalances and cash-outs per tariff and contract | prepare and submit authorized payment or receipt instructions through the designated finance processes and track status
Out of scope: Gas nominations and scheduling (gas scheduling processes) | gas deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Confirmed nominations and allocated flowing-gas statements | index prices and contract terms | transporter and counterparty invoices"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Settled gas transactions"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Complete gas transactions financially on allocated quantities, applicable indices, and tariff terms."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-NAESB-WGQ-001 | SRC-EIA-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Capture Physical Trades | precedes: Manage AR/AP & Actuals"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Q5: pipeline allocation, flow, imbalance, and invoice statements provide the applicable quantity and charge basis under the relevant tariff, contract, and allocation methodology — pipeline tariffs and bilateral agreements govern the specific transaction and take precedence; NAESB WGQ standards are US industry-practice context, jurisdiction-specific elsewhere. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-1-11 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "17058" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 17058"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-1 ;
    skos:definition "Settle bilateral financial over-the-counter derivatives — determining settlement amounts from contractual fixings and floating-price observations per the confirmed terms, exchanging and validating settlement calculations with counterparties, netting payments under the governing master agreements, and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions — so financial trades complete per their documented terms."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.1.11" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Settle Financial OTC Trades"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers financial OTC settlement: observing fixings and floating prices per the confirmed trade terms, calculating and validating settlement amounts, agreeing amounts with counterparties, applying payment netting under the governing master agreements (ISDA or equivalent), preparing and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions through the designated finance processes, and evidencing completion. Cleared OTC positions settle through the clearing channel (CM-1-2-4-1-3, CM-1-2-4-1-16). Collateral and credit-support administration is owned by Manage Collateral (CM-1-2-2-1-3); close-out netting on default is executed under the master agreement with Legal and Credit Risk. Excludes trade capture (CM-1-2-1-3-7); confirmation (CM-1-2-2-2); settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).
In scope: Determine settlement amounts from fixings per confirmed terms | agree and validate amounts with counterparties | apply payment netting under master agreements | prepare and submit authorized payment or receipt instructions through the designated finance processes and track status
Out of scope: Cleared settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-3, CM-1-2-4-1-16) | collateral administration (CM-1-2-2-1-3) | close-out netting on default (master agreement, Legal/Credit Risk) | capture and confirmation (CM-1-2-1-3-7, CM-1-2-2-2) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Confirmed financial trades (CM-1-2-2-2) | price fixings and observations | master agreement netting terms"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Settled financial OTC payments, netted per agreements"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Complete financial OTC trades exactly per documented terms and netting provisions."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-ISDA-MA-001 | SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Capture Financial (OTC) Trades | informed-by: Confirmations Management | precedes: Manage AR/AP & Actuals"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Payment netting (routine settlement of offsetting amounts, applied only where legally enforceable and operationally configured) is distinguished from close-out netting (default remedy) per ISDA master agreement structure. APQC 17058 is an unrelated finance candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-1-12 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19948" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19948"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-1 ;
    skos:definition "Settle pipeline transportation charges with carriers — validating pipeline invoices for tariff transportation, fuel, loss allowance, and related charges against actualized pipeline movements and verified tariff determinants, resolving variances, and submitting authorized payment instructions — so pipeline services complete financially on verified movement actuals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.1.12" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Settle Pipelines"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers logistics-service settlement for pipeline movements: validating carrier invoices for transportation, fuel and power, loss allowance, quality bank, and related tariff charges against actualized pipeline movements and the tariff and charge determinants recorded at actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-1), resolving rate and volume variances with carriers, and preparing and submitting authorized payment instructions through the designated finance processes. The governing tariff and transportation agreements determine the applicable charges. The commodity value of the moved product settles under the commodity processes; bundled delivered-price contracts follow the L4 allocation rule. Excludes pipeline actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-1); capacity contracting and nominations (transportation processes); settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).
In scope: Validate pipeline tariff invoices against actualized movements and determinants | resolve rate and volume variances with carriers | prepare and submit authorized payment or receipt instructions through the designated finance processes and track status
Out of scope: Commodity-value settlement (commodity processes) | pipeline actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-1) | capacity contracting and nominations (transportation processes) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Actualized pipeline movements and tariff determinants (CM-1-2-3-1-1) | carrier invoices | tariff rate references"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Settled pipeline transportation charges"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Pay pipeline carriers correctly for verified transportation services under tariff terms."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-DOE-RPPL-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Perform Pipeline Actualization | precedes: Manage AR/AP & Actuals"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Q2 logistics-service reading applied; tariff mechanisms (PLA, quality bank, proration) are recorded at actualization and validated here per the batch 13 charge-determinant rule; the governing tariff takes precedence for the specific transaction. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-1-13 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12931" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12931"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-1 ;
    skos:definition "Settle rail transportation charges with railroads and rail service providers — validating freight and accessorial invoices against actualized rail movements and verified charge determinants, applying contracted and tariff rates, resolving variances, and submitting authorized payment instructions — so rail services complete financially on verified movement actuals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.1.13" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Settle Rail"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers logistics-service settlement for rail movements: validating railroad freight invoices and accessorial charges (car-hire and detention charges included where the rail agreement governs them) against actualized rail movements and determinants (CM-1-2-3-1-3), applying contracted and tariff rates, resolving variances, and preparing and submitting authorized payment instructions through the designated finance processes. Railcar lease and fleet costs follow their governing agreements through the owning fleet processes. The commodity value settles under the commodity processes; bundled delivered-price contracts follow the L4 allocation rule. Excludes rail actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-3); rail contracting and fleet management (logistics processes); marine/terminal demurrage claims (CM-1-2-4-4); settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).
In scope: Validate rail freight and accessorial invoices against actualized movements | apply contracted and tariff rates | resolve variances with rail providers | prepare and submit authorized payment or receipt instructions through the designated finance processes and track status
Out of scope: Commodity-value settlement (commodity processes) | rail actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-3) | rail contracting and fleet management (logistics processes) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Actualized rail movements and determinants (CM-1-2-3-1-3) | railroad invoices | contracted and tariff rates"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Settled rail transportation charges"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Pay rail providers correctly for verified rail services."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EIA-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Perform Railcar Actualization | precedes: Manage AR/AP & Actuals"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Q2 logistics-service reading applied. Rail car-hire/detention charges settle here when the rail agreement governs them; marine and terminal demurrage remains with CM-1-2-4-4 per the batch 10 partition. APQC 12931 'Schedule rail' is a scheduling candidate, not settlement."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-1-14 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19558" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19558"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-1 ;
    skos:definition "Settle terminal and storage service charges — validating operator invoices for throughput, storage, blending, handling, and ancillary services against actualized terminal transactions and verified charge determinants and contractual bases, resolving variances, and submitting authorized payment instructions — so terminal and storage services complete financially on verified actuals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.1.14" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Settle Terminal/Storage"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers logistics-service settlement for terminal and storage arrangements: validating terminal and storage operator invoices against actualized transaction-level actuals and the charge determinants and contractual bases recorded at actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-5), applying agreement rates including minimum-throughput and capacity-reservation provisions, resolving variances, and preparing and submitting authorized payment instructions through the designated finance processes. Storage-deal commodity economics settle under the commodity and channel processes; the deal terms come from Capture Storage Deals (CM-1-2-1-3-5); bundled arrangements follow the L4 allocation rule. Excludes terminal actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-5); terminal contracting (commercial/logistics processes); network stock reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5); settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).
In scope: Validate terminal and storage invoices against actualized transactions and determinants | apply agreement rates including minimum-throughput and capacity provisions | resolve variances with operators | prepare and submit authorized payment or receipt instructions through the designated finance processes and track status
Out of scope: Storage-deal commodity economics (commodity/channel processes) | terminal actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-5) | terminal contracting (commercial/logistics processes) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Actualized terminal transactions and determinants (CM-1-2-3-1-5) | operator invoices | terminal and storage agreement rates"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Settled terminal and storage charges"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Pay terminal and storage operators correctly for verified services under agreement terms."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EI-HM32-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Perform Terminal/Storage Actualization | precedes: Manage AR/AP & Actuals"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Q2 logistics-service reading applied. Minimum-throughput and capacity-reservation charges settle per the agreement even when unused; determinants come from actualization per batch 13. APQC 19558 is a storage-constraints candidate, not settlement."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-1-15 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19948" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19948"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-1 ;
    skos:definition "Settle bilateral power transactions outside ISO markets — applying contract and index pricing to scheduled and delivered quantities, validating counterparty invoices and settlement data, netting where agreements provide, and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions — so bilateral power transactions complete financially on verified quantities."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.1.15" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Settle Power"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers bilateral (non-ISO-cleared) power settlement: applying contract and index pricing to confirmed scheduled and delivered quantities, validating counterparty invoices, netting, and preparing and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions through the designated finance processes. Delivered quantities may be derived from meter data, scheduling coordinator records, bilateral schedules, ISO/RTO data, or other contractually agreed sources (batch 14 Q5 note). ISO/RTO-cleared activity settles under Settle ISO/Power Pools (CM-1-2-4-1-7); a bilateral transaction scheduled through an ISO may have both a bilateral settlement here and ISO charges there. Excludes power trading and capture (CM-1-2-1-3); transmission deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3-11); settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).
In scope: Apply contract and index pricing to scheduled and delivered power quantities | validate counterparty invoices | net where agreements provide | prepare and submit authorized payment or receipt instructions through the designated finance processes and track status
Out of scope: ISO-cleared settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-7) | power deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3) | transmission capture (CM-1-2-1-3-11) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Confirmed schedules and metering data | contract and index prices | counterparty invoices"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Settled bilateral power transactions"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Complete bilateral power transactions financially on verified scheduled and delivered quantities."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-FERC-RTO-001 | SRC-EIA-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Capture Physical Trades | precedes: Manage AR/AP & Actuals"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Boundary to -7: bilateral contract settlement here; ISO statement settlement there; one transaction may touch both. Delivered-quantity sources are contractually agreed and market-design-specific (meter data, scheduling coordinator records, bilateral schedules, ISO/RTO data, or other agreed sources). APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-1-16 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19948" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19948"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-1 ;
    skos:definition "Settle broker and clearing-member account activity — reconciling broker statements against internal trade, position, and margin records, validating commissions, fees, and charges, preparing, submitting, tracking, and reconciling authorized broker-facing payment or collateral-transfer instructions, including margin transfers, through the designated Treasury, banking, or payment-operations process, and resolving statement discrepancies — so broker accounts remain accurate, funded, and evidenced."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.1.16" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Settle Broker"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the broker-facing account relationship for executing and clearing brokers: daily reconciliation of broker statements against internal trades, positions, and cash and margin balances; validation of commissions, exchange and clearing fees, and other charges against agreed schedules; preparation, submission, tracking, and reconciliation of authorized broker-facing payment and collateral-transfer instructions, including margin transfers, through the designated Treasury, banking, or payment-operations process, per the batch 11 binding and the batch 14 Q3 payment-authority rule; and resolution of statement discrepancies with brokers. Margin requirement validation and calls are owned by Perform Margining (CM-1-2-2-1-5); trade-level exchange settlement values by Settle Exchange Trades (CM-1-2-4-1-3). Excludes broker relationship management and selection (commercial processes); margining (CM-1-2-2-1-5); bank payment release and execution (Treasury/banking processes); settlement disputes escalated beyond broker statement resolution (CM-1-2-4-1-17).
In scope: Reconcile broker statements against internal trade, position, and margin records | validate commissions, fees, and charges | prepare, submit, track, and reconcile authorized broker-facing payment and margin-transfer instructions through the designated process | resolve statement discrepancies
Out of scope: Margin requirement validation and calls (CM-1-2-2-1-5) | trade-level exchange settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-3) | bank payment release and execution (Treasury/banking processes) | broker selection and relationship management (commercial processes) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Broker and clearing statements | internal trade, position, and margin records | commission and fee schedules | margin calls validated by CM-1-2-2-1-5"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Reconciled broker accounts with authorized broker-payment and margin-transfer instructions, status, and evidence"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep broker accounts reconciled, correctly charged, and funded through authorized, tracked instructions with an evidenced trail."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Settle Exchange Trades | informed-by: Perform Margining | precedes: Manage AR/AP & Actuals"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Q4 decision (Hamid) applied: broker-facing account relationship here; trade-level cleared settlement at -3; margin requirement control at CM-1-2-2-1-5. The batch 11 binding is preserved as the operational broker-account side of margin cash movements — this process prepares, submits, tracks, and reconciles the authorized instructions; the designated Treasury, banking, or payment-operations process executes the bank movement. Whether this process submits payment instructions or only requests them is operating-model-specific (open question carried in the batch 14 PR). APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-1-17 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11743" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11743"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-1 ;
    skos:definition "Manage disputes over what is owed, invoiced, received, paid, netted, or applied — logging and classifying settlement discrepancies, investigating against actualized records, contractual terms, and settlement calculations, coordinating resolution with counterparties, carriers, brokers, and operators under the governing agreements, escalating aging or material disputes, and closing them through corrected settlements and evidenced adjustments."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.1.17" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Settlement Disputes"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the settlement dispute lifecycle across all settlement processes: discrepancy logging and classification; investigation against actualized quantities and determinants, contractual prices and rates, and settlement calculations; coordination of resolution with the relevant external parties under the governing agreements; escalation of aging or material disputes; and closure through corrected settlements, credit notes, or evidenced adjustments. Under the batch 10 dispute partition, this process answers 'what is owed, invoiced, received, paid, netted, or applied'. Where a dispute concerns an invoice calculation but not the underlying actual or agreed terms, it remains a settlement dispute; a dispute whose root cause is a physical or measurement disagreement is redirected to Manage Actualization Disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8). Excludes confirmation disputes (CM-1-2-2-2-5); actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8); demurrage claims (CM-1-2-4-4); ISO statement disputes within ISO processes (CM-1-2-4-1-7); financial write-offs (Finance, locked rule); legal proceedings (Legal & Corporate Communications).
In scope: Log and classify settlement discrepancies | investigate against actuals, terms, and calculations | coordinate resolution under governing agreements | escalate and close through corrected settlements and evidenced adjustments
Out of scope: Confirmation disputes (CM-1-2-2-2-5) | actualization disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8) | demurrage claims (CM-1-2-4-4) | ISO statement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-7) | write-offs (Finance, locked rule) | legal proceedings (Legal & Corporate Communications)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Settlement discrepancies from the settlement processes | actualized records and determinants | contractual terms and calculations | counterparty communications"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Closed settlement disputes with corrected, evidenced settlements"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Resolve financial-settlement disagreements so transactions close on correct, evidenced amounts."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Settlements | informed-by: Manage Actualization Disputes | informed-by: Manage Confirm Disputes"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Dispute partition rule (Hamid, batch 10) applied verbatim: confirmation disputes answer 'what did the parties agree the terms were'; actualization disputes answer 'what was physically delivered, received, measured, or performed'; settlement disputes answer 'what is owed, invoiced, received, paid, netted, or applied' (here); demurrage claims answer 'was delay or capacity usage compensable'; legal proceedings belong to Legal & Corporate Communications. Invoice-calculation rule (Hamid, batch 14): a dispute about the invoice calculation, not the underlying actual or agreed terms, remains a settlement dispute. The requested 'may-be-informed-by' relations have no controlled relationship_type; per the batch 10/13 conditional-relation precedent, informed-by is used with the conditional rule recorded verbatim: a settlement dispute may be caused by an unresolved actualization dispute or a confirmation mismatch, may be purely financial (rate, tax, invoice, netting, fee, timing, or duplicate-charge issues), and may continue after the underlying physical or confirmation question is settled — no strict lifecycle sequencing is implied. APQC 11743 is an unrelated routing candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-1-18 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19948" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19948"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-1 ;
    skos:definition "Settle purchases and sales of refinery intermediates and blend components — applying contractual and index-based pricing to actualized volumes and qualities, generating and validating invoices, netting where agreements provide, and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions — so intermediate transactions complete financially on verified actuals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.1.18" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Settle Intermediates"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers commodity-value settlement for intermediates and blend components (for example vacuum gasoil, naphtha, reformate, alkylate, blendstocks): applying contract and index pricing with quality adjustments to actualized volumes, generating and validating invoices, netting, and preparing and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions through the designated finance processes. The product-versus-intermediate boundary follows the commodity classification, product master, and commercial classification captured with the deal — not the physical location or whether a stream is used inside a refinery; finished refined products settle under Settle Products (CM-1-2-4-1-2). Transportation and terminal charges settle under the mode processes. Excludes actualization (CM-1-2-3-1); settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).
In scope: Apply contractual and index pricing with quality adjustments to actualized intermediate volumes | generate and validate invoices | net where agreements provide | prepare and submit authorized payment or receipt instructions through the designated finance processes and track status
Out of scope: Finished-product settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-2) | transportation and terminal charges (mode processes) | actualization (CM-1-2-3-1) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Actualized intermediate movements | contract and index pricing | counterparty invoices"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Settled intermediate transactions"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Complete intermediate and blend-component transactions financially on verified actuals."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EIA-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Actualizations | precedes: Manage AR/AP & Actuals"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Product-versus-intermediate partition rule (Hamid, batch 14): follows the commodity classification, product master, and commercial classification captured with the deal — not simply the physical location or refinery use; matters for blendstocks, refinery intermediates, bio-components, and imported feedstocks. Consistent with the batch 04 finished-products-plus-intermediates material scope. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-1-19 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19948" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19948"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-1 ;
    skos:definition "Settle purchases and sales of other commodities traded by the organization — such as NGLs, petrochemicals, sulfur, petroleum coke, and other by-products or ancillary commodities — applying contractual pricing to actualized quantities, generating and validating invoices, and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions on the same settlement discipline as the named commodity processes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.1.19" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Settle Other Commodities"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """This is a controlled residual category, not a default dumping ground: a commodity belongs here only if no named sibling owns its commercial settlement and its settlement mechanics do not require a distinct regulated, market-channel, or logistics-service process. Covers commodity-value settlement for such commodities: applying contract and index pricing to actualized quantities, generating and validating invoices, netting where agreements provide, and preparing and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions through the designated finance processes. If trading volume or control needs justify it, a dedicated row should be proposed through taxonomy governance rather than broadening this row. Transportation and terminal charges settle under the mode processes. Excludes crude (CM-1-2-4-1-1), products (CM-1-2-4-1-2), intermediates (CM-1-2-4-1-18), gas (CM-1-2-4-1-10), power (CM-1-2-4-1-7/-15), environmental instruments (CM-1-2-4-1-8); actualization (CM-1-2-3-1); settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).
In scope: Apply contractual pricing to actualized quantities of unlisted commodities | generate and validate invoices | net where agreements provide | prepare and submit authorized payment or receipt instructions through the designated finance processes and track status
Out of scope: Commodities with named settlement rows (CM-1-2-4-1-1/-2/-7/-8/-10/-15/-18) | actualization (CM-1-2-3-1) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Actualized movements of unlisted commodities | contract pricing | counterparty invoices"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Settled other-commodity transactions"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Give every traded commodity a settlement owner without diluting the named processes."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EIA-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Actualizations | precedes: Manage AR/AP & Actuals"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Controlled residual category (Hamid, batch 14): membership requires that no named sibling owns the commodity's commercial settlement and that its mechanics need no distinct regulated, market-channel, or logistics-service process; growth routes through taxonomy governance, not scope creep. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-1-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12914" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12914"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-1 ;
    skos:definition "Settle physical refined product purchases and sales — applying contractual and index-based pricing to actualized volumes on the contractual measurement basis, generating and validating invoices, netting where agreements provide, and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions — so product transactions complete financially on verified actuals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.1.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Settle Products"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers refined-product commodity-value settlement: assembling actualized volumes and qualities, applying contract and index pricing with differentials and adjustments, generating outbound and validating inbound invoices, netting, and preparing and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions through the designated finance processes. The product-versus-intermediate boundary follows the commodity classification, product master, and commercial classification captured with the deal — not the physical location or whether a stream is used inside a refinery; intermediates settle under Settle Intermediates (CM-1-2-4-1-18). Product exchange agreements settle per their agreement terms with imbalance treatment administered under Manage Product Exchange Agreements (CM-1-2-7-1-1). Transportation and terminal charges settle under the mode processes; bundled delivered-price contracts follow the L4 allocation rule. Excludes actualization (CM-1-2-3-1); settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).
In scope: Apply contractual and index pricing to actualized product volumes | generate and validate invoices | net where agreements provide | prepare and submit authorized payment or receipt instructions through the designated finance processes and track status
Out of scope: Intermediates settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-18) | exchange-agreement administration (CM-1-2-7-1-1) | transportation and terminal charges (mode processes) | actualization (CM-1-2-3-1) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Actualized product movements | contract and index pricing terms | counterparty invoices"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Settled product transactions"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Complete refined product purchases and sales financially on actualized volumes and contractual pricing."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001 | SRC-EIA-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Actualizations | precedes: Manage AR/AP & Actuals"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Q2 commodity-value reading applied. Product-versus-intermediate partition rule (Hamid, batch 14): follows the commodity classification, product master, and commercial classification captured with the deal — not simply the physical location or refinery use; matters for blendstocks, refinery intermediates, bio-components, and imported feedstocks. APQC 12914 'Sell by-products' is a WEAK sales candidate, not settlement."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-1-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19582" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19582"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-1 ;
    skos:definition "Settle exchange-traded and cleared transactions — validating and allocating daily settlement variation and final settlement amounts to trades and positions against exchange and clearinghouse values, settling expiries, exercises, and deliveries per contract specifications, and reconciling trade-level settlement outcomes — so cleared positions complete financially per exchange rules."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.1.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Settle Exchange Trades"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers trade-level settlement of exchange-traded and cleared instruments: validating daily settlement prices and variation amounts against clearinghouse determinations and allocating them to trades and positions, settling contract expiries, option exercises and assignments, and physically delivered exchange contracts per contract specifications, and reconciling settlement outcomes at the trade and position level. Daily settlement variation may operationally flow through the broker or clearing account: this process validates and allocates the exchange and clearing settlement values; Settle Broker (CM-1-2-4-1-16) reconciles the broker account and coordinates the associated cash movement. Margin requirement validation and calls are owned by Perform Margining (CM-1-2-2-1-5). Excludes exchange trade capture (CM-1-2-1-3-4); settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).
In scope: Validate and allocate daily settlement variation against clearinghouse values | settle expiries, exercises, assignments, and deliveries per contract specifications | reconcile trade-level settlement outcomes
Out of scope: Margining (CM-1-2-2-1-5) | broker account reconciliation and associated cash movement (CM-1-2-4-1-16) | exchange trade capture (CM-1-2-1-3-4) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Captured exchange trades (CM-1-2-1-3-4) | exchange and clearinghouse settlement values | contract specifications"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Settled exchange transactions reconciled to clearinghouse values"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Ensure cleared transactions settle exactly per exchange and clearinghouse determinations, with discrepancies surfaced."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Capture Exchange Trades | informed-by: Perform Margining | precedes: Settle Broker"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Q4 decision (Hamid): trade/position-level settlement under exchange and clearing rules here; the broker/clearing-member account relationship at CM-1-2-4-1-16; margin requirement control at CM-1-2-2-1-5. Daily settlement variation is often handled operationally through the broker/clearing account rather than as a standalone trade-level payment — the split is validate-and-allocate here, reconcile-and-coordinate-cash there. Settlement values are determined by the exchange/clearinghouse; this process validates and applies them, consistent with the batch 11 validate-not-recalculate pattern. APQC 19582 is an FX-execution candidate, not settlement."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-1-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19948" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19948"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-1 ;
    skos:definition "Settle truck transportation charges with carriers — validating freight invoices against actualized movements and verified freight-charge determinants, applying contracted rates and accessorials, resolving invoice variances, and submitting authorized payment instructions — so trucking services complete financially on verified movement actuals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.1.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Settle Trucks"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers logistics-service settlement for truck movements: validating carrier freight invoices against actualized truck movements and the freight-charge determinants recorded at actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-2), applying contracted rates, fuel surcharges, and accessorial charges, resolving rate and quantity variances with carriers, netting where agreements provide, and preparing and submitting authorized payment instructions through the designated finance processes. The commodity value of the moved product settles under the commodity processes; bundled delivered-price contracts follow the L4 allocation rule. Excludes truck actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-2); carrier contracting (the owning logistics and procurement processes); settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).
In scope: Validate carrier freight invoices against actualized movements and determinants | apply contracted rates, surcharges, and accessorials | resolve carrier invoice variances | prepare and submit authorized payment or receipt instructions through the designated finance processes and track status
Out of scope: Commodity-value settlement (commodity processes) | truck actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-2) | carrier contracting (logistics/procurement processes) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Actualized truck movements and freight determinants (CM-1-2-3-1-2) | carrier invoices | contracted rates"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Settled truck freight charges"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Pay carriers correctly for verified trucking services."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EIA-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Perform Truck Actualization | precedes: Manage AR/AP & Actuals"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Q2 logistics-service reading applied: this row settles the trucking service charge; the product value settles under the commodity rows; one delivery may produce both settlement streams. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-1-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12927" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12927"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-1 ;
    skos:definition "Settle marine transportation charges — validating freight, charter hire, and voyage-related invoices against actualized voyages and verified freight, laytime, and voyage-cost determinants, applying charter party and freight-agreement terms, resolving variances, and submitting authorized payment instructions — so marine services complete financially on verified voyage actuals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.1.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Settle Vessels & Barge"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers logistics-service settlement for marine movements: validating owner and operator invoices for freight, charter hire, deadfreight, and other agreed voyage-service charges against actualized voyages and the determinants recorded at actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-4), applying charter party and contract-of-affreightment terms, resolving variances, and preparing and submitting authorized payment instructions through the designated finance processes. Freight and agreed voyage-service charges are settled here; demurrage, detention, despatch, and delay compensation follow the governing charter party, terminal agreement, or transport contract and are handled by Demurrage Management (CM-1-2-4-4) where that process is in scope, per the batch 10 partition. The cargo value settles under the commodity processes. Excludes marine actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-4); chartering and voyage operations (marine logistics processes); demurrage, detention, despatch, and delay compensation (CM-1-2-4-4); settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).
In scope: Validate freight, charter hire, and voyage-charge invoices against actualized voyages | apply charter party and freight-agreement terms | resolve variances with owners and operators | prepare and submit authorized payment or receipt instructions through the designated finance processes and track status
Out of scope: Cargo-value settlement (commodity processes) | marine actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-4) | demurrage, detention, despatch, and delay compensation (CM-1-2-4-4) | chartering (marine logistics processes) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Actualized voyages and voyage-cost determinants (CM-1-2-3-1-4) | owner and operator invoices | charter party and freight terms"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Settled marine transportation charges"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Pay marine service providers correctly for verified voyage services."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EIA-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Perform Vessel & Barge Actualization | precedes: Manage AR/AP & Actuals"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Q2 logistics-service reading applied. Agreed freight and voyage-service charges settle here; delay and capacity compensation (demurrage, detention, despatch) stays wholly with CM-1-2-4-4 — not all maritime claims are demurrage, and the governing agreement decides the claim type. APQC 12927 'Qualify vessels' is a vetting candidate, not settlement."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-1-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19948" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19948"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-1 ;
    skos:definition "Settle crude and condensate first-purchase transactions — calculating amounts due from actualized lease runs under contractual pricing, gravity and quality adjustments, and deductions, producing settlement statements consistent with the division-of-interest references, and submitting authorized payment instructions — so lease purchases complete financially on verified run actuals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.1.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Settle First Purchaser/Leases"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Included only where the downstream Supply & Trading organization purchases crude or condensate at lease/wellhead or administers first-purchaser arrangements, per the batch 09 scope caveat. Covers first-purchaser settlement: assembling actualized lease runs (Perform Lease Actualization, CM-1-2-3-1-6), applying contract pricing, gravity and quality adjustments, and deductions maintained under Manage Lease Contract (CM-1-2-1-2-8), producing lease settlement statements consistent with division-of-interest references, and preparing and submitting authorized payment instructions through the designated finance processes. Royalty and interest payment execution to owners is owned by Manage Marketing Interests & Royalty Payments (CM-1-2-4-2-3). This process does not absorb upstream land and lease administration, production allocation, royalty policy, exploration-and-production accounting, owner-payment entitlement calculation policy, or statutory first-purchaser compliance obligations not delegated to downstream. Excludes lease actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-6); lease contract administration (CM-1-2-1-2-8); settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).
In scope: Calculate first-purchase amounts from actualized runs and contract terms | produce lease settlement statements consistent with division-of-interest references | prepare and submit authorized payment or receipt instructions through the designated finance processes and track status | synchronize with royalty-payment execution
Out of scope: Royalty and interest payment execution (CM-1-2-4-2-3) | lease actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-6) | lease contract administration (CM-1-2-1-2-8) | upstream land/lease administration, production allocation, royalty policy, E&P accounting, owner-payment entitlement policy, and non-delegated statutory first-purchaser compliance (outside scope) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Actualized lease runs (CM-1-2-3-1-6) | lease contract pricing and deduction terms (CM-1-2-1-2-8) | division-of-interest references"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Settled first-purchase transactions with statements for payment execution"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Complete lease purchases financially on verified runs and contractual terms, feeding correct interest-level payment execution."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-API-MPMS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Perform Lease Actualization | precedes: Manage Marketing Interests & Royalty Payments"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Conditional downstream scope: 'first purchaser' per the batch 09 crude-marketing reading; the standing batch 09 follow-up on whether lease-purchase activity is genuinely downstream-owned or a cross-segment/integrated-company exception applies here and is carried in the batch 14 PR. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-1-7 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19948" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19948"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-1 ;
    skos:definition "Settle transactions with independent system operators, regional transmission organizations, and power pools — validating ISO settlement statements across the day-ahead and real-time settlement cycles and their subsequent true-up versions, performing independent shadow-settlement checks, disputing discrepancies within ISO timelines, and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions — so ISO-cleared activity completes financially per market rules."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.1.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Settle ISO/Power Pools"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers ISO/RTO and power-pool settlement: receiving and validating settlement statements for energy, capacity, ancillary services, transmission, and associated charge types under the market's two-settlement (day-ahead/real-time) design and multi-version true-up calendar; performing shadow settlement against internal scheduling and metering data; raising statement disputes within the ISO's dispute windows; and preparing and submitting authorized payment and receipt instructions through the designated finance processes. The applicable ISO/RTO settlement statement is the governing market settlement record under that market's tariff, subject to the organization's validation and dispute rights; the ISO tariff is authoritative for charge types, timing, dispute windows, and true-up treatment. Bilateral (non-ISO) power settles under Settle Power (CM-1-2-4-1-15). Excludes market participation strategy and bidding (trading processes); transmission deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3-11); settlement disputes outside ISO statement processes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).
In scope: Validate ISO settlement statements across versions and charge types | perform shadow settlement against internal data | raise statement disputes within ISO timelines | prepare and submit authorized payment or receipt instructions through the designated finance processes and track status
Out of scope: Bilateral power settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-15) | bidding and market participation strategy (trading processes) | transmission deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3-11) | non-ISO settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "ISO settlement statements and true-ups | internal scheduling and metering data | market tariff and charge-type references"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Validated and settled ISO statements"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Ensure ISO-cleared activity settles exactly per validated market statements, with discrepancies disputed in time."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-FERC-RTO-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Capture Transmission Deals | precedes: Manage AR/AP & Actuals"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Q5: no actualization sibling — the applicable ISO/RTO settlement statement is the governing market settlement record under that market's tariff, subject to validation and dispute rights; shadow settlement is the internal verification and control step. ISO/RTO applicability is jurisdiction-specific (US market design per FERC; other jurisdictions differ), and each ISO's tariff and business-practice manuals govern its settlement calendar, billing determinants, and dispute procedures. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-1-8 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19511" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19511"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Settle Environmental Instruments"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-1 ;
    skos:definition "Settle environmental and renewable instrument transactions — RINs, allowances, RECs, and similar program instruments — calculating amounts due from actualized registry transfers and contractual pricing, generating and validating invoices, and submitting authorized payment instructions against confirmed instrument delivery — so instrument trades complete financially on verified registry actuals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.1.8" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Settle Emissions"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers financial settlement of environmental-instrument transactions: assembling actualized registry lifecycle events (Perform RINs/REC Actualization, CM-1-2-3-1-7), applying contract pricing, generating and validating invoices, preparing and submitting authorized payment instructions through the designated finance processes against confirmed registry transfer or retirement where the contract links payment to delivery, and evidencing completion. Program applicability is jurisdiction-specific. Excludes instrument actualization and registry-event processing (CM-1-2-3-1-7); deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3-6); compliance filings and RVO demonstrations (EHS & Government Reporting, locked rule); allowance-position economics (CM-1-1-7-3-3); settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).
In scope: Calculate amounts due from actualized registry events and contract pricing | generate and validate instrument invoices | prepare and submit authorized payment or receipt instructions through the designated finance processes and track status | evidence completion against confirmed transfers
Out of scope: Registry-event processing and reconciliation (CM-1-2-3-1-7) | deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3-6) | compliance filings (EHS & Government Reporting) | allowance-position economics (CM-1-1-7-3-3) | settlement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Actualized registry events (CM-1-2-3-1-7) | contract pricing | counterparty invoices"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Settled environmental-instrument transactions"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Complete environmental-instrument trades financially against verified registry delivery."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EPA-RFS-001 | SRC-EPA-REC-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Perform RINs/REC Actualization | precedes: Manage AR/AP & Actuals"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Row name reads 'Emissions' but the settled scope spans the environmental-instrument families handled by capture (CM-1-2-1-3-6) and actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-7): RINs, allowances, RECs, and similar program instruments; 'Settle Environmental Instruments' queued for the naming pass. Payment-versus-transfer linkage is contract-specific. APQC 19511 is a vehicle-testing candidate, unrelated."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-1-9 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19601" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19601"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-1 ;
    skos:definition "Manage the controlled working and evidentiary document set used by trading settlement — generating, receiving, validating, distributing, and retaining settlement statements, payment instructions, supporting actualization evidence, and applicable invoices — so every settlement is executed and evidenced from controlled, complete documents."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.1.9" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Settlement Documents"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the settlement document working set across all settlement processes: outbound invoice and statement generation and distribution; inbound document receipt, registration, and completeness checking; association of supporting evidence (tickets, inspection reports, statements) with the settlement record; netting statement documentation; and retention and retrieval of the settlement working set per the applicable requirements. This process controls settlement documents for trading settlement purposes; it is not the legal system of record for every enterprise invoice and does not own enterprise records-retention policy. Excludes confirmation documentation (Manage Confirmation Documentation, CM-1-2-2-2-2); contract and legal documents (Contract Management, CM-1-2-1-2; Legal & Corporate Communications); accounting records and financial-reporting documentation and workpapers (CM-1-2-4-2-7); enterprise records-retention policy (the accountable governance owner); the settlement calculations themselves (the owning settlement processes).
In scope: Generate and distribute outbound invoices and statements | receive, register, and completeness-check inbound documents | associate supporting evidence with settlement records | retain and retrieve the settlement working set per applicable requirements
Out of scope: Confirmation documents (CM-1-2-2-2-2) | contract and legal documents (CM-1-2-1-2, Legal & Corporate Communications) | accounting and financial-reporting records and workpapers (CM-1-2-4-2-7) | enterprise records-retention policy (governance owner) | settlement calculation (owning settlement processes)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Settlement calculations and statements from the settlement processes | inbound counterparty and carrier documents | retention requirements"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Controlled, retained settlement documentation linked to settlement records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep settlement execution running on complete, controlled, retrievable documents."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "assures: Settlements | informed-by: Manage Confirmation Documentation"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Document-control process serving the settlement family, parallel to Manage Confirmation Documentation (batch 10); scope is the trading-settlement working and evidentiary set, not the enterprise document system of record. Record-retention requirements are jurisdiction- and instrument-specific. APQC 19601 is an HSSE-records candidate, not trading settlement."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-2-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10731" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10731"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-2 ;
    skos:definition "Assess, document, and apply the accounting treatment of trading transactions under approved enterprise policy — derivative versus normal purchase/normal sale scope, own-use, lease, storage, and exchange treatments, and gross-versus-net presentation — obtaining Finance/controller approval or escalation for material, new, complex, or policy-sensitive conclusions, so transactions are recorded under the correct, evidenced treatment."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.2.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Accounting Treatments"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers transaction-level treatment assessment and documentation: assessing new deal types, structures, and amendments against the applicable accounting standards and enterprise policy (for US GAAP, ASC 815 derivative scope, the normal purchases and normal sales election, and hedge accounting requirements are the recurring assessments), documenting the treatment conclusion and its evidence, supporting hedge designation documentation, and maintaining the treatment reference used by posting and reconciliation processes. Material, new, complex, or policy-sensitive conclusions require Finance/controller approval or escalation; hedge designation approval follows the approved hedging policy through its accountable owners (Finance, Treasury, or Controller per the operating model), not this process. Treatment assessments apply enterprise accounting policy owned by Finance under the locked rule. Excludes enterprise accounting policy and material technical-accounting conclusions (Finance, locked rule); hedge designation approval (per approved hedging policy); valuation adjustment execution (Manage Commodity Valuations, CM-1-2-2-3-15, batch 12); trading-procedure documentation (CM-1-2-4-2-12); posting execution (the recording processes).
In scope: Assess deal types and structures against standards and approved policy | document treatment conclusions with evidence | escalate material, new, complex, or policy-sensitive conclusions to Finance | maintain the treatment reference and support hedge designation documentation
Out of scope: Enterprise accounting policy and material technical-accounting conclusions (Finance, locked rule) | hedge designation approval (per approved hedging policy) | valuation adjustments (CM-1-2-2-3-15) | procedure documentation (CM-1-2-4-2-12) | posting execution (recording processes)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "New and amended deal types and structures (CM-1-2-1-3) | enterprise accounting policy (Finance) | accounting standards references"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Documented treatment assessments and the treatment reference, with material conclusions Finance-approved"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Ensure every transaction is recorded under a correct, documented, policy-compliant treatment with material conclusions properly approved."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-FASB-ASC815-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Trade Capture | enables: Manage Trading Sub-Ledger | governed-by: Develop Accounting Policies & Procedures"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Finance-enabled Supply & Trading"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 15 (2026-09-20). Assess-document-apply with escalation (Hamid, batch 15): treatment work is performed under approved policy with Finance/controller approval for material conclusions — delegated technical-accounting authority is operating-model-specific and not assumed. Batch 12 exclusion on CM-1-2-2-3-15 honored: valuation execution does not approve accounting classification; classification assessment lands here, policy and material conclusions with Finance. ASC 815 supports the accounting concepts, not the organization's approval design. Standards are jurisdiction-specific: ASC 815 under US GAAP; IFRS 9 and related standards elsewhere. APQC 10731 is a fixed-asset candidate, unrelated."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-2-10 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11743" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11743"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-2 ;
    skos:definition "Calculate, post, and true up accruals for trading activity — un-invoiced delivered or received volumes, expected fees and charges, and estimated prices pending final determination — per the enterprise accrual methodology, so each period's books are complete for activity not yet settled."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.2.10" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Accruals"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the accrual cycle: identifying accruable activity from actualized movements and open settlement items, estimating accrual values per the enterprise methodology, posting accruals at cut-off, reversing or truing up against actual settlements, and analyzing accrual accuracy. Accruals may be based on actualized but uninvoiced activity, estimated unactualized activity where policy permits, expected service charges, or deferred pricing, depending on the approved close methodology (including provisional-price estimates pending final price determination per the batch 14 crude pricing pattern). Accrual methodology and materiality policy are owned by Finance under the locked rule; this process executes them. Excludes accrual methodology and policy (Finance, locked rule); settlement (CM-1-2-4-1); actualization (CM-1-2-3-1); AR/AP booking of settled items (CM-1-2-4-2-4).
In scope: Identify accruable activity per the approved close methodology | estimate accrual values per enterprise methodology | post at cut-off and true up against settlements | analyze accrual accuracy
Out of scope: Accrual methodology and policy (Finance, locked rule) | settlement (CM-1-2-4-1) | actualization (CM-1-2-3-1) | booking of settled items (CM-1-2-4-2-4)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Actualized movements not yet settled (CM-1-2-3-1) | open settlement items (CM-1-2-4-1) | enterprise accrual methodology (Finance)"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Posted, evidenced, trued-up accruals"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Make each period's books complete for real activity that has not yet settled."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "monthly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Actualizations | uses-input: Settlements | enables: Manage Trading Sub-Ledger"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Finance-enabled Supply & Trading"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 15 (2026-09-20). Execute-within-policy: methodology is Finance-owned, execution here. Accrual bases (Hamid, batch 15) span actualized-but-uninvoiced, policy-permitted estimated unactualized activity, expected service charges, and deferred pricing — not only delivered/received-not-invoiced. Accrual-to-actual analysis feeds estimation improvement. APQC 11743 is an unrelated routing candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-2-11 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10736" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10736"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-2 ;
    skos:definition "Execute transaction- and commodity-tax accounting for supply and trading activity by applying approved tax rules and tax-determination data to transactions, recording tax amounts, maintaining required transaction-level evidence, and preparing controlled tax-accounting and filing-support outputs for the accountable enterprise Tax function."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.2.11" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Taxes"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers transaction-tax execution for trading activity: applying approved tax rules and tax-determination data to purchases, sales, blends, and movements (in the United States, federal motor-fuels excise taxes under the IRS Publication 510 regime, plus state motor-fuel, sales and use, and similar taxes; other jurisdictions per their regimes); recording and accruing tax amounts; maintaining required transaction-level evidence; and preparing controlled tax-accounting and filing-support outputs for the accountable enterprise Tax function. This process consumes approved tax rules, jurisdiction and product tax classifications, licenses, exemption certificates, and tax-determination master data from their accountable owners; it may validate their presence and applicability to an individual transaction but does not establish tax policy, determine legal tax positions, own tax registrations or licenses, or file tax returns unless expressly delegated. Excludes tax policy, strategy, legal tax positions, registrations and licenses, corporate income tax, and tax-return filing (enterprise Tax function/Finance and accountable owners); settlement of tax-inclusive invoices (CM-1-2-4-1); customs and trade compliance where separately owned.
In scope: Apply approved tax rules and determination data to trading transactions | validate presence and applicability of licenses, exemptions, and classifications per transaction | record and accrue tax amounts with transaction-level evidence | prepare controlled tax-accounting and filing-support outputs for the Tax function
Out of scope: Tax policy, strategy, legal positions, registrations, licenses, and return filing (enterprise Tax function/Finance and accountable owners) | corporate income tax (enterprise Tax function) | settlement of invoices (CM-1-2-4-1) | separately owned customs and trade compliance"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Captured and actualized transactions (CM-1-2-1-3, CM-1-2-3-1) | approved tax rules, classifications, licenses, and exemption data (accountable owners) | jurisdictional tax rates"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Recorded, accrued, evidenced transaction taxes with controlled filing-support outputs"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Ensure every trading transaction carries correct, evidenced transaction-tax treatment under approved rules."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-IRS-P510-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Actualizations | enables: Manage Trading Sub-Ledger | informed-by: Develop Accounting Policies & Procedures"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Finance-enabled Supply & Trading"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 15 (2026-09-20). Q3 decision (Hamid): transaction- and commodity-tax accounting execution under approved rules — tax-determination data, licenses, exemption certificates, and classifications are consumed from their accountable owners (Tax, Legal, EHS, or master-data governance per the operating model), validated for presence and applicability, never owned here; separation-of-duties in downstream fuels taxation. Motor-fuels excise is jurisdiction-specific; IRS Publication 510 covers the US federal regime as context, not tax advice; state and non-US regimes differ. APQC 10736 'Manage taxes' scored 1.0 at 3b but NO SOURCE — honored; that candidate is the corporate tax function, broader than this execution row."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-2-12 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16592" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16592"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Maintain Trading Accounting Procedures and Guidance"@en,
        "Manage Trading Accounting Procedures"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-2 ;
    skos:definition "Develop, maintain, version, and communicate trading-specific accounting procedures, close instructions, and implementation guidance that operationalize Finance-owned enterprise accounting policy for supply and trading activity."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.2.12" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Develop Accounting Policies & Procedures"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers trading accounting procedure management: developing and maintaining desk-level procedures, close instructions, and implementation guidance that operationalize enterprise accounting policy for trading transaction types; assessing new products, structures, and standards changes for procedural impact and coordinating with Manage Accounting Treatments (CM-1-2-4-2-1) on treatment consequences; escalating policy gaps and interpretation questions to Finance; and maintaining procedure currency and version control. Boundary rule: Finance/Controller establishes enterprise accounting policy and decides material new policy questions (supported by technical accounting where applicable); Manage Accounting Treatments determines transaction-level treatment under approved policy; this process maintains the procedures, close instructions, and operational guidance; the accounting processes execute them. This process implements policy and never sets it, on the set-versus-apply pattern established in batches 07 and 11. Excludes enterprise accounting policy and material policy interpretation (Finance, locked rule); transaction-level treatment assessment (CM-1-2-4-2-1); enterprise records-retention and governance policy (governance owners).
In scope: Develop, maintain, version, and communicate trading accounting procedures and close instructions implementing enterprise policy | assess new products and standards changes for procedural impact | escalate policy gaps and questions to Finance | maintain procedure currency and version control
Out of scope: Enterprise accounting policy and material policy interpretation (Finance, locked rule) | transaction-level treatment assessment (CM-1-2-4-2-1) | enterprise governance and retention policy (governance owners)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Enterprise accounting policy (Finance) | new products and structures (CM-1-2-1-3) | standards changes | treatment assessments (CM-1-2-4-2-1)"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Current, version-controlled trading accounting procedures and guidance"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep trading accounting execution consistent, current, and provably within enterprise policy."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-FASB-ASC815-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Accounting | enables: Manage Accounting Treatments | informed-by: Trade Capture"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Finance-enabled Supply & Trading"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 15 (2026-09-20). Q4 decision (Hamid): despite the row name, this process develops procedures within — never policy above — the Finance-owned framework; the set-versus-apply pattern (batches 07/11) governs. The displayed label overstates the mandate: preferred normalized label 'Maintain Trading Accounting Procedures and Guidance' (alternative 'Manage Trading Accounting Procedures') recorded as alt labels and queued for the naming pass with priority. APQC 16592 scored STRONG at 3b but NO SOURCE — honored; it is a retail omni-channel candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-2-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10224" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10224"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Manage Production & Inventory Accounting"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-2 ;
    skos:definition "Maintain the financial accounting of production and inventory for supply and trading activity — recording and reconciling financial inventory balances and cost-of-sales effects under the enterprise-approved inventory valuation methodology, from verified operational balances — so the financial books carry a complete, correctly valued representation of physical activity."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.2.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Production Accounting"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the financial-accounting side of production and inventory: recording and valuing production receipts, inventory movements, transfers, and exchange imbalances into the sub-ledger and GL; recording and reconciling financial inventory balances and cost-of-sales effects under the enterprise-approved inventory valuation methodology; and reconciling the financial inventory records to the operational balances. Operational quantities arrive as controlled inputs: the refinery daily material balance is owned by Perform Production Accounting (CM-1-1-7-1-3, batch 06) and network book-to-physical reconciliation by Perform Inventory Reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5, batch 04) — this process values what those processes have verified; it does not re-perform the operational balance. Financial reconciliation to operational balances may identify differences for investigation but does not authorize operational stock adjustments, production remeasurement, or physical loss determination. Inventory valuation methodology, write-off approval, and valuation entries policy are owned by Finance under the locked rule. Excludes the operational balances (CM-1-1-7-1-3, CM-1-1-3-7-5); operational stock adjustments and loss determination (operational owners); valuation policy and write-offs (Finance); settlement (CM-1-2-4-1).
In scope: Record and value production receipts, inventory movements, and exchanges into the accounting records | record and reconcile financial inventory balances and cost-of-sales effects per the approved methodology | reconcile financial records to operational balances and refer differences for investigation | post correcting entries per approved corrections
Out of scope: Refinery material balance (CM-1-1-7-1-3) | network stock reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5) | operational stock adjustments, remeasurement, and loss determination (operational owners) | valuation methodology and write-offs (Finance, locked rule) | settlement (CM-1-2-4-1)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Refinery accounted balances (CM-1-1-7-1-3) | reconciled stock positions (CM-1-1-3-7-5) | actualized movements (CM-1-2-3-1) | enterprise valuation methodology (Finance)"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Valued production and inventory accounting records reconciled to operational balances"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep the financial books correctly valued and complete against verified physical activity."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EI-HM31-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Perform Production Accounting | uses-input: Perform Inventory Reconciliation | enables: Manage Trading Sub-Ledger"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Finance-enabled Supply & Trading"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 15 (2026-09-20). Q2 decision: financial production and inventory accounting here; operational material balance and stock reconciliation with their batch 04/06 owners — the same operational source feeds each process, but each owns a different record and decision, extending the batch 13 record-partition principle into the accounting layer. Record-not-compute (Hamid, batch 15): this process maintains the financial representation of verified balances; it does not compute inventory independently of operational systems. Preferred normalized label 'Manage Production & Inventory Accounting' recorded as alt label and queued for the naming pass (row names mirror the locked taxonomy). APQC 10224 is a scheduling candidate, unrelated."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-2-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20008" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20008"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-2 ;
    skos:definition "Execute royalty and interest disbursements arising from first-purchase and marketing arrangements — calculating owner-level distributions from settled lease transactions and division-of-interest references, managing suspense and unclaimed amounts per the applicable requirements, and submitting authorized disbursement instructions — so interest owners are paid correctly and on time."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.2.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Marketing Interests & Royalty Payments"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Included only where the downstream organization purchases at lease/wellhead or administers first-purchaser arrangements, per the batch 09 caveat carried through lease contract administration (CM-1-2-1-2-8), lease actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-6), and first-purchaser settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-6). This process is not in scope for ordinary downstream product marketing unless the organization is the first purchaser or contractually responsible disburser for lease/wellhead production. Covers disbursement execution: computing owner-level distributions from settled first-purchase transactions and current division-of-interest references, applying contractual deductions, managing suspense accounts and unclaimed-property handling per the applicable jurisdiction's requirements, responding to owner inquiries on payments, and preparing and submitting authorized disbursement instructions through the designated finance processes (batch 14 Q3 rule). Owner-payment entitlement calculation policy, upstream royalty policy, division-order legal determinations, and E&P accounting remain outside scope per batches 13-14. Excludes first-purchaser settlement calculation (CM-1-2-4-1-6); lease contract administration (CM-1-2-1-2-8); entitlement and royalty policy (outside scope); payment release and bank execution (Treasury/banking).
In scope: Compute owner-level distributions from settled transactions and division-of-interest references | manage suspense and unclaimed amounts per applicable requirements | respond to owner payment inquiries | prepare and submit authorized disbursement instructions and track status
Out of scope: First-purchaser settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-6) | lease contract administration (CM-1-2-1-2-8) | entitlement policy, royalty policy, division-order legal determinations, and E&P accounting (outside scope) | ordinary downstream product marketing (not applicable) | payment release and bank execution (Treasury/banking)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Settled first-purchase transactions and statements (CM-1-2-4-1-6) | division-of-interest references (CM-1-2-1-2-8) | jurisdictional suspense and unclaimed-property requirements"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Authorized, submitted, tracked, and evidenced royalty and interest disbursement instructions"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Pay interest owners correctly from settled first-purchase transactions with a controlled, evidenced trail."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "monthly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EIA-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Settle First Purchaser/Leases | uses-input: Manage Lease Contract"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Finance-enabled Supply & Trading"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 15 (2026-09-20). Execution-only row completing the lease chain (contract b09, actualization b13, settlement b14, disbursement here); conditional downstream scope and the standing batch 09 ownership follow-up apply. Suspense and unclaimed-property requirements are jurisdiction-specific. APQC 20008 is a marketing-plans candidate, a false lexical match on 'marketing'."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-2-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11743" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11743"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-2 ;
    skos:definition "Record trading receivables, payables, and actuals in the accounting records — recording authorized settlement outcomes and validated invoices in AR/AP, posting actualized transaction values, monitoring open items and aging, and keeping the payables and receivables position complete and current — so the financial position reflects settled and actualized activity."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.2.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage AR/AP & Actuals"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers AR/AP and actuals recording for trading activity: recording authorized settlement outcomes and validated invoices in AR/AP (invoice validation itself remains with Settlements per the batch 14 Q3 chain), posting actualized values and settlement outcomes to the accounting records, monitoring open items, aging, and unmatched instructions, and coordinating overdue items with Settlements and Credit Risk through the accountable owners. Cash receipts are applied by Manage Cash Application (CM-1-2-4-2-9); accrual of un-invoiced activity by Manage Accruals (CM-1-2-4-2-10); payment release and bank execution by Treasury and banking processes; collection risk decisions and credit holds by Credit Risk (CM-1-2-2-1) under the batch 11 boundary. Excludes settlement calculation and invoice validation (CM-1-2-4-1); cash application (CM-1-2-4-2-9); accruals (CM-1-2-4-2-10); credit decisions (CM-1-2-2-1); payment execution (Treasury/banking).
In scope: Record authorized settlement outcomes and validated invoices in AR/AP | post actualized values and settlement outcomes | monitor open items, aging, and unmatched instructions | coordinate overdue items with the accountable owners
Out of scope: Settlement calculation and invoice validation (CM-1-2-4-1) | cash application (CM-1-2-4-2-9) | accruals (CM-1-2-4-2-10) | credit decisions and holds (CM-1-2-2-1) | payment release (Treasury/banking)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Authorized settlement outcomes and validated invoices (CM-1-2-4-1) | actualized transaction values (CM-1-2-3-1)"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Booked, monitored AR/AP and actuals records"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep the trading payables and receivables position complete, current, and evidenced."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Settlements | enables: Manage Cash Application | enables: Manage Trading Sub-Ledger"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Finance-enabled Supply & Trading"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 15 (2026-09-20). Receiving end of the batch 14 Q3 chain: Settlements validates and submits, this process records. 'Actuals' means actualized transaction values posted to the financial records, consistent with batch 13 terminology. Whether AR/AP operations sit in the trading organization or a shared-services center is operating-model-specific (organizational neutrality, batch 10). APQC 11743 is an unrelated routing candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-2-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11743" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11743"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-2 ;
    skos:definition "Maintain the trading sub-ledger as the complete transactional accounting record — ensuring every captured, actualized, and settled transaction posts correctly under its determined treatment, reconciling the sub-ledger to the general ledger, and enforcing period cut-off — so the general ledger can rely on a complete, reconciled trading record."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.2.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Trading Sub-Ledger"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers sub-ledger control, whether implemented in the CTRM/ETRM platform, an ERP sub-ledger, or an integrated accounting architecture: monitoring posting completeness from capture, actualization, settlement, and accounting sources; validating that postings follow the determined treatments (CM-1-2-4-2-1); reconciling sub-ledger balances to the general ledger and resolving breaks; enforcing period cut-off and controlled reopening; and evidencing the reconciliation. Ledger and account structures are administered by Manage Financial System/GL (CM-1-2-4-2-8, per the batch 12 binding); the postings themselves originate in the owning recording processes. Excludes GL and ledger-structure administration (CM-1-2-4-2-8); treatment assessment (CM-1-2-4-2-1); source postings (owning processes); financial reporting (CM-1-2-4-2-7).
In scope: Monitor posting completeness across sources | validate postings against determined treatments | reconcile sub-ledger to GL and resolve breaks | enforce period cut-off with evidence
Out of scope: Ledger-structure administration (CM-1-2-4-2-8) | treatment assessment (CM-1-2-4-2-1) | source postings (owning processes) | financial reporting (CM-1-2-4-2-7)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Postings from capture, actualization, settlement, and accounting processes | treatment reference (CM-1-2-4-2-1) | GL balances (CM-1-2-4-2-8)"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Reconciled sub-ledger with evidenced cut-off"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Guarantee the GL rests on a complete, reconciled, cut-off-controlled trading record."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Manage AR/AP & Actuals | uses-input: Manage Accounting Treatments | enables: Reconcile Economic PNL To Accounting PNL"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Finance-enabled Supply & Trading"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 15 (2026-09-20). The sub-ledger is the transactional accounting record beneath the GL; its physical implementation (CTRM/ETRM, ERP, or integrated architecture) is system-design-specific and not assumed — relevant to the future system-integration workstream. This row owns the completeness and reconciliation control, not the structures or the source postings. APQC 11743 is an unrelated routing candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-2-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12940" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12940"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-2 ;
    skos:definition "Reconcile the economic profit and loss produced by the trading risk processes to the profit and loss recorded in the accounting books — identifying, quantifying, explaining, and evidencing expected and unexpected differences by cause — so management, risk, and financial views of trading results are provably consistent."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.2.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Reconcile Economic PNL To Accounting PNL"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the economic-to-accounting P&L reconciliation at each close: taking the controlled economic P&L inputs delivered by the end-of-month process (Perform End-of-Month (EOM) Process, CM-1-2-2-3-5, per the batch 12 binding) and the accounting P&L from the reconciled sub-ledger and GL; identifying, quantifying, explaining, and evidencing expected and unexpected differences by cause — accounting treatment effects (hedge accounting, normal purchase/normal sale election, own-use), accrual and timing differences, valuation-basis differences, inventory valuation and cost-allocation timing, realized-versus-unrealized timing, valuation adjustments, tax and statutory adjustments, and scope differences; and escalating unexplained or out-of-tolerance differences. The reconciliation is a bridge: expected differences are explained and evidenced, not eliminated. Interim indicative reconciliations may run more frequently per local procedure. Excludes producing the economic P&L (Market Risk Management, CM-1-2-2-3, batch 12); producing the accounting P&L (the recording processes); valuation policy (Finance, locked rule).
In scope: Assemble economic and accounting P&L on a consistent basis | quantify and explain expected and unexpected differences by cause | evidence the reconciliation bridge | escalate unexplained or out-of-tolerance differences
Out of scope: Economic P&L production (CM-1-2-2-3) | accounting P&L production (recording processes) | valuation policy (Finance, locked rule)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Controlled economic P&L inputs (CM-1-2-2-3-5) | accounting P&L from the reconciled sub-ledger (CM-1-2-4-2-5) | treatment reference (CM-1-2-4-2-1)"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Evidenced economic-to-accounting P&L reconciliation bridge"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Prove management and financial views of trading results agree, with every difference explained."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "monthly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-FASB-ASC815-001 | SRC-CCRO-002A"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Perform End-of-Month (EOM) Process | uses-input: Manage Trading Sub-Ledger | informed-by: Manage Accounting Treatments"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Finance-enabled Supply & Trading"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 15 (2026-09-20). Receiving end of the batch 12 EOM binding: Market Risk coordinates and evidences the month-end economic close and provides controlled economic inputs; this process owns the reconciliation to the books. Bridge-not-elimination (Hamid, batch 15): expected differences (NPNS/own-use treatment, hedge-accounting timing, accrual timing, valuation basis, inventory and cost-allocation timing, realized-versus-unrealized, tax and statutory adjustments) are systematic and are explained, not defects to remove. 'PNL' retained in the technical label; 'P&L' in prose per the batch 12 convention, label queued for the naming pass. APQC 12940 'Reconcile inventories' is unrelated."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-2-7 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20765" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20765"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Manage Financial Information Documentation and Reporting"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-2 ;
    skos:definition "Prepare and control the financial documentation and internal financial reporting for supply and trading activity — management reporting on financial results, accounting workpapers and reconciliation evidence, and controlled inputs to external financial reporting through the accountable owners — so financial information about trading is complete, evidenced, and consistently produced."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.2.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Financial Info. Documentation & Reporting"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers financial information control for trading: producing internal financial and management reporting on trading results; maintaining accounting workpapers, reconciliation evidence, and close documentation (including the settlement-accounting records excluded from the batch 14 settlement-documents working set); and preparing controlled inputs to external financial reporting, statutory filings, and audit requests through the accountable Finance owners, per the batch 11 internal-reports-and-controlled-inputs precedent. Excludes external financial reporting ownership and statutory filings (Finance); risk reporting (Market Risk and Credit Risk reporting processes, batches 11-12); the settlement working document set (CM-1-2-4-1-9); enterprise records-retention policy (governance owner).
In scope: Produce internal financial and management reporting on trading results | maintain accounting workpapers and close documentation | prepare controlled inputs to external reporting and audits through accountable owners
Out of scope: External financial reporting and statutory filings (Finance) | risk reporting (CM-1-2-2-1, CM-1-2-2-3) | settlement working documents (CM-1-2-4-1-9) | records-retention policy (governance owner)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Reconciled sub-ledger and GL balances | P&L reconciliation bridge (CM-1-2-4-2-6) | reporting calendars and audit requests"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Internal financial reporting and controlled external-reporting inputs with evidence"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Make trading financial information complete, evidenced, and reliably reportable."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "monthly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Reconcile Economic PNL To Accounting PNL | uses-input: Manage Trading Sub-Ledger | assures: Accounting"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Finance-enabled Supply & Trading"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 15 (2026-09-20). Internal reports plus controlled inputs through accountable owners, per the batch 11 precedent; external reporting accountability stays with Finance. Normalized label 'Manage Financial Information Documentation and Reporting' recorded as alt label and queued for the naming pass — the 'Info.' abbreviation should not survive into the canonical glossary. APQC 20765 'Manage information' is a generic IT candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-2-8 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "17058" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 17058"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-2 ;
    skos:definition "Administer the financial system structures used by trading accounting — chart-of-accounts mapping, ledger and book structures, posting rules and business interface configuration from trading systems to the general ledger, and accounting master data — so postings land correctly, completely, and controllably in the financial records."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.2.8" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Financial System/GL"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers accounting-structure administration: maintaining ledger and book structures for trading accounting (the accounting counterpart of the batch 12 decision that risk book structures live with Market Risk and ledger structures here); chart-of-accounts mapping for trading transactions; posting rules and business-side interface configuration between trading, settlement, and financial systems; accounting master data within the accounting domain; and posting-interface error monitoring and business resolution. Changes follow the applicable change control. Enterprise master-data governance frameworks are excluded per the batch 05 precedent; ownership and operation of the enterprise GL platform itself follows the IT and Shared Services operating model. Excludes technical application development, integration-platform operation, identity and security administration, infrastructure, and release deployment, owned by Process Excellence & IT or the relevant technology delivery function; enterprise GL platform ownership (IT/Shared Services per operating model); enterprise master-data governance (batch 05 exclusion); risk book structures (CM-1-2-2-3-1, batch 12); sub-ledger reconciliation control (CM-1-2-4-2-5).
In scope: Maintain ledger and book structures and chart-of-accounts mapping | configure posting rules and business-side interface settings | maintain accounting master data within the accounting domain | monitor posting-interface errors and drive business resolution
Out of scope: Technical development, integration-platform operation, security administration, infrastructure, and release deployment (Process Excellence & IT / technology delivery) | enterprise GL platform ownership (IT/Shared Services) | enterprise master-data governance (batch 05 exclusion) | risk book structures (CM-1-2-2-3-1) | sub-ledger reconciliation (CM-1-2-4-2-5)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Approved structure and mapping change requests | interface specifications from trading and settlement systems | accounting master data requirements"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Controlled ledger structures, mappings, and business interface configuration"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep the structural plumbing of trading accounting correct and controlled on the business side."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Manage Trading Sub-Ledger | enables: Manage AR/AP & Actuals | informed-by: Manage Trading Books & Strategies Structure"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Finance-enabled Supply & Trading"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 15 (2026-09-20). Business administration versus technical ownership (Hamid, batch 15): this process owns accounting-specific configuration and business interface administration; technical development, platform operation, security, infrastructure, and deployment belong to the technology delivery function. Receiving end of the batch 12 book-structure partition: risk-reporting hierarchies at CM-1-2-2-3-1, ledger structures here. APQC 17058 scored STRONG at 3b but NO SOURCE — honored; comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-2-9 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10759" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10759"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-2 ;
    skos:definition "Apply cash receipts to open trading receivables — matching bank-reported receipts against invoices, settlement statements, and netting arrangements, investigating and coordinating resolution of unapplied, unidentified, short, and over payments, and evidencing application — so the receivables position is accurate and cash is accounted for daily."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.2.9" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Cash Application"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers cash application for trading receivables: matching bank-reported receipts against open items using invoice references, settlement statements, and netting agreements; applying receipts and posting application entries; investigating and coordinating resolution of unapplied, unidentified, short, and over payments with Settlements, AR, Credit, and counterparty contacts as applicable — Credit Risk makes any resulting credit decision under the batch 11 boundary, not this process; and evidencing daily application. Bank account operation, cash positioning, and funds movement are owned by Treasury and banking processes (batch 14 Q3 rule); whether cash application runs in the trading organization or a shared-services center is operating-model-specific. Excludes bank account operation and cash management (Treasury/banking); receivables booking (CM-1-2-4-2-4); settlement calculation (CM-1-2-4-1); credit decisions (CM-1-2-2-1).
In scope: Match bank-reported receipts to open items and netting arrangements | apply receipts and post application entries | investigate and coordinate resolution of unapplied, unidentified, short, and over payments | evidence daily application
Out of scope: Bank operation and cash management (Treasury/banking) | receivables booking (CM-1-2-4-2-4) | settlement calculation (CM-1-2-4-1) | credit decisions (CM-1-2-2-1)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Bank receipt reporting (Treasury/banking) | open receivables (CM-1-2-4-2-4) | netting agreements and settlement statements"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Applied cash with resolved exceptions and evidence"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Account for every receipt against the right open item, daily and evidenced."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Manage AR/AP & Actuals | uses-input: Settlements"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Finance-enabled Supply & Trading"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 15 (2026-09-20). Application-not-custody: this process accounts for cash received; Treasury owns the cash itself. Short payments or delinquency may trigger credit involvement, but the credit decision belongs to Credit Risk (batch 11 boundary). APQC 10759 'Manage cash' scored STRONG at 3b but NO SOURCE — honored; that candidate is treasury cash management, a false match for application."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-3-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "17469" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 17469"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-3 ;
    skos:definition "Develop and maintain the trading compliance program's policies and procedures — market-conduct rules, information barriers, personal-account dealing, communications and conflicts requirements, regulatory-change management, and compliance monitoring and escalation procedures — within the enterprise compliance and legal governance framework, so trading activity operates under current, communicated compliance requirements."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.3.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Develop Compliance Policies & Procedures"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers trading compliance program documentation: developing and maintaining trading-specific compliance policies and procedures; translating regulatory change into updated requirements through a managed regulatory-change process; defining monitoring, surveillance, escalation, and training requirements for the trading business; and communicating and version-controlling the program. It defines trading-program requirements within — never above — the enterprise framework: enterprise-wide compliance policy, legal interpretation, and regulatory legal positions are owned by the enterprise compliance function and Legal & Corporate Communications; the risk-policy framework is owned by risk governance (batches 11-12); this process holds no independent policy-setting authority outside that delegation. Excludes enterprise compliance policy and legal interpretation (enterprise compliance, Legal & Corporate Communications); risk policies and limits frameworks (risk governance, CM-1-2-2-1, CM-1-2-2-3); trading accounting procedures (CM-1-2-4-2-12); executing the monitoring the policies require (CM-1-2-4-3-2/-3/-4).
In scope: Develop and maintain trading compliance policies and procedures within the enterprise framework | manage regulatory change into program requirements | define monitoring, escalation, and training requirements | communicate and version-control the program
Out of scope: Enterprise compliance policy and legal interpretation (enterprise compliance, Legal & Corporate Communications) | risk-policy frameworks (CM-1-2-2-1, CM-1-2-2-3) | accounting procedures (CM-1-2-4-2-12) | monitoring execution (CM-1-2-4-3-2/-3/-4)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Applicable law, regulation, and market-rule changes | enterprise compliance and legal frameworks | monitoring findings (CM-1-2-4-3-2/-3/-4) and exception patterns (CM-1-2-4-3-7)"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Current, version-controlled trading compliance policies and procedures"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep the trading compliance program current, complete, and communicated."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-P180-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Monitor External Trading Compliance | enables: Monitor Internal Trading Compliance | governed-by: Regulatory & Compliance"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 16 (2026-09-20). Trading-program-within-enterprise-framework, parallel to the batch 15 Q4 pattern: this row owns the trading compliance program's documentation under enterprise delegation; enterprise policy and legal interpretation stay above it. APQC 17469 scored STRONG at 3b but NO SOURCE — honored; comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-3-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10935" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10935"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-3 ;
    skos:definition "Monitor supply and trading activity for compliance with external law, regulation, and market rules — market-conduct and anti-manipulation surveillance, position-limit monitoring, exchange and market-rule adherence, licensing and registration status, and completion of regulatory reporting obligations by their owners — surfacing potential violations for assessment and escalation."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.3.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Monitor External Trading Compliance"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers external-compliance monitoring for trading: trade and communications surveillance for market conduct under the applicable anti-manipulation and anti-fraud regimes (in the United States, CEA section 6(c) as implemented by 17 CFR Part 180, and the FERC anti-manipulation rule for jurisdictional energy markets; other jurisdictions per their regimes); position-limit and accountability-level monitoring; exchange, clearinghouse, and market-rule adherence; licensing and registration status monitoring; and completion monitoring of regulatory reporting obligations executed by their lifecycle owners (SDR reporting at CM-1-2-1-3-10/CM-1-2-2-2-4, EMTS at CM-1-2-3-1-7) — this process checks that required reporting happened, it does not perform it. Potential violations are assessed and escalated per the compliance procedures; legal determination of liability, regulator interaction on enforcement, and proceedings are owned by Legal & Corporate Communications. Excludes reporting execution (lifecycle owners); legal interpretation and enforcement response (Legal & Corporate Communications); internal-policy monitoring (CM-1-2-4-3-3); control-operation monitoring (CM-1-2-4-3-4).
In scope: Conduct trade and communications surveillance for market conduct | monitor position limits, market rules, and licensing status | monitor completion of regulatory reporting obligations by their owners | assess and escalate potential violations per procedure
Out of scope: Regulatory reporting execution (CM-1-2-1-3-10, CM-1-2-2-2-4, CM-1-2-3-1-7) | legal interpretation and enforcement response (Legal & Corporate Communications) | internal-policy adherence (CM-1-2-4-3-3) | control-operation monitoring (CM-1-2-4-3-4)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Trade, order, and communications data | position and limit data | regulatory reporting completion evidence | applicable regime requirements (CM-1-2-4-3-1)"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Surveillance results and escalated potential violations with evidence"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Detect potential external-compliance violations early and route them to controlled assessment."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-P180-001 | SRC-CFTC-P45-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Develop Compliance Policies & Procedures | informed-by: Trade Capture | triggers: Manage Exceptions"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 16 (2026-09-20). Monitor-not-execute: reporting obligations are executed by their lifecycle owners (batch 08/10/13 decisions); this process monitors completion. Compliance surveillance detects and escalates; legal interpretation and enforcement response belong to Legal — the surveillance-versus-legal distinction (Hamid, batch 16). Applicable regimes are jurisdiction-specific; 17 CFR Part 180 (CFTC anti-manipulation/anti-fraud) and the FERC anti-manipulation rule (18 CFR 1c) anchor the US context; exchange and market rules are source-specific. Receives the Monitor Contracts exclusion (batch 09). APQC 10935 is a tax-compliance candidate, a false match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-3-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10935" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10935"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-3 ;
    skos:definition "Monitor adherence to the organization's internal trading compliance policies — conduct rules, information barriers, personal-account dealing, gifts and entertainment, communications requirements, and required compliance training — surfacing violations and adverse patterns for assessment and escalation."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.3.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Monitor Internal Trading Compliance"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers internal compliance-policy adherence monitoring: checking trading personnel's adherence to the compliance policies maintained under Develop Compliance Policies & Procedures (CM-1-2-4-3-1) — conduct rules, information barriers and need-to-know restrictions, personal-account dealing requirements, gifts and entertainment rules, communications and recordkeeping requirements, training completion, and required declarations and attestations — and escalating violations and adverse patterns per the compliance procedures. The partition with its siblings: external law and market rules are monitored by CM-1-2-4-3-2; the operation of compliance-supporting controls by CM-1-2-4-3-4; the internal market-risk framework by Monitor Risk Policy Compliance (CM-1-2-2-3-10). Excludes external-compliance monitoring (CM-1-2-4-3-2); control-operation monitoring (CM-1-2-4-3-4); risk-framework monitoring (CM-1-2-2-3-10); risk-limit and credit-limit monitoring (CM-1-2-2-3, CM-1-2-2-1); disciplinary action (management, HR); policy authorship (CM-1-2-4-3-1).
In scope: Monitor adherence to conduct, information-barrier, personal-dealing, and communications policies | monitor required training completion and declarations | detect and escalate violations and adverse patterns | evidence the monitoring performed
Out of scope: External-compliance monitoring (CM-1-2-4-3-2) | control-operation monitoring (CM-1-2-4-3-4) | risk-framework monitoring (CM-1-2-2-3-10) | risk and credit limit monitoring (CM-1-2-2-3, CM-1-2-2-1) | disciplinary action (management, HR) | policy authorship (CM-1-2-4-3-1)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Compliance policies and requirements (CM-1-2-4-3-1) | personnel activity, communications, and training data per policy | declarations and attestations"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Adherence monitoring results and escalated violations with evidence"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep internal compliance-policy adherence visible and violations escalated."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Develop Compliance Policies & Procedures | triggers: Manage Exceptions"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 16 (2026-09-20). People-adherence monitor: this row watches whether personnel follow internal compliance policies; CM-1-2-4-3-4 watches whether compliance-supporting controls operate. Receives the Monitor Contracts exclusion (batch 09). APQC 10935 is a tax-compliance candidate, a false match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-3-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10735" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10735"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-3 ;
    skos:definition "Monitor the operation of internally implemented controls that support the organization's external trading obligations — verifying that required deal approvals are evidenced, regulatory reporting controls are complete and functioning, surveillance and recordkeeping controls operate as designed, and audit evidence exists — surfacing control failures and gaps to the accountable owners."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.3.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Monitor Internal Trade Controls"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers compliance-control monitoring: testing and monitoring that internally implemented controls supporting external obligations operate and produce evidence — required pre-trade and deal approvals evidenced in the transaction record, regulatory reporting controls (SDR, EMTS, and registry reporting completeness and error handling by their owners), surveillance and communications-recordkeeping controls, and audit-evidence availability — and escalating control failures, design gaps, and recurring weaknesses to the accountable control owners. Reciprocal boundary per the batch 12/16 decision: this process does not test the internal market-risk framework — risk appetite, approved limits, book mandates, delegation of authority, valuation and risk-control procedures, control-cycle evidence, and risk-exception approvals are monitored by Monitor Risk Policy Compliance (CM-1-2-2-3-10); neither node tests or owns the other's framework, and each may consume information from the other where relevant. Excludes the market-risk framework (CM-1-2-2-3-10, reciprocal exclusion); performing the controls themselves (control owners); external-rule surveillance (CM-1-2-4-3-2); internal-policy adherence (CM-1-2-4-3-3); internal audit's independent assurance (internal audit function).
In scope: Verify required approvals are evidenced in transaction records | monitor regulatory reporting control completeness and error handling | test surveillance and recordkeeping control operation | escalate control failures and design gaps to accountable owners
Out of scope: Internal market-risk framework monitoring (CM-1-2-2-3-10, reciprocal exclusion) | control execution (control owners) | external-rule surveillance (CM-1-2-4-3-2) | policy-adherence monitoring (CM-1-2-4-3-3) | independent internal audit (internal audit function)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Control designs and requirements (CM-1-2-4-3-1) | transaction, approval, and reporting evidence from the lifecycle processes | control-owner attestations"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Control-monitoring results with escalated failures and gaps"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Prove the controls the organization relies on for external obligations actually operate, with failures escalated."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-P45-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Develop Compliance Policies & Procedures | triggers: Manage Exceptions"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 16 (2026-09-20). Batch 12 dependency resolved (Hamid, Option A): this node monitors internally implemented controls supporting external obligations; Monitor Risk Policy Compliance (CM-1-2-2-3-10) monitors the internal market-risk framework; the reciprocal exclusion is recorded on both rows and neither tests the other's framework. Relationship note (Hamid, batch 16): no typed relation to Monitor Risk Policy Compliance is recorded — 'related-to' is not a controlled relationship_type and no defined data dependency exists; the non-hierarchical boundary lives in the scope notes per the conditional-relation precedent (batches 10/13/14). Control-operation monitor, distinct from the people-adherence monitor (CM-1-2-4-3-3). APQC 10735 'Manage internal controls' is a WEAK generic candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-3-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "17467" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 17467"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-3 ;
    skos:definition "Monitor and assure compliance with transaction- and commodity-tax obligations affecting supply and trading activity by reviewing evidence of registration, licensing, exemption-certificate, filing-calendar, and tax-determination-control compliance; coordinating relevant tax-authority notices and examinations with the enterprise Tax function; and escalating material gaps."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.3.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Tax Compliance"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers tax compliance monitoring and assurance for trading activity: reviewing evidence that licenses and registrations are current, exemption certificates valid, filing calendars met by the accountable filers, and transaction-tax determination controls operating; coordinating responses to tax-authority notices, information requests, and examinations touching trading transactions with the enterprise Tax function and the affected process owners; and escalating material gaps. Consumes status and evidence from the accountable registration, licensing, tax, commercial, and master-data owners; it does not register, license, renew, file, or determine the legal tax position itself. Completes the batch 15 three-way split: transaction-tax accounting execution is owned by Manage Taxes (CM-1-2-4-2-11); tax policy, legal positions, registrations, and return filing by the enterprise Tax function and accountable owners; monitoring and assurance here. Excludes transaction-tax execution (CM-1-2-4-2-11); tax policy, legal positions, registration, licensing, renewal, and return filing (enterprise Tax function and accountable owners); customs and trade compliance where separately owned; income tax (enterprise Tax function).
In scope: Review evidence of license, registration, and exemption-certificate compliance | monitor filing-calendar adherence by accountable filers | monitor tax determination control operation | coordinate tax-authority notices and examinations with the Tax function and escalate material gaps
Out of scope: Transaction-tax accounting execution (CM-1-2-4-2-11) | tax policy, registration, licensing, renewal, and return filing (enterprise Tax function, accountable owners) | customs and trade compliance (separately owned) | income tax (enterprise Tax function)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Status and evidence from accountable registration, licensing, tax, commercial, and master-data owners | filing calendars and completion evidence | tax determination control evidence (CM-1-2-4-2-11) | tax-authority correspondence"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Tax compliance assurance results and coordinated authority responses"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Assure that trading tax obligations are actually being met by their owners, with material gaps escalated."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-IRS-P510-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "assures: Manage Taxes | informed-by: Develop Compliance Policies & Procedures | triggers: Manage Exceptions"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 16 (2026-09-20). Q4 decision (Hamid): evidence-based monitoring and assurance, completing the batch 15 split (execute at CM-1-2-4-2-11; own policy, registrations, and filing at the Tax function and accountable owners; monitor here) — this process reviews evidence, it does not own the underlying data or renewal activity. Jurisdiction-specific regimes per batch 15; IRS Publication 510 anchors the US federal fuels context. APQC 17467 scored STRONG at 3b but NO SOURCE — honored; comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-3-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10522" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10522"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-3 ;
    skos:definition "Coordinate the trading organization's Renewable Fuel Standard reporting obligations — assembling and quality-assuring RIN transaction, position, and renewable-volume-obligation data from the trading systems and registries, reconciling it to EMTS, and preparing controlled reporting inputs and supporting evidence for the accountable filer — so RFS reports attributable to trading activity are complete, accurate, and evidenced."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.3.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage RINs Reporting"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the trading-side coordination of RFS reporting: assembling RIN generation, separation, trade, retirement, and position data from actualized registry records (Perform RINs/REC Actualization, CM-1-2-3-1-7) and trading systems; reconciling reportable data to EMTS; computing and quality-assuring renewable-volume-obligation inputs; preparing controlled reporting inputs, workpapers, and supporting evidence for the accountable filer; performing operational preparation, data-entry, workflow, or submission-support tasks only where a documented delegation assigns those tasks to the trading organization, while the accountable filer retains filing and attestation accountability; and tracking reporting-calendar completion. Boundary per the EHS & Government Reporting locked rule and the batch 13 Q3 decision: periodic RFS compliance reports, attestations, audit responses, and RVO demonstrations are filed and attested by EHS & Government Reporting (or the formally designated accountable filer per the operating model); per-transaction EMTS submissions are owned by CM-1-2-3-1-7; this process coordinates, assembles, reconciles, and evidences in support of the accountable filer. Program applicability is jurisdiction-specific; this row does not cover REC, LCFS, emissions-allowance, or other environmental-credit reporting programs. Excludes filing, attestation, and submission accountability (EHS & Government Reporting, locked rule); per-transaction EMTS and registry submissions (CM-1-2-3-1-7); instrument settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-8); position forecasting (CM-1-2-1-1-3).
In scope: Assemble and quality-assure RIN transaction, position, and RVO data | reconcile reportable data to EMTS | prepare controlled reporting inputs and evidence for the accountable filer | perform documented delegated support tasks and track reporting-calendar completion
Out of scope: Filing, attestation, and submission accountability (EHS & Government Reporting, locked rule) | per-transaction EMTS submissions (CM-1-2-3-1-7) | instrument settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-8) | position forecasting (CM-1-2-1-1-3) | REC, LCFS, and other environmental-credit reporting programs (their accountable owners)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Actualized registry events and positions (CM-1-2-3-1-7) | EMTS records | blending and production volumes | RFS reporting calendars and requirements"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Controlled, reconciled RFS reporting inputs and evidence for the accountable filer"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Ensure that RFS reporting attributable to trading activity is complete, reconciled, controlled, and evidenced for the accountable filer."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EPA-RFS-001 | SRC-EPA-EMTS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Perform RINs/REC Actualization | enables: Monitor External Trading Compliance | governed-by: Develop Compliance Policies & Procedures"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 16 (2026-09-20). Q3 decision (Hamid, Option A with tightened accountability): controlled-inputs coordination on the batch 11 precedent — this process supports the accountable filer and never becomes the de facto owner of regulatory filing; operational tasks require a documented delegation with the accountable filer retaining filing and attestation accountability; the batch 13 Q3 split (per-transaction EMTS at CM-1-2-3-1-7, periodic compliance at EHS&GR) is preserved exactly. EPA RFS/EMTS sources cover US RIN mechanics only — not internal ownership, delegation of account authority, EHS-versus-trading responsibilities, non-US programs, or other environmental-credit reporting. RFS mechanics per 40 CFR 80 subpart M (EMTS at 80.1452). APQC 10522 scored STRONG at 3b but NO SOURCE — honored; generic reporting candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-3-7 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20807" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20807"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-3 ;
    skos:definition "Manage compliance exceptions through their lifecycle — logging and classifying identified violations, control failures, and approved deviations, assessing severity and regulatory implications, coordinating waiver, remediation-plan, and closure approvals under the documented compliance delegation, tracking remediation to closure, and evidencing the record — so every compliance gap is visibly owned, decided, and closed."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.3.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Exceptions"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the compliance exception lifecycle across the cluster: intake from the monitoring processes (CM-1-2-4-3-2/-3/-4/-5) and self-reports; classification and severity assessment including regulatory-notification implications assessed with Legal; coordination of waiver, remediation-plan, and closure approvals under the documented compliance delegation — the authorized approver may be Compliance leadership, Legal, risk governance, management, or a committee per the delegation, and this process manages the workflow, evidence, and closure rather than approving every waiver itself; remediation planning, tracking, and closure; and pattern analysis feeding policy and control improvement (CM-1-2-4-3-1). Regulatory notification decisions and enforcement interaction are owned by Legal & Corporate Communications; risk-limit and credit exceptions follow their own documented procedures (CM-1-2-2-3, CM-1-2-2-1, batches 11-12); disciplinary action is management and HR. Excludes regulatory notification decisions and legal proceedings (Legal & Corporate Communications); risk and credit exception procedures (CM-1-2-2-1, CM-1-2-2-3); disciplinary action (management, HR); fixing the underlying controls (control owners, tracked here to closure).
In scope: Log and classify violations, control failures, and deviations | assess severity and regulatory implications with Legal | coordinate waiver, remediation-plan, and closure approvals under the documented delegation | track remediation to evidenced closure and analyze patterns
Out of scope: Regulatory notification and proceedings (Legal & Corporate Communications) | risk and credit exceptions (CM-1-2-2-1, CM-1-2-2-3) | disciplinary action (management, HR) | control remediation execution (control owners)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Escalations from the monitoring processes | self-reports | compliance procedures and delegations (CM-1-2-4-3-1)"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Decided, tracked, evidenced compliance exceptions closed through remediation"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Ensure no compliance gap dies in a log — every one is decided under the right authority, remediated, and evidenced."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Monitor External Trading Compliance | follows: Monitor Internal Trade Controls | enables: Develop Compliance Policies & Procedures"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 16 (2026-09-20). Coordinate-not-approve (Hamid, batch 16): this process manages the exception workflow, evidence, and closure; the authorized approver is whoever the documented compliance delegation designates. Compliance-exception lifecycle only: risk-limit exceptions (batch 12) and credit exceptions (batch 11) stay with their documented procedures; the exception partition mirrors the dispute partition discipline (batch 10). Pattern analysis closes the loop to CM-1-2-4-3-1. APQC 20807 scored STRONG at 3b but NO SOURCE — honored; it is an IT-architecture candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-4-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10188" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10188"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-4 ;
    skos:definition "Screen completed voyages and terminal operations for potential demurrage, despatch, and detention entitlements and exposures — comparing used against allowed laytime from actualized notices, statements of fact, and timesheets under the governing terms, computing preliminary claim values in both directions, and identifying the contractual deadlines and evidence needed to preserve or defend each claim — so every potential claim is identified before rights lapse."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.4.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Identify Potential Demurrage"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers claim identification: screening completed movements using the actualized time, notice, and event facts (CM-1-2-3-1-4); performing preliminary laytime calculations under the governing charter party, contract, or terminal terms; identifying both outbound entitlements and inbound exposures; identifying relevant contractual notice, documentation, and time-limit requirements and flagging the deadlines and evidence needed to preserve or defend the claim; and routing identified potential claims to creation (outbound, CM-1-2-4-4-2) or anticipating inbound receipt (CM-1-2-4-4-3). Excludes the time-fact actualization itself (CM-1-2-3-1-4); claim creation and pursuit (CM-1-2-4-4-2, -5); inbound assessment (CM-1-2-4-4-4).
In scope: Screen completed movements against allowed laytime under governing terms | compute preliminary claim values in both directions | identify contractual notice, documentation, and time-limit requirements and flag deadlines and evidence needed | route identified potential claims
Out of scope: Time-fact actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-4) | claim creation and negotiation (CM-1-2-4-4-2, -5) | inbound assessment (CM-1-2-4-4-4)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Actualized voyage and terminal time facts (CM-1-2-3-1-4) | governing laytime terms (CM-1-2-1-2-9) | contractual notice and time-limit provisions"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Identified potential claims with preliminary calculations, deadlines, and evidence requirements flagged"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Catch every compensable delay — in either direction — before claim rights expire."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-BIMCO-LD-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Perform Vessel & Barge Actualization | precedes: Create Claim"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 17 (2026-09-20). Receiving end of the batch 13 handoff: actualization supplies the facts, this process makes the potential-claim determination. Contract-first rule (Hamid, batch 17): notice, documentation, and time-limit requirements vary by agreement and are identified per contract, not assumed uniform. APQC 10188 'Identify potential customers' scored STRONG at 3b but NO SOURCE — honored; false lexical match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-4-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10147" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10147"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Create Outbound Delay Compensation Claim"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-4 ;
    skos:definition "Build outbound demurrage, despatch, and detention claims — performing the definitive laytime calculation under the governing terms, assembling the supporting evidence (notices of readiness, statements of fact, timesheets, port and terminal logs), verifying contractual notice, documentation, and time-limit requirements are met, obtaining the designated internal review, and issuing the claim to the responsible party — so outbound claims are complete, timely, and defensible."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.4.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Create Claim"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers outbound claim preparation and issuance: definitive laytime and quantum calculation under the governing charter party, contract, or terminal terms including the exceptions and interruptions the terms allow; evidence assembly; verification of contractual notice, documentation, and time-limit requirements; the designated internal review per the documented delegation, including legal review or privileged advice where required by the governing agreement, claim value, dispute status, or escalation threshold; and formal claim issuance to the counterparty, charterer, terminal, or other responsible party. A missed contractual time limit or required document may prejudice, limit, or bar recovery under the governing agreement; the claim process identifies and escalates such conditions promptly. This process routes legal questions; it does not itself make legal conclusions about enforceability. Excludes identification (CM-1-2-4-4-1); negotiation of the issued claim (CM-1-2-4-4-5); claim invoicing (CM-1-2-4-4-7); inbound claims (CM-1-2-4-4-3/-4/-6/-8); legal opinions (Legal & Corporate Communications).
In scope: Perform definitive laytime and quantum calculations under governing terms | assemble notices, statements of fact, and supporting evidence | verify contractual notice, documentation, and time-limit compliance and obtain designated review including legal review where required | issue claims to responsible parties
Out of scope: Identification (CM-1-2-4-4-1) | negotiation (CM-1-2-4-4-5) | claim invoicing (CM-1-2-4-4-7) | inbound claims (CM-1-2-4-4-3/-4/-6/-8) | legal opinions (Legal & Corporate Communications)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Identified potential claims (CM-1-2-4-4-1) | actualized time facts (CM-1-2-3-1-4) | governing terms and time limits (CM-1-2-1-2-9)"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Issued outbound claims with complete supporting evidence"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Issue outbound claims that are complete, timely, and hard to reject."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-BIMCO-LD-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Identify Potential Demurrage | precedes: Communicate and Negotiate Claim"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 17 (2026-09-20). Outbound leg of the two-directional lifecycle (Q3 reading); normalized label 'Create Outbound Delay Compensation Claim' queued for the naming pass. Legal-boundary rule (Hamid, batch 17): high-risk, high-value, or disputed claims route to legal review per threshold; time-limit consequences are contract-specific (may prejudice, limit, or bar recovery), never assumed. APQC 10147 is a budgeting candidate, a false match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-4-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20096" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20096"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Receive Inbound Delay Compensation Claim"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-4 ;
    skos:definition "Register inbound demurrage, despatch, and detention claims — logging received claims, acknowledging where the governing terms require, recording receipt-stage time-limit and documentation observations, and routing claims to assessment — so inbound claims enter a controlled process with their receipt-stage observations captured."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.4.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Receive Demurrage Claim"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers inbound claim intake: registration and logging of claims received from owners, counterparties, terminals, and other claimants; acknowledgement where contractually required; receipt-stage time-limit and documentation observations recorded and made available for assessment, legal/commercial review, and the response position; and routing to Assess Claim (CM-1-2-4-4-4). Receipt-stage observations inform the response position; their legal effect is assessed downstream, not prejudged at intake. Excludes merit and quantum assessment (CM-1-2-4-4-4); negotiation (CM-1-2-4-4-6); invoice processing (CM-1-2-4-4-8); outbound claims (CM-1-2-4-4-1/-2/-5/-7).
In scope: Register and log received claims | acknowledge where required | record receipt-stage time-limit and documentation observations for assessment and review | route claims to assessment
Out of scope: Merit and quantum assessment (CM-1-2-4-4-4) | negotiation (CM-1-2-4-4-6) | invoice processing (CM-1-2-4-4-8) | outbound claims (CM-1-2-4-4-1/-2/-5/-7)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Inbound claims and supporting documents | governing terms and time limits (CM-1-2-1-2-9)"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Registered inbound claims routed to assessment with receipt-stage observations"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Give every inbound claim a controlled, recorded entry with receipt-stage observations captured at the door."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-BIMCO-LD-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "precedes: Assess Claim | uses-input: Manage Carrier Contracts"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 17 (2026-09-20). Inbound leg intake (Q3 reading); normalized label 'Receive Inbound Delay Compensation Claim' queued. Receipt-stage observations are recorded without prejudging their legal effect (Hamid, batch 17) — assessment and legal/commercial review determine the response position. APQC 20096 'Receive warranty claim' scored STRONG at 3b but NO SOURCE — honored; warranty analogy, comparative only."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-4-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20105" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20105"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Assess Inbound Claim"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-4 ;
    skos:definition "Assess the merit and quantum of inbound claims — recalculating laytime independently from the organization's own actualized records under the governing terms, applying contractual exceptions, interruptions, allocation provisions, notices, and responsibility rules as the governing agreement provides, and determining an evidenced accept, counter, or reject position — so inbound claims are paid only to the extent actually owed."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.4.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Assess Claim"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers inbound claim assessment: independent laytime and quantum recalculation from the organization's own actualized time facts (CM-1-2-3-1-4) rather than the claimant's figures; application of contractual exceptions, interruptions, allocation provisions, notices, and responsibility rules as the governing agreement provides — specific causes such as weather or berth availability have whatever effect the contract gives them, no more; comparison against the claimed amount; and determination of an evidenced accept, counter, or reject position with the designated review per the documented delegation. Where the underlying physical or measurement facts are themselves disputed, the actualization dispute is handled by Manage Actualization Disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8) per the batch 10 partition. Excludes intake (CM-1-2-4-4-3); negotiation of the position (CM-1-2-4-4-6); physical-fact disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8); legal opinions (Legal & Corporate Communications).
In scope: Recalculate laytime and quantum independently from own records | apply contractual exceptions, interruptions, allocation provisions, notices, and responsibility rules as the agreement provides | determine evidenced accept, counter, or reject positions with designated review
Out of scope: Intake (CM-1-2-4-4-3) | negotiation (CM-1-2-4-4-6) | underlying physical-fact disputes (CM-1-2-3-1-8) | legal opinions (Legal & Corporate Communications)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Registered inbound claims (CM-1-2-4-4-3) | own actualized time facts (CM-1-2-3-1-4) | governing terms, exceptions, and responsibility rules"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Evidenced accept, counter, or reject positions"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Pay only what is actually owed, on an evidenced recalculation, never on the claimant's arithmetic."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-BIMCO-LD-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Receive Demurrage Claim | precedes: Validate and Negotiate Claim | informed-by: Perform Vessel & Barge Actualization"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 17 (2026-09-20). Inbound assessment leg (Q3 reading); normalized label 'Assess Inbound Claim' queued. Independent-recalculation discipline mirrors the batch 11 validate-not-accept pattern. Contract-first rule: no cause is automatically exculpatory or compensable — the governing agreement decides. Dispute-partition routing: a disagreement about what physically happened goes to CM-1-2-3-1-8; a disagreement about compensability stays here. APQC 20105 is a claim-closure candidate, comparative only."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-4-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10046" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10046"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Negotiate Outbound Claim"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-4 ;
    skos:definition "Pursue issued outbound claims to resolution — presenting the claim and its evidence, responding to counterarguments and counter-calculations, negotiating commercial resolution within delegated claim-settlement authority, obtaining additional approval where a proposed compromise, waiver, credit, set-off, or write-down exceeds that authority, and agreeing resolution or escalating unresolved claims — so outbound entitlements convert to agreed, collectible amounts."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.4.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Communicate and Negotiate Claim"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers outbound claim negotiation and resolution: claim presentation and follow-up; evidence exchange and response to counterpositions; negotiation of commercial resolution within the documented delegation of claim-settlement authority, with additional approval obtained where a proposed compromise, waiver, credit, set-off, or write-down exceeds delegated authority — a resolution creating a financial concession routes through Finance or the designated authority; agreement of the settled amount; and escalation of aging, material, or deadlocked claims per procedure. Resolution may also come by contractual determination, surveyor or terminal evidence, settlement agreement, credit note, set-off, arbitration, or legal process; arbitration and litigation are conducted by Legal & Corporate Communications. Time-limit-preserving communications are maintained where negotiation extends near contractual limits. Excludes claim creation (CM-1-2-4-4-2); inbound negotiation (CM-1-2-4-4-6); claim invoicing (CM-1-2-4-4-7); write-off approval beyond delegated authority (Finance/designated authority); arbitration and litigation (Legal & Corporate Communications).
In scope: Present claims and exchange evidence | respond to counterarguments and counter-calculations | negotiate commercial resolution within delegated authority and obtain additional approval for concessions beyond it | escalate aging, material, or deadlocked claims
Out of scope: Claim creation (CM-1-2-4-4-2) | inbound negotiation (CM-1-2-4-4-6) | claim invoicing (CM-1-2-4-4-7) | write-offs and concessions beyond delegated authority (Finance/designated authority) | arbitration and litigation (Legal & Corporate Communications)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Issued claims and evidence (CM-1-2-4-4-2) | counterparty responses | delegated claim-settlement authority"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Agreed outbound claim resolutions or controlled escalations"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Convert issued claims into agreed, collectible amounts without surrendering entitled value or exceeding authority."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-BIMCO-LD-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Create Claim | precedes: Send Claim Invoice"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 17 (2026-09-20). Outbound negotiation leg (Q3 reading); normalized label 'Negotiate Outbound Claim' queued. Authority limitation (Hamid, batch 17): claim resolution can create a financial concession — compromises, waivers, credits, set-offs, and write-downs beyond the documented delegation require the designated approval; this process never silently owns a Finance write-off or legal settlement authority. Resolution modes extend beyond bilateral negotiation. APQC 10046 'Communicate mission' is an unrelated candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-4-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12671" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12671"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Validate and Negotiate Inbound Claim"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-4 ;
    skos:definition "Resolve inbound claims from the assessed position — communicating the assessed position with its evidence, negotiating, accepting, partially accepting, countering, or rejecting the claim within delegated authority, routing concessions, waivers, or formal settlement agreements through the required approval path, and agreeing resolution or escalating unresolved claims — so inbound exposure closes at the evidenced amount, not the claimed one."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.4.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Validate and Negotiate Claim"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers inbound claim negotiation and resolution: communicating the assessed position (CM-1-2-4-4-4) and its recalculation evidence to the claimant; negotiating, accepting, partially accepting, countering, or rejecting the claim within the documented delegation of claim-settlement authority; routing concessions, waivers, or formal settlement agreements through the required approval path — a resolution creating a financial concession routes through Finance or the designated authority; and escalating material, aging, or deadlocked claims per procedure. Resolution may also come by contractual determination, surveyor or terminal evidence, settlement agreement, credit note, set-off, arbitration, or legal process; arbitration and litigation are conducted by Legal & Corporate Communications. Excludes the assessment itself (CM-1-2-4-4-4); outbound negotiation (CM-1-2-4-4-5); invoice processing (CM-1-2-4-4-8); concessions beyond delegated authority (Finance/designated authority); arbitration and litigation (Legal & Corporate Communications).
In scope: Communicate assessed positions with evidence | negotiate, accept, partially accept, counter, or reject within delegated authority | route concessions, waivers, and settlement agreements through the required approval path | escalate material or deadlocked claims
Out of scope: Assessment (CM-1-2-4-4-4) | outbound negotiation (CM-1-2-4-4-5) | invoice processing (CM-1-2-4-4-8) | concessions beyond delegated authority (Finance/designated authority) | arbitration and litigation (Legal & Corporate Communications)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Assessed positions and evidence (CM-1-2-4-4-4) | claimant responses | delegated claim-settlement authority"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Agreed inbound claim resolutions or controlled escalations"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Close inbound exposure at the evidenced amount, under the right authority."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-BIMCO-LD-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Assess Claim | precedes: Receive Claim Invoice"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 17 (2026-09-20). Inbound negotiation leg (Q3 reading); normalized label 'Validate and Negotiate Inbound Claim' queued. Same authority limitation as the outbound leg: concessions beyond the documented delegation route to the designated approval. APQC 12671 'Validate warranty claim' scored STRONG at 3b but NO SOURCE — honored; warranty analogy, comparative only."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-4-7 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20105" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20105"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Invoice Agreed Outbound Claim"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-4 ;
    skos:definition "Invoice agreed outbound claims — issuing the claim invoice and settlement statement for the negotiated amount with its supporting calculation, submitting the receivable into the designated finance processes, tracking expected receipt, and obtaining confirmation from Accounting that the receivable is booked and cash applied — so agreed entitlements are collected and evidenced."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.4.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Send Claim Invoice"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers outbound claim invoicing: generating the claim invoice and supporting settlement statement for the agreed amount; issuing to the paying party; submitting the receivable into the designated finance processes for booking (Manage AR/AP & Actuals, CM-1-2-4-2-4) and receipt application (Manage Cash Application, CM-1-2-4-2-9) per the batch 14 payment-authority rule; tracking expected receipt and coordinating follow-up; obtaining confirmation from Accounting that the receivable has been booked and cash applied; and evidencing closure. A payment disagreement after agreement is a settlement dispute (CM-1-2-4-1-17) per the batch 10 partition. Excludes negotiation (CM-1-2-4-4-5); AR booking and cash application (CM-1-2-4-2-4, CM-1-2-4-2-9); post-agreement payment disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).
In scope: Generate claim invoices and settlement statements for agreed amounts | issue to paying parties and submit receivables to the designated finance processes | track expected receipt and coordinate follow-up | obtain booking and cash-application confirmation from Accounting and evidence closure
Out of scope: Negotiation (CM-1-2-4-4-5) | AR booking and cash application (CM-1-2-4-2-4, CM-1-2-4-2-9) | post-agreement payment disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Agreed resolutions (CM-1-2-4-4-5) | claim calculations and evidence | receivables process references"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Issued claim invoices with tracked receipt and Accounting-confirmed closure"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Collect agreed claim amounts with a complete evidence trail."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-BIMCO-LD-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Communicate and Negotiate Claim | precedes: Manage AR/AP & Actuals"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 17 (2026-09-20). Outbound invoicing leg (Q3 reading); normalized label 'Invoice Agreed Outbound Claim' queued. Batch 14 Q3 rule applied: this process prepares and submits; Accounting books and applies — closure is confirmed by Accounting, not assumed here. Agreed-then-unpaid transitions to the settlement-dispute quadrant. APQC 20105 is a claim-closure candidate, comparative only."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-4-8 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20096" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20096"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Process Invoice for Agreed Inbound Claim"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4-4 ;
    skos:definition "Process invoices for agreed inbound claims — validating the received invoice against the negotiated resolution and its evidence, preparing and submitting a payment request supported by the agreed resolution and required approvals to the designated Accounts Payable/Treasury process, and evidencing closure — so the organization pays exactly what was agreed, once, with a complete trail."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.4.8" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Receive Claim Invoice"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers inbound claim invoice processing: validating received claim invoices against the agreed resolution (CM-1-2-4-4-6) and its recalculation evidence; rejecting or querying invoices that do not match the agreement; preparing and submitting a payment request supported by the agreed resolution and required approvals to the designated Accounts Payable/Treasury process per the batch 14 payment-authority rule — the designated payment authority approves and releases payment, not this process; and evidencing closure. Duplicate or mismatched invoicing is surfaced back through negotiation or the settlement-dispute quadrant (CM-1-2-4-1-17). Excludes negotiation (CM-1-2-4-4-6); AP booking, payment approval, and payment execution (CM-1-2-4-2-4, Treasury/banking, designated payment authority); post-agreement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17).
In scope: Validate received invoices against agreed resolutions and evidence | reject or query mismatched invoices | prepare and submit payment requests with required approvals to the designated AP/Treasury process | evidence closure
Out of scope: Negotiation (CM-1-2-4-4-6) | AP booking, payment approval, and payment execution (CM-1-2-4-2-4, Treasury/banking, designated authority) | post-agreement disputes (CM-1-2-4-1-17)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Agreed resolutions (CM-1-2-4-4-6) | received claim invoices | payables process references"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Validated invoices with submitted, approved payment requests and closure evidence"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Pay agreed inbound claims exactly and once, with the trail to prove it."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-BIMCO-LD-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Validate and Negotiate Claim | precedes: Manage AR/AP & Actuals"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 17 (2026-09-20). Inbound invoicing leg (Q3 reading); normalized label 'Process Invoice for Agreed Inbound Claim' queued. Batch 14 Q3 rule applied: this process prepares the request; the designated payment authority approves and releases. Pay-what-was-agreed control: the invoice validates against the negotiated resolution, not the original claim. APQC 20096 scored STRONG at 3b but NO SOURCE — honored; warranty analogy, comparative only."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-5-1-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11749" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11749"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-5-1 ;
    skos:definition "Steward the crude and feedstock quality data used in evaluation, planning, and trading — maintaining current, validated assays and quality records, tracking assay age and representativeness, incorporating new laboratory and cargo data under controlled update procedures, and distributing consistent quality data to the consuming models and processes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.5.1.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage feedstock Data Quality"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers feedstock quality-data stewardship: maintaining the assay and quality-data library used by evaluation (CM-1-1-4-7-2), LP models, slate development, and trading; validating and versioning updates from laboratory analyses, inspection results, and cargo experience; tracking assay currency and representativeness against actual received quality; and controlled distribution so all consumers work from the same data. Laboratory testing itself is performed by Refining and laboratory functions; cargo inspection data arrives through actualization (CM-1-2-3-1); enterprise master-data governance frameworks are excluded per the batch 05 precedent. Excludes laboratory operations (Refining/laboratory); inspection execution (independent inspectors, CM-1-2-3-1 evidence); model structures (CM-1-2-5-1-2 reviews, model owners own); enterprise master-data governance (batch 05 exclusion).
In scope: Maintain validated, versioned assay and quality records | track assay currency and representativeness against received quality | incorporate laboratory, inspection, and cargo data under controlled updates | distribute consistent quality data to consumers
Out of scope: Laboratory testing (Refining/laboratory) | inspection execution (CM-1-2-3-1 evidence chain) | model ownership (model owners) | enterprise master-data governance (batch 05 exclusion)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Laboratory assays and analyses | inspection and cargo quality results (CM-1-2-3-1) | received-quality experience"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Current, validated, versioned feedstock quality data"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "One current, validated set of quality data behind every feedstock decision."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-DOE-SPRASSAY-001 | SRC-API-MPMS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Review LP Model Integrity | enables: Develop Term Slate | uses-input: Perform Vessel & Barge Actualization"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Assay stewardship in the SPR Crude Oil Assay Manual sense: standardized characterization data maintained current and validated for planning and evaluation use; implementation-level work should cite the actual contractual assay clauses, refinery lab standards, inspector procedures, and applicable API/ASTM methods for the specific attribute governed (Hamid, batch 18). The row name's lowercase 'feedstock' is a typographical artifact — corrected capitalization queued for the naming pass. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-5-1-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19977" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19977"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-5-1 ;
    skos:definition "Review whether the feedstock data, yield representations, operating constraints, capacities, market assumptions, and other decision inputs used by feedstock-evaluation and planning models remain current, complete, internally consistent, and representative of the refinery and market conditions; document findings and escalate required corrections to accountable data and model owners."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.5.1.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Review LP Model Integrity"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers input-and-representation integrity review: periodic verification that the LP and evaluation models consuming feedstock data reflect current validated assays (CM-1-2-5-1-1), actual refinery configuration, constraints, and capacities, and current price and market assumptions; back-checking model predictions against actual outcomes where available; documenting findings; and escalating stale, inconsistent, or incomplete representations to the accountable data and model owners for correction. This process does not independently certify model validation, approve model methodology, alter model code, change LP constraints, or approve planning conclusions. Model methodology, structure, and ownership remain with their accountable owners: the refinery planning LP with Refinery Planning (CM-1-1-4) and its tooling; model-methodology approval and independent validation with the applicable model governance and validation functions (batch 12 rule). Excludes model validation certification and methodology approval (model governance/validation functions); model ownership and implementation (model owners, CM-1-1-4); evaluation conclusions (CM-1-1-4-7-2); data stewardship (CM-1-2-5-1-1).
In scope: Verify model inputs against validated quality data and current configuration | check price and assumption currency | back-check predictions against outcomes where available | document findings and escalate corrections to accountable data and model owners
Out of scope: Model validation certification and methodology approval (model governance/validation) | model ownership, code, and constraints (model owners, CM-1-1-4) | evaluation conclusions (CM-1-1-4-7-2) | data stewardship (CM-1-2-5-1-1)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Validated quality data (CM-1-2-5-1-1) | refinery configuration and constraint data | model input sets and predictions versus actuals"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Documented model-integrity findings escalated to accountable owners"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep the models behind feedstock decisions honest about the refinery and the market as they are today."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CMU-REFPLAN-001 | SRC-EIA-LFMM-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Manage feedstock Data Quality | enables: Develop Term Slate | informed-by: Refinery Planning"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Q4 decision (Hamid): fitness-of-representation review distinguished from model ownership, methodology approval, and formal model validation — four responsibilities, four owners (data stewardship at CM-1-2-5-1-1; model architecture with Refinery Planning/designated owner; methodology approval and independent validation with model governance; representation review here). Review-and-escalate, never own (batch 11/12 patterns). LP modeling context per the refinery planning literature. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-5-1-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10941" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10941"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-5-1 ;
    skos:definition "Develop the recommended term feedstock procurement slate — the portfolio of crudes and feedstocks proposed for supply under agreements with multi-period or continuing commercial commitments, screened for refinery quality compatibility and evaluated for sustained economic attractiveness — so term procurement and the feedstock recommendation rest on a quality-assured base-load supply plan."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.5.1.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Develop Term Slate"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers term procurement-slate development: identifying candidate term supplies, screening them against refinery quality capability using validated data (CM-1-2-5-1-1) and current evaluations (CM-1-1-4-7-2), assessing sustained economic attractiveness and supply reliability, and recommending the term slate that anchors base-load feedstock supply. Term supply means feedstock sourced under an agreement with a multi-period or continuing commercial commitment; the term-versus-spot classification follows the organization's own commercial definitions, not a fixed contractual duration. The output is a procurement recommendation: under PTC-002 the binding production slate is Refinery Planning's (CM-1-1-4), and term-contract negotiation and execution belong to the contract and trading processes (CM-1-2-1-2, CM-1-2-1-3). Excludes the binding production slate (CM-1-1-4, PTC-002); crude evaluation (CM-1-1-4-7-2); contract negotiation and trade execution (CM-1-2-1-2, CM-1-2-1-3); spot procurement (CM-1-2-5-1-4).
In scope: Identify and screen candidate term supplies against refinery capability | assess sustained economics and supply reliability | recommend the term procurement slate | feed the feedstock recommendation and contract processes
Out of scope: Binding production slate (CM-1-1-4, PTC-002) | crude evaluation (CM-1-1-4-7-2) | contract negotiation and execution (CM-1-2-1-2, CM-1-2-1-3) | spot slate (CM-1-2-5-1-4)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Validated quality data (CM-1-2-5-1-1) | crude evaluations (CM-1-1-4-7-2) | feedstock demand (CM-1-2-5-2-2) | market availability and term pricing"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Recommended term procurement slate"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Anchor base-load feedstock supply on quality-compatible, economically sustained term choices."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "monthly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CMU-REFPLAN-001 | SRC-EIA-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Plan Optimal Feedstock Slate And Run Rate | informed-by: Evaluate Crude & Feedstock | precedes: Create New Contract"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Q3 decision: 'term slate' read as the term procurement portfolio recommendation — base-load supply anchored by continuing commitments — not the production slate (PTC-002 forecloses that reading). Glossary rule (Hamid, batch 18): term supply is feedstock sourced under an agreement with a multi-period or continuing commercial commitment; spot supply is assessed or acquired for a discrete, near-term market opportunity or short-duration requirement; no fixed duration threshold is assumed — classifications vary by company, market, and contract. Quality screening justifies the Quality Management placement. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-5-1-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10428" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10428"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-5-1 ;
    skos:definition "Develop the recommended spot feedstock procurement slate — the quality-screened spot supply candidates that address residual demand, supply disruption, quality constraints, timing needs, or economic opportunities after considering committed term supply, ranked by current economic attractiveness and availability — so spot procurement decisions start from a vetted, refinery-compatible option set."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.5.1.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Develop Spot Slate"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers spot procurement-slate development: quantifying the requirement against current feedstock demand (CM-1-2-5-2-2) and committed term supply, screening available spot cargoes and grades for refinery quality compatibility, ranking candidates on current economics, logistics feasibility, and timing, and recommending the spot slate to the feedstock recommendation and trading processes. Spot supply means feedstock assessed or acquired for a discrete, near-term market opportunity or short-duration requirement — spot purchases may fill residual demand, replace disrupted term supply, resolve quality constraints, optimize economics, or create flexibility. The output is a procurement recommendation: the binding production slate is Refinery Planning's (CM-1-1-4, PTC-002); spot deal negotiation and execution belong to trading (CM-1-2-1-3). Excludes the binding production slate (CM-1-1-4, PTC-002); trade negotiation and execution (CM-1-2-1-3); term slate (CM-1-2-5-1-3); arrival and logistics planning (CM-1-2-5-3).
In scope: Quantify the spot requirement against demand and committed term supply | screen spot candidates for quality compatibility | rank candidates on economics, logistics feasibility, and timing | recommend the spot slate
Out of scope: Binding production slate (CM-1-1-4, PTC-002) | trade execution (CM-1-2-1-3) | term slate (CM-1-2-5-1-3) | logistics planning (CM-1-2-5-3)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Feedstock demand and committed term supply (CM-1-2-5-2-2, CM-1-2-5-1-3) | validated quality data (CM-1-2-5-1-1) | spot availability and pricing"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Recommended spot procurement slate"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Give spot procurement a vetted, ranked, refinery-compatible option set at market speed."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "weekly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CMU-REFPLAN-001 | SRC-EIA-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Plan Optimal Feedstock Slate And Run Rate | enables: Coordinate Feedstock Trading | informed-by: Develop Term Slate"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Q3 decision with Hamid's wording correction: spot is not only deficit-filling — it addresses residual demand, disruption replacement, quality constraints, timing needs, and economic opportunity. Glossary rule per CM-1-2-5-1-3 note: no fixed duration threshold. Faster cadence than the term slate reflects spot market rhythm. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-5-2-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19527" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19527"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-5-2 ;
    skos:definition "Coordinate feedstock supply requirements with the trading processes — translating demand, slate recommendations, and schedule imbalances into requested purchases, sales, or exchanges with their grade, quality, quantity, timing, and delivery requirements, evaluating proposed trades for plan and schedule fit, and incorporating agreed volumes back into the supply plan."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.5.2.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Coordinate Feedstock Trading"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the feedstock-side interface to trading, mirroring Coordinate Product Trading (CM-1-1-3-5-2, batch 05): specifying what the feedstock plan needs — grade, quality, quantity, timing, location, delivery basis; requesting trading action; evaluating proposed deals for refinery compatibility, schedule feasibility, and plan fit; and incorporating agreed volumes into the supply plan and downstream logistics planning (CM-1-2-5-3). Trading decides the deal; planning decides the plan (decide-versus-advise, batch 05); the binding plan is Refinery Planning's (PTC-002). Excludes trade negotiation and execution (CM-1-2-1-3); the binding production plan (CM-1-1-4); transportation arrangement (CM-1-2-5-3-1); exchange agreement administration (CM-1-2-7-1-1).
In scope: Specify feedstock requirements for trading action | request purchases, sales, or exchanges | evaluate proposed deals for plan, schedule, and refinery fit | incorporate agreed volumes into the supply plan
Out of scope: Trade negotiation and execution (CM-1-2-1-3) | binding production plan (CM-1-1-4) | transportation arrangement (CM-1-2-5-3-1) | exchange agreement administration (CM-1-2-7-1-1)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Feedstock demand (CM-1-2-5-2-2) | slate recommendations (CM-1-2-5-1-3/-4, CM-1-2-5-2-3) | proposed trades from trading"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Coordinated trading requests and incorporated agreed volumes"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Convert feedstock needs into trading requests and trading results into a workable supply plan."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "weekly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-ORP-DPSC-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Develop Feedstock Demand | enables: Trade Capture | precedes: Manage Feedstock Transportation"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Declared feedstock mirror of CM-1-1-3-5-2 (batch 05): this node specifies the need and consumes the result; Supply And Trading owns the trade. Decide-versus-advise pattern applied. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-5-2-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10235" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10235"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-5-2 ;
    skos:definition "Develop the projected feedstock requirement — quantity, grade, quality, and timing — from the refinery production plan and current inventory and supply positions, maintaining it as plans and positions change, so procurement, slate development, and trading coordination work from one validated demand signal."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.5.2.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Develop Feedstock Demand"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers feedstock demand development: deriving requirements by grade family, quality envelope, quantity, and timing window from the binding production plan (CM-1-1-4) net of committed supply, in-transit volumes, and inventory positions (CM-1-2-5-4-2); maintaining the demand signal through plan revisions; and publishing it to slate development (CM-1-2-5-1-3/-4), the recommendation (CM-1-2-5-2-3), and trading coordination (CM-1-2-5-2-1). The demand derives from the plan; it does not set the plan (PTC-002). Excludes the production plan itself (CM-1-1-4); product demand forecasting (CM-1-2-6-2-3, batch 03 constraint honored); procurement and trading action (CM-1-2-5-2-1, CM-1-2-1-3).
In scope: Derive feedstock requirements from the production plan net of committed supply and inventory | maintain the demand signal through plan revisions | publish one validated demand to slate development, recommendation, and coordination
Out of scope: Production plan (CM-1-1-4, PTC-002) | product demand forecasting (CM-1-2-6-2-3) | procurement and trading action (CM-1-2-5-2-1, CM-1-2-1-3)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Binding production plan (CM-1-1-4) | committed supply and in-transit volumes | feedstock inventory positions (CM-1-2-5-4-2)"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Validated feedstock demand by grade, quality, quantity, and timing"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "One validated feedstock demand signal behind every supply decision."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "monthly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CMU-REFPLAN-001 | SRC-ORP-DPSC-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Refinery Planning | uses-input: Manage Feedstock Inventory | precedes: Coordinate Feedstock Trading"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Derived-not-decided: demand is computed from the binding plan and positions; the plan is Refinery Planning's. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-5-2-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10215" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10215"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Recommend Feedstock Slate And Run Rate"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-5-2 ;
    skos:definition "Recommend the economically attractive feedstock slate and run rate to Refinery Planning — combining the demand signal, procurement slates, market prices, and supply economics into a trading-side recommendation that feeds the binding plan without setting it — so the refinery plan is informed by the best current supply-market view."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.5.2.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Plan Optimal Feedstock Slate And Run Rate"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the trading-side slate and run-rate recommendation under PTC-002 (Option 1, closed 2026-09-18): assembling the economically attractive feedstock slate and run-rate case from the demand signal (CM-1-2-5-2-2), term and spot procurement slates (CM-1-2-5-1-3/-4), current market prices and differentials, and supply logistics; submitting the recommendation to Refinery Planning; and iterating as market conditions change. Refinery Planning (CM-1-1-4) decides the binding crude slate and run rate; this process advises and never sets, approves, or revises the plan. Crude evaluation is owned by Evaluate Crude & Feedstock (CM-1-1-4-7-2, batch 02). Excludes the binding slate and run-rate decision (CM-1-1-4, PTC-002); crude evaluation (CM-1-1-4-7-2); procurement slate development (CM-1-2-5-1-3/-4); trade execution (CM-1-2-1-3).
In scope: Assemble the economically attractive slate and run-rate case from demand, slates, and market economics | submit the recommendation to Refinery Planning | iterate the recommendation as markets move
Out of scope: Binding slate and run-rate decision (CM-1-1-4, PTC-002) | crude evaluation (CM-1-1-4-7-2) | procurement slate development (CM-1-2-5-1-3/-4) | trade execution (CM-1-2-1-3)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Feedstock demand (CM-1-2-5-2-2) | term and spot slates (CM-1-2-5-1-3/-4) | market prices, differentials, and logistics costs"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Trading-side slate and run-rate recommendation to Refinery Planning"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Put the best current supply-market case in front of the planner who decides."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "monthly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CMU-REFPLAN-001 | SRC-SNF-REFPS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Refinery Planning | uses-input: Develop Feedstock Demand | uses-input: Develop Spot Slate"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). PTC-002 implementation (Option 1, Hamid, closed 2026-09-18, quoted): 'Refinery Planning decides; Supply And Trading advises. CM-1-1-4 sets the binding crude slate and run rate. CM-1-2-5-2-3 is the trading-side recommendation of an economically attractive feedstock slate and run rate, feeding the plan without setting it.' The row label overstates the mandate ('Plan Optimal') and, per Hamid's batch 18 direction, the normalized label 'Recommend Feedstock Slate And Run Rate' is strongly queued for the naming pass to protect the PTC-002 decision from later misreading. Decide-versus-advise pattern (PTC-002 is its founding instance). APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-5-3-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12923" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12923"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-5-3 ;
    skos:definition "Coordinate and monitor the commercial and logistical arrangements required to move agreed feedstock volumes under available carrier contracts, capacity rights, nominations, fixtures, and transport agreements — selecting modes and routings, tracking movement status, and resolving in-transit issues with carriers and operators — so every purchased cargo has a managed path to the refinery."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.5.3.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Feedstock Transportation"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers movement coordination and monitoring: mode and routing selection within the supply plan and carrier arrangements; requesting or coordinating nominations, fixtures, bookings, and carrier actions under contracts administered at CM-1-2-1-2-9 and deals captured at CM-1-2-1-3-9; movement status tracking; and in-transit issue resolution with carriers and operators, escalating schedule impacts to CM-1-2-5-3-2/-3. The process may request or coordinate nominations, fixtures, bookings, and carrier actions, but it does not execute charter-party negotiation, carrier contracting, physical transport, refinery discharge operations, or movement actualization. Excludes charter-party negotiation and chartering execution (marine logistics processes); carrier contracting (CM-1-2-1-2-9); delivery scheduling (CM-1-2-5-3-2); arrival planning (CM-1-2-5-3-3); actualization (CM-1-2-3-1).
In scope: Select modes and routings within the supply plan | request or coordinate nominations, fixtures, bookings, and carrier actions | track movement status | resolve in-transit issues and escalate schedule impacts
Out of scope: Charter-party negotiation and chartering execution (marine logistics) | carrier contracting (CM-1-2-1-2-9) | delivery scheduling (CM-1-2-5-3-2) | arrival planning (CM-1-2-5-3-3) | actualization (CM-1-2-3-1)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Agreed purchases (CM-1-2-5-2-1) | carrier contracts and capacity (CM-1-2-1-2-9) | movement status from carriers"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Coordinated, tracked feedstock movements"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "A managed, tracked path to the refinery for every purchased cargo."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "weekly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-ORP-DPSC-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Coordinate Feedstock Trading | precedes: Schedule Feedstock Delivery | uses-input: Manage Carrier Contracts"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Coordinate-not-execute (Hamid, batch 18): this process requests and coordinates under existing authority; charter negotiation, contracting, physical transport, discharge, and actualization stay with their owners — matching the Trade Capture, Contract Management, and nominations boundaries. Whether it holds authority to request nominations/fixtures directly or only coordinates already-authorized actions is an operating-model follow-up carried in the batch 18 PR. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-5-3-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20882" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20882"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-5-3 ;
    skos:definition "Schedule feedstock deliveries into agreed refinery receipt windows by sequencing confirmed movements against available berth, pipeline-receipt, tankage, quality-segregation, and refinery-receipt constraints, coordinating and confirming proposed receipt windows with the relevant refinery and terminal scheduling and operations owners, and maintaining the delivery schedule as movements and refinery conditions change."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.5.3.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Schedule Feedstock Delivery"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers delivery scheduling: sequencing coordinated movements (CM-1-2-5-3-1) into agreed refinery receipt windows against berth availability, pipeline receipt capacity, tank space, and quality-segregation needs; coordinating and confirming proposed receipt windows with the relevant refinery/terminal scheduling and operations owners, who retain authority over operational feasibility; and maintaining the schedule under movement delays and refinery changes. Refinery-side unit and tank operations scheduling belongs to Refining and the production-scheduling processes under the approved planning-scheduling-operations hierarchy (batch 06); this process schedules the deliveries, not the refinery. Excludes refinery unit and tank operations scheduling (Refining, CM-1-1-7-2, batch 06 hierarchy); receipt-window operational approval (refinery/terminal operations owners); movement coordination (CM-1-2-5-3-1); arrival planning (CM-1-2-5-3-3); discharge operations (terminal/refinery operators).
In scope: Sequence confirmed movements into agreed receipt windows against constraints | coordinate and confirm proposed receipt windows with refinery/terminal scheduling and operations owners | maintain the delivery schedule under changes
Out of scope: Refinery operations scheduling (Refining, CM-1-1-7-2) | receipt-window operational approval (refinery/terminal operations owners) | movement coordination (CM-1-2-5-3-1) | arrival planning (CM-1-2-5-3-3) | discharge operations (operators)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Coordinated movements (CM-1-2-5-3-1) | berth, receipt, and tankage constraints | refinery schedule interfaces (CM-1-1-7-2)"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Confirmed, maintained feedstock delivery schedule"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Deliveries land in windows the refinery has actually agreed it can receive."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "weekly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CMU-REFPLAN-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Manage Feedstock Transportation | precedes: Plan Feedstock Arrival | informed-by: Production Scheduling"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Proposes-and-confirms, does not decide feasibility: refinery/terminal operations owners retain authority over operational receipt feasibility (Hamid, batch 18; batch 06 hierarchy). APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-5-3-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10943" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10943"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-5-3 ;
    skos:definition "Plan individual feedstock arrivals — coordinating laycans, arrival timing, and berth and discharge sequencing with the terminal and refinery, recommending or coordinating proposed tank destinations with refinery/terminal operations subject to their operational authority, and adjusting the arrival plan as vessels, pipelines, and refinery conditions develop — so each arrival is prepared for timely, safe, and quality-compatible receipt, subject to operational conditions and the governing agreements."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.5.3.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Plan Feedstock Arrival"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers per-arrival planning: coordinating laycan and arrival timing with carriers; planning berth assignment and discharge sequence with terminal and refinery operations; recommending or coordinating proposed tank destinations with refinery/terminal operations, subject to their operational authority over safe discharge, line-up, and receipt feasibility; monitoring approach status and replanning under delay; and providing arrival facts context that downstream actualization and demurrage processes consume. Time, notice, and event facts are recorded at actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-4) and demurrage determination follows CM-1-2-4-4 (batch 17); physical berth and discharge operations belong to terminal and refinery operators. Excludes discharge operations and tank-destination operational decisions (terminal/refinery operators); time-fact actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-4); demurrage claims (CM-1-2-4-4); delivery scheduling (CM-1-2-5-3-2).
In scope: Coordinate laycans and arrival timing per cargo | plan berth and discharge sequence with operators | recommend or coordinate proposed tank destinations subject to operational authority | monitor approach, replan under delay, and provide arrival context downstream
Out of scope: Discharge operations and tank-destination operational decisions (terminal/refinery operators) | time-fact actualization (CM-1-2-3-1-4) | demurrage claims (CM-1-2-4-4) | delivery scheduling (CM-1-2-5-3-2)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Delivery schedule (CM-1-2-5-3-2) | vessel/pipeline approach status | berth, tankage, and segregation constraints"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Per-cargo arrival plans maintained to discharge"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every arrival prepared for receipt — timed, sequenced, and quality-compatible — under the operators' authority."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EI-HM32-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Schedule Feedstock Delivery | enables: Perform Vessel & Barge Actualization | informed-by: Manage Feedstock Inventory"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Prepares-not-guarantees (Hamid, batch 18): weather, congestion, force majeure, and operational constraints sit outside this process; it prepares each arrival for timely, safe, quality-compatible receipt subject to conditions and the governing agreements, and operators keep operational authority (batch 06 boundary). APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-5-4-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12942" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12942"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-5-4 ;
    skos:definition "Assess the price, supply-availability, disruption, quality, concentration, and working-capital risk associated with feedstock inventory positions; recommend inventory, supply, operational, and hedging mitigations to accountable owners; and submit approved hedging requirements to the authorized trading process so risk is visible, understood, and managed within approved appetite."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.5.4.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Assess & Manage Inventory Risk"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers feedstock inventory risk: assessing price exposure from inventory valuation and forward prices; supply and disruption risk from delayed cargoes, outages, unavailable grades, or constrained logistics; quality and segregation risk from off-spec, incompatible, or constrained positioning; concentration risk from excessive exposure to one grade, source, geography, supplier, route, or facility; and working-capital risk from inventory value, dwell time, and cash tied up in stock; recommending inventory, supply, operational, and hedging mitigations to the accountable owners; and submitting approved hedging requirements or mitigation requests to the authorized trading process — Trading Strategy Management, Trade Approval, and Trade Capture govern strategy, authorization, and execution (batches 07-08). Receiving row of the batch 11 exclusion: inventory risk here, counterparty credit risk at CM-1-2-2-1. Boundary with Market Risk (batch 12): once a hedge or position is booked into the trading book, its measurement, limits, and P&L belong to Market Risk Management (CM-1-2-2-3); non-trading inventory exposure is assessed here. Risk appetite, limit approval, and risk-measurement methodology remain with risk governance (batches 11-12). Excludes hedge strategy, authorization, and execution (CM-1-2-1-4, CM-1-2-1-3); trading-book measurement (CM-1-2-2-3, batch 12 rule); counterparty credit (CM-1-2-2-1); risk-appetite, limit, and methodology approval (risk governance); inventory policy (CM-1-1-3-7-1).
In scope: Assess price, supply, disruption, quality, concentration, and working-capital risk of feedstock positions | recommend inventory, supply, operational, and hedging mitigations to accountable owners | submit approved hedging requirements to the authorized trading process | keep inventory risk visible against requirements
Out of scope: Hedge strategy, authorization, and execution (CM-1-2-1-4, CM-1-2-1-3) | trading-book risk measurement (CM-1-2-2-3) | counterparty credit (CM-1-2-2-1) | risk-appetite, limit, and methodology approval (risk governance) | inventory policy (CM-1-1-3-7-1)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Feedstock positions (CM-1-2-5-4-2) | market prices and forward curves | refinery requirements (CM-1-2-5-2-2) | working-capital parameters"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Assessed inventory risk with recommended mitigations and submitted hedging requirements"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "No feedstock inventory risk that nobody has measured, owned, and decided to carry."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-ORP-DPSC-001 | SRC-CCRO-002A"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Manage Feedstock Inventory | enables: Trading Management | informed-by: Market Risk Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Q5 decision (Hamid): receiving row of the batch 11 exclusion; request-not-initiate — this process submits approved hedging requirements to the authorized trading process; Trading Strategy Management, Trade Approval, and Trade Capture govern strategy, authorization, and execution (batch 07/08 chain); booked hedges are measured by Market Risk (batch 12 rule). Risk categories are illustrative, not a mandated metric set — measurement methodology is governance-controlled and company-specific. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-5-4-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12942" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12942"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-5-4 ;
    skos:definition "Monitor and control actual and projected crude and feedstock inventory positions — identifying operating-limit, availability, quality, location, and balance exceptions against refinery requirements and the supply plan, coordinating timely response, and providing reliable current-position information to planning, trading, and logistics."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.5.4.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Feedstock Inventory"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers feedstock position stewardship, the feedstock counterpart of Manage Inventory (CM-1-1-3-7-12) under the batch 04 reciprocal constraint: recurring review of physical and book feedstock positions, movements, in-transit volumes, and commitments; minimum, maximum, safety-stock, and operating-limit exceptions against refinery run requirements; short-term availability and quality-segregation risks; escalation and coordination of corrective actions through the accountable owners; and maintenance of the position view that demand development (CM-1-2-5-2-2), arrival planning (CM-1-2-5-3-3), and inventory risk (CM-1-2-5-4-1) consume. Excludes inventory policy design (CM-1-1-3-7-1); measurement methodology (CM-1-1-3-7-4); book-to-physical reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5); finished-product and intermediates positions (CM-1-1-3-7-12, batch 04 reciprocal); tank operations (Refining); feedstock sourcing, purchase, exchange, and replenishment decision rights, which belong to the applicable supply-planning, Refinery Planning, and authorized trading processes.
In scope: Monitor physical, book, and in-transit feedstock positions against requirements | identify and escalate operating-limit, availability, and quality exceptions | coordinate corrective action through accountable owners | maintain the current-position view for planning, trading, and logistics
Out of scope: Inventory policy (CM-1-1-3-7-1) | measurement methodology (CM-1-1-3-7-4) | reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5) | finished-product/intermediates positions (CM-1-1-3-7-12) | tank operations (Refining) | sourcing, purchase, exchange, and replenishment decisions (supply-planning, Refinery Planning, and authorized trading processes)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Tank gaugings and book positions | in-transit movements (CM-1-2-5-3) | refinery run requirements | commitments and receipts"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Controlled feedstock position view with escalated exceptions"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "A current, reliable feedstock position with every exception owned and answered."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EI-HM32-001 | SRC-API-MPMS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Develop Feedstock Demand | enables: Assess & Manage Inventory Risk | informed-by: Crude/Feed Transportation Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Receiving row of the batch 04 reciprocal: CM-1-1-3-7-12 holds finished products and intermediates so this row holds feedstock — the mirrored wording is deliberate. Identifies-and-escalates: feedstock sourcing, purchase, exchange, and replenishment decision rights belong to the applicable supply-planning, Refinery Planning, and authorized trading processes (Hamid, batch 18) — replenishment stays aligned to the binding Refinery Plan, never independent of it. EI HM 32 daily stock-control practice context. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-6-1-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19968" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19968"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-6-1 ;
    skos:definition "Segment the customers and demand points served by the refined-product supply network on supply-economics dimensions — volume, product mix, location, delivery mode, service requirements, and margin contribution — so participation, service, and cost-to-serve analysis work from an economically meaningful segmentation."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.6.1.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Segment Customers"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers supply-economics segmentation: grouping customers and demand points by the dimensions that drive supply cost and value — volume and mix, location and logistics reach, delivery mode and service requirements, and contribution; maintaining the segmentation as the network and market change; and supplying it to attractiveness evaluation (CM-1-2-6-1-2) and cost-to-serve assessment (CM-1-2-6-1-3). Commercial, brand, and marketing segmentation for customer strategy, pricing, and offer design is owned by the Marketing processes (CM-1-3) where defined — the same customer may appear in both segmentations for different purposes. Excludes commercial and marketing segmentation (CM-1-3); customer strategy and pricing (CM-1-3); order and credit management (CM-1-3-7).
In scope: Group customers and demand points on supply-economics dimensions | maintain the segmentation as network and market change | supply the segmentation to attractiveness and cost-to-serve analysis
Out of scope: Commercial and marketing segmentation (CM-1-3) | customer strategy and pricing (CM-1-3) | order and credit management (CM-1-3-7)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Customer volume, mix, location, and delivery data | logistics and service records | margin contribution"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Maintained supply-economics segmentation"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "An economically meaningful customer segmentation behind every network analysis."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-LNL-T2S-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Evaluate Market Attractiveness | enables: Assess Cost-to-Serve Performance"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 19 (2026-09-20). Four-lens rule (Hamid, batch 19, consistent with the Party-plus-Role customer-domain model): the same customer can legitimately exist in supply-economics segmentation (here), Marketing/commercial segmentation (CM-1-3), credit segmentation (credit and order processes), and the party/account hierarchy (customer domain/MDM) — no one segmentation is universal, and this row claims only the supply-economics lens; parallels the batch 11 relationship-based credit split. APQC 'Validate with customers' is a WEAK unrelated candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-6-1-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10107" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10107"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-6-1 ;
    skos:definition "Evaluate market attractiveness for supply-network participation using accessible demand, realizable supply margin, logistics reach and cost, supply reliability, specification and regulatory constraints, and network fit — so participation recommendations rest on a consistent supply-network economic evaluation."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.6.1.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Evaluate Market Attractiveness"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers market attractiveness evaluation centered on the supply-network question: assessing markets and segments on accessible demand, realizable supply margin from the network's supply points, logistics reach and cost, supply reliability, specification and regulatory constraints, and fit with the existing and planned network; ranking participation options; and feeding the evaluation into network recommendations (CM-1-2-6-1) and demand management (CM-1-2-6-2). This is not generic market-entry strategy: participation, supply, and allocation decisions stay with the accountable owners (CM-1-1-2, commercial leadership); market-entry strategy and marketing programs with CM-1-3. Excludes participation and allocation decisions (CM-1-1-2, accountable owners); marketing strategy and programs (CM-1-3); demand forecasting (CM-1-2-6-2-3).
In scope: Assess markets on accessible demand, realizable margin, logistics reach, reliability, and constraints | rank participation options consistently | feed evaluations into network recommendations and demand management
Out of scope: Participation and allocation decisions (CM-1-1-2, accountable owners) | marketing strategy (CM-1-3) | demand forecasting (CM-1-2-6-2-3)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Segmentation (CM-1-2-6-1-1) | market demand and margin intelligence | logistics cost and access data"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Ranked market attractiveness evaluations for network participation"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "A consistent economic answer to 'is this market worth serving from our network?'"@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EIA-STEO-001 | SRC-LNL-T2S-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Segment Customers | enables: Network Management | informed-by: Assess Cost-to-Serve Performance"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 19 (2026-09-20). Supply-network-centered evaluation (Hamid, batch 19), not generic market-entry strategy — the question is what the network can serve profitably. Evaluate-and-rank, decide-elsewhere (batch 11 pattern); jurisdiction and specification requirements vary by market. APQC candidate is a WEAK generic match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-6-1-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10314" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10314"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-6-1 ;
    skos:definition "Assess the full cost of serving customers, segments, and markets from the refined-product supply network — supply, storage, handling, primary and secondary logistics, and service costs against realized margins — identifying where the network serves profitably, marginally, or at a loss, so participation and service recommendations rest on evidenced economics."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.6.1.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Assess Cost-to-Serve Performance"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers cost-to-serve assessment: assembling supply, storage, throughput, handling, transportation, and service costs by segment, customer group, and market from the accountable cost owners' data; setting them against realized volumes and margins; identifying profitable, marginal, and loss-making service; and feeding results to segmentation, attractiveness evaluation, and network recommendations. Costs are reported at an agreed allocation and attribution basis; cost-to-serve analysis does not itself create accounting cost allocations or transfer-pricing rules — those remain with Accounting, Finance, and the applicable legal-entity policy. Uses governed cost and margin data from Accounting and the logistics processes; it does not produce cost accounting (CM-1-2-4-2), set prices (CM-1-3), or decide service changes (accountable owners). Excludes cost accounting production and allocation rules (CM-1-2-4-2, Finance); transfer pricing (Finance/legal-entity policy); pricing (CM-1-3); service and participation decisions (accountable owners); distribution cost-performance backcasting (CM-1-1-6, locked rule).
In scope: Assemble cost-to-serve by segment, customer group, and market from governed data at the agreed allocation basis | set costs against realized volumes and margins | identify profitable, marginal, and loss-making service | feed evidenced findings to network recommendations
Out of scope: Cost accounting production and allocation rules (CM-1-2-4-2, Finance) | transfer pricing (Finance/legal-entity policy) | pricing (CM-1-3) | service and participation decisions (accountable owners) | distribution backcasting (CM-1-1-6, locked rule)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Segmentation (CM-1-2-6-1-1) | governed cost data (CM-1-2-4-2, logistics processes) | realized volumes and margins"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Evidenced cost-to-serve assessments by segment and market"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Know what serving each customer and market truly costs — and show it with evidence."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-LNL-T2S-001 | SRC-ORP-DPSC-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Segment Customers | enables: Network Management | uses-input: Accounting"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 19 (2026-09-20). Assessment on governed data at an agreed allocation basis — consumes Accounting and logistics costs, never creates allocation or transfer-pricing rules (Hamid, batch 19); plan-variance backcasting remains with Distribution Backcasting under its locked rule (batch 03). APQC candidate is a WEAK production-performance match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-6-2-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16630" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16630"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-6-2 ;
    skos:definition "Manage the operational refined-product demand picture — consolidating current orders, lifting patterns, committed volumes, and forecast demand by product and location, comparing it against available supply, and surfacing imbalances for supply, replenishment, and trading action — so shortfalls and surpluses are visible and acted on before they become failures."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.6.2.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Refined Product Demand"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers operational demand management: consolidating the current demand view from orders, rack liftings, contract commitments, and the operational forecast (CM-1-2-6-2-3); comparing demand against available and planned supply by product and location; identifying imbalances, allocation pressure, and demand risks; and routing actions to the accountable owners — replenishment requirements to the batch 04 decision owner (CM-1-1-3-7-9), trading needs through Coordinate Product Trading (CM-1-1-3-5-2), exchange utilization to CM-1-2-6-2-2. It may recommend supply actions and contingency options, but allocation decisions remain with Regional Optimization (CM-1-1-2, batch 03 rule); order commitments with Order Management (CM-1-3-7). Excludes allocation decisions (CM-1-1-2); order capture and commitment (CM-1-3-7); replenishment decisions (CM-1-1-3-7-9); trade execution (CM-1-2-1-3).
In scope: Consolidate the current operational demand view by product and location | compare demand against available and planned supply | identify imbalances and recommend supply actions and contingency options | route actions to the accountable owners
Out of scope: Allocation decisions (CM-1-1-2) | order capture and commitment (CM-1-3-7) | replenishment decisions (CM-1-1-3-7-9) | trade execution (CM-1-2-1-3)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Orders and lifting data | operational forecast (CM-1-2-6-2-3) | supply availability | exchange positions (CM-1-2-6-2-2)"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Current demand-supply picture with recommended actions routed to owners"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "No demand shortfall or surplus that nobody saw coming."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "weekly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-ORP-DPSC-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Forecast Refined Product Demand | enables: Manage Replenishment | enables: Coordinate Product Trading"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 19 (2026-09-20). Recommends-and-routes (Hamid, batch 19): the process actively recommends supply actions and contingencies while the batch 03/04 decision rights stay with their owners — allocation with Regional Optimization, replenishment with CM-1-1-3-7-9. APQC candidate is an unrelated image-management match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-6-2-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16630" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16630"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-6-2 ;
    skos:definition "Coordinate operational utilization of product-exchange entitlements to support approved demand and supply requirements by monitoring available exchange positions, planning proposed lifting and delivery use by location and period, and communicating utilization requirements to the relevant agreement, trading, supply, and scheduling processes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.6.2.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Refined Product Exchanges"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the operational use of product exchanges: monitoring available positions, entitlements, and imbalance status under existing exchange agreements for operational visibility; planning proposed lifting and delivery utilization by location and period against demand needs (CM-1-2-6-2-1) and supply plans; communicating utilization requirements to the agreement owner (CM-1-2-7-1-1), trading coordination, supply operations (CM-1-2-6-3), and the scheduling processes; and flagging utilization or imbalance concerns to the agreement owner. Operational visibility of imbalance status does not constitute contractual imbalance calculation, commercial decision, agreement administration, or settlement — exchange agreements, terms, and imbalance treatment are owned by Manage Product Exchange Agreements (CM-1-2-7-1-1, batch 08/14 bindings); 'exchange' here means product exchange arrangements, not futures exchanges (CM-1-2-1-3-4, batch 08). Excludes exchange agreement administration, contractual imbalance calculation, and imbalance treatment (CM-1-2-7-1-1); futures-exchange trading (CM-1-2-1-3-4); settlement (CM-1-2-4-1); movement scheduling (CM-1-1-3-6).
In scope: Monitor available exchange positions and entitlements for operational visibility | plan proposed lifting and delivery utilization by location and period | communicate utilization requirements to agreement, trading, supply, and scheduling processes | flag utilization and imbalance concerns to the agreement owner
Out of scope: Exchange agreement administration and contractual imbalance calculation and treatment (CM-1-2-7-1-1) | futures-exchange trading (CM-1-2-1-3-4) | settlement (CM-1-2-4-1) | movement scheduling (CM-1-1-3-6)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Exchange positions and entitlements (CM-1-2-7-1-1) | demand needs (CM-1-2-6-2-1) | location supply plans"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Proposed exchange utilization plans communicated to the owning processes"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Exchange entitlements pulled at the right place and time — never wasted, never overdrawn."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "weekly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-ORP-DPSC-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Manage Refined Product Demand | enables: Supply Operations | informed-by: Manage Product Exchange Agreements"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 19 (2026-09-20). Q4 decision with Hamid's caution applied: operational visibility and proposed utilization here; contractual imbalance calculation, commercial decisions, administration, and settlement at CM-1-2-7-1-1 (batch 08 'not product exchange agreements' and batch 14 imbalance-treatment bindings honored). Use-versus-administer split parallels the batch 11 set-versus-apply pattern. APQC candidate is an unrelated image-management match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-6-2-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20634" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20634"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Forecast Operational Refined Product Demand"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-6-2 ;
    skos:definition "Forecast refined-product demand operationally for the supply-chain context — projecting short-horizon demand by product, location, and period from lifting history, orders, seasonality, and local market signals, calibrated against the published enterprise forecast — so supply operations and replenishment run on location-level demand projections at operational granularity."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.6.2.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Forecast Refined Product Demand"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers operational demand forecasting under the batch 03 constraint: short-horizon, location-level projections by product and period for supply operations, replenishment support, and exchange utilization; built from rack lifting history, current orders, seasonality, weather and local market signals; calibrated against the published enterprise forecast (CM-1-1-1-1) and reconciled through governed exception and variance processes where material differences arise — operational forecasts can legitimately differ on local timing, geography, product granularity, and immediate operational signals, and this process consumes the enterprise forecast without producing, revising, republishing, or overriding it. The batch 03 three-way partition: enterprise published forecast at CM-1-1-1-1; trading intake at CM-1-2-1-1-2; operational SCM-context forecasting here. Excludes the enterprise published forecast (CM-1-1-1-1, batch 03); trading forecast intake and forward views (CM-1-2-1-1-2); the refinery-planning demand basis (CM-1-1-4-7-1); feedstock demand (CM-1-2-5-2-2, batch 18).
In scope: Project short-horizon demand by product, location, and period | build from lifting history, orders, and local signals | calibrate against the published enterprise forecast and reconcile material differences through governed variance processes | publish operational projections to supply operations and replenishment
Out of scope: Enterprise published forecast (CM-1-1-1-1) | trading forecast intake (CM-1-2-1-1-2) | refinery demand basis (CM-1-1-4-7-1) | feedstock demand (CM-1-2-5-2-2)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Published enterprise forecast (CM-1-1-1-1) | rack lifting history and orders | seasonality and local market signals"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Operational location-level demand projections reconciled to the published forecast"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Location-level demand projections at the granularity supply operations actually runs on."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "weekly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EIA-STEO-001 | SRC-ORP-DPSC-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Demand Forecasting | enables: Manage Refined Product Demand | enables: Manage Replenishment"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 19 (2026-09-20). Batch 03 constraint implemented: operational SCM-context forecasting consuming the published forecast as calibration input; legitimate local differences reconcile through governed exception/variance processes, not forced equality (Hamid, batch 19). Normalized label 'Forecast Operational Refined Product Demand' queued to make the boundary visible. The variance threshold that triggers escalation is an operating-model follow-up carried in the batch 19 PR. APQC 'Forecast IT service demand' is a false lexical match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-6-3-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10734" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10734"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Coordinate S&T Source-Point Supply Operations"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-6-3 ;
    skos:definition "Coordinate commercial supply status at the source points where Supply And Trading supplies primary distribution — monitoring availability, loading capability, and product quality at purchased-supply, exchange, transfer, and third-party terminal locations, resolving supply issues with operators and counterparties, and keeping source-point status current for replenishment fulfillment and scheduling."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.6.3.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Primary Distribution Operations"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers S&T-side source-point coordination: monitoring product availability, allocation, loading capability, and quality status at locations where the network is supplied from purchases, exchanges, transfers, and third-party terminals; resolving availability, quality, and loading issues with terminal operators, exchange partners, and suppliers; and maintaining current source-point status for replenishment fulfillment (CM-1-2-6-3-2), demand management (CM-1-2-6-2-1), and the scheduling processes. Physical terminal operations belong to the operators; own-refinery rack and terminal operations to Refining and Midstream; movement scheduling to CM-1-1-3-6. Excludes physical terminal operations (operators, Midstream); own-production scheduling and administration (CM-1-1-3-5/-6); terminal contracting (commercial processes); terminal/storage settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-14).
In scope: Monitor availability, loading capability, and quality at S&T-supplied source points | resolve supply issues with operators and counterparties | keep source-point status current for replenishment fulfillment and scheduling
Out of scope: Physical terminal operations (operators, Midstream) | own-production scheduling (CM-1-1-3-5/-6) | terminal contracting (commercial processes) | terminal settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-14)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Source-point status from operators and partners | agreed purchases, transfers, and exchange entitlements (CM-1-2-1-3, CM-1-2-7-1-1) | replenishment and demand needs"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Current, coordinated source-point supply status"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every S&T-supplied source point ready to load the right product when the network needs it."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EI-HM32-001 | SRC-ORP-DPSC-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Manage Replenishment | uses-input: Manage Refined Product Exchanges | uses-input: Coordinate Product Trading"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 19 (2026-09-20). Coordinates-commercial-status, never operates (owner-versus-executor pattern, batch 05): operators run the terminals; this process coordinates the S&T supply interest at them. Normalized label 'Coordinate S&T Source-Point Supply Operations' queued to prevent mistaken inference of physical operations (Hamid, batch 19). APQC 'Manage treasury operations' is a false lexical match on 'operations'."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-6-3-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10256" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10256"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Fulfill Replenishment From Trading Sources"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-6-3 ;
    skos:definition "Fulfill network replenishment requirements from Supply And Trading sources — receiving the replenishment quantities and timing decided by the network replenishment owner, sourcing them from purchased, exchanged, and transferred product where own production does not cover them, and confirming fulfillment plans, constraints, and exceptions back — so replenishment decisions are met without S&T re-deciding them."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.6.3.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Replenishment"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers replenishment fulfillment from commercial sources: receiving replenishment requirements decided by Manage Inventory Replenishment (CM-1-1-3-7-9, batch 04 — that process owns when and how much to resupply each stock point); determining the feasible S&T source — purchased product, exchange liftings (CM-1-2-6-2-2), or transfers — where own production and the regional plan do not cover the requirement; initiating the sourcing through the coordination and trading processes; and confirming fulfillment plans, timing, constraints, and exceptions back to the requirement owner — under supply constraint, fulfillment may differ from the request, and the confirmation returns what is feasible for the owner's decision. This process fulfills, and never makes, the replenishment decision. Excludes the replenishment decision (CM-1-1-3-7-9, batch 04); allocation decisions (CM-1-1-2); trade execution (CM-1-2-1-3); movement scheduling (CM-1-1-3-6); feedstock replenishment (CM-1-2-5, batch 18).
In scope: Receive replenishment requirements from the deciding owner | determine the feasible S&T source where own production does not cover | initiate sourcing through coordination and trading processes | confirm fulfillment plans, constraints, and exceptions back to the requirement owner
Out of scope: Replenishment decisions (CM-1-1-3-7-9, batch 04) | allocation decisions (CM-1-1-2) | trade execution (CM-1-2-1-3) | movement scheduling (CM-1-1-3-6) | feedstock replenishment (CM-1-2-5)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Replenishment requirements (CM-1-1-3-7-9) | source-point status (CM-1-2-6-3-1) | exchange entitlements (CM-1-2-6-2-2) | available purchases and transfers"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Confirmed fulfillment plans with constraints and exceptions returned to the decision owner"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Fulfill approved replenishment requirements from feasible commercial sources while returning confirmed timing, constraints, and exceptions to the replenishment decision owner."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-ORP-DPSC-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Manage Inventory Replenishment | uses-input: Manage Primary Distribution Operations | enables: Coordinate Product Trading"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 19 (2026-09-20). Q2 decision at its sharpest point: the batch 04 decision right ('Manage Inventory Replenishment owns the replenishment decision') is preserved verbatim — this row is the S&T fulfillment counterpart, decide-versus-fulfill mirroring decide-versus-advise. Normalized label 'Fulfill Replenishment From Trading Sources' queued with priority (Hamid, batch 19: highest naming collision in the batch; names mirror the locked taxonomy, so carried as alt label until the naming pass). APQC 'Manage collaborative replenishment planning' scored STRONG at 3b but NO SOURCE — honored."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-6-3-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12923" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12923"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Coordinate S&T-Sourced Primary Transportation"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-6-3 ;
    skos:definition "Coordinate transportation arrangements for Supply And Trading-sourced primary product movements — pipeline, marine, rail, and truck arrangements under available carrier contracts, capacity, and transport agreements for volumes S&T supplies into the network — providing confirmed movement requirements to the processes that own scheduling and actualization."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.6.3.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Primary Distribution Transportation"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers transportation coordination for S&T-sourced product movements, the product-side counterpart of the batch 18 feedstock transportation cluster: coordinating and monitoring the commercial and logistical arrangements to move S&T-sourced volumes — purchases, exchange liftings, transfers — under carrier contracts (CM-1-2-1-2-9), capacity rights, and transportation deals (CM-1-2-1-3-9); requesting or coordinating nominations, bookings, and carrier actions under the batch 18 coordinate-not-execute rule; tracking status and resolving in-transit issues; and providing the confirmed source, transport, capacity, timing, and commercial-movement requirements to Physical Distribution Scheduling (CM-1-1-3-6), which owns primary movement nomination and shipper-side administration, and to Actualizations (CM-1-2-3-1), which owns commercial movement actuals. Excludes primary movement nomination, scheduling, and shipper-side administration (CM-1-1-3-6, batch 05); carrier contracting (CM-1-2-1-2-9); transportation deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3-9); charter-party negotiation and physical transport (marine logistics, carriers); actualization and freight settlement (CM-1-2-3-1, CM-1-2-4-1); secondary distribution (CM-1-1-3-8).
In scope: Coordinate transport arrangements for S&T-sourced product movements under existing contracts and capacity | request or coordinate nominations, bookings, and carrier actions | track status and resolve in-transit issues | provide confirmed movement requirements to Physical Distribution Scheduling and Actualizations
Out of scope: Primary movement nomination and administration (CM-1-1-3-6) | carrier contracting (CM-1-2-1-2-9) | transportation deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3-9) | charter negotiation and physical transport (marine logistics, carriers) | actualization and settlement (CM-1-2-3-1, CM-1-2-4-1) | secondary distribution (CM-1-1-3-8)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Fulfillment plans (CM-1-2-6-3-2) | carrier contracts and capacity (CM-1-2-1-2-9) | movement status from carriers"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Coordinated transport arrangements with confirmed requirements provided to scheduling and actualization"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "S&T-sourced product moved on managed arrangements, handed unconditionally to the processes that schedule and account for it."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "weekly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-ORP-DPSC-001 | SRC-DOE-RPPL-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Manage Replenishment | uses-input: Manage Carrier Contracts | precedes: Physical Distribution Scheduling"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 19 (2026-09-20). Deterministic handoff (Hamid, batch 19): confirmed movement requirements go to Physical Distribution Scheduling, which owns primary movement nomination and shipper-side administration (batch 05), and to Actualizations for commercial movement actuals — the handoff is unconditional, making the batch 05 boundary enforceable. Coordinate-not-execute per batch 18. Normalized label 'Coordinate S&T-Sourced Primary Transportation' queued. APQC 'Manage bulk transportation' is a WEAK generic match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-7-1-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16630" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16630"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-7-1 ;
    skos:definition "Negotiate and administer product exchange agreements — the terms under which product is exchanged with other suppliers across locations and time — owning the entitlement rules, differentials, and contractual imbalance calculation and treatment, and recommending or making delegated commercial decisions on imbalance resolution, so exchange supply operates against governed agreements with controlled balances."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.7.1.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Product Exchange Agreements"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the exchange-agreement lifecycle: negotiating exchange agreements with counterparty suppliers — locations, products, volumes, differentials and location/quality adjustments, entitlement rules, imbalance provisions, and termination terms; administering agreements through amendments and renewals; performing the contractual imbalance calculation and treatment the agreements provide (batch 14 binding); recommending or making delegated commercial decisions on imbalance resolution, make-up, cash-out, or other contractual remedies under the governing agreement — authority thresholds, Finance impact, and Legal review apply per the documented delegation; and supplying entitlement and position data to the operational utilization process. The batch 19 split: operational visibility and proposed utilization at Manage Refined Product Exchanges (CM-1-2-6-2-2); contractual calculation and commercial decisions here. 'Exchange' means product exchange arrangements, not futures exchanges (CM-1-2-1-3-4, batch 08). Exchange settlement executes per the agreements at Settlements (CM-1-2-4-1-2, batch 14). Excludes operational utilization planning (CM-1-2-6-2-2); futures-exchange trading (CM-1-2-1-3-4); settlement execution (CM-1-2-4-1); legal drafting and execution authority (Legal & Corporate Communications); movement scheduling (CM-1-1-3-6).
In scope: Negotiate exchange agreements with entitlement, differential, and imbalance terms | administer amendments and renewals | perform contractual imbalance calculation and treatment and recommend or make delegated decisions on remedies | supply entitlement and position data to operational utilization
Out of scope: Operational utilization (CM-1-2-6-2-2) | futures-exchange trading (CM-1-2-1-3-4) | settlement execution (CM-1-2-4-1) | legal drafting and execution (Legal & Corporate Communications) | movement scheduling (CM-1-1-3-6)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Exchange requirements from demand and supply processes (CM-1-2-6-2-2, CM-1-2-6-3) | counterparty proposals | lifted and delivered volumes (CM-1-2-3-1)"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Governed exchange agreements with calculated balances, delegated decisions, and current entitlement data"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Exchange supply governed by sound agreements, with every balance calculated, decided under the right authority, and controlled under contract."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EIA-GLOSS-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Manage Refined Product Exchanges | informed-by: Actualizations | precedes: Settle Products"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 20 (2026-09-20). Receiving row of six bindings (batches 08/09/14/18/19): 'not product exchange agreements' (futures split, b08); imbalance treatment administered here (b14); contractual imbalance calculation, commercial decision, agreement administration, and settlement conditions here versus operational visibility at CM-1-2-6-2-2 (b19). Delegated-authority rule (Hamid, batch 20): imbalance remedies are recommended or decided within the documented delegation and the governing agreement, respecting authority thresholds, Finance impact, and Legal review. The entitlement, not a parcel, is the governed object (b13 entitlement concept). APQC 'Manage product images' is a false lexical match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-7-1-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16630" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16630"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-7-1 ;
    skos:definition "Negotiate and administer customer-facing product transport and delivery agreements — the delivery terms linked to product sale and marketing arrangements, including delivered pricing bases, delivery service commitments, and receiving requirements — so product deliveries to customers operate against current, correct commercial delivery terms."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.7.1.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Product Transport Agreements"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the customer-facing side of the batch 09 boundary rule: negotiating and administering the transport and delivery agreements connected to product sale and marketing arrangements — delivered-price and freight bases, delivery windows and service commitments, receiving-facility requirements, and delivery-related liability terms — through amendments and renewals, keeping order management, secondary distribution, and settlement supplied with current terms. The classification test is the agreement's commercial purpose, not the physical transport mode: a truck contract can be a carrier agreement when it procures capacity or rates for supply movements (Manage Carrier Contracts, CM-1-2-1-2-9), or a customer-facing delivery agreement when it forms part of a product-sale commitment (here) — the batch 09 rule applied to every mode. Excludes carrier contracts for supply and trading movements (CM-1-2-1-2-9, batch 09 rule); customer order capture and fulfillment (CM-1-3-7); secondary delivery scheduling (CM-1-1-3-8); legal drafting and execution (Legal & Corporate Communications); transportation deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3-9).
In scope: Negotiate customer-facing delivery and transport terms linked to product sales | administer amendments and renewals | maintain delivered-price, service, and receiving terms | keep order, distribution, and settlement processes supplied with current terms
Out of scope: Carrier contracts for supply/trading movements (CM-1-2-1-2-9) | order capture and fulfillment (CM-1-3-7) | secondary delivery scheduling (CM-1-1-3-8) | legal drafting and execution (Legal & Corporate Communications) | transportation deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3-9)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Product sale and marketing arrangements (CM-1-3) | delivery capability and cost data | counterparty and customer proposals"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Administered customer-facing transport and delivery agreements"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every customer delivery running on negotiated, current delivery terms."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EIA-GLOSS-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Manage Carrier Contracts | enables: Secondary Distribution Management | enables: Settlements"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 20 (2026-09-20). Receiving row of the batch 09 boundary rule with Hamid's classification test recorded verbatim: the agreement's commercial purpose decides — capacity/rates for supply movements versus part of a product-sale commitment — never the transport mode alone. APQC 'Manage product images' is a false lexical match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-7-1-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10815" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10815"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-7-1 ;
    skos:definition "Negotiate storage and throughput agreements — leased tankage, cavern capacity, terminal throughput, and handling arrangements — establishing capacity, fee structures, service levels, and quality-protection terms, obtaining required commercial approvals, coordinating execution through Legal and authorized signatories, and handing executed agreements to the contract record and deal capture, so storage and throughput capacity is secured on sound commercial terms."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.7.1.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Negotiate Storage / Throughput Agreements"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the negotiation position in the batch 09 storage split: identifying storage and throughput needs with the supply and trading processes; negotiating capacity, term, fee structures (throughput, storage, blending, handling, minimums), service levels, and quality-protection provisions; obtaining required commercial approvals and coordinating execution of the agreement through Legal and authorized signatories — execution authority remains with Legal & Corporate Communications and the authorized signatories, not this process. On execution the agreement hands off per the split: the contract record is maintained by Manage Storage Contract (CM-1-2-1-2-10); commercially distinct capacity transactions are captured by Capture Storage Deals (CM-1-2-1-3-5) — per the batch 09 caveat, not every storage agreement results in a separately captured deal, only where capacity is commercially distinct, traded, allocated, acquired, released, or modeled as a transaction; company stock in third-party facilities is administered by Manage Third Party Storage (CM-1-1-3-7-11); charges settle at Settle Terminal/Storage (CM-1-2-4-1-14). Excludes contract record administration (CM-1-2-1-2-10); capacity deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3-5); third-party storage inventory administration (CM-1-1-3-7-11); storage settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-14); legal drafting and execution authority (Legal & Corporate Communications).
In scope: Identify storage and throughput needs with supply and trading processes | negotiate capacity, fees, service levels, and quality-protection terms | obtain commercial approvals and coordinate execution through Legal and authorized signatories | hand executed agreements to the contract record and deal capture
Out of scope: Contract record administration (CM-1-2-1-2-10) | capacity deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3-5) | third-party storage administration (CM-1-1-3-7-11) | storage settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-14) | legal drafting and execution authority (Legal & Corporate Communications)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Capacity needs from supply, trading, and network processes | market capacity availability and rates | quality-protection requirements"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Executed storage and throughput agreements handed to record and capture"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Storage and throughput capacity secured when needed, on terms that protect product and economics."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EI-HM32-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "precedes: Manage Storage Contract | precedes: Capture Storage Deals | informed-by: Supply Operations"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 20 (2026-09-20). Receiving row of the batch 09 three-way storage split, applied verbatim, with Hamid's execution correction: this process obtains commercial approvals and coordinates execution; Legal and authorized signatories execute. The batch 09 capture caveat retained: a captured storage deal exists only where capacity is commercially distinct. APQC 'Negotiate service-level agreements' is a WEAK IT candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-7-2-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "11741" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 11741"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-7-2 ;
    skos:definition "Operate the safety programs at company-managed terminal and fleet operations — hazardous-materials handling safety, staff safety training and procedures, and contractor safety qualification and control — executing the enterprise EHS framework at site level, so people and operations at company-managed facilities are protected and compliant."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.7.2.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Safety (Materials, Staff, Contractor)"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers line-management safety execution at company-managed sites: hazardous-materials handling and storage safety procedures for the products handled; staff safety training, competence, and procedure compliance; contractor safety qualification, induction, and work control; site safety inspections and observations; participating in site incident response, fact gathering, initial notification, and corrective-action execution, and providing required incident information to EHS for governed investigation, reportability assessment, and regulatory reporting. Where an operation is subject to applicable process-safety regulations or standards — such as OSHA PSM in the United States where regulated substances meet threshold conditions — the site executes applicable operating controls within the enterprise EHS/process-safety framework. Executes-within-framework: EHS & Government Reporting owns safety policy, the EHS management framework, investigation governance, reportability assessment, regulatory reporting, and filings under its locked rule; this process operates the programs at site level and feeds it. Excludes EHS policy, framework, investigation governance, reportability assessment, and regulatory reporting (EHS & Government Reporting, locked rule); separately established emergency-response ownership; third-party site safety (their operators); fleet transport-safety compliance administration (CM-1-2-7-2-2).
In scope: Operate hazardous-materials handling safety procedures | run staff safety training and procedure compliance | qualify, induct, and control contractors | participate in incident response and provide required incident information to EHS
Out of scope: EHS policy, investigation governance, reportability assessment, and regulatory reporting (EHS & Government Reporting, locked rule) | separately established emergency-response ownership | third-party site safety (their operators) | fleet transport-safety administration (CM-1-2-7-2-2)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Enterprise EHS framework and policies | site hazard and product data | contractor and staff records"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Operated site safety programs with incident information and evidence into the EHS framework"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Nobody hurt, nothing released, everything evidenced — at every company-managed site."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-OSHA-PSM-001 | SRC-EI-HM32-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Terminal Operations Management | enables: Manage Site Assets (Tank, Rack, Etc.) | informed-by: Manage Fleet (Own/Third Party)"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 20 (2026-09-20). Q3 decision with Hamid's incident split: the site executes response, gathers facts, notifies, and executes corrective actions; EHS governs investigation method, assesses reportability, and reports — execution versus accountability. Process-safety phrasing conditioned: applicable regimes (such as OSHA PSM where regulated substances meet threshold conditions) are executed within the enterprise EHS/process-safety framework; jurisdiction-specific. Executes-within-policy (batch 07/11/15 pattern). APQC 'Manage materials master lists' is a false lexical match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-7-2-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12944" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12944"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-7-2 ;
    skos:definition "Manage the capacity, readiness, safety, compliance, maintenance, and performance of company-operated and contracted road-delivery fleet resources so Secondary Distribution Management can assign safe, qualified, and available vehicles and carriers to approved delivery schedules."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.7.2.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Fleet (Own/Third Party)"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers fleet management for product distribution: fleet-capacity planning; vehicle standards, inspections, and maintenance-program governance; driver qualification and safety compliance; carrier qualification and carrier-performance management; transport-safety and hazardous-materials compliance programs (in the United States, the FMCSA motor-carrier safety regime under 49 CFR Parts 300-399 and the applicable PHMSA Hazardous Materials Regulations for cargo tanks; state and jurisdictional requirements additional); and fleet readiness. Where the company operates its own fleet, it provides the vehicles and drivers that execute scheduled deliveries; where third parties operate the fleet, it manages contractual qualification and performance. Route, load, and delivery-window scheduling and dispatch decisioning remain with Secondary Distribution Management (CM-1-1-3-8, batch 05). Excludes route, load, and delivery-window scheduling and dispatch decisioning (CM-1-1-3-8); customer order capture (CM-1-3-7); carrier contract negotiation where that belongs to Contract Management (CM-1-2-1-2-9); customer delivery terms (CM-1-2-7-1-2); marine, rail, and pipeline capacity (their owning processes); fleet financing and capital decisions (Finance, accountable owners); settlement (CM-1-2-4-1).
In scope: Plan fleet capacity across own and contracted resources | govern vehicle standards, inspections, and maintenance programs | administer driver qualification and transport-safety compliance | qualify and manage carriers and provide execution-ready fleet resources to scheduling
Out of scope: Route, load, and dispatch scheduling (CM-1-1-3-8) | order capture (CM-1-3-7) | carrier contract negotiation (CM-1-2-1-2-9) | customer delivery terms (CM-1-2-7-1-2) | marine, rail, and pipeline capacity (owning processes) | fleet financing and capital decisions (Finance, accountable owners)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Delivery demand and schedules (CM-1-1-3-8) | vehicle condition and compliance data | driver and carrier records | applicable transport regulations"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Execution-ready, compliant fleet capacity assigned through scheduling"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "A safe, compliant, available fleet behind every scheduled delivery — driving it where the company operates it."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-FMCSA-001 | SRC-EI-HM32-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Secondary Distribution Management | governed-by: Terminal Operations Management | informed-by: Manage Safety (Materials, Staff, Contractor)"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 20 (2026-09-20). Hamid's consistency correction applied: where the company operates its own fleet, this function's vehicles and drivers execute the scheduled deliveries — execution is not categorically excluded; what stays excluded is the scheduling and dispatch decision (CM-1-1-3-8, batch 05 split). US transport-safety context spans two regimes: FMCSA motor-carrier safety regulations and PHMSA hazardous-materials/cargo-tank rules — different agencies; state, hours-of-service, CDL, drug/alcohol, inspection, and insurance requirements apply additionally; jurisdiction-specific elsewhere. Dispatch execution ownership after scheduling is an operating-model follow-up carried in the batch 20 PR. APQC 'Manage third party bulk storage' scored STRONG at 3b but NO SOURCE — honored; storage candidate, not fleet."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-7-2-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "13930" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 13930"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-7-2 ;
    skos:definition "Manage the operational availability, condition, integrity coordination, and business-use allocation of company-managed marketing and distribution terminal assets — including tanks, racks, meters, additive systems, vapor-recovery equipment, and supporting infrastructure — within enterprise engineering, maintenance, and EHS standards."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.7.2.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Manage Site Assets (Tank, Rack, Etc.)"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers site asset management at company-managed terminals: asset registers and condition monitoring; coordinating inspection, maintenance, and integrity requirements with the accountable engineering and maintenance providers (in-service aboveground storage tank inspection follows API 653 practice where applicable; measurement equipment per the applicable API MPMS chapters); maintenance planning with operations; maintaining business-use configuration and availability information for racks, bays, tanks, and associated assets and coordinating operational allocation with the designated terminal and scheduling owners; and asset performance and availability management. The process coordinates inspection, maintenance, and integrity requirements with accountable engineering and maintenance providers; it does not itself establish engineering standards, approve technical integrity methodology, perform regulated inspection certification, own capital-project decisions, or define measurement methodology. Excludes engineering standards and integrity methodology (enterprise engineering function); regulated inspection certification (certified inspectors/providers); capital investment decisions and projects (enterprise capital process, accountable owners); Refining- and Midstream-operated assets (locked rules); measurement methodology (CM-1-1-3-7-4); site safety programs (CM-1-2-7-2-1).
In scope: Maintain asset registers and condition monitoring | coordinate inspection, maintenance, and integrity requirements with accountable engineering and maintenance providers | plan maintenance with operations | maintain business-use configuration and availability information and coordinate allocation with designated owners
Out of scope: Engineering standards and integrity methodology (enterprise engineering) | regulated inspection certification (certified providers) | capital decisions and projects (enterprise capital process) | Refining/Midstream assets (locked rules) | measurement methodology (CM-1-1-3-7-4) | site safety programs (CM-1-2-7-2-1)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Asset condition and inspection data | engineering and maintenance provider programs | operations requirements | product allocation needs"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Available, integrity-coordinated site assets with maintained business-use configuration"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every tank, rack, and meter at company-managed sites fit for service when operations needs it."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-API-653-001 | SRC-API-MPMS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Terminal Operations Management | informed-by: Manage Safety (Materials, Staff, Contractor) | enables: Supply Operations"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 20 (2026-09-20). Coordinates-integrity-and-availability (Hamid, batch 20): the process coordinates with accountable engineering and maintenance providers and maintains business-use configuration; standards, certification, methodology, and capital stay with their owners. API 653 anchors in-service aboveground storage tank inspection practice for API 650/12C-constructed tanks; applicability is tank- and jurisdiction-specific and the enterprise's engineering standards govern. Whether this row owns business-use allocation or only maintains availability data for a scheduling owner is an operating-model follow-up carried in the batch 20 PR. APQC 'Manage rack allocations' is a WEAK partial match, comparative only."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-1-1-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10195" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10195"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-1-1 ;
    skos:definition "Source and collect the market data the market-analysis cycle needs — government statistics, licensed market data, industry publications, and internal sales and channel data — qualifying sources, respecting licensing terms, and organizing collected data for analysis."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.1.1.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Source & Collect Information"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers market-data sourcing: identifying and qualifying sources for market size, demand, supply, price, and channel data; collecting under the applicable licensing and use terms; recording provenance and currency; and organizing data for the analysis step (CM-1-3-1-1-3). Data-vendor selection and licensing ownership follow the batch 12 follow-up owner; enterprise data governance frameworks apply where established. Excludes the analysis itself (CM-1-3-1-1-3); vendor licensing ownership (accountable owner per batch 12 follow-up); enterprise data governance (its owner).
In scope: Identify and qualify market data sources | collect under licensing and use terms | record provenance and currency | organize data for analysis
Out of scope: Analysis (CM-1-3-1-1-3) | vendor licensing ownership (batch 12 follow-up owner) | enterprise data governance (its owner)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Government statistics (EIA) | licensed vendor data | internal sales and channel records"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Qualified, organized market data with provenance"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The right market data, legitimately sourced, ready to analyze."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EIA-PMM-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Define Analysis Objectives & Scope | precedes: Analyze Data | governed-by: Market Analysis"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 21 (2026-09-20). Sourcing step of the shared analysis lifecycle, market instance. Licensed data used within its terms — the licensing owner question stands (batch 12). APQC candidate is a WEAK account-data match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-1-1-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19521" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19521"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-1-1 ;
    skos:definition "Define each market-analysis cycle's objectives, questions, scope, and method with the consuming stakeholders — which markets, products, channels, and periods, answering which decisions — so analysis effort lands on the questions strategy and planning actually need answered."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.1.1.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Define Analysis Objectives & Scope"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers analysis framing: agreeing the cycle's decision-supporting questions with the strategy, planning, and commercial consumers; setting market, product, channel, geography, and period scope; selecting methods and required data; and setting the cycle's deliverable and timing. The consumers own their decisions; this step owns the framing. Excludes the decisions the analysis supports (consumers); the analysis itself (CM-1-3-1-1-3); data collection (CM-1-3-1-1-1).
In scope: Agree decision-supporting questions with consumers | set market, product, channel, and period scope | select methods and required data | set deliverables and timing
Out of scope: The supported decisions (consumers) | analysis execution (CM-1-3-1-1-3) | data collection (CM-1-3-1-1-1)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Consumer decision needs (CM-1-3-2/3/5) | prior findings | available data inventory"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Agreed analysis objectives, scope, and method per cycle"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Analysis aimed at real decisions, not analysis for its own sake."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "precedes: Source & Collect Information | precedes: Analyze Data | precedes: Document Findings | governed-by: Market Analysis"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 21 (2026-09-20). Framing step of the shared lifecycle, market instance — the lifecycle's first step per the normalized order. APQC 'Define objectives' scored STRONG at 3b but NO SOURCE — honored; generic-step match, comparative only."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-1-1-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20962" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20962"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-1-1 ;
    skos:definition "Analyze the collected market data against the defined objectives — market sizing, demand and supply balance, channel and margin structure, trend and scenario analysis — producing evidenced analytical results with their assumptions and confidence stated."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.1.1.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Analyze Data"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers market-analysis execution: applying the defined methods to the qualified data; sizing markets and segments; analyzing demand-supply balances, channel structures, pricing environments, and margin pools; testing trends and scenarios; and stating assumptions, limitations, and confidence with the results. Results are analytical inputs to decisions, not decisions; forecast production stays with CM-1-1-1-1. Excludes decision-making (consumers); demand forecast production (CM-1-1-1-1); documentation and distribution (CM-1-3-1-1-4).
In scope: Apply defined methods to qualified data | size markets and analyze demand-supply, channels, and margins | test trends and scenarios | state assumptions, limitations, and confidence
Out of scope: Decisions (consumers) | forecast production (CM-1-1-1-1) | documentation (CM-1-3-1-1-4)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Qualified data (CM-1-3-1-1-1) | objectives and methods (CM-1-3-1-1-2)"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Evidenced market-analysis results"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Evidenced answers to the framed questions, with honesty about confidence."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EIA-PMM-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Source & Collect Information | precedes: Document Findings"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 21 (2026-09-20). Analysis step of the shared lifecycle, market instance. APQC 'Analyze data' scored STRONG at 3b but NO SOURCE — honored; generic-step match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-1-1-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "14095" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 14095"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-1-1 ;
    skos:definition "Document and distribute the market-analysis findings — governed findings records with their evidence, assumptions, and confidence — to the consuming strategy, planning, and insight processes, maintaining the findings library so analysis is reusable and auditable."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.1.1.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Document Findings"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers findings documentation: producing the governed findings record with evidence, assumptions, limitations, and confidence; distributing to the agreed consumers and Insight and Metrics (CM-1-3-1-6); and maintaining the findings library with versioning and currency. Findings records preserve the evidence lineage, source-use restrictions, assumptions, analytical method, confidence/uncertainty, and applicable commercial, privacy, and competition-sensitivity classification. Excludes the analysis itself (CM-1-3-1-1-3); the consumers' decisions; enterprise records-retention policy (governance owner).
In scope: Produce governed findings records with evidence and confidence | distribute to agreed consumers and Insight and Metrics | maintain the versioned findings library
Out of scope: Analysis (CM-1-3-1-1-3) | consumer decisions | records-retention policy (governance owner)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Analysis results (CM-1-3-1-1-3) | distribution agreements with consumers"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Governed, distributed market findings"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Findings that outlive the meeting — governed, evidenced, findable."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Analyze Data | enables: Insight and Metrics"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 21 (2026-09-20). Documentation step of the shared lifecycle, market instance; the shared evidence-lineage statement (review) applies. APQC 'Document trade' is a false lexical match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-1-2-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10195" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10195"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Source and collect the data the company-analysis cycle needs — governed internal financial, volume, share, and capability data, and the external benchmarks to compare against — respecting the data owners' controls and organizing the data for analysis."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.1.2.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Source & Collect Information"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers company-analysis sourcing: internal performance, volume, margin, portfolio, and capability data from their accountable owners; external share and benchmark data under licensing terms; provenance and currency recording; organization for analysis (CM-1-3-1-2-3). Excludes producing the internal data (its owners); the analysis (CM-1-3-1-2-3); vendor licensing ownership (batch 12 follow-up owner).
In scope: Collect governed internal performance and capability data | collect external benchmarks under licensing terms | record provenance and organize for analysis
Out of scope: Internal data production (accountable owners) | analysis (CM-1-3-1-2-3) | licensing ownership (batch 12 follow-up owner)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Governed internal data (CM-1-2-4-2, operations) | licensed benchmarks"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Qualified, organized company and benchmark data"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The company's own numbers and fair benchmarks, ready to analyze."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Define Analysis Objectives & Scope | precedes: Analyze Data | governed-by: Company Analysis"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 21 (2026-09-20). Shared lifecycle step, company instance — consumes governed data, never produces it. APQC candidate is a WEAK account-data match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-1-2-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19521" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19521"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Define each company-analysis cycle's objectives, questions, scope, and method with the consuming stakeholders — which businesses, markets, capabilities, and periods, answering which strategic questions — so self-analysis lands where strategy needs it."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.1.2.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Define Analysis Objectives & Scope"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers framing for company analysis: agreeing decision-supporting questions with strategy and planning consumers; setting business, market, capability, and period scope; selecting methods and benchmarks; setting deliverables and timing. Excludes the supported decisions (consumers); analysis execution (CM-1-3-1-2-3); data collection (CM-1-3-1-2-1).
In scope: Agree strategic questions with consumers | set business, market, capability, and period scope | select methods and benchmarks | set deliverables and timing
Out of scope: Supported decisions (consumers) | analysis (CM-1-3-1-2-3) | collection (CM-1-3-1-2-1)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Consumer decision needs | prior findings | data inventory"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Agreed objectives, scope, and method per cycle"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Self-analysis aimed at strategy's real questions."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "precedes: Source & Collect Information | precedes: Analyze Data | precedes: Document Findings | governed-by: Company Analysis"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 21 (2026-09-20). Shared lifecycle step, company instance — first step per the normalized order. APQC 'Define objectives' STRONG/NO SOURCE — honored."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-1-2-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20962" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20962"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Analyze the company's position against the defined objectives — share and share-trend analysis, portfolio and asset-position assessment, capability strengths and gaps, and performance versus market and benchmarks — producing evidenced results with assumptions and confidence stated."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.1.2.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Analyze Data"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers company-analysis execution: share and trend computation; portfolio and asset-position assessment; capability strength/gap analysis against market requirements; relative-performance analysis against benchmarks; assumptions, limitations, and confidence stated, including category definitions, data coverage, and comparability assumptions behind share and performance conclusions. Excludes decisions (consumers); financial result production (CM-1-2-4-2, Finance); documentation (CM-1-3-1-2-4).
In scope: Compute share and trends | assess portfolio and asset position | analyze capability strengths and gaps | benchmark relative performance with stated confidence
Out of scope: Decisions (consumers) | financial production (CM-1-2-4-2) | documentation (CM-1-3-1-2-4)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Qualified data (CM-1-3-1-2-1) | objectives (CM-1-3-1-2-2)"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Evidenced company-position results"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "An honest, evidenced answer to how the company is really positioned."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Source & Collect Information | precedes: Document Findings"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 21 (2026-09-20). Shared lifecycle step, company instance; comparability assumptions stated with results (review qualifier). APQC 'Analyze data' STRONG/NO SOURCE — honored."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-1-2-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "14095" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 14095"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Document and distribute the company-analysis findings — governed records with evidence, assumptions, and confidence — to strategy, planning, and Insight and Metrics, maintaining the findings library."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.1.2.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Document Findings"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers findings documentation for company analysis: governed records with evidence and confidence; distribution to agreed consumers and CM-1-3-1-6; versioned library maintenance. Sensitive internal assessments distribute per their classification. Findings records preserve the evidence lineage, source-use restrictions, assumptions, analytical method, confidence/uncertainty, and applicable commercial, privacy, and competition-sensitivity classification. Excludes the analysis (CM-1-3-1-2-3); consumer decisions; records-retention policy (governance owner).
In scope: Produce governed findings records | distribute per classification to agreed consumers | maintain the versioned library
Out of scope: Analysis (CM-1-3-1-2-3) | decisions (consumers) | retention policy (governance owner)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Analysis results (CM-1-3-1-2-3) | classification rules"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Governed, distributed company findings"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Self-knowledge that is governed, findable, and appropriately protected."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Analyze Data | enables: Insight and Metrics"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 21 (2026-09-20). Shared lifecycle step, company instance; internal-assessment classification respected; shared evidence-lineage statement applies. APQC 'Document trade' is a false match."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-1-3-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10195" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10195"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "Source and collect the consumer data the analysis cycle needs — research studies, panels, transaction and loyalty data, and demographic and market data — within consent, privacy, and licensing constraints, and organize it for analysis."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.1.3.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Source & Collect Information"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers consumer-data sourcing: research commissioning inputs, panel and study data, transaction and loyalty data used within its consent basis, demographic and market data; provenance, consent-basis, and currency recording; organization for analysis (CM-1-3-1-3-3). Sourcing operates under the shared personal-data governance statement recorded at CM-1-3-1-3: approved purposes, lawful basis, notice/consent status, retention, access, and minimization controls set by the accountable Data Governance, Privacy, Legal, and Security owners. Excludes privacy framework ownership (governance owners); the analysis (CM-1-3-1-3-3); loyalty program data operations (CM-1-3-6-6).
In scope: Collect research, panel, transaction, and demographic data within consent and licensing | record provenance and consent basis | organize for analysis
Out of scope: Privacy framework ownership (governance owners) | analysis (CM-1-3-1-3-3) | loyalty data operations (CM-1-3-6-6)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Research and panel data | consented transaction/loyalty data | demographic data"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Qualified consumer data with provenance and consent basis"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Consumer data that is useful and lawful, ready to analyze."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Define Analysis Objectives & Scope | precedes: Analyze Data | governed-by: Consumer Analysis"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 21 (2026-09-20). Shared lifecycle step, consumer instance — consent basis recorded with the data; the cluster's shared personal-data governance statement governs sourcing. APQC candidate is a WEAK account-data match."@en .

