proc:L1-ehs-gov-reporting a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10522" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10522"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:L0-enabling-functions ;
    skos:definition "Protect people, assets, and the environment through process safety; occupational health and safety; environmental management and compliance; emergency preparedness and response; incident, release, and near-miss management; corrective-action learning; and required regulatory reporting to government authorities."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:prefLabel "EHS & Gov Reporting"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "This domain governs EHS management, environmental and safety compliance, required regulator-facing reporting, and the enterprise coordination of incidents, releases, investigations, corrective actions, and emergency response. It does not replace operational ownership: operating sites, asset owners, maintenance, logistics, product stewardship, and business functions remain accountable for safely executing their work, preventing and containing events, and completing assigned corrective actions. Legal & Corporate Communications advises on legal privilege, enforcement exposure, disclosure, litigation hold, regulator strategy, and approved internal/external communications when those dimensions arise."@en .

proc:L1-finance a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10922" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10922"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:L0-enabling-functions ;
    skos:definition "Planning, recording, controlling, reporting, and managing the financial resources, performance, obligations, and risks of the downstream business, including financial planning and analysis, accounting, reporting, payroll, treasury, tax, and internal financial controls."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:prefLabel "Finance"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Includes budgeting, forecasting, financial and management accounting, period close, statutory and management reporting, accounts payable, expense reimbursement, payroll, treasury, liquidity and cash management, tax, financial compliance, internal controls, audit support, and financial risk management. Supports commercial, supply-chain, refining, midstream, distribution, and enabling-function processes through financial governance, measurement, and transaction processing. Excludes operational execution in those business domains and fixed-asset project accounting where it is intentionally governed and executed as an ERP-native capability outside this taxonomy's modeled scope."@en .

proc:L1-human-resources a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20958" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20958"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:L0-enabling-functions ;
    skos:definition "The function that plans, attracts, develops, rewards, engages, retains, and transitions the workforce to enable the downstream business, including workforce planning, talent acquisition, onboarding, learning and development, performance management, compensation and benefits, employee and labor relations, HR policy and compliance, workforce information, and HR service delivery."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:prefLabel "Human Resources"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Includes workforce strategy and planning; organizational and position management; recruiting, sourcing, selection, hiring, and onboarding; learning, development, performance, talent, succession, and career management; compensation, benefits, recognition, and payroll-input administration; employee and labor relations; workforce policy, employment compliance, ethics-related workforce processes, and employee communications; workforce data, HR information systems, people analytics, and HR service delivery; and employee transition, redeployment, separation, and retirement. Human Resources owns workforce policies, people processes, employee experience, and workforce information. It provides authorized employee, time, attendance, compensation, and deduction inputs to payroll; Finance or a designated payroll service owns pay calculation, payment execution, withholding/remittance, payroll accounting, and financial control."@en .

proc:L1-legal-corp-comm a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "18948" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 18948"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:L0-enabling-functions ;
    skos:definition "Provide legal counsel and legal-risk management, including contract and transaction support, litigation and dispute resolution, regulatory and compliance advice, corporate governance and entity support, and management of external legal providers; develop and deliver internal corporate communications and approved enterprise messaging."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:prefLabel "Legal & Corp Comm"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "In scope: general counsel and legal advice to management and functions; legal review and support for contracts, transactions, claims, disputes, investigations, and litigation; regulatory interpretation and legal/compliance advice; corporate governance, entity management, board/committee administration where retained in the function, and corporate records; intellectual-property and outside-counsel management where not separate domains; employee, leadership, change, policy, crisis, and enterprise-wide internal communications; approval, stewardship, and consistency of official corporate messages. Out of scope (Step 8 decision): government relations, lobbying, and industry-association engagement; investor relations, shareholder engagement, and external board relations; public relations, media relations, community relations, philanthropy, and external reputation campaigns; distributor incentives, trade discounts, channel allowances, and other commercial-program design."@en .

proc:L1-midstream a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19948" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19948"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:L0-downstream-operations ;
    skos:definition "The transportation, terminaling, storage, and bulk handling of crude oil, intermediates, and refined products between supply points, refineries, terminals, and downstream distribution locations."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:prefLabel "Midstream"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Includes receipt, dispatch, transfer, pipeline, marine, rail, and bulk-truck logistics; terminal and tank-farm operations; inventory management; blending and additive operations where performed outside refinery process units; product segregation; custody transfer; nominations; scheduling; and bulk delivery to downstream depots, customers, or other facilities."@en .

proc:L1-process-excellence-it a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20094" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20094"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:L0-enabling-functions ;
    skos:definition "The enabling function that drives operational excellence through process governance, standardization, performance management, continuous improvement, and transformation; and enables the enterprise through technology strategy, architecture, platforms, infrastructure, cybersecurity, data and integration, and digital capabilities."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:prefLabel "Process Excellence & IT"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Includes enterprise process architecture and governance; process ownership, documentation, standardization, measurement, benchmarking, and maturity assessment; continuous improvement, lean/six-sigma methods, automation, and business transformation; enterprise and solution architecture; portfolio, product, project, and demand management; application, data, integration, cloud, infrastructure, network, end-user, and IT service management; cybersecurity, technology risk, resilience, and disaster recovery; and delivery of digital, analytics, AI, and automation capabilities. Enables and governs cross-functional ways of working and enterprise technology. It does not own the business outcomes or physical execution of refining, midstream, distribution, commercial, finance, HR, legal, or EHS processes, except where a specifically delegated enterprise service is modeled as such."@en .

proc:L1-refining a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19948" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19948"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:L0-downstream-operations ;
    skos:definition "The operation of petroleum refineries to convert crude oil and other hydrocarbon feedstocks into specification-compliant fuels, blendstocks, lubricants, asphalt, petrochemical feedstocks, and other refined products through separation, conversion, treatment, blending, and associated refinery operations."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:prefLabel "Refining"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Encompasses refinery production operations and the planning, execution, and recovery activities required to maintain refinery assets, reliability, integrity, safety, and operating availability, including major maintenance and turnarounds."@en .

proc:L1-shared-services a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19951" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19951"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:L0-enabling-functions ;
    skos:definition "An enterprise service-delivery model that consolidates common, repeatable business-support services for multiple business units, legal entities, or regions through a shared organization, standardized processes, common platforms, and defined service levels."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:prefLabel "Shared Services"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Delivers selected operational and transactional services on behalf of enterprise functions and business domains, including finance operations, HR operations, payroll administration, procurement operations, IT service delivery, master-data administration, customer and supplier support, and other standardized administrative services. Services may be delivered centrally, regionally, or virtually and are governed through service catalogs, service levels, performance measures, and, where applicable, chargeback or cost-allocation arrangements."@en .

proc:L1-supply-chain-mgmt a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10215" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10215"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:L0-enabling-functions ;
    skos:definition "The integrated planning, coordination, and optimization of feedstock, intermediate, product, inventory, capacity, logistics, and information flows across the downstream petroleum value chain — from supply and sourcing through refining, terminaling, distribution, and customer fulfilment."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:prefLabel "Supply Chain Mgmt."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Includes demand and supply balancing; sourcing and replenishment planning; production and refinery supply planning; inventory and working-capital optimization; network, terminal, and distribution planning; transportation and delivery planning; materials management; supplier and carrier coordination; performance management; and supply-chain risk and disruption management. It orchestrates, but does not replace, the physical execution performed by refining, midstream, distribution, procurement, and commercial operations."@en .

src:eia-glossary a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    dcterms:publisher "U.S. Energy Information Administration"@en ;
    dcterms:rights "U.S. federal government work; public domain."@en ;
    dcterms:title "U.S. Energy Information Administration (EIA) Glossary"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-1 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1 ;
    skos:definition "Demand forecasting processes for commercial planning across the value chain."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Demand Forecasting"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-2-2-2 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-2-2 ;
    skos:definition "Gather committed deals, spot opportunities, and export opportunities for Clean Products in Planning and Scheduling UI"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.2.2.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Gather Automated Data Inputs"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-6-1 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-6 ;
    skos:definition "Update data inputs and obtain model to run distribution backcast"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.6.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Set up and stabilize base case for Distribution Backcast"@en .

proc:CM-1-2-3 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Actualizations is performed by scheduler who assigns the final ticket volumes to the trades according to the preset priority."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Actualizations"@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-8 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6 ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.8" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Compliance Management"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-2-2-1 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-2-2 ;
    skos:definition "Review draft refinery and network premise to align on period definition and updates to regional premise"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.2.2.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Conduct Regional Premise Review"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-2-2-5 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-2-2 ;
    skos:definition "Check data for inconsistencies or gaps"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.2.2.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perform data validation checks"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-2-2-6 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-2-2 ;
    skos:definition "Update data to address data inconsistencies"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.2.2.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Update data inputs to address data inconsistencies and gaps"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-2-4 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Base Regional Plan is key input for XPIMS <> DPO Reconciliation"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.2.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Develop Base Regional Plan"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-2-5-6 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-2-5 ;
    skos:definition "Make updates to models inputs based on outcomes of XPIMS <> DPO Reconciliation"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.2.5.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Update Model Constraints based on Outcomes of XPIMS <> DPO Reconciliation"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-2-9 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Handover Regional Plan for execution"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.2.9" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Handover Regional Plan"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-2 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "Base Distribution Plan is reconciled with Base Regional Plan in XPIMS <> DPO Reconciliation"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Develop Base Distribution Plan"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-3 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "Check and Update (as needed) Demand, Pricing, Crude, Premise, and Inventory Levels for Local Level for month M"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Develop Final Distribution Plan"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-4-2 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-4 ;
    skos:definition "Receive Handover of M+1 Month Plan from Planning to Business Including Refinery Level Demand Targets from Regional Plan Output"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.4.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Receive RO Targets"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-5-1 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-5 ;
    skos:definition "Receive actuals and forecast data for backcast runs"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.5.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Generate Inputs for Regional Backcast"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-5-4 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-5 ;
    skos:definition "Evaluate impact of LP price adjusted case run"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.5.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Evaluate the LP price adjusted forecast against the original forecast"@en .

proc:CM-1-2-5-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12960" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12960"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Feedstock Supply Position Management"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-5 ;
    skos:definition "The capability to keep the feedstock supply position sound — monitoring and controlling feedstock inventory positions against operating requirements, and assessing and managing the price, supply, and working-capital risk those positions carry — so the refinery is supplied without avoidable risk or trapped capital."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.5.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Crude/Feed Supply Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the feedstock supply-position capability: feedstock inventory position monitoring and control (CM-1-2-5-4-2, the feedstock counterpart of the batch 04 finished-products row under its reciprocal constraint) and feedstock inventory risk assessment and management (CM-1-2-5-4-1, the receiving row of the batch 11 Credit Risk exclusion). Inventory policy — including crude and feedstock policy — is owned by the batch 04 policy rows (CM-1-1-3-7-1); reconciliation by CM-1-1-3-7-5; refinery tank operations by Refining. Excludes inventory policy design (CM-1-1-3-7-1); book-to-physical reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5); tank operations (Refining); trading-book market risk measurement (CM-1-2-2-3, batch 12); counterparty credit risk (CM-1-2-2-1).
In scope: Monitor and control feedstock inventory positions | assess and manage feedstock inventory risk | keep supply-position information current for planning and trading
Out of scope: Inventory policy (CM-1-1-3-7-1) | reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5) | tank operations (Refining) | trading-book risk measurement (CM-1-2-2-3) | counterparty credit (CM-1-2-2-1)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "capability"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Feedstock positions, movements, and commitments | market prices | supply plan and demand (CM-1-2-5-2)"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Controlled feedstock positions with assessed, managed risk"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "A supplied refinery, a controlled position, a bounded risk."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EI-HM32-001 | SRC-ORP-DPSC-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Develop Feedstock Demand | informed-by: Crude/Feed Transportation Management | assures: Crude/Feed Demand Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: capability per the batch 11/12/15 test — two concurrent control functions, mirroring the batch 04 Inventory capability precedent. Receiving end of two bindings: the batch 04 reciprocal (finished products/intermediates there, feedstock here) and the batch 11 inventory-risk exclusion. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-7 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Product distribution management involves managing product and transportation agreements, terminal operations."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Products Distribution Management"@en .

proc:CM-1-3-2-4 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10103" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10103"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-2 ;
    skos:definition "Develop and maintain the approved strategic channel-of-trade direction for downstream sales and distribution — which routes to market the company participates in, their strategic roles, and the target channel mix — through a recurring cycle of channel diagnostics and hypothesis-driven strategy development under the applicable enterprise approvals, so channel design and channel management execute a deliberate, evidenced, approved direction."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.2.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sales & Distribution Channel Strategy"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the channel-strategy lifecycle: conducting channel-of-trade diagnostics (CM-1-3-2-4-1) and establishing channel operating objectives and the channel-strategy hypothesis (CM-1-3-2-4-2), with the approved strategy developed and maintained at this L4. Channels of trade span company-operated retail, branded dealer, branded marketer/jobber and distributor, unbranded rack and wholesale, and direct commercial channels. These are illustrative external channel-of-trade labels. The enterprise's approved commercial/channel taxonomy governs internal classification. EIA's DTW/rack/retail categories are external statistical context only, per the SRC-EIA-PMM-001 limitations. Marketing Strategy develops and maintains approved brand, value-proposition, channel, and customer-experience direction using governed evidence from Marketing Analysis. It does not perform the analyses; design detailed offers, prices, channels, networks, communications, or operating models; plan campaigns and sales; or execute channel, sales, brand, loyalty, or service programs. Material strategic changes follow the applicable enterprise delegation, investment, legal, and governance approvals. Where an arrangement meets the applicable statutory and contractual criteria for a branded motor-fuel franchise, changes to trademark, supply, lease, renewal, termination, or non-renewal arrangements may be constrained by the PMPA and applicable state law. Legal classification, interpretation, notice requirements, termination/non-renewal analysis, and franchise documentation remain the responsibility of Legal & Corporate Communications. Strategy may set the strategic role of price in the proposition or channel — such as value, premium, parity, transparency, index linkage, or loyalty emphasis — but does not establish pricing strategy, price architecture, price rules, price levels, discounts, formula terms, or transaction prices. Those activities belong to Pricing Strategy Management and Sales Pricing. Strategy only: detailed channel design at CM-1-3-3-3, retail network design at CM-1-3-3-4, partner management at CM-1-3-6-1, sales strategy and planning at CM-1-3-5-1. Excludes channel design (CM-1-3-3-3); retail network design (CM-1-3-3-4); channel/partner management (CM-1-3-6-1); sales planning (CM-1-3-5-1); secondary distribution operations (CM-1-1-3-8, SCM-vs-Commercial locked rule).
In scope: Run the recurring channel-strategy cycle | develop and maintain approved channel-of-trade participation, roles, and target mix | recommend and maintain approved direction for channel investment, expansion, de-emphasis, or exit, subject to delegated investment, legal, franchise, and corporate approvals | issue channel direction to design, planning, and management
Out of scope: Channel design (CM-1-3-3-3) | retail network design (CM-1-3-3-4) | channel/partner management (CM-1-3-6-1) | sales planning (CM-1-3-5-1) | secondary distribution operations (CM-1-1-3-8, locked rule) | pricing strategy, rules, and levels (CM-1-3-3-2, CM-1-3-6-3)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Channel diagnostics (CM-1-3-2-4-1) | customer/marketer findings (CM-1-3-1-4) | cost-to-serve assessments (CM-1-2-6-1-3) | value propositions (CM-1-3-2-2/-3)"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The approved channel-of-trade strategy and target mix"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every route to market chosen on purpose, with evidence and the right approvals."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "strategic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EIA-PMM-001 | SRC-PMPA-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Insight and Metrics | enables: Sales & Distribution Channel Design | enables: Channel & Partner Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 22 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one diagnostics-to-strategy lifecycle. Lifecycle order: Conduct Channel of trade diagnostics → Establish operating Objectives and develop Channel Strategy Hypothesis → approved strategy developed and maintained at this L4. Channel-of-trade vocabulary is illustrative (review caveat verbatim in scope note); enterprise taxonomy governs. Investment-authority wording per review: recommend and maintain approved direction, subject to delegated approvals — material for retail-network acquisitions, divestitures, dealer conversions, and branded/unbranded transitions. PMPA applicability fact-dependent (verbatim in scope note). Batch 22 durable rule and price-role boundary recorded verbatim in scope note. APQC 'Develop sales strategy' is a WEAK adjacent match (sales planning is CM-1-3-5-1)."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-3 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6 ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sales Pricing Management"@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-7 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6 ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Brand Standard Management"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "16820" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 16820"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Network Product Scheduling"@en,
        "Short-Term Product Supply Scheduling"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "Develop and maintain the short-term product supply schedule for the primary distribution network — deciding which products move between refineries, terminals, and supply points, in what quantities and sequence over the coming days and weeks — so the approved regional plan is translated into an executable movement program."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Product Scheduling"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers short-term network scheduling within the approved regional plan: consolidating demand, stock, and supply projections into a rolling network schedule, sequencing bulk product movements, coordinating the trades needed to balance the schedule, and adjusting to disruptions. Excludes plan-period supply and disposition decisions, owned by Regional Optimization (CM-1-1-2); excludes mode-level nomination, load, and movement administration, owned by Physical Distribution Scheduling (CM-1-1-3-6); excludes secondary deliveries, owned by Secondary Distribution Management (CM-1-1-3-8); excludes trade negotiation and execution, owned by Supply And Trading.
In scope: Consolidate short-term demand, stock, and supply projections | develop and maintain the rolling network product schedule | sequence and adjust bulk movements within plan and policy | initiate schedule-balancing trade requests
Out of scope: Plan-period supply and disposition optimization (Regional Optimization, CM-1-1-2) | nomination, load, and movement administration (CM-1-1-3-6) | secondary delivery scheduling (CM-1-1-3-8) | trade negotiation and execution (Supply And Trading)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "approved regional plan | stock positions and projections | short-term demand and lifting forecasts | transport and terminal availability"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "rolling short-term network product schedule"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Decide the short-term product movement schedule the primary network runs on."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "weekly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-ORP-DPSC-001 | SRC-LNL-T2S-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Regional Optimization | precedes: Physical Distribution Scheduling | uses-input: Manage Inventory"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 05 (2026-09-19). Sits between the approved monthly regional plan (CM-1-1-2) and movement administration (CM-1-1-3-6): this L4 decides the short-term schedule; its sibling administers the movements it creates. APQC 16820 'Design for product servicing' is an unrelated lexical match; no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-4-4 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-4 ;
    skos:definition "Evaluate aligned scenarios for prompt month horizon"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.4.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Perform Prompt Month Evaluations"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-4-6 a skos:Concept ;
    owl:deprecated true ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-4 ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.4.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Commercial Development"@en ;
    intake:status "retired"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Retired per PTC-001 (step3c-parked-tree-changes.md), decided by Hamid 2026-09-18. The node carries no local meaning: its Step 3b provenance is a WEAK match against the APQC element “Manage employee development”, and under Option A all three of its children leave. Nothing remains to define, so it is retired rather than defined or left pending. The node itself is still present in downstream_process_map.json — removing it is a Step 3d tree change, not a workbook change. Retired here means “will not be defined”, not “gone from the tree”."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-5-9 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-5 ;
    skos:definition "Review notes and actions from key meetings and finalize backcasting report"@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.5.9" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Distribute final report with agreed upon action plans"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-6 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "17042" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 17042"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1 ;
    skos:definition "Reconcile actual distribution inventory availability, movements, deliveries, transportation costs, and customer fulfillment outcomes against the approved distribution plan and its contemporaneous assumptions and constraints; quantify and explain variances; and use validated lessons to improve subsequent distribution planning and optimization cycles."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Distribution Backcasting"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Distribution Backcasting preserves and compares the approved distribution baseline, actual inventory availability and deployment, shipments, deliveries, transportation performance and cost, service outcomes, material constraints, decisions, and variance explanations for a completed period. It identifies commercially meaningful drivers — such as demand, supply availability, allocation, inventory, route or mode, freight, carrier capacity, terminal/rack availability, delivery reliability, and customer-service effects — and feeds validated findings into subsequent distribution planning. It does not dispatch equipment, operate terminals or transportation assets, conduct product movements, perform delivery, or replace Finance's freight-payment and financial-close processes."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-7 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1 ;
    skos:definition "Refinery scheduling covering daily execution planning, production scheduling, and energy & utility management."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Refinery Scheduling"@en .

proc:CM-1-1-7-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19243" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19243"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Daily Refinery Scheduling"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-7 ;
    skos:definition "Translate the current production schedule into each day's refinery execution basis — updating the day's scheduling instructions and commitments, confirming the schedule-level resources the day depends on, and closing the daily production accounting balance — so the refinery works each day from a current, feasible, accounted-for scheduling basis."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.7.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Daily Execution Planning"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the daily schedule-to-execution cycle: issuing and updating the day's schedule-level instructions and commitments, assigning the schedule-level resources the day's plan assumes, and closing the daily production balance with actualized yields. Under the approved planning-scheduling-operations hierarchy, Refining owns unit-operation scheduling, tank and transfer line-ups, blend and process execution, and safe operational departures from the schedule; this process owns the daily schedule basis and its accounting. Excludes building the dated schedule itself, owned by Production Scheduling (CM-1-1-7-2); excludes the monthly plan (Refinery Planning, CM-1-1-4).
In scope: Issue and update daily schedule-level instructions and commitments | assign schedule-level resources for the day | close the daily production accounting balance | confirm daily feasibility of the issued basis
Out of scope: Unit-operation scheduling, line-ups, and execution (Refining) | dated schedule construction (CM-1-1-7-2) | monthly plan targets (CM-1-1-4)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "current production schedule | execution feedback and measured actuals | resource availability"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "issued daily scheduling basis with closed production balance"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Give the refinery a current, feasible, accounted-for daily scheduling basis."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "daily"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-BJCE-REFPS-001 | SRC-SNF-REFPS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Production Scheduling | enables: Regional Backcasting"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 06 (2026-09-19). Q1 decision (Hamid, 2026-09-19): the refinery planning-scheduling-operations hierarchy is approved. CM-1-1-7 owns the refinery production-scheduling layer: translating approved refinery plans into dated, feasible campaign, production, movement, and resource schedules; managing schedule exceptions and cross-functional interfaces; and maintaining schedule-level performance visibility. Refining owns detailed unit-operation scheduling and all operational execution, including process-control decisions, line-ups, transfers, blend execution, utility operation, and immediate safety/reliability departures from the schedule. This L4 is the daily rhythm of that scheduling layer. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-7-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20682" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20682"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Energy & Utilities Optimization"@en,
        "Refinery Energy Economics"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-7 ;
    skos:definition "Optimize energy, utility, and emissions-allowance economics within refinery planning and scheduling by setting sourcing strategies, economic targets, and allowance-position recommendations that inform the production schedule."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.7.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Energy & Utility Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers planning- and scheduling-level energy economics: evaluating internal generation versus purchased-energy options; incorporating energy, utility, and carbon costs into refinery planning and production scheduling; establishing economic operating targets and constraints for schedule development; maintaining an emissions-allowance and credit position; and preparing recommendations for allowance banking, purchase, or sale. Refining operates and controls utility systems, including boilers, cogeneration, steam, fuel-gas, power-distribution, and energy-recovery equipment. EHS & Government Reporting owns regulatory emissions monitoring, calculation, submission, and reporting under its locked rule. The relevant commercial or trading function owns energy contracting and allowance-trade negotiation and execution.
In scope: Evaluate internal generation versus purchased-energy options | incorporate energy, utility, and carbon costs into planning and scheduling | establish economic operating targets and constraints for schedule development | maintain the emissions-allowance and credit position and prepare banking, purchase, and sale recommendations
Out of scope: Utility system operation and control (Refining) | regulatory emissions monitoring and reporting (EHS & Government Reporting) | energy contracting and allowance-trade execution (commercial and trading owner) | financial accounting treatment of allowances and credits (Finance)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "energy prices and contract terms | utility system capabilities and constraints | production schedule and emissions position"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "energy sourcing strategy, economic targets, and allowance-position recommendations"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Minimize energy cost and carbon exposure from the planning side, within reliable operations."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "monthly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EI-HM31-001 | SRC-EPA-EMTR-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Production Scheduling | enables: Daily Execution Planning"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 06 (2026-09-19). Q2 decision (Hamid, Option A, narrowed): defined explicitly as the economic and scheduling layer of energy and utilities — the bare label could suggest ownership of utility systems, which belong to Refining. EHS reports (locked rule); the trading function executes; Finance records the accounting treatment of allowances and credits. Whether energy contracting is centralized or delegated to site commercial teams is a follow-up noted in the batch 06 PR. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Confirmations and Risk management consists of processes performed in the middle office to minimize risk of losses in the trading book due to change in market conditions i.e. commodity prices, interest rates, credit spreads, foreign-exchange rates. Middle office is responsible for managing confirmations, scenario modelling and price forecasting."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Confirmations & Risk Management"@en .

proc:CM-1-2-5-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10222" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10222"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Feedstock Demand Planning"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-5 ;
    skos:definition "Translate the refinery production plan into feedstock supply action — developing the feedstock demand signal, recommending the economically attractive feedstock slate and run rate to Refinery Planning, and coordinating the resulting requirements with the trading processes — so feedstock supply is planned against a validated demand and an economically informed recommendation."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.5.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Crude/Feed Demand Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the feedstock demand cycle: developing projected feedstock requirements by grade, quality, quantity, and timing from the production plan (CM-1-2-5-2-2); recommending the economically attractive feedstock slate and run rate to Refinery Planning under PTC-002 (CM-1-2-5-2-3); and coordinating the resulting purchase, sale, and exchange requirements with the trading processes (CM-1-2-5-2-1). Under PTC-002 (Option 1, closed): Refinery Planning decides the binding slate and run rate; this cluster develops demand, recommends, and coordinates — it decides none of the plan. Trading decides the deal (batch 05 decide-versus-advise pattern). Excludes the binding production plan, slate, and run rate (CM-1-1-4, PTC-002); trade negotiation and execution (CM-1-2-1-3); crude evaluation (CM-1-1-4-7-2); procurement slate development (CM-1-2-5-1-3/-4).
In scope: Develop the feedstock demand signal from the production plan | recommend the economically attractive slate and run rate to Refinery Planning | coordinate resulting requirements with trading | keep demand, recommendation, and coordination consistent
Out of scope: Binding slate, run rate, and production plan (CM-1-1-4, PTC-002) | trade execution (CM-1-2-1-3) | crude evaluation (CM-1-1-4-7-2) | procurement slates (CM-1-2-5-1-3/-4)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Refinery production plan (CM-1-1-4) | procurement slates (CM-1-2-5-1-3/-4) | market conditions"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Feedstock demand, slate/run-rate recommendation, and coordinated trading requirements"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep feedstock supply planned against a validated demand and an economically informed, advisory recommendation."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "monthly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CMU-REFPLAN-001 | SRC-ORP-DPSC-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Refinery Planning | enables: Trading Management | uses-input: Crude/Feed Quality Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one demand-to-coordination planning cycle (develop, recommend, coordinate), like the batch 03 Demand Forecasting precedent. PTC-002 honored at cluster level: this cluster advises and coordinates; Refinery Planning decides. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-5-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10269" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10269"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Feedstock Logistics Planning"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-5 ;
    skos:definition "Plan and manage the transportation of purchased crude and feedstocks to the refinery — coordinating movements across pipeline, marine, rail, and truck modes against the supply plan, scheduling deliveries into agreed refinery receipt windows, and planning arrivals with the refinery and terminal operations owners — so purchased feedstock arrives when, where, and how the refinery can receive it."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.5.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Crude/Feed Transportation Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers feedstock transportation planning: coordinating and monitoring movements for purchased volumes under the applicable carrier and capacity arrangements (CM-1-2-5-3-1); scheduling deliveries into agreed refinery receipt windows (CM-1-2-5-3-2); and planning arrival timing and sequence with the refinery and terminal operations owners (CM-1-2-5-3-3). Operates against carrier contracts administered under Manage Carrier Contracts (CM-1-2-1-2-9) and transportation deals captured under CM-1-2-1-3-9; movement actuals are recorded by Actualizations (CM-1-2-3-1); transportation charges settle under the mode settlement processes (CM-1-2-4-1); delay-compensation claims follow Demurrage Management (CM-1-2-4-4); physical movement execution belongs to carriers and operators. Excludes carrier contracting (CM-1-2-1-2-9); transportation deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3-9); actualization (CM-1-2-3-1); freight settlement and demurrage (CM-1-2-4-1, CM-1-2-4-4); physical movement execution (carriers, operators); product distribution (CM-1-1-3, locked SCM boundary).
In scope: Coordinate feedstock movements across modes against the supply plan | schedule deliveries into agreed refinery receipt windows | plan arrivals with refinery and terminal operations owners | adjust movements as plans and conditions change
Out of scope: Carrier contracting (CM-1-2-1-2-9) | transportation deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3-9) | actualization (CM-1-2-3-1) | freight settlement and demurrage (CM-1-2-4-1, CM-1-2-4-4) | physical execution (carriers, operators) | product distribution (CM-1-1-3)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Agreed purchases and supply plan (CM-1-2-5-2-1) | carrier terms and capacity (CM-1-2-1-2-9) | refinery receipt constraints and tankage positions"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Planned, scheduled feedstock movements and arrival plans"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Get purchased feedstock to the refinery gate at the right time, mode, and cost."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "weekly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-ORP-DPSC-001 | SRC-CMU-REFPLAN-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Coordinate Feedstock Trading | uses-input: Manage Carrier Contracts | precedes: Actualizations"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 18 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one per-movement transport-planning lifecycle (coordinate, schedule, plan arrival), the batch 13 variant test. Inbound-feedstock counterpart of the product-side distribution planning in CM-1-1-3 under the locked SCM boundary. APQC: no meaningful candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-6 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2 ;
    skos:definition "Refined product supply chain management consist of processes that are required to maintain an optimal supply network. This covers the generation of the demand forecast, associated customer allocations, the demand & supply balancing process & the execution of terminal replenishment."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Refined Product Supply Chain Management"@en .

proc:CM-1-2-6-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12899" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12899"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Supply Network Participation Analysis"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-6 ;
    skos:definition "Analyze the refined-product supply network's market participation — segmenting customers and markets on supply economics, evaluating market attractiveness for supply-network participation, and assessing cost-to-serve performance — and recommend where and whom the network should serve profitably, informing the network and participation decisions of the accountable commercial and planning owners."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.6.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Network Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the market-participation analysis cycle: supply-economics customer and market segmentation (CM-1-2-6-1-1), market attractiveness evaluation (CM-1-2-6-1-2), and cost-to-serve assessment (CM-1-2-6-1-3), combined into recommendations on network participation, service levels, and supply-envelope choices. Analyze-and-recommend (batch 11 pattern): supply, production, inventory, and allocation decisions are owned by Regional Optimization (CM-1-1-2, batch 03 rule); commercial and brand segmentation, pricing, and customer strategy by the Marketing processes (CM-1-3) where defined; network-investment decisions by their accountable owners. Excludes supply and allocation decisions (CM-1-1-2); commercial segmentation, pricing, and customer strategy (CM-1-3); network investment decisions (accountable owners); demand forecasting (CM-1-2-6-2-3, CM-1-1-1-1).
In scope: Segment customers and markets on supply economics | evaluate market attractiveness for supply-network participation | assess cost-to-serve performance | recommend network participation and service choices to accountable owners
Out of scope: Supply and allocation decisions (CM-1-1-2) | commercial segmentation, pricing, and customer strategy (CM-1-3) | network investment decisions (accountable owners) | demand forecasting (CM-1-2-6-2-3, CM-1-1-1-1)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Sales, volume, and margin history | supply and logistics cost data | market and competitor intelligence"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Network participation and service recommendations with segmentation and cost-to-serve evidence"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Know which markets and customers the supply network serves profitably — and recommend accordingly."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "periodic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-LNL-T2S-001 | SRC-ORP-DPSC-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Regional Optimization | informed-by: Assess Cost-to-Serve Performance | enables: Refined Product Demand Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 19 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one periodic analysis cycle (segment, evaluate, assess, recommend). Q5 decision: supply-economics analysis, distinct from Marketing's commercial segmentation; analyze-and-recommend with decisions at the accountable owners. Normalized label 'Supply Network Participation Analysis' queued — the current label sounds like network design/operations ownership (Hamid, batch 19). APQC candidates are WEAK generic matches."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-6-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19697" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19697"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Operational Product Demand Management"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-6 ;
    skos:definition "Manage refined-product demand on the supply side — maintaining the operational demand picture the supply chain runs on, forecasting location-level product demand for supply operations, and managing product-exchange volumes as a balancing lever — so supply operations, replenishment, and trading coordination work from one current, operational demand view."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.6.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Refined Product Demand Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the operational demand cycle: managing the current demand picture against supply capability (CM-1-2-6-2-1), operational location-level demand forecasting for the supply-chain context (CM-1-2-6-2-3, under the batch 03 constraint), and operational management of product-exchange volumes as a supply and demand balancing lever (CM-1-2-6-2-2). The enterprise published demand forecast is owned by Demand Forecasting (CM-1-1-1-1) and consumed here as an input; trading's forward-view intake is Receive Demand Forecast (CM-1-2-1-1-2); the refinery-planning demand basis is Capture Product Demand Requirements (CM-1-1-4-7-1). Excludes the enterprise published forecast (CM-1-1-1-1, batch 03); trading forecast intake (CM-1-2-1-1-2); the refinery demand basis (CM-1-1-4-7-1); order capture and commitment (CM-1-3-7); supply and allocation decisions (CM-1-1-2).
In scope: Maintain the operational demand picture against supply capability | forecast location-level demand for supply operations | manage exchange volumes as a balancing lever | supply one operational demand view to supply operations and trading coordination
Out of scope: Enterprise published forecast (CM-1-1-1-1) | trading forecast intake (CM-1-2-1-1-2) | refinery demand basis (CM-1-1-4-7-1) | order capture (CM-1-3-7) | supply and allocation decisions (CM-1-1-2)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Published enterprise forecast (CM-1-1-1-1) | orders and lifting patterns | exchange positions and entitlements | supply capability"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Operational demand picture, location-level forecasts, and exchange-utilization plans"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "One current operational demand view behind supply operations, replenishment, and trading coordination."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "weekly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EIA-STEO-001 | SRC-ORP-DPSC-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Demand Forecasting | enables: Supply Operations | enables: Coordinate Product Trading"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 19 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one operational demand cycle. Batch 03 three-way forecast partition implemented: enterprise published forecast (CM-1-1-1-1), trading intake (CM-1-2-1-1-2), operational SCM-context forecasting here. APQC candidate is an unrelated documentation match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-6-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19948" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19948"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "S&T Product Supply Fulfillment"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-6 ;
    skos:definition "Fulfill approved refined-product replenishment and supply requirements from Supply & Trading sources by coordinating product availability at purchased-supply, exchange, transfer, and third-party source points; confirming feasible supply and transport arrangements; and handing approved movements to the processes that schedule, actualize, and settle them."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.6.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Supply Operations"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the S&T-sourced supply-fulfillment cycle under the Q2 (Option A) reading: coordinating commercial supply status and availability at source points where Supply And Trading supplies the network — purchased volumes, exchange locations, transfers, third-party terminals (CM-1-2-6-3-1); fulfilling network replenishment requirements from traded sources, consuming the replenishment decisions owned by Manage Inventory Replenishment (CM-1-1-3-7-9, batch 04) (CM-1-2-6-3-2); and coordinating transportation arrangements for S&T-sourced primary movements (CM-1-2-6-3-3). Supply Operations may identify feasible commercial sources and communicate fulfillment constraints, but it does not decide network replenishment quantity or timing, allocate constrained product, execute trades, administer primary movements, or direct physical operations. The complementarity rule: Physical Distribution Scheduling (CM-1-1-3-6, batch 05) owns primary-movement nominations and shipper-side administration; Secondary Distribution Management (CM-1-1-3-8) owns terminal-to-customer delivery; this cluster supplies the network from commercial sources and provides its movements to the owning scheduling, actualization, and settlement processes. Excludes replenishment decisions (CM-1-1-3-7-9, batch 04); allocation decisions (CM-1-1-2); primary movement scheduling and administration (CM-1-1-3-6, batch 05); secondary distribution (CM-1-1-3-8); trade execution (CM-1-2-1-3); exchange agreement administration (CM-1-2-7-1-1); physical terminal and transport operations (operators, carriers, Midstream).
In scope: Coordinate commercial supply status at S&T-supplied source points | fulfill approved replenishment requirements from traded, exchanged, and transferred product | confirm feasible supply and transport arrangements | hand approved movements to the owning scheduling, actualization, and settlement processes
Out of scope: Replenishment decisions (CM-1-1-3-7-9) | allocation decisions (CM-1-1-2) | primary movement scheduling (CM-1-1-3-6) | secondary distribution (CM-1-1-3-8) | trade execution (CM-1-2-1-3) | exchange administration (CM-1-2-7-1-1) | physical operations (operators, carriers, Midstream)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Replenishment requirements (CM-1-1-3-7-9) | operational demand and exchange plans (CM-1-2-6-2) | agreed purchases and transfers (CM-1-2-1-3)"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Fulfilled supply requirements handed to the owning scheduling and actualization processes"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "The network reliably supplied from commercial sources, with every decision right left where earlier batches put it."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "weekly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-ORP-DPSC-001 | SRC-LNL-T2S-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "uses-input: Refined Product Demand Management | uses-input: Manage Inventory Replenishment | precedes: Physical Distribution Scheduling"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 19 (2026-09-20). Q2 decision (Hamid, Option A with fulfillment language): commercial-source fulfillment and supply coordination — never a parallel scheduling or dispatch organization; 'operate' wording removed because it can imply terminal-operations or dispatch ownership. The product-side mirror of the batch 18 feedstock logistics cluster, complementing the batch 04 replenishment decision and batch 05 distribution scheduling ownerships. Concept-type: process — one fulfillment cycle. APQC candidate is an unrelated internal-operations match."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-7-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12895" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12895"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Product Commercial Agreements"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-7 ;
    skos:definition "Negotiate, maintain commercial requirements for, and coordinate lifecycle changes to product-side agreements that enable product exchanges, customer delivery commitments, and storage/throughput access, so dependent supply, distribution, and settlement processes operate against current commercially agreed terms."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.7.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Contractual Agreements Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the commercial-agreement lifecycle for three product-side agreement families: product exchange agreements including their terms, entitlement rules, and contractual imbalance treatment (CM-1-2-7-1-1); customer-facing product transport and delivery agreements (CM-1-2-7-1-2, under the batch 09 boundary rule); and negotiation of storage and throughput agreements (CM-1-2-7-1-3, under the batch 09 storage split). The handoff model: commercial negotiation and commercial-requirement ownership sit in this cluster; legal drafting, legal review, and execution authority with Legal & Corporate Communications under its locked rule; the controlled contract record, document lifecycle, and renewal/termination/novation administration with Contract Management (CM-1-2-1-2) where the agreement is within its trading-contract scope; deal capture with Trade Capture (CM-1-2-1-3); operational use with Supply Operations, Distribution, and the relevant operating domains; settlement with Settlements (CM-1-2-4-1). Excludes trading contract administration and the controlled contract record (CM-1-2-1-2); legal services and execution authority (Legal & Corporate Communications, locked rule); deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3); settlement (CM-1-2-4-1); operational exchange utilization (CM-1-2-6-2-2, batch 19).
In scope: Negotiate product exchange agreements with entitlement and imbalance terms | negotiate and maintain commercial requirements for customer-facing delivery agreements | negotiate storage and throughput agreements | coordinate lifecycle changes and keep dependent processes supplied with current terms
Out of scope: Contract record and lifecycle administration (CM-1-2-1-2) | legal services and execution authority (Legal & Corporate Communications, locked rule) | deal capture (CM-1-2-1-3) | settlement (CM-1-2-4-1) | operational exchange utilization (CM-1-2-6-2-2)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Commercial requirements from demand, supply, and network processes | counterparty proposals | legal review (Legal & Corporate Communications)"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Negotiated product-side commercial agreements with maintained commercial requirements"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every exchange, delivery, and storage arrangement running on current, correct, commercially negotiated terms."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EIA-GLOSS-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Manage Refined Product Exchanges | enables: Supply Operations | informed-by: Contract Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 20 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one agreement-lifecycle discipline across three agreement families (batch 09 pattern). Handoff model (Hamid, batch 20): negotiation and commercial requirements here; record administration with Contract Management where in trading scope — the two never both 'administer' the same lifecycle. Receiving end of the batch 09 exclusions and the batch 08/14/19 exchange bindings. APQC 'Establish lifting agreements' scored NONE; comparative only."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-7-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19948" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19948"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Company-Managed Terminal & Fleet Operations"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-7 ;
    skos:definition "The capability to operate and maintain company-managed marketing and distribution terminals, loading facilities, and associated distribution fleet assets where the business unit is the designated operating entity, including site safety execution, fleet capability management, and site-asset integrity and availability."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.7.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Terminal Operations Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the management of company-managed marketing and distribution terminal and fleet operations under the Q2 reading: site safety management (CM-1-2-7-2-1), fleet management (CM-1-2-7-2-2), and site asset management (CM-1-2-7-2-3), applied where the Marketing/Distribution business is the designated operating entity. Asset ownership alone does not determine scope: this capability applies only where the Marketing/Distribution business is operationally accountable for the site or fleet — Refining-operated, Midstream-operated, third-party-operated, and centrally operated assets remain with their designated operating domains or providers, and where the company owns but does not operate a facility, its interest is held through agreements (CM-1-2-7-1-3), source-point coordination (CM-1-2-6-3-1), storage administration (CM-1-1-3-7-11), and settlement (CM-1-2-4-1-14). The capability is located in the Supply & Trading taxonomy because it supports marketing/distribution supply operations; its operational delivery may sit in a dedicated terminal operations, distribution operations, fleet, Midstream, engineering, or shared-services organization, depending on asset ownership and operating accountability. Scheduling remains with its owners (CM-1-1-3-6, CM-1-1-3-8, batch 05); EHS policy, regulatory reporting, and filings with EHS & Government Reporting under its locked rule. Excludes Refining-, Midstream-, centrally-, and third-party-operated assets (their designated operators, locked rules); movement and delivery scheduling (CM-1-1-3-6, CM-1-1-3-8); EHS policy and regulatory reporting (EHS & Government Reporting, locked rule); storage/terminal commercial agreements (CM-1-2-7-1-3).
In scope: Operate site safety programs within the enterprise EHS framework at company-managed sites | manage own and contracted fleet capability and compliance | manage site-asset integrity coordination and availability | keep company-managed operations safe, compliant, and available
Out of scope: Refining-, Midstream-, centrally-, and third-party-operated assets (designated operators, locked rules) | movement and delivery scheduling (CM-1-1-3-6, CM-1-1-3-8) | EHS policy and regulatory reporting (EHS & Government Reporting, locked rule) | storage/terminal agreements (CM-1-2-7-1-3)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "capability"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Enterprise EHS framework and policies | fleet and asset condition data | supply and distribution requirements"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Safely and compliantly operated company-managed terminals and fleet"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Company-managed terminals and fleet that are safe, compliant, and reliably available."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-OSHA-PSM-001 | SRC-EI-HM32-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Secondary Distribution Management | enables: Supply Operations | governed-by: Develop Compliance Policies & Procedures"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 20 (2026-09-20). Q2 decision (Hamid, narrowed): this capability applies where the company is the designated operator — never categorically 'the terminal operator', since the same legal entity may own a facility operated by a third party, Midstream, Refining, a joint venture, or a terminal services company. Every prior operator exclusion is preserved: for facilities operated elsewhere, the operator remains external to this cluster. Concept-type: capability per the batch 11 test. Which terminals, tanks, racks, and fleet assets are actually company-managed by Marketing/Distribution is an asset-inventory follow-up carried in the batch 20 PR. APQC 'Analyze internal operations' scored NONE; comparative only."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-2-5 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19959" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19959"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-2 ;
    skos:definition "Develop and maintain the approved customer-experience strategy for the company's downstream customer and consumer interactions — the experience aspiration, guiding principles, and strategic pillars that every channel, offer, and service interaction is designed against — through a recurring strategy cycle grounded in governed consumer and customer evidence."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.2.5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Customer Experience Strategy"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the experience-strategy lifecycle: identifying the customer-experience aspiration (CM-1-3-2-5-1), defining experience guiding principles (CM-1-3-2-5-2), and establishing the strategic pillars (CM-1-3-2-5-3). Develop and maintain the approved customer-experience strategy by consolidating the aspiration, principles, and pillars into a governed strategic direction issued to design, service, channel, and execution processes. 'Customer' includes consumers, commercial customers, channel partners, and marketers where they interact with the company's products, services, channels, support, digital touchpoints, and operational processes. The experience promise must be consistent with the value propositions (CM-1-3-2-2/-3) and brand strategy (CM-1-3-2-1). Marketing Strategy develops and maintains approved brand, value-proposition, channel, and customer-experience direction using governed evidence from Marketing Analysis. It does not perform the analyses; design detailed offers, prices, channels, networks, communications, or operating models; plan campaigns and sales; or execute channel, sales, brand, loyalty, or service programs. Material strategic changes follow the applicable enterprise delegation, investment, legal, and governance approvals. Strategy may set the strategic role of price in the proposition or channel — such as value, premium, parity, transparency, index linkage, or loyalty emphasis — but does not establish pricing strategy, price architecture, price rules, price levels, discounts, formula terms, or transaction prices. Those activities belong to Pricing Strategy Management and Sales Pricing. Strategy only: experience delivery sits with the channel, service, and execution processes (CM-1-3-6, CM-1-3-9); experience measurement with Service & Support Measurement (CM-1-3-9-3) and Marketing Performance Analysis (CM-1-3-5-5); experience evidence from Consumer and Customer/Marketer Analysis under their privacy controls. Excludes experience delivery (CM-1-3-6, CM-1-3-9); experience measurement (CM-1-3-9-3, CM-1-3-5-5); the analyses (CM-1-3-1-3/-4); loyalty operation (CM-1-3-6-6).
In scope: Run the recurring experience-strategy cycle | set the experience aspiration, principles, and pillars | consolidate them into the approved, governed experience strategy and maintain it | keep the experience promise consistent with brand and value propositions | issue experience direction to design, service, and execution
Out of scope: Experience delivery (CM-1-3-6, CM-1-3-9) | experience measurement (CM-1-3-9-3, CM-1-3-5-5) | the analyses (CM-1-3-1-3/-4) | loyalty operation (CM-1-3-6-6) | pricing strategy, rules, and levels (CM-1-3-3-2, CM-1-3-6-3)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Consumer and customer/marketer findings (CM-1-3-1-3/-4) | value propositions (CM-1-3-2-2/-3) | brand direction (CM-1-3-2-1)"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "The approved, maintained customer-experience strategy (aspiration, principles, pillars)"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Every customer interaction designed against one deliberate, approved experience promise."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "strategic"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Consumer Analysis | informed-by: Customer And Marketer Analysis | enables: Service & Support Enablement"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 22 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process — one aspiration-to-strategy lifecycle. Lifecycle order per review: Identify Customer Experience Aspiration → Define Customer Experience Guiding Principles → Establish Customer Experience Strategic Pillars → develop and maintain the approved customer-experience strategy at this L4 (explicit strategy-maintenance output added per review, so the L4 is a strategy node, not a container). Cross-audience per review: 'Customer' definition verbatim in scope note — consumers, commercial customers, channel partners, and marketers at their interaction points. Experience evidence under batch 21 privacy controls. Batch 22 durable rule and price-role boundary recorded verbatim in scope note. APQC 'Develop customer experience strategy' scored 0.857 STRONG — the branch's strongest genuine APQC anchor; no validator agreement recorded, treated as comparative anchor."@en .

