proc:CM-1-1-7-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10303" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10303"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Refinery Production Scheduling"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-7 ;
    skos:definition "Develop and maintain the refinery production schedule that implements the approved monthly plan — sequencing campaigns, schedule-level unit commitments, blends, and movements over the scheduling horizon, managing schedule exceptions, and coordinating the interfaces the schedule depends on — so the plan becomes a current schedule that provides the approved basis for refinery execution."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.7.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Production Scheduling"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the refinery scheduling layer between planning and operations: maintaining scheduling master data, reviewing the production wheel and run lengths, creating the dated sequencing plan, managing exceptions, measuring scheduling performance, and managing interfaces to planning, operations, and engineering and maintenance. Under the approved planning-scheduling-operations hierarchy, Refining owns detailed unit-operation scheduling and all operational execution; this L4 owns the production schedule that provides the approved basis for that execution. Excludes the monthly plan itself, owned by Refinery Planning (CM-1-1-4); excludes the daily scheduling basis, owned by Daily Execution Planning (CM-1-1-7-1).
In scope: Maintain the scheduling basis and master data | maintain the production wheel and run lengths | create and refresh the dated sequencing plan | manage schedule exceptions and interfaces | measure scheduling performance
Out of scope: Monthly production plan (CM-1-1-4) | unit-operation scheduling and execution (Refining) | daily instruction issuance (CM-1-1-7-1)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "approved monthly plan | production wheel and constraints | outage and turnaround windows | execution feedback"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "current production schedule providing the approved basis for execution"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Turn the approved monthly plan into a current schedule that provides the approved basis for refinery execution."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "weekly"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-BJCE-REFPS-001 | SRC-SNF-REFPS-001 | SRC-CMU-REFPLAN-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Refinery Planning | precedes: Daily Execution Planning"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 06 (2026-09-19). Q1 decision (Hamid, 2026-09-19): the refinery planning-scheduling-operations hierarchy is approved. CM-1-1-7 owns the refinery production-scheduling layer: translating approved refinery plans into dated, feasible campaign, production, movement, and resource schedules; managing schedule exceptions and cross-functional interfaces; and maintaining schedule-level performance visibility. Refining owns detailed unit-operation scheduling and all operational execution, including process-control decisions, line-ups, transfers, blend execution, utility operation, and immediate safety/reliability departures from the schedule. Supported by the planning/scheduling/operations separation documented in SNF R29/08 and Moro (BJCE); the locked rule's 'detailed unit-operation scheduling' is the operations layer, not this one. APQC 10303 is a WEAK generic candidate; comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19569" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19569"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Pre-Trade Forecasting & Optimization"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1 ;
    skos:definition "The front-office capability to assemble and maintain a forward commercial view of supply, demand, generation, renewables, market conditions, and revenue, and to identify how the asset portfolio can be used commercially within approved plans and constraints."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Forecasting & Optimization"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers receiving and consolidating production, demand, and power-generation forecasts from their owning processes; producing the renewables forward view; identifying and valuing commercial use of asset flexibility; and projecting trading revenue, margin, and exposure. This capability consumes forecasts rather than owning their production: the approved demand forecast is owned by Demand Forecasting (CM-1-1-1-1) per the batch 03 decision, and production plans and schedules by Refinery Planning (CM-1-1-4) and Refinery Scheduling (CM-1-1-7). Excludes trade execution and capture, owned by Trade Capture (CM-1-2-1-3); excludes setting the regional or refinery plans, owned under the locked Regional Optimization and Refinery Planning rules; excludes risk measurement and limits, owned by Market Risk Management (CM-1-2-2-3).
In scope: Receive and consolidate production, demand, and generation forecasts | develop the renewables forward view | identify and value commercial use of asset flexibility | project trading revenue, margin, and exposure
Out of scope: Forecast production ownership (CM-1-1-1-1, CM-1-1-4, CM-1-1-7) | trade execution and capture (CM-1-2-1-3) | regional and refinery plan setting (locked rules) | risk measurement and limits (CM-1-2-2-3)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "capability"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "published demand forecast | production plans and schedules | generation forecasts | market prices and forward curves"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "current trading forward view and asset-optimization recommendations"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Keep trading running on a current forward commercial view and an identified, valued set of asset opportunities."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001 | SRC-EIA-STEO-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "informed-by: Demand Forecasting | informed-by: Refinery Planning | enables: Trading Strategy Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 07 (2026-09-19). Q1 decision (Hamid, Option A): capability — it comprises distinct forecast-intake, renewable-position forecasting, asset-optimization, and revenue-projection processes that collectively enable a current front-office forward view and commercially optimized asset position; it is not a single sequential process. Same test as the batch 04 Inventory L4. Front-office framing per the approved Trading Management (CM-1-2-1) definition; 'market conditions' made explicit at Hamid's direction so the forward-curve context is not read as physical volumes only. Q2 boundary recorded on CM-1-2-1-1-4. APQC 19569 'Perform real-time optimization' is process-control software; no meaningful candidate."@en .

