    skos:prefLabel "Marketing"@en .

proc:CM-1-3-5-3 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-5 ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.5.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Marketing Communications Planning"@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20682" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20682"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Counterparty & Contract Administration"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1 ;
    skos:definition "Administer trading counterparties and commercial agreements through their lifecycle — onboarding and maintaining counterparty records; establishing, maintaining, terminating, and novating master agreements, standing agreements, and relevant transaction frameworks; managing specialized agreement types; and monitoring contractual obligations — so trading occurs with authorized counterparties under current, governed terms."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Contract Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the controlled lifecycle administration of trading counterparties (create, maintain, terminate or novate) and trading agreements (create, maintain, terminate or novate), the specialized administration of lease, carrier, and storage contracts, the counterparty and agreement consequences of merger-and-acquisition events, and contract monitoring. Accommodates physical contracts, master agreements such as ISDA-type frameworks, transaction confirmations governed by a master agreement, capacity and lease arrangements, and spot transactions governed by standard terms or market rules rather than a bespoke contract record. Excludes legal drafting, legal review, and legal-risk management, owned by Legal & Corporate Communications under its locked rule; excludes credit reviews, limits, and collateral, owned by Credit Risk Management (CM-1-2-2-1); excludes commercial negotiation of agreements where a negotiating owner exists (for example Negotiate Storage / Throughput Agreements, CM-1-2-7-1-3); excludes deal capture (Trade Capture, CM-1-2-1-3) and deal-level confirmation (CM-1-2-2-2).
In scope: Onboard, maintain, and terminate or novate counterparties | establish, maintain, and terminate or novate agreements and master agreements | administer lease, carrier, and storage contract specializations | handle M&A consequences for counterparties and agreements | monitor contractual obligations
Out of scope: Legal services and legal-risk management (Legal & Corporate Communications) | credit reviews, limits, and collateral (CM-1-2-2-1) | agreement negotiation where separately owned (CM-1-2-7-1) | deal capture and confirmation (CM-1-2-1-3, CM-1-2-2-2)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "counterparty applications and due-diligence results | negotiated agreement terms | corporate event notices | contract obligations and calendars"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "governed counterparty and agreement records through their lifecycle"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Trading occurs with authorized counterparties under current, governed terms."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001 | SRC-ISDA-MA-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Trade Capture | enables: Confirmations Management | governed-by: Credit Risk Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 09 (2026-09-19). Q1 decision (Hamid, Option A): process — lifecycle stages applied to two entity kinds plus type variants and control activities, per the batch 08 variant test. This taxonomy locates the commercial lifecycle-administration process under Trading Management; actual execution may involve Trading, Credit Risk, Regulatory & Compliance, Legal, Finance Operations, or Shared Services according to the enterprise operating model. The negotiate, administer, capture, use/actualize/settle pattern from batch 08 is a taxonomy heuristic, not a universal sequential lifecycle: some agreements are frameworks rather than tradable deals, some deals execute under existing agreements, and some contractual rights are used without a separately captured transaction. APQC 20682 is an unrelated IT candidate."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-1-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "14095" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 14095"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Deal Capture"@en,
        "Trade Entry & Reporting"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-1 ;
    skos:definition "Perform the controlled pre-trade analysis, authorization, and post-execution recording of trades in the trading system — capturing complete commercial, operational, and regulatory terms by deal type and submitting required creation-data reports — so authorized transactions are available to confirmation, risk, actualization, settlement, and accounting processes."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.1.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Trade Capture"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the per-transaction lifecycle: analyzing prospective deals, obtaining approval within the delegation of authority, capturing executed physical, exchange, storage, environmental, financial, structured, transportation, and transmission deals after execution, and reporting creation data to swap data repositories where an applicable regime requires it. Trading strategies and approved asset-optimization opportunities inform prospective transactions; the authorized trader or delegated execution role executes a transaction only after required pre-trade controls and approvals have been satisfied — capture is the controlled record-creation activity, not the act of purchasing or selling. Excludes trade confirmation and matching with counterparties, owned by Confirmations Management (CM-1-2-2-2); excludes risk measurement and limits (Market Risk Management, CM-1-2-2-3); excludes actualization (CM-1-2-3-1) and settlement (CM-1-2-4-1); excludes counterparty and contract lifecycle administration (Contract Management, CM-1-2-1-2).
In scope: Analyze prospective deals before execution | obtain trade approval within the delegation of authority | capture executed deals by type with full commercial, operational, and regulatory terms | report creation data where an applicable regime requires it
Out of scope: Trade execution as an act of purchasing or selling (authorized trader or delegated execution role) | strategy definition (CM-1-2-1-4, CM-1-2-1-1-4) | confirmation and matching (CM-1-2-2-2) | risk measurement and limits (CM-1-2-2-3) | actualization and settlement (CM-1-2-3-1, CM-1-2-4-1) | counterparty and contract administration (CM-1-2-1-2)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "approved strategies and recommendations | executed transaction terms | delegation of authority | applicable reporting rules"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "authorized, complete, reported deal records available to downstream processes"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Get every executed trade authorized, completely recorded, and reported where required, once and correctly."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Trading Strategy Management | precedes: Confirmations Management | enables: Market Risk Management"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 08 (2026-09-19). Q1 decision (Hamid, Option A): process — the children form a per-transaction lifecycle (analyze, authorize, capture by deal type, report) with the eight capture nodes as instrument-specific variants of one controlled deal-entry activity; the Demand Forecasting process precedent applies, not the batch 04 capability test. Reworded at Hamid's direction to distinguish trade execution from trade capture: this process controls what happens before, at, and immediately after execution and records the trade; it does not execute it. Whether the enterprise's system of record supports a single deal record or a governed golden-deal model across systems is a follow-up noted in the batch 08 PR. APQC 14095 'Document trade' is a WEAK international-trade candidate; comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-3-7 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "12900" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 12900"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Inventory Management"@en,
        "Stock Management"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1-3 ;
    skos:definition "The capability to govern, measure, reconcile, control, position, replenish, and account for petroleum feedstocks, intermediates, and refined products across the downstream network, including their quantity, quality, location, ownership, entitlement, identity, and availability."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.3.7" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Inventory"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers development and maintenance of inventory policies by material class; physical inventory measurement and the volumetric inputs to valuation; book-to-physical reconciliation; replenishment management; and stewardship of inventory held under consignment, commingling, third-party-storage, lot/batch, and re-brand arrangements, including controls over ownership, entitlement, product identity, custody, quality, location, and availability. Excludes setting plan-period finished-product inventory targets inside the regional plan, owned by Plan Finished Goods Inventory (CM-1-1-2-13); excludes physical tank, terminal, and pipeline operation, owned by Midstream; excludes inventory valuation, accounting, and financial reporting, owned by Finance; excludes day-to-day crude and refinery-feedstock position management, owned by Manage Feedstock Inventory (CM-1-2-5-4-2) per the batch 04 decision.
In scope: Inventory policy by material class | physical inventory measurement and volumetric inputs to valuation | book-to-physical reconciliation | replenishment management | consignment, commingled, re-brand, lot/batch, and third-party storage stewardship
Out of scope: Plan-period inventory targets in the regional plan (Plan Finished Goods Inventory, CM-1-1-2-13) | physical storage and movement operation (Midstream) | inventory valuation, accounting, and financial reporting (Finance) | day-to-day feedstock position management (Manage Feedstock Inventory, CM-1-2-5-4-2) | trading inventory risk management (Supply And Trading)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "capability"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "approved regional and distribution plans | measured tank and meter data | movement and transaction records | storage and consignment agreements"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "governed, reconciled inventory positions and policies for the distribution network"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Ensure the network's stock positions are governed by policy, measured accurately, reconciled, and managed across ownership and storage arrangements."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-EI-HM32-001 | SRC-API-MPMS-001 | SRC-ASCM-CSCP-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "enables: Regional Optimization | enables: Capture Inventory | informed-by: Plan Finished Goods Inventory"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 04 (2026-09-19). Q1 decision (Hamid, Option A): capability, not process — the children are distinct recurring processes that jointly enable inventory stewardship, not stages of one cycle. The bare-noun label can denote the asset/state, a count, or the function; this node denotes the capability. Preferred normalized label 'Inventory Management' queued for the consolidated naming pass; current label retained for batch traceability, together with the L4-versus-L5 'Manage Inventory' label collision. Placement rule: Supply Chain Management answers what should be held, moved, made, or replenished, where and when; this capability answers what inventory exists, under what identity, ownership, and entitlement, in what quantity and location, and whether it is controlled and reconciled; Midstream answers how bulk material is physically received, stored, transferred, and transported. Feedstock boundary constraint (Q3 decision, Hamid, Option A): Manage Feedstock Inventory (CM-1-2-5-4-2) owns the day-to-day monitoring, control, and operational management of crude and refinery-feedstock inventory positions within Crude/Feed Supply Management; this cluster owns cross-material inventory policy, measurement, reconciliation, and specialized inventory-arrangement controls, and does not own day-to-day feedstock position management. A reciprocal note is recorded on CM-1-2-5-4-2. Hydrocarbon loss-control ownership beyond reconciliation reporting has no home in the current tree; playbook step 7 carries that question — deliberately not resolved here."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "10774" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 10774"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Product Control"@en,
        "Trading Accounting"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4 ;
    skos:definition "The capability to record, value, reconcile, and report the financial effects of supply and trading activity by maintaining controlled sub-ledger and general-ledger records; booking authorized settlement outcomes, receivables, payables, actuals, accruals, taxes, and interest obligations; reconciling economic P&L to accounting P&L; and producing controlled financial evidence and reporting within the enterprise accounting-policy framework."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Accounting"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the trading and supply accounting capability: transaction-level treatment assessment, sub-ledger and GL recording, AR/AP and actuals booking from authorized settlement outcomes (batch 14 Q3 chain), cash application, accruals, financial production and inventory accounting, royalty and interest disbursement execution, economic-to-accounting P&L reconciliation using the controlled economic inputs delivered by Market Risk at end of month (batch 12), transaction and commodity tax execution, financial documentation and reporting, financial system and ledger-structure administration, and trading-specific accounting procedures. This capability supports Supply & Trading and may be operated by Product Control, Trading Accounting, Finance Operations, Accounting, Shared Services, or another designated accounting function; the taxonomy placement does not prescribe the accountable legal, finance, or organizational owner. Operates within the enterprise accounting policy framework: financial write-off approval, valuation policy, material technical-accounting conclusions, and enterprise accounting policy are owned by Finance under the locked Finance rule; payment release and bank execution by Treasury and banking processes; settlement calculation and invoice validation by Settlements (CM-1-2-4-1); the refinery operational material balance by Perform Production Accounting (CM-1-1-7-1-3); network book-to-physical stock reconciliation by Perform Inventory Reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5).
In scope: Book, value, and reconcile trading activity in sub-ledger and GL | apply cash, accrue, and post actuals from authorized settlement outcomes | reconcile economic P&L to accounting P&L | assess treatments, execute transaction taxes, and produce controlled financial documentation
Out of scope: Enterprise accounting policy, valuation policy, material technical-accounting conclusions, and write-off approval (Finance, locked rule) | payment release and bank execution (Treasury/banking) | settlement calculation and invoicing (CM-1-2-4-1) | refinery material balance (CM-1-1-7-1-3) | network stock reconciliation (CM-1-1-3-7-5) | credit exposure and limits (CM-1-2-2-1)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "capability"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Authorized settlement outcomes (CM-1-2-4-1) | controlled economic P&L inputs at EOM (CM-1-2-2-3-5) | operational production and inventory balances (CM-1-1-7-1-3, CM-1-1-3-7-5) | enterprise accounting policy (Finance)"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Complete, reconciled trading financial records, postings, and controlled financial reporting"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Give supply and trading activity a complete, controlled, and reconciled financial record within enterprise policy."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-FASB-ASC815-001 | SRC-APQC-PCF-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Settlements | uses-input: Perform End-of-Month (EOM) Process | governed-by: Develop Accounting Policies & Procedures"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Finance-enabled Supply & Trading"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 15 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: capability per the batch 11/12 test — heterogeneous concurrent record-and-control functions, not one per-transaction lifecycle. Organizational neutrality (Hamid, batch 15): responsible domain recorded as Finance-enabled Supply & Trading — a trading-accounting capability serving Supply & Trading, delivered through Product Control, Trading Accounting, Finance Operations, Accounting, Shared Services, or another designated function per the operating model; taxonomy branch placement under Commercial & Marketing does not prescribe the accountable owner. Execute-within-policy pattern: accounting executes within the Finance-owned policy framework, parallel to the batch 11 limits and batch 12 valuation-control precedents. 'P&L' in prose per the batch 12 convention. APQC 10774 is a WEAK financial-accounting candidate, comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-1-2 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19569" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19569"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-1 ;
    skos:definition "Optimize the integrated regional supply, demand, production, inventory, and product-movement plan to maximize commercial margin and reliably fulfill customer commitments within refinery, terminal, transportation, product-quality, and contractual constraints."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.1.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Regional Optimization"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Regional Optimization determines the commercially preferred regional supply-and-disposition plan, including demand allocation, supply sourcing, product placement, inventory positioning, exchanges, and inter-regional movements. It does not directly operate refinery units, terminals, pipelines, vessels, trucks, or retail/wholesale delivery activities; those functions provide capabilities, constraints, and execution feedback to the optimization cycle."@en .

proc:CM-1-2-2-3 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "20711" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 20711"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Trading Market Risk Control"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-2 ;
    skos:definition "The capability to independently measure, control, monitor, analyze, and report market risk in the trading book by governing risk-book structures, delegated authority, market-data inputs, valuations, limits, control cycles, positions, profit and loss, scenarios, and risk reporting."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.2.3" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Market Risk Management"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the independent market-risk control over the trading book: control-framework structures (risk-book structure, delegation of authority, risk limits), market-data and valuation ownership (curves, volatilities and correlations, commodity valuations), the daily and monthly control cycles, risk measurement and monitoring, position and P&L control, risk-policy compliance monitoring, scenarios, advanced analytics, and reporting. An independent control function per the approved L3 definition; the delivery model is not prescribed. Excludes enterprise-wide strategic commodity-price risk, corporate hedging policy, capital allocation, and non-trading operating exposure unless those exposures are expressly booked into the governed trading portfolio. Excludes trading decisions and strategy stewardship, which are governed by these controls (CM-1-2-1-4); excludes credit risk (CM-1-2-2-1); excludes regulatory trading compliance (CM-1-2-4-3); excludes accounting P&L and its reconciliation ownership (Accounting, CM-1-2-4-2, with the economic-to-accounting reconciliation at CM-1-2-4-2-6); excludes independent model validation (designated validation function).
In scope: Maintain risk-book structure, delegation of authority, and risk limits | own curves, volatilities, and valuations | run daily and monthly control cycles | measure, monitor, and analyze market risk, positions, and P&L | report the risk picture
Out of scope: Enterprise-wide commodity-price risk, corporate hedging policy, and non-trading exposure not booked into the trading portfolio | trading decisions and strategy stewardship (CM-1-2-1-4) | credit risk (CM-1-2-2-1) | regulatory trading compliance (CM-1-2-4-3) | accounting P&L (CM-1-2-4-2) | independent model validation (designated validation function)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "capability"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "captured positions | market prices and data | risk appetite and policy | governance decisions"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "measured, bounded, visible trading-book market risk with governed control structures"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Losses from market moves in the trading book are measured, bounded, and visible before they compound."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "continuous"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CCRO-002A | SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "governed-by: Regulatory & Compliance | enables: Trading Strategy Management | enables: Trade Capture"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 12 (2026-09-19). Concept type: capability (Hamid confirmed), per the batch 11 reasoning — concurrent heterogeneous control processes, no single lifecycle. Bound explicitly to the trading book at Hamid's direction: refinery-margin, procurement-price, inventory, and corporate hedging exposures are governed here only when expressly booked into the governed trading portfolio; whether a separate corporate hedging program exists is a follow-up noted in the batch 12 PR. Middle-office framing per the approved L3 definition names the control function, not a mandated organization (batch 10 neutrality). APQC 20711 is an IT-risk candidate; comparative evidence only."@en .

proc:CM-1-3-6-6 a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-3-6 ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.3.6.6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Loyalty And Cards Management - Marketing"@en .

proc:CM-1-2-4-1 a skos:Concept ;
    dcterms:references [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:identifier "19948" ;
            dcterms:isPartOf src:apqc-pcf-7.2.2 ;
            dcterms:title "APQC Downstream Petroleum PCF v7.2.2, element 19948"@en ] ;
    dcterms:source [ a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
            dcterms:title "Step 3c definition workbook, approved row"@en ] ;
    skos:altLabel "Commercial Settlement"@en,
        "Trade Settlement"@en ;
    skos:broader proc:CM-1-2-4 ;
    skos:definition "Determine and complete the financial settlement of trading transactions — calculating amounts owed and due from actualized quantities, contractual prices, and verified charge determinants; generating and validating invoices; netting where agreements provide; preparing, validating, and submitting authorized settlement instructions or payment requests to the designated Accounts Payable, Accounts Receivable, Treasury, banking, or shared-services process; tracking payment or receipt status; and retaining settlement evidence — so every transaction closes financially on verified actuals and contractual terms."@en ;
    skos:inScheme mod:core ;
    skos:notation "CM 1.2.4.1" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Settlements"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Covers the per-transaction settlement lifecycle across commodity, market-channel, and logistics-service variants: assembling actualized quantities and charge determinants, applying contractual and market prices and rates, calculating settlement amounts, generating outbound and validating inbound invoices and statements, netting under the governing agreements, preparing and submitting authorized settlement instructions and payment requests to the designated finance processes, tracking payment and receipt status, and retaining settlement evidence. Under the batch 10 partition, settlement answers 'what is owed, invoiced, received, paid, netted, or applied'. Consumes actualized records and charge determinants from Actualizations (CM-1-2-3-1) per the batch 13 charge-determinant rule. Where a contract bundles commodity value and logistics charges into one commercial settlement, the settlement process retains the contractual charge components and allocates them to the appropriate commodity and service settlement records where the accounting, tax, operational, or control model requires that distinction. Settlement does not independently approve payment release, initiate an unauthorized bank transfer, post accounting entries, apply cash, determine accounting treatment, or perform treasury cash management unless those authorities are expressly delegated in the enterprise operating model. Excludes actualization of quantities and determinants (CM-1-2-3-1); AR/AP booking, cash application, accruals, sub-ledger, general ledger, and tax, owned by Accounting (CM-1-2-4-2); payment release and bank execution (Treasury and banking processes per the enterprise operating model); financial write-offs and valuation (Finance, locked rule); demurrage claims (CM-1-2-4-4); credit exposure and limits (CM-1-2-2-1).
In scope: Calculate settlement amounts from actualized quantities, prices, and charge determinants | generate and validate invoices and settlement statements | net obligations under governing agreements | prepare, validate, and submit authorized settlement instructions and payment requests to the designated finance processes | track payment and receipt status and retain settlement evidence
Out of scope: Actualization (CM-1-2-3-1) | AR/AP booking, cash application, accruals, and GL (CM-1-2-4-2) | payment release and bank execution (Treasury/banking processes) | write-offs and valuation (Finance, locked rule) | demurrage claims (CM-1-2-4-4) | credit exposure and limits (CM-1-2-2-1)"""@en ;
    intake:conceptTypeCheck "process"@en ;
    intake:keyInputs "Actualized deal, movement, and entitlement records (CM-1-2-3-1) | contractual prices, rates, and netting terms | counterparty, carrier, exchange, broker, ISO, and registry invoices and statements"@en ;
    intake:primaryOutput "Settled transactions with validated invoices, netting, and authorized settlement instructions evidenced"@en ;
    intake:primaryPurpose "Close every trading transaction financially on verified actuals and contractual terms, with a controlled and evidenced trail from actual to authorized settlement instruction."@en ;
    intake:processHorizon "event-driven"@en ;
    intake:referenceSources "SRC-CFTC-GLOSS-001 | SRC-EIA-GLOSS-001"@en ;
    intake:relatedConcepts "follows: Actualizations | informed-by: Confirmations Management | enables: Manage AR/AP & Actuals"@en ;
    intake:responsibleDomain "Commercial & Marketing"@en ;
    intake:terminologyNotes "Batch 14 (2026-09-20). Concept-type: process per the batch 08/13 variant test — one settlement lifecycle across commodity, channel, and logistics-service variants. Q2 decision (Hamid): commodity-value settlement (value of the commodity or instrument, with trade counterparties) and logistics-service settlement (freight, tariff, charter hire, throughput, storage, handling, with carriers and operators) are distinct; one physical delivery may legitimately produce both; bundled delivered-price contracts are allocated per the scope-note rule. Q3 decision (Hamid): Settlements determines contractual amounts, validates invoices and statements, manages netting, and prepares/submits authorized settlement instructions; Accounting owns booking, cash application, accruals, and GL; Treasury/banking executes payment release and bank movement; Finance owns write-offs and accounting policy (locked rule); the operating model locates these authorities (organizational neutrality, batch 10). Dispute partition (batch 10): settlement disputes answer 'what is owed, invoiced, received, paid, netted, or applied'. APQC 19948 is an unrelated candidate."@en .

